lsd:Entity_1745 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "futurist architecture is an early_20th century form of architecture born in Italy, characterized by strong chromaticism, long dynamic lines, suggesting speed, motion, urgency and lyricism: it was a part of futurism, an artistic movement founded by the poet Filippo Tommaso Marinetti, who produced its first manifesto, the Manifesto of futurism, in 1909. The routine use of the term futurism – although influenced by Antonio Sant'Elia's vision of futurist architecture – must be well differentiated from the values and political implications of the Futurist movement of the years 1910–1920. The futurist architecture created since 1960 may be termed Neo_futurism, and is also referred as Post Modern futurism or Neo_Futuristic architecture." .

