lsd:Entity_22154 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the United Kingdom, the Results of the United Kingdom Alternative Vote referendum, 2011 also known as the United Kingdom_wide referendum on the Parliamentary voting system was a referendum that took place on 2011, on whether to change the system for electing the House of Commons, the lower house of the national Parliament at Westminster. Under the provisions of the Parliamentary Voting System and Constituencies Act 2011 there was a total of 440 voting areas across twelve regions using the same boundaries as used in European Parliamentary elections since 1999 under the provisions of the European Parliamentary Elections Act 2002 with the exception of Gibraltar which did not participate in the referendum. The national result for the whole United Kingdom was announced at the Platinum Suite at the Exhibition Centre London (ExCel) by the Chief counting officer (CCO) and Chair of the Electoral Commission, Jenny Watson, at 01:00 BST on Saturday 2011 after all 440 voting areas and United Kingdom regions had declared their results. Northern Ireland was a single voting area, as well as being a regional count for the United Kingdom_wide referendum. It was the last region of the United Kingdom to declare its result after major delays due to slow pace of counting and declaring results for the 2011 Northern Ireland Assembly Elections meant that the start of the local count for the referendum could not begin until the evening on Friday 2011, several hours after counting had started in the rest of the country and the result was not finally known until 1am on Saturday 2011. The constituencies of the Scottish Parliament were used as the local voting areas for the United Kingdom_wide referendum throughout Scotland. The constituencies of the National Assembly for Wales were used as the local voting areas for the United Kingdom_wide referendum throughout Wales." .

