lsd:Entity_29385 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 United States presidential election in Utah took place on 06 November 2012, as part of the 2012 United States presidential election in which all 50 states plus the District of Columbia participated. Utah voters chose six electors to represent them in the Electoral College via a popular vote pitting incumbent Democratic President of the United States Barack Obama and his running mate, Vice President of the United States Joe Biden, against Republican challenger and former Massachusetts Governor Mitt Romney and his running mate, Congressman Paul Ryan. Utah is among the most heavily Republican states, voting for the Republican ticket in every presidential election after 1964 and all but once since 1952, and is also the only state to have a majority Mormon population, benefiting Romney, the first Mormon to head a major party presidential ticket. Romney, who became a heavy critic of now_President of the United States Donald Trump, played a factor in Trump's mere 20.48% win in 2020, representing a significant decline in support for the Republican Party likely attributable to Romney's popularity among Utah voters. Due to President of the United States Barack Obama running for reelection without serious opposition for the Democratic Party in 2012, no Democratic Primary was held in Utah." .

