lsd:Entity_35801 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The CONMEBOL Recopa Sudamericana (Portuguese: CONMEBOL Recopa Sul_Americana), known also as the Recopa Sudamericana or CONMEBOL Recopa, and simply as the Recopa (Spanish: , Portuguese: ; \"Winners' Cup\"), is an annual international club association football competition organized by CONMEBOL since 1988. CONMEBOL named the new competition Recopa Sudamericana after the defunct Recopa Sudamericana de Clubes played in 1970 and 1971. The Recopa Sudamericana, disputed between the winners of South America's two premier club competitions, is not related chronologically to the Recopa Sudamericana, created in 1968 which was contested between former South American winners of the Intercontinental Cup, Recopa Sudamericana de Clubes, disputed among Cup winners of South America. Since São Paulo won both the Copa Libertadores and Supercopa Sudamericana, CONMEBOL had Copa CONMEBOL winners Botafogo dispute the Recopa Sudamericana only to lose 3–1 to the defending champions. Unlike most other competitions around the world, the Recopa Sudamericana do not use extra time, an additional period of play specified under the rules of a sport to bring a game to a decision and avoid declaring the match a tie or draw, or away goals, a method of breaking ties in association football and other sports when teams play each other twice, once at each team's home ground, to decide a tie that was level on aggregate. From 1995 onwards, the \"three points for a win\" standard, a system adopted by FIFA in 1995 that places additional value on wins, was adopted in CONMEBOL, with teams now earning 3 points for a win, 1 point for a draw and 0 points for a loss. Thus, CONMEBOL states that Nacional from Uruguay won the first Recopa of 1989, whereas the RSSSF refers to that championship as Recopa 1988. The Recopa Sudamericana trophy is retained by CONMEBOL at all times." .

