lsd:Entity_48736 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Potter v Broken Hill Proprietary Company Ltd, was a significant Australia court legal case, decided in the High Court of Australia on 20 March 1906. The legal case was an influential decision in Australia Private International Law which is generally regarded as based on an extension of the Moçambique rule to actions for infringement of patents.:at. In 2002 a majority of the High Court indicated that it would like to reconsider this authority, with Gleeson CJ, Gaudron, McHugh, Gummow and Hayne JJ stating \"We also would reserve for further consideration in an appropriate legal case the Moçambique rule, and the standing of Potter v Broken Hill Propriety Co Ltd.\" The matter arose again in the High Court in 2011 where French CJ, Gummow, Hayne, Crennan, Kiefel and Bell JJ noted that Potter v Broken Hill Propriety Co Ltd. was argued on a basis which, though conceded, can now be seen to be false, namely, 'that, for the purposes of the question ... under consideration, the several States of Australia stand towards each other in the position of foreign States'. As of June 2017 the High Court has not reconsidered the standing of Potter v Broken Hill Propriety Co Ltd. 'It received no attention in the English legal case_law until it was mentioned by Lord Wilberforce in Hesperides Hotels Ltd v Aegean Turkish Holidays Ltd AC 508, 536 as authority for the proposition that the Moçambique rule applied in Australia. This legal case was decided at a time when patents were granted by the several States in Australia, and was subject to the common law rule that each Australia State and Territory is treated as a 'distinct and separate country or law area'." .

