lsd:Entity_4915 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Comanche Wars were a series of armed conflicts fought between Comanche peoples and Spanish, Mexican, and American militaries and civilians in the United States and Mexico from as early as 1706 until at least the mid_1870s. The Comanche Wars began in 1706 with raids by Comanche warriors on the Spanish colonies of New Spain and continued until the last bands of Comanche surrendered to the United States Army in 1875, although a few Comanche continued to fight in later conflicts such as the Buffalo Hunters' War in 1876 and 1877. Comanche power peaked in the 1840s when they conducted large_scale raids hundreds of miles into Mexico proper, while also Texas–Indian wars against the Anglo_Americans and Tejanos who had settled in independent Texas. The Council House Fight was a Peace delegation turned conflict between the Comanche delegates and the Texas officials on March 19, 1840. The conflict started over negotiations regarding Texan and Mexican captives that the Comanches were holding in order to gain back sections of Comancheria that Texas had claimed. The Battle of Plum Creek was a conflict in Lockhart, Texas that took place on August 12, 1840." .

