lsd:Entity_52412 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United States–Uzbekistan relations formally began when the United States of America recognized the independence of Uzbekistan on December 25, 1991, and opened an embassy in Tashkent in March 1992. The United States of America' humanitarian and technical assistance to Uzbekistan has decreased markedly since 2004, both as a result of government actions against U.S. implementing partners and U.S. Government restrictions on aid. In FY 2003, the United States of America provided roughly $87.4 million in humanitarian aid, technical assistance, military_to_military funding, and micro_credit support in Uzbekistan. Human rights conditions in Uzbekistan under President Shavkat Mirziyoyev have improved slightly because he wants to assure investors, like the United States of America, that their investments will not be adversely affected by political instability. Not only does this show Western countries stability, but also a willingness to align more with the United States of America ideals, making it easier for the United States of America to justify a close economic relationship with Uzbekistan. Mirziyoyev's shift towards the United States of America aligns with his previously mentioned goal of transforming Uzbekistan's economy by developing a strong relationship with United States of America. Thus, Uzbekistan must continue to undergo significant reform to fit the United States of America definition of democracy. Following the 9/11 terrorist attacks in the United States of America, Uzbekistan approved of the U.S. Central Command's request for access to an air base, the Karshi_Khanabad airfield, in southern Uzbekistan, to station 1,500 of its armed forces, in exchange for security guarantees and assistance with its own internal terrorism. The Embassy of Uzbekistan is located on Embassy Row in Washington, D.C. Media related to Relations of the United States of America and Uzbekistan at Wikimedia Commons." .

