lsd:Entity_55484 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The UEFA Intertonto (abbreviated as UI Cup), originally called the International Football Cup, was a summer association football competition for Europe clubs that had not qualified for one of the major Union of European Football Associations competitions, the Champions League, the Union of European Football Associations Cup and until 1999, the Cup Winners' Cup. The tournament was founded in 1961–62, but was only taken over by Union of European Football Associations in 1995. In 1995, the tournament came under official Union of European Football Associations sanctioning and Union of European Football Associations Cup qualification places were granted. By 1995, Union of European Football Associations had reconsidered their opinion, took official control of the tournament and changed its format. The success of one of the first winners, Bordeaux, in reaching the final of the 1995–96 Union of European Football Associations Cup encouraged Union of European Football Associations to add a third Union of European Football Associations Cup place in 1996. When the competition was taken over by Union of European Football Associations in 1995, the format was both a group stage and a knock_out stage; 60 teams were split into 12 groups of five with the 16 best teams then contesting the knock_out stage with two_legged ties at each stage, the two winning finalists qualifying for the Union of European Football Associations Cup. Listed are each year's three teams (two in 1995) that won the final matches, qualifying them for the Union of European Football Associations Cup." .

