lsd:Entity_55559 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1890 Australian maritime dispute was an industrial dispute that began on 1890 when the Mercantile Marine Officers' Association directed its members to give 24 hours' notice to their employers after negotiations broke down with the Steamship Owners' Association of Victoria over longstanding pay and conditions claims. By 1890, 28,500 workers were on strike. While police had been used in strikes before 1890, the military had not usually been called in other than at the Lambton Colliery strike in the Hunter Valley two years earlier. The strike was defeated when the Marine Officers returned to work on the employers terms in 1890, with Illawarra coal miners being the last workers to return to work in January 1891. The defeat of the 1890 maritime strike and the 1891 Australian shearers' strike, laid the framework for the Australian labour movement entry into parliamentary politics." .

