lsd:Entity_61501 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nicaragua–Spain relations refers to the diplomatic relations between Nicaragua and Spain. In 1522, Spanish conquistador Gil González Dávila arrived to Nicaragua and claimed the territory for Spain. The territory of Nicaragua soon officially became part of the Spanish Empire under the Viceroy of New Spain based in Mexico City and administered by the Captaincy General of Guatemala based in Santiago de Guatemala. As Nicaragua and most Central American nations were governed by Mexico City; New Spain declared its independence from Spain in 1810. Nicaragua declared its own independence from Spain on 15 September 1821 and chose to join the Mexican Empire under Emperor Agustín de Iturbide. On 20 March 1851, Nicaragua and Spain officially established diplomatic relations and signed a Treaty of Peace and Friendship. In April 1991, King Juan Carlos I of Spain paid his first and only visit to Nicaragua to celebrate the end of the Nicaraguan Revolution. In 2017, trade between Nicaragua and Spain totaled €71.5 million Euros. Nicaragua's main exports to Spain include: fish, crustaceans and mussels. Spain's main exports to Nicaragua include: machinery, pharmaceuticals, electronic equipment, clothing and food." .

