lsd:Entity_918 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1913 Paterson silk strike was a strike involving silk mill workers in Paterson, New Jersey. The strike began in 1913, and ended five months later, on July 28th. Paterson's strike was part of a series of industrial strikes in the garment and textile industries of the American East from 1909 to 1913. On 1913, the first day of the strike, the IWW’s prominent feminist leader Elizabeth Gurley Flynn was arrested after giving a talk on uniting strikers across racial boundaries. The 1913 strike in Paterson was preempted by the Doherty Silk Company’s construction of a modernized mill in nearby Clifton, New Jersey in 1911. The following year, Edwald Koettgen formed the Eight_Hour League in Paterson, championing the idea of an eight_hour work day, sowing the final seeds of the 1913 strike. Ultimately, the strike ended in failure on 1913." .

