lsd:Entity_9823 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The carnival is held during the week leading up to Ash Wednesday, and typically includes masked balls, fancy dress and grotesque mask competitions, lavish late_night parties, a colourful, ticker_tape parade of allegorical floats presided over by King Maltese Carnival (Maltese: ir_Re tal_Karnival), marching bands and costumed revellers. During the Maltese Carnival of 1823, about 110 children died in a human crush after attending catechism at the Convent of the Minori Osservanti on the last day of carnival. Since 1926 carnival in Malta started to be centered in the capital city of Valletta, and saw the decline in other cities and villages, with the major reasons being the regulations set by the Maltese Carnival Committee and the participation prizes awarded by it." .

