lsd:Entity_9860 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Japan–Spain relations refers to the bilateral relations between Japan and Spain. The first contact between Japan and Spain occurred in 1549 when Spanish missionary Francis Xavier arrived to Japan. In Mexico City (the capital of New Spain), Hasekura met with several colonial leaders and offered the New Spanish government free commerce between the New Spanish territories and Japan and asked for a group of Christian missionaries to return to Japan. In 1618, Hasekura and his diplomatic mission set sail from New Spain and returned to Japan. Since Hasekura's diplomatic mission to Spain, Japan entered a time of isolation and refused to trade with foreign nations. It wasn't until 12 November 1868 that Japan and Spain officially established diplomatic relations with the signing of the Treaty of Friendship, Commerce and Navigation. Russell, in 1896 Spain was rumoured to have offered to sell the islands to Japan for $3,000,000 gold dollars, but this offer was rejected. The Japanese had also blocked arms sales to the rebels and, at the request of Spain, had kept Filipino rebels in Japan under close surveillance. However, no steps were taken to undermine the 1897 Treaty of Friendship and General Intercourse that was then being negotiated between Japan and Spain recognising each other's spheres of interest. Both Japan and Nationalist Spain went on to join the Anti_Comintern Pact. Japan chose Spain for the representation of Japanese interests in the Latin_American republics. On 12 April 1945 Spain declared the rupture of diplomatic relations with Japan. Soon, Japan began suffering setbacks in the Pacific and Spain decided not to pursue further actions against Japan. In 2015, trade between Japan and Spain totaled €5.5 billion Euros (729 billion Yen). Japan's main exports to Spain include: Chemical products, machinery and transportation equipment. Spain's exports to Japan include: Transportation equipment and machinery. Japan is Spain's second largest trading partner in Asia and Spain is Japan's sixth largest trading partner within the European Union." .

