lsd:Entity_10422 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Goliad massacre was an event of the Texas Revolution that occurred on 27 March 1836, following the Battle of Coleto; 425–445 prisoners of war from the Texian Army of the Republic of Texas were killed by the Mexican Army in the town of Goliad. Meanwhile, General Sam Houston had persuaded all but 70 to 100 men and their leaders, Frank W. Johnson and James Grant, to give up on the expedition and to defend locations in Texas, principally Goliad. The high number of Texian prisoner_of_war casualties throughout the Goliad Campaign led to Goliad being called a \"massacre\" by Texian forces." .

