lsd:Entity_12774 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "social realism was an art movement that came to the fore in the USA between the two world wars and was a reaction to the increasing hardship for ordinary people. social realism traces back to 19th_century European Realism, including the art of Honoré Daumier, Gustave Courbet and Jean_François Millet. In the more limited meaning of the term, Social Realism with roots in European Realism became an important art movement during the Great Depression in the United States in the 1930s. social realism has been summarized as follows: Social Realism developed as a reaction against idealism and the exaggerated ego encouraged by Romanticism. Social Realism became an important art movement during the Great Depression in the United States in the 1930s. Socialist Realism, representing socialist ideologies, was an art movement that represented social and political contemporary life in the 1930s, from a left_wing standpoint." .

