lsd:Entity_12996 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Portland Rose Festival is an annual civic festival held during the month of June in Portland, Oregon. It is organized by the volunteer non_profit Portland Rose Festival Association with the purpose of promoting the Portland region. In 1905, Portland Mayor Harry Lane is remembered for his rousing speech at the Lewis and Clark Exposition, telling the large crowd that Portland needed a \"festival of roses\". In 1907, Portland hosted its first official Portland Rose Festival. The 1907 festival also included an \"electric parade\" with illuminated floats; this evolved into the Merrykhana Parade but after a two_season suspension was renamed the Starlight Parade in 1976. The festival also hosts the Starlight Parade, a fireworks display, and carnival rides along the Portland waterfront, among other events." .

lsd:Entity_1301 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1928 German federal election were held in Germany on 20 May 1928." .

lsd:Entity_13011 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Cork Intermediate Hurling Championship was the 107th staging of the Cork Intermediate Hurling Championship since its establishment by the Cork County Board in 1909. The championship began on 2016 and ended on 30 October 2016. On 30 October 2016, Fr. O'Neill's GAA won the championship following a 1_18 to 0_14 defeat of Kildorrery in a replay of the final." .

lsd:Entity_13017 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999–2000 Taça de Portugal was the 60th edition of the Portuguese association football knockout tournament, organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The 1999–2000 Taça de Portugal began in 01 September 1999. Futebol Clube do Porto defeated Sporting CP, 2–0 in the cup final replay to win their tenth 1999–2000 Taça de Portugal." .

lsd:Entity_13024 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Meistriliiga was the 11th season of the Meistriliiga, Estonia's premier football league. Lootus won on away goals and retained their Meistriliiga spot for the 2002 Meistriliiga." .

lsd:Entity_13033 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Durban Riot were an anti_Indian pogrom that took place between 1949–1949, primarily by African people targeting Indians in Durban, South Africa. The two days of rioting were exceptionally violent resulting in rape and massacre of Indians, looting, burning of their properties, claiming 142 lives and creating 40,000 Indian refugees. The riots resulted in the massacre of mostly Indians in which 142 people died and 1087 people were injured." .

lsd:Entity_13042 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1988–89 Galatasaray S.K. Volleyball season is the 1988–89 volleyball season for Turkey professional basketball club Galatasaray Men's Volleyball Team." .

lsd:Entity_13063 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Rammasun, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Butchoy, was recognized as the second typhoon of the 2008 Pacific typhoon season by the Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) and the Philippine Atmospheric, Geophysical and Astronomical Services Administration (PAGASA). Rammasun was also recognised as the third tropical storm, the second typhoon and the first super typhoon of the 2008 Pacific typhoon season by the Joint Typhoon Warning Center (JTWC). In all, the typhoon killed four people, injured 40 others and caused $9.6 million (2008 USD) worth of damage in both the Philippines and Japan. Typhoon Rammasun's minimum pressure was officially estimated at 915 hPa which tied with Intense Tropical Cyclone Hondo as second strongest storm of 2008. The name was last used in the 2002 Pacific typhoon season, though it was retired in 2015 after Typhoon Rammasun (the same name used for the 2014 season, Typhoon Glenda in the Philippines) left considerable damages in the Philippines, China and Vietnam." .

lsd:Entity_13068 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Light Me Up Tour is the first headline tour by the hard rock band The Pretty Reckless." .

lsd:Entity_13074 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Millî Görüş (Turkish: , \"National Outlook\" or \"National Vision\") is a religio_political movement and a series of Islamist parties inspired by Necmettin Erbakan. Founded in 1969, the movement claimed to have \"87,000 members across Europe, including 50,000 in Germany,\" as of 2005. In 1969 the Turkish politician Necmettin Erbakan published a manifesto that he gave the title Millî Görüş. It warned against further rapprochement towards Europe, considering the Common Market to be a Zionist and Catholic project for the assimilation and de_Islamization of Turkey and called instead for Pan_Islamism. According to author Banu Eligur, Erbakan and the party \"used the code words national and culture to refer to Islam, and National Vision to refer to the project of Islamism\" as \"it is illegal\" in Turkey \"to use religious symbols for political purposes.\" The name of Millî Görüş would remain associated with a religio_political movement and a series of Islamist parties inspired by Erbakan, one succeeding the other as they were banned for violating Turkey’s secularist legislation. Milli Görüş' public profile shows considerable differences from one country to the next, suggesting that nature of the interaction with the 'host' societies may have as much of an impact on its character as a religious movement as the relationship with the 'mother' movement in Turkey. According to several sources in Germany the attitude of the German branch towards Turkey has completely changed." .

lsd:Entity_13081 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 Open Clarins was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court in Paris, France, and was part of the Category 1 tier of the 1988 WTA Tour. It was the second edition of the tournament and was held from 19 September 1988 until 25 September 1988." .

lsd:Entity_13091 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Damascus International Film Festival is the 18th incarnation of the international film festival held in Damascus, Syria which ran from November 7 to 13, 2010. This edition of the Damascus International Film Festival, organised under the patronage of Syrian Minister of Culture Dr. Riad Ismat, opened with a ceremony at the Damascus Opera building, at which Turkish actress Türkan Şoray and Syrian actor Ghassan Massoud were among the guests of honor, and a screening of Honey (Turkish: Bal) directed by Semih Kaplanoğlu." .

