lsd:Entity_14204 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "German auxiliary cruiser Orion (HSK_1) was an auxiliary cruiser of Nazi Germany's Kriegsmarine which operated as a merchant raider in World War II. Blohm + Voss built her in Hamburg in 1930–31 as the cargo ship Kurmark. The navy requisitioned her at the start of World War II, had her converted into the auxiliary cruiser German auxiliary cruiser Orion, and commissioned her on 9 December 1939. Blohm + Voss in Hamburg built the ship as a freighter for the Hamburg America Line (HAPAG). One of Germany's first auxiliary cruisers in World War II, German auxiliary cruiser Orion left Germany on 6 April 1940, under the command of Korvettenkapitän (later Fregattenkapitän) Kurt Weyher. It was dispatched on board the supply ship Münsterland to rendezvous with German auxiliary cruiser Orion at the Maug Islands in the Northern Marianas. They met on 1 February 1941, and German auxiliary cruiser Orion thus became the only German naval ship of World War II to use a Japanese float plane. In January 1945 she was again renamed German auxiliary cruiser Orion and was used to take refugees from Germany's eastern provinces across the Baltic Sea to ports in northern Germany and occupied Denmark." .

lsd:Entity_14207 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 All_Ireland Senior Hurling Championship Final was the 68th All_Ireland Final and the culmination of the All_Ireland Senior Hurling Championship 1955, an inter_county hurling tournament for the top teams in Ireland. The match was held at Croke Park, Dublin, on 1955, between Wexford and Galway." .

lsd:Entity_14246 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 Imperial Conference met in London in the autumn of 1923, the first attended by the new Irish Free State. In addition, prior to the Imperial Conference, Canada negotiated the Halibut Treaty with the United States and did so without involving the United Kingdom or allowing the British government to sign on Canada's behalf." .

lsd:Entity_14263 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Djiboutian protests were widespread demonstrations and riots that took place between January and 2011 in Djibouti, situated in the Horn of Africa. A member of the Arab League, the protests in Djibouti showed a clear influence from the concurrent Arab Spring protests in North Africa and the Arabian peninsula. On January 25, thousands of people turned out to protest in Djibouti City. On March 3, Djibouti ordered its opposition party to cancel its anti_government protests which were to be held on 2011 due to a previous rally a month earlier turning violent." .

