lsd:Entity_14541 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United States v. Paramount Pictures, Inc., 334 U.S. 131 (1948) (also known as the Hollywood Antitrust Case of 1948, the Paramount Case, the Paramount Decision or the Paramount Decree), was a landmark Supreme Court of the United States antitrust case that decided the fate of film studios owning their own theatres and holding exclusivity rights on which theatres would show their movies. The Paramount decision is a bedrock of corporate competition law law and as such is cited in most cases where issues of vertical integration play a prominent role in restricting fair trade." .

