lsd:Entity_1521 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Recapture of Bahia (Spain: Jornada del Brasil; Portuguese: Jornada dos Vassalos) was a Spain_Portuguese military expedition in 1625 to retake the city of Salvador in Brazil from the forces of the Dutch West India Company (WIC). Sailing from the port of Lisbon, under the command of Fadrique Álvarez de Toledo y Mendoza, who was appointed Captain General of the Army of Brazil, the fleet crossed the Atlantic Ocean, and arrived at Salvador on April 1 of 1625. There Willekens was revealed that his objective was the capture of the city of Salvador, on the coast of Brazil, in order to use its port as a commercial base to ensure the Dutch trade with the East Indies. These intentions to invade Brazil were soon reported to the court of Madrid by the Spain spies in the Netherlands, but Count_Duke of Olivares did not give them credit. The Portuguese governor of Salvador, Diogo de Mendonça Furtado, tried to organized the defense of the town with 3,000 men hastily recruited, mostly Portuguese militia of peasant levees and black slaves, all of them resentful to Spain rule. When news of the loss of Salvador arrived to Spain in August 1624, Philip IV ordered to assemble a joint Spain_Portuguese fleet under Admiral Fadrique Álvarez de Toledo y Mendoza with the mission to retake the city. One of them returned to Holland with the supplies and ammunition for the garrison of Salvador; the other two attacked respectively the Spain Caribbean colonial town of San Juan de Puerto Rico and the Portuguese African trading post of the Castle of Elmina but were both decisively defeated." .

lsd:Entity_15217 a lsd:Context_narration ;
