lsd:Entity_16262 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Congressional Baseball Game is an annual baseball game played each summer by members of the United States Congress. In the game, Republican Party and Democratic Party form separate teams and play against each other. Deep, dark rumors were in circulation that 'ringers' would be introduced, but when they lined up at 4 o'clock the nine Republican Party were stalwart, grand old party men, while the Democratic Party were of the pure Jeffersonian strain.\" The Democratic Party beat their Republican Party opponents, 26–16 in the first game and continued their winning streak for the first six games. As of the 2017 game, 14 trophies have been awarded—ten to the Republican Party' team and four to the Democratic Party' team. On June 14, 2017, one day before the annual event, a gunman opened fire on Republican Party members of Congress who were practicing for the next day's game. Bicameral baseball was inaugurated in 1950, when Senator Harry P. Cain of Washington joined the Republican Party team and Senator_elect George Smathers of Florida, a former Representative, joined the Democratic team. In the early years of the game, each team wore a uniform that was either plain or had the words \"Republican Party\" or \"Democratic Party\" embroidered on it. The official website of the Congressional Baseball Game for Charity seemingly does not count three of those Republican Party wins, because its History page listed (in 2017, but prior to the 2017 game) the series record as 39–39–1 (in a blue, red, white, and black bar near the bottom of the page). The same page, however, states—in reference to the Roll Call Trophy—that \"o date, 10 of these coveted trophies have been awarded, eight to the Republican Party and two to the Democratic Party.\" Although the series record that is set forth in the bar near the bottom of the page includes the Democratic Party' seven wins between 2003 and 2016, the bar includes only four of the Republican Party' seven wins during that same time period." .

