lsd:Entity_18540 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Novi Sad Jazz Festival (Serbian Cyrillic: Новосадски Џез фестивал or Novosadski jazz festival) is an annual jazz festival in Novi Sad, Serbia. The festival was first held in 1999 in the Novi Sad Cultural Centre. The first jazz festival was held in the Cultural Centre of Novi Sad, from November 25 to 1999. More than 100 ensembles from Serbia and abroad have performed at the Novi Sad Jazz Festival for the past seven years, and nearly 600 musicians, including Johnny Griffin, Jimmy Cobb, Al Foster, Benny Bailey, Andy Bay, Tania Maria, Trilok Gurtu, Steve Coleman, Keith Copeland, Eddie Henderson, Al Di Meola, Jerry Bergonzi, Kenny Garrett, Erik Rothenstein, Sheila Jordan, and Curtis Fuller to name a few." .

lsd:Entity_18561 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Dansk Melodi Grand Prix is an annual music competition organised by Denmark public broadcaster DR, which determines the country's representative for the Eurovision Song Contest. In 2005, DR made a bold step for Dansk Melodi Grand Prix. For the 2009 of Dansk Melodi Grand Prix, DR reverted to their one_night final, that was held on 31 January 2009. Almost all winners of Dansk Melodi Grand Prix have gone on to represent Denmark at Eurovision." .

lsd:Entity_18576 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 United Kingdom general election was held on 01 May 1997. The incumbent governing Conservative Party led by Prime Minister of the United Kingdom John Major was defeated in a landslide by the Labour Party led by Tony Blair. The final result of the election on 2 01 May 1997 revealed that Labour had won a landslide majority, making a net gain of 146 seats and winning 43.2% of the vote. Immediately following the election Major resigned both as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom and as party leader. Although the Conservatives lost many ministers such as Michael Portillo, Tony Newton, Malcolm Rifkind, Ian Lang and William Waldegrave and controversial MPs such as Neil Hamilton and Jonathan Aitken, some of the Conservative newcomers in this election were future Prime Minister of the United Kingdom Theresa May, future Chancellor of the Exchequer Philip Hammond as well as future Speaker John Bercow. Therefore, the latest date the election could have been held on was 22 01 May 1997 (which happened to be a Thursday). Prime Minister of the United Kingdom John Major called the election on Monday 17 March 1997, ensuring the formal campaign would be unusually long, at six weeks (Parliament was dissolved on 8 April). The election was scheduled for 01 May 1997, to coincide with the local elections on the same day. The election was fought under new boundaries, with a net increase of eight seats compared to the 1992 United Kingdom general election (651 to 659). Between the 1992 United Kingdom general election and the 1997 election there had also been major steps to \"modernise\" the party, including scrapping Clause IV that had committed the party to extending public ownership of industry." .

lsd:Entity_18579 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The gymnastics at the 2015 European Games – women's floor exercise competition at the 2015 European Games was held in the National Gymnastics Arena, Baku on 2015." .

