lsd:Entity_24997 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Monte Grappa were a series of three battles which were fought during World War I between the armies of the Austro_Hungarian Empire and the Kingdom of Italy for control of the Monte Grappa massif, as it covered the left flank of the Italian Piave front. When the Austrian summer offensive of 1917 routed the Italians, Cadorna's foresight saved Italy from total defeat, as the Austrians, with help from the German Army's Alpenkorps failed to take the mountain's summit during the first battle of Monte Grappa from 1917 to 1917. The second battle of Monte Grappa was complementary to the wider Austrian summer offensive of 1918, which was the last offensive operation of the Austro–Hungarian Army in World War I. The third battle of Monte Grappa started on 1918, as part of the final Italian offensive of the war, when 9 Italian divisions attacked the Austrian positions on Monte Grappa. The map is set during the final Italian offensive in the Third Battle of Monte Grappa pitting Austro_Hungarian forces against the Kingdom of Italy." .

