lsd:Entity_26068 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mexican War of Independence (Spanish: Guerra de Independencia de México) was an armed conflict and political process, lasting from 1808 to 1821, resulting in Mexico's independence from Spain. Before the events of 1808 upended the political situation in Viceroyalty of New Spain, there was an isolated and abortive 1799 Conspiracy of the Machetes by a small group in Mexico City seeking independence. In Mexico City, the city council (ayuntamiento), a stronghold of American_born Spaniards, began promoting ideas of autonomy for Viceroyalty of New Spain, and declaring Viceroyalty of New Spain to be on an equal basis to Spain. His uprising on 16 September 1810 is considered the spark igniting the Mexican War of Independence. From 1815 to 1821 most of the fighting for independence from Spain was by guerrilla forces in the tierra caliente (hot country) of southern Mexico and to a certain extent in northern Viceroyalty of New Spain. When news of the liberal charter reached Viceroyalty of New Spain, Iturbide perceived it both as a threat to the status quo and a catalyst to rouse the creole elites to gain control of Mexico. The Spanish government denied that O'Donojú had the authority to sign the treaty, but events on the ground saw Iturbide and the Army of Three Guarantees march into Mexico City in triumph on 27 September 1821. On 27 September 1821, the Army of the Three Guarantees entered Mexico City, and the following day Iturbide proclaimed the independence of the Mexican Empire, as Viceroyalty of New Spain was henceforth to be called. Although Mexico gained its independence in 27 September 1821, the marking of this historical event did not take hold immediately. The creation of a committee of powerful men to mark independence celebrations, the Junta Patriótica, organized celebrations of both 16 September 1810, to commemorate Hidalgo's grito and the start of the independence insurgency, and 27 September 1821, to celebrate actual political independence." .

lsd:Entity_2607 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Stanley Cup Finals was a best_of_five series played entirely in Montreal between the New York Rangers and the Montreal Maroons. This was also the first of only two times that a Montreal_based team lost the clinching game of the Stanley Cup Finals at home in the Montreal Forum, the other occurred in 1989 when the Calgary Flames defeated the Montreal Canadiens. Frank Boucher became the fourth member of his family to win the Stanley Cup, after his brother George won it four times with Ottawa, and his brothers Billy and Bobby had won it with Montreal in 1924. 1928 New York Rangers." .

