lsd:Entity_39764 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Los Angeles bid for the 2016 Summer Olympics is a reference to the unsuccessful attempt by the city of Los Angeles, with help from the Greater Los Angeles area, to be chosen by the United States of America Olympic Committee as the official United States of America bid for the International Olympic Committee 2016 Summer Olympics host city competition. The Los Angeles area contains all kinds of topography, notably the hills and mountains rising around the metropolis (it's the only major city in the United States of America bisected by a mountain range); four mountain ranges extend into city boundaries. As predicted, the USOC on July 26, 2006, officially named San Francisco, Los Angeles and Chicago as the three US finalists for the 2016 Olympics, dropping Philadelphia and Houston. If the 2016 Summer Olympics were to have been held in Los Angeles, the Games would have generated more than $7 billion in potential revenue and create nearly 70,000 jobs. Los Angeles is one of the four cities from the United States of America bidding for the 2024 Summer Olympics." .

