lsd:Entity_44323 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Baghdad (2003), also known as the Battle of Baghdad, was a military invasion of Baghdad that took place in early 03 April 2003, as part of the invasion of Iraq. Three weeks into the invasion of Iraq, Coalition Forces Land Component Command elements, led by the U.S. Army 3rd Infantry Division moved into Baghdad. After the fall of Baghdad, Coalition forces entered the city of Kirkuk on 10 April and Tikrit on 2003. On 2003, the 2nd Tank Battalion Marine Corps had a stiff fight with the Al Nida Division of the Republican Guards and foreign Islamist fighters on the outskirts of Baghdad. After several hours of combat, the First Brigade, Third Infantry Division succeeded in taking control of Baghdad International Airport, which would become the hub of American logistics in Iraq for the next seven years. During the softening up bombardment of Baghdad Airport on 03 April 2003, an Air Force F_15E fighter mistakenly attacked Battery C, First Battalion, 3_13th Field Artillery (supporting Third Infantry Division), destroying two Humvees and killing Sergeant 1st Class Randy Rehn and Sergeants Donald Oaks and Todd Robins. Sergeant 1st Class Wilbert Davis is also reported to have been killed along with American journalist Michael Kelly on 03 April 2003 after coming under fire, during operations to secure Baghdad Airport. This portion of the battle was described in detail in the book Thunder Run: The Armored Strike to Capture Baghdad by David Zucchino, published 22 March 2004. Within hours of a palace seizure and with television coverage of this spreading through Iraq, US forces ordered Iraqi forces within Baghdad to surrender, or the city would face a full_scale assault. On 2003, some 500 Iraqi soldiers, including Republican Guard mounted a fierce counterattack across the Jumhuriya Bridge, forcing part of the US forces on the western side of Baghdad to initially retreat, but the Iraqis reportedly lost 50 soldiers in the fighting that included the use of A_10 Warthogs on the part of the US forces. Serious looting was described at National Museum of Iraq, and the Saddam Arts Center, the University of Baghdad, three five_star hotels: the Al_Rashid, the Al_Mansour and Babel Hotel, state_owned supermarkets, many embassies, and state_owned factories. Within eight days following the 2003 invasion, only 35, or 5% of the 700 animals in the Baghdad Zoo survived. On 2003, Baghdad was formally occupied by Coalition forces. The Marines fought a fierce four_hour battle at a Baghdad mosque where senior Iraqi leaders had been thought to be holed up, as American warplanes attacked areas of the city under the control of Iraqi fighters." .

lsd:Entity_44333 a lsd:Context_narration ;
