lsd:Entity_47097 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1904 United States presidential election in North Carolina took place on 08 November 1904. North Carolina voters chose 12 electors to the Electoral College, which selected the President of the United States and vice President of the United States. Although this strategy had allowed William McKinley to retain almost the same share of North Carolina’s reduced electorate in 1900 that he had won with large_scale black voting in 1896, Republican nominee Theodore Roosevelt would decline substantially in 1904, due to a shift to a more pro_business Democratic candidate and opposition to Roosevelt’s hosting of Booker T. Washington." .

