lsd:Entity_47681 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Great Britain competed at the Great Britain at the 2018 Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang County, South Korea, from 9 to 2018, with 58 competitors in 11 sports. On 2018, the funding body UK Sport announced their medal targets for Team GB at the 2018 Winter Olympic Games in Pyeongchang County with the target to win at least five medals which if achieved would be a record_breaking haul for a Winter Olympic Games. In the Winter Olympic Cycle running from 2014 to 2018 the UK government body UK Sport allocated a record budget of over £28 million to fund Team GB for the individual athletes as well as the bobsleigh and curling teams for the Great Britain at the 2018 Winter Olympics in Pyeongchang County. On 2018, the British Olympic Association announced the selection of the athletes who will compete for Team GB in the luge in Pyeongchang County." .

lsd:Entity_4770 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics competed at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics, in Nanjing, China from 16 August to 2014. Qualification Legend: Q=Final A (medal); qB=Final B (non_medal); qC=Final C (non_medal); qD=Final D (non_medal); qE=Final E (non_medal) Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified two athletes based on the 2014 BWF Junior World Rankings. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified a boys' team from their performance at the 2014 CEV Youth Continental Cup Final. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified one boxer based on its performance at the 2014 AIBA Youth World Championships. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified four athletes based on its performance at the 2014 FIE Cadet World Championships. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified one athlete based on its performance at the 2014 European WAG Championships. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified one athlete based on its performance at the 2014 Youth A World Championships. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified two athletes based on the 2014 ITF World Junior Rankings. Poland at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics qualified one athlete based on its performance at the 2014 European Cadet Championships." .

lsd:Entity_47710 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Super 12 season was the third season of the Super Rugby, contested by teams from Australia, New Zealand and South Africa. The winner of each semi final qualified for the final, which was contested by the Auckland Blues and the Canterbury Crusaders at Eden Park, Auckland. The Canterbury Crusaders won 20 – 13 to win their first Super Rugby title. The 1998 Super Rugby competition consisted of 12 teams, they were: The Blues (New Zealand) The Brumbies (Australia) The Bulls (South Africa) The Cats (South Africa) The Chiefs (New Zealand) The Crusaders (New Zealand) The Highlanders (New Zealand) The Hurricanes (New Zealand) The Reds (Australia) The Sharks (South Africa) The Stormers (South Africa) The Waratahs (Australia)" .

