lsd:Entity_48626 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Arizona gubernatorial election took place on 05 November 2002. The Democratic nominee, Arizona Attorney General Janet Napolitano, narrowly defeated Republican Matt Salmon, a former U.S. Representative. The election was extremely close: Napolitano won by just 11,819 votes out of 1,226,111 cast, the closest gubernatorial election in Arizona in many years." .

lsd:Entity_48631 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1924 United States presidential election in New York took place on 04 November 1924. New York was won by incumbent Republican President Calvin Coolidge of Massachusetts, who was running against Democratic Ambassador John W. Davis of West Virginia and the Progressive Party's Senator Robert M. La Follette of Wisconsin. The economic boom and social good feelings of the Roaring Twenties under popular Republican leadership virtually guaranteed Calvin Coolidge an easy win in the state against the conservative Southern Democrat John Davis, who had little appeal in Northern states like New York where his reticence on the Ku Klux Klan was opposed by large Catholic populations. No Republican since has been able to outperform Coolidge’s county_level performance or surpass his statewide margin of victory; the only stronger Republican win in New York’s history was when Coolidge was running for Vice President four years earlier in Warren G. Harding’s massive landslide of 1920 United States presidential election in New York. 1924 also made Calvin Coolidge one of only three presidential candidates of either party who has been able to sweep every county in New York State, the others being Republican Warren G. Harding in 1920 United States presidential election in New York and Democrat Lyndon Johnson in 1964." .

lsd:Entity_48639 a lsd:Context_narration ;
