lsd:Entity_5157 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 CAF Confederation Cup (officially the 2018 Total CAF Confederation Cup for sponsorship reasons) was the 15th edition of Africa's secondary club association football tournament organized by the Confederation of African Football (Confederation of African Football), under the current CAF Confederation Cup title after the merger of Confederation of African Football Cup and African Cup Winners' Cup. Raja Casablanca won the title for the first time, defeating AS Vita Club in the final, and earned the right to play against the winners of the 2018 Confederation of African Football Champions League in the 2019 Confederation of African Football Super Cup. TP Mazembe were the two_time defending champions, but as they qualified for the 2018 Confederation of African Football Champions League and reached the group stage, they were not able to defend their title. All 56 Confederation of African Football member associations may enter the CAF Confederation Cup, with the 12 highest ranked associations according to their Confederation of African Football 5_Year Ranking eligible to enter two teams in the competition. For the 2018 CAF Confederation Cup, the Confederation of African Football uses the 2012–2016 Confederation of African Football 5_Year Ranking, which calculates points for each entrant association based on their clubs’ performance over those 5 years in the Confederation of African Football Champions League and CAF Confederation Cup. A further 16 teams eliminated from the 2018 Confederation of African Football Champions League entered the play_off round. The draw for the group stage was held on 2018, 14:00 EET (UTC+2), at the Confederation of African Football headquarters in Cairo, Egypt. The bracket was decided after the draw for the knockout stage (quarter_finals and semi_finals), which was held on 2018, 19:00 EET (UTC+2), at the Confederation of African Football headquarters in Cairo, Egypt." .

