lsd:Entity_8634 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Baidoa began on 20 December 2006 when the Somali Somali Transitional Federal Government's forces (Transitional Federal Government) allied with occupying Ethiopian forces attacked the Islamic Courts Union (Islamic Courts Union). On December 3, it was reported in an Amharic newspaper The Ethiopian Herald that 60 Islamic Courts Union militiamen had surrendered to Transitional Federal Government forces in Baidoa, along with their technicals, the newspaper reported that were dissatisfied by extremist policies arising within the Islamic Courts Union. Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti were barred from taking part in the peacekeeping operation, leaving it up to Uganda, Tanzania and Eritrea, primarily Uganda. In a press statement the Eritrea Permanent Mission to the United Nations officially denied its nation had any troops in Somalia. On December 8, Transitional Federal Government backed by Ethiopian troops (according to eyewitnesses) attacked the Islamic Courts Union militias in the town of Dinsoor, 110km southwest of Baidoa, sparking heavy fighting. The UN had accused Ethiopia of breaking the arms embargo and it was their intention to send more neutral foreign forces to support the Transitional Federal Government. He said that the deadline the Islamic Courts Union had given the Ethiopians to remove their armed forces from Somalia had been for guidance and that the Islamic Courts Union were still \"hoping for peace\". The Battle of Baidoa began on 20 December 2006 when the Transitional Federal Government allied with occupying Ethiopian forces attacked the Islamic Courts Union. Transitional Federal Government forces and the Islamic Courts Union militiamen exchanged mortar fire at Daynunay, 20km from Baidoa according to eyewitnesses. The Transitional Federal Government claimed that the Islamic Courts Union started the fighting, while the Islamic Courts Union said the Transitional Federal Government had done so." .

lsd:Entity_8659 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss World Italy 2018, the 13th Miss World Italy pageant, was held in the Teatro Italia, Gallipoli." .

lsd:Entity_8664 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Song conquest of Southern Han (Chinese: 宋灭南汉之战) occurred in 971, when the forces of the Song Dynasty captured the Southern Han capital of Guangzhou in present_day Guangdong Province." .

lsd:Entity_8688 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1919 West Virginia Mountaineers football team represented the West Virginia Mountaineers during the 1919 college football season." .

lsd:Entity_8692 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 19 October 1956, Japan and the Soviet Union signed a Soviet–Japanese Joint Declaration of 1956 providing for the end of the state of war, and for restoration of diplomatic relations between USSR and Japan. The Soviet–Japanese Joint Declaration of 1956 provided, in its article 9, for continuation of negotiations for the conclusion of a peace treaty after the restoration of diplomatic relations between the countries and further stipulated, that \"in this connexion, the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, desiring to meet the wishes of Japan and taking into consideration the interests of the Japanese State, agrees to transfer to Japan the Habomai Islands and the island of Shikoton , the actual transfer of these islands to Japan to take place after the conclusion of a Peace Treaty between the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics and Japan\". The formula did not pass, however: in spite of preliminary agreement with the Soviets to shelve the territorial issue, Japan raised it at the negotiations and managed to get aforementioned territorial clause in the declaration, yet \"interpreted in such a manner as to preserve the plenipotentiaries' face at home\": \"Habomais and Shikotan were promised in the Soviet–Japanese Joint Declaration of 1956, and the question of Kunashiri and Etorofu was to be settled during negotiations for a peace treaty\"." .

lsd:Entity_8713 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Auckland Blackout was a major electrical blackout in Auckland, the largest city in New Zealand, on 12 June 2006. Power went off at around 8:30 am local time on 12 June 2006 over half of Auckland in New Zealand. Power was restored to the central business district of Auckland at 12:40 local time, 12 June 2006." .

lsd:Entity_8721 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 British Columbia general election (formally the 41st British Columbia general election) was held on 2017, to elect 87 members (MLAs) to the Legislative Assembly to serve in the 41st Parliament of the Canadian province of British Columbia. In the 40th Parliament prior to this general election, the British Columbia Liberal Party (BC Liberals) formed the government under the leadership of Christy Clark, while the BC New Democratic Party (BC NDP), under the leadership of Adrian Dix and then John Horgan, formed the Official Opposition; the BC Green Party were also represented in the legislature with sole MLA and later leader Andrew Weaver. The election was notable in that it marked the end of the Liberal majority government that had led the province since the 2001 election, and the first election in Canada at the federal or provincial level that saw more than one member of a Green party elected. Section 23 of British Columbia's Constitution Act provides that general elections occur on the second Tuesday in May of the fourth calendar year after the last election. As an election was held on May 14, 2013, the subsequent election was conducted on 2017. In preparation for the 2017 provincial election, the Electoral Boundaries Commission Amendment Act, 2014 increased the number of electoral districts from 85 to 87 and required that the number of electoral districts in the North, Cariboo_Thompson, and the Columbia_Kootenay regions not be decreased despite their lower populations since the last adjustment of electoral boundaries. On 2017, the New York Times published a story calling British Columbia the \"Wild West\" of Canadian political cash. According to the New York Times, \"critics of and her party, the conservative British Columbia Liberal Party, say the provincial government has been transformed into a lucrative business, dominated by special interests that trade donations for political favors, undermining Canada's reputation for functional, consensus_driven democracy.\" In terms of election spending, British Columbia currently has no spending limits ahead of the election period. Spending limits for the 2017 election period were adjusted for changes to the consumer price index before being confirmed during the second week in 2017. Summary of the 2017 British Columbia Legislative election." .

lsd:Entity_8738 a lsd:Context_narration ;
