    rdfs:label "The May 2016 Dürümlü bombing (also known as Dürümlü massacre in Turkey) was a truck bombing that took place in Dürümlü hamlet in Diyarbakır's Sur district, Turkey on 12 May 2016 at approximately 22:30 local time." .

lsd:Entity_10013 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Harvard–Penn football rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the Harvard Crimson football and Penn Quakers football." .

lsd:Entity_10059 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 South Asian Association of Regional Co_operation Gold Cup was the first SAFF Championship, held in Lahore, Pakistan (at Railway Stadium) between 16 July 1993 and 1993." .

lsd:Entity_10127 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "TNA No Surrender is an annual professional wrestling event produced by Impact Wrestling. The first TNA No Surrender event took place on 17 July 2005 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The second TNA No Surrender event took place on September 24, 2006 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The third TNA No Surrender event took place on September 9, 2007 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The fourth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 14, 2008 at the General Motors Centre in Oshawa, marking the first time that a TNA No Surrender event and a TNA event was held outside the United States. The fifth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 20, 2009 at Impact Zone in Orlando. The sixth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 5, 2010 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The seventh TNA No Surrender event took place on September 11, 2011 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The eighth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 9, 2012 at the Impact Zone in Orlando. The ninth TNA No Surrender event took place on September 12, 2013 at the Chaifetz Arena in St. Louis. The tenth TNA No Surrender event took place on August 7, 2014 at the Grand Ballroom in New York City. The eleventh TNA No Surrender event took place at the Impact Wrestling Zone in Orlando on June 27, 2015 and aired as a special edition of TNA's weekly broadcast of Impact Wrestling on Destination America on August 5, 2015." .

lsd:Entity_10134 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Virginia Slims Championships were the seventh WTA Tour Championships, the annual tennis tournament for the best female tennis players in singles on the 1978 WTA Tour. The tournament was held from 29 March 1978 to 02 April 1978, in the Oakland Coliseum Arena in Oakland, United States." .

lsd:Entity_10167 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The LaTeX Project Public License (LPPL) is a software license originally written for the LaTeX system. This feature made some people deny that the LPPL is a free software license. However, version 1.3 of the LPPL has weakened this restriction." .

lsd:Entity_10235 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Nuestra Belleza Nayarit 2016 was the 23rd edition of Nuestra Belleza Nayarit. The finale was on 2016 in the municipality of Compostela." .

lsd:Entity_10350 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Vienna, in 1529, was the first attempt by the Ottoman Empire to capture the city of Vienna, Archduchy of Austria. Nevertheless, Vienna was able to survive the siege, which ultimately lasted just over two weeks, from 27 September to 15 17 October 1529. The failure of the Siege of Vienna marked the beginning of 150 years of bitter military tension between the Habsburgs and Ottomans, punctuated by reciprocal attacks, and culminating in a second siege of Vienna in 1683." .

lsd:Entity_10357 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Texas Longhorns football team (variously \"Texas\" or the \"Horns\") represented the University of Texas at Austin in the 2008 NCAA Division I FBS football season. The Texas Longhorns play their home games in Darrell K Royal–Texas Memorial Stadium (DKR), which during 2006–2008 was undergoing renovations to improve older sections as well as to add extra seating capacity. Five Texas Longhorns were selected in the 2008 NFL Draft: Limas Sweed (53rd pick), Jamaal Charles (73rd), Jermichael Finley (91st), Tony Hills (130th), and Frank Okam (151st). The Texas Longhorns hired Will Muschamp as defensive coordinator; he will also coach linebackers in 2008. With the departure of running back Jamaal Charles for the 2008 NFL Draft, McCoy became the leading returning rusher for the Texas Longhorns. The 2008 game marks the first meeting between the Texas Longhorns and the Florida Atlantic Owls. This 2008 game marks the first meeting between the Texas Longhorns and the University of Texas at El Paso Miners to occur in El Paso. Texas won the 2008 game, 35–7; Notre Dame won vs. Navy the same day, leaving the Texas Longhorns and the Fighting Irish tied for second place in all_time wins. Although Texas entered the 2008 contest with a record of 73–36–5, the Texas Longhorns had lost the last two games to the Aggies. The Texas Longhorns will hold their traditional Hex Rally 2008." .

lsd:Entity_10364 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Dodge Charger 500, the 56th running of the Bojangles' Southern 500, was a NASCAR Nextel Cup Series race held on 07 May 2005 at Darlington Raceway in Darlington County." .

lsd:Entity_10404 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Devil's River was an 1857 Comanche Wars skirmish which took place in Texas along the Devils River." .

lsd:Entity_10472 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The No Rest for the Wicked Tour was a tour by Ozzy Osbourne in 1988 and 1989, supporting his album No Rest for the Wicked." .

lsd:Entity_10477 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The second Siege of Itami (伊丹城の戦い), also called the siege of Arioka Castle (有岡城の戦い) during the Sengoku period of Japanese history, occurred in 1579, five years after it was seized by Oda Nobunaga from a lord named Itami, and entrusted to Araki Murashige." .

lsd:Entity_1053 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shamrock Rovers XI vs Brazil association football exhibition match was played in Lansdowne Road in Dublin, Ireland, on 03 July 1973 between the Brazil national football team and a team billed as \"Shamrock Rovers XI\", made up of Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland international players." .

lsd:Entity_10538 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 29th People's Choice Awards, honoring the best in popular culture for 2002, were held on 12 January 2003 at the Shrine Auditorium in Los Angeles, California. They were hosted by Tony Danza, and broadcast on CBS." .

lsd:Entity_10559 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1914 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Carlton Football Club and Sydney Swans, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 26 September 1914. It was the 17th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1914 VFL season." .

lsd:Entity_10564 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 International 200 was a NASCAR Grand National Series event that was held on 1960, at Bowman Gray Stadium in Winston_Salem. Bowman Gray Stadium is a NASCAR sanctioned 1⁄4_mile (0.40 km) asphalt flat oval short track and longstanding football stadium located in Winston_Salem. Bowman Gray Stadium is part of the Winston_Salem Sports and Entertainment Complex and is home of the Winston_Salem State University Rams football team. This would be the 22nd race out of the 44 raced that year; making this race the official halfway point of the 1960 Grand National Series season." .

lsd:Entity_10568 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Princeton Tigers men's basketball team represented Princeton University in intercollegiate college basketball during the 2010–11 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The team was coming off of a 22–9 2009–10 Princeton Tigers men's basketball team in which it achieved the most wins by a Tigers men's basketball team since the 1998–99 team and its first back_to_back finishes of at least second place in the Ivy since 2001–02 season. Princeton entered the season having not won a championship since the 2003–04 Princeton Tigers men's basketball team achieved the feat and went to the 2004 NCAA Men's Division I Basketball Tournament." .

lsd:Entity_10586 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Alaska gubernatorial election took place on 2018, to elect the governor and lieutenant governor of Alaska. Incumbent independent governor Bill Walker was running for reelection in what was originally a three_way race between Walker, Republican nominee Mike J. Dunleavy, a former State Senator, and Democratic nominee Mark Begich, a former U.S." .

lsd:Entity_10652 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Conquest of Tunis marked the final conquest of Tunis by the Ottoman Empire over the Spanish Empire. The Bey of Algiers Uluj Ali Pasha captured Tunis in 1569 for the Ottoman Empire, but in the aftermath of the 1571 Christian victory at the Battle of Lepanto, John of Austria managed to take Tunis in October 1573. The army attacked Tunis and La Goulette; the presidio of La Goulette, defended by 7,000 men, fell on 24 August 1574. The last Christian troops in a small fort opposite Tunis surrendered on 03 September 1574. The battle marked the final establishment of Ottoman rule in Tunis, putting an end to the Hafsid dynasty and the Spanish presence in Tunis." .

lsd:Entity_10658 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Bolaven, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Julian, was regarded as the most powerful storm to strike the Korean Peninsula in nearly a decade, with wind gusts measured up to 186 km/h (116 mph). At that time, the JMA estimated the typhoon to have reached its peak intensity with winds of 185 km/h (115 mph) and a barometric pressure of 910 mbar (hPa; 26.87 inHg)." .

lsd:Entity_10691 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 English Badminton Championships was held in K2 Crawley, from 30 January 2015 to 01 February 2015." .

lsd:Entity_10695 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 FIA GT1 World Championship season was the third and final season of the SRO Group's FIA GT1 World Championship, an auto racing series for grand tourer cars. Stéphane Ratel, CEO of the SRO Group, stated during the course of the 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season that the 2012 calendar will include rounds in Europe, as well as Brazil, Argentina, and the United Arab Emirates. During the 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season finale, Ratel confirmed that the 2012 season will open at the Circuit Paul Armagnac in Nogaro, France, a departure from the opening round of the past two seasons at the Yas Marina Circuit, Abu Dhabi. During the final 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season meeting of the FIA World Motor Sport Council, following discussions between the SRO, teams, and manufacturers/tuners concerning the organisation of the championship, it was agreed to run the 2012 FIA GT1 World Championship season with GT3 cars only, as the existing field of GT1 cars was no longer sufficient. 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season manufacturers Nissan and Chevrolet were not represented on the 2012 grid. Several drivers from the 2011 FIA GT1 World Championship season return for 2012, most notably the drivers of the defending Teams Champions Hexis Racing." .

lsd:Entity_10736 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1976 EuroBasket Women, commonly called EuroBasket 1976 Women, was the 15th regional championship held by FIBA Europe. The competition was held in France and took place from 20 May 1976 to 29 May 1976." .

lsd:Entity_10823 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Rostov Oblast bus crash was a collision between a bus and a Mercedes_Benz Actros 1841LS oil tanker near Rostov_on_Don, Rostov Oblast, Russia. 21 people died, and 8 others were injured." .

lsd:Entity_10842 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1978 Kiribati parliamentary election were held in the Gilbert Islands on 01 February 1978, with a second round on 06 February 1978." .

lsd:Entity_10864 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Cup of China was the fourth event of six in the 2004–05 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior_level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Capital Indoor Stadium in Beijing on 11 November 2004–14." .

lsd:Entity_10879 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Spain participated in the Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017 with the song \"Do It for Your Lover\" written by Manel Navarro and Antonio Rayo \"Rayito\". Prior to the 2017 contest, Spain had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest fifty_six times since its first entry in 1961. In Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016, Spain placed twenty_second with the song \"Say Yay!\" TVE confirmed their intentions to participate at the 2017 Eurovision Song Contest on 14 September Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016. For their 2017 entry, the broadcaster revealed details regarding their selection procedure on 26 October Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 and announced the organization of a further edition of Objetivo Eurovisión. Objetivo Eurovisión 2017 was the competition that selected Spain's entry for the Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017. The 30 shortlisted entries were revealed via TVE's official website rtve.es on 1 December Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016, including songs by Javi Soleil, who represented Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2007 as part of D'Nash, and Brequette, runner_up at the 2014 national final. The top ten entries that qualified for the next stage were announced on 15 December Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 during the webcast show Spain Calling, hosted by Irene Mahía and Paloma G. Quirós and streamed on TVE's official website rtve.es. The members of the jury panel were: The three finalists were announced on 20 December Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 during the webcast show Spain Calling, hosted by Irene Mahía and Paloma G. Quirós and streamed on TVE's official website rtve.es. The Spain in the Eurovision Song Contest 2017 took place at the International Exhibition Centre in Kyiv, Ukraine and consisted of two semi_fina9 Mays on 11 May11 May and the final on 2017. At the semi_final allocation draw on 2017, Spain was drawn to vote in the first semi_final on 9 May; the country also performed in the first semi_final jury show on 8 May, and an extended clip of the performance was broadcast in the televised semi_final show the following evening." .

lsd:Entity_10907 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Hardihood was a security operation conducted from 16 May to 08 June 1966 during the Vietnam War by the U.S. 503rd Infantry Regiment, the 1st Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment (1RAR) and the 5th Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment (5 RAR) in Phước Tuy, South Vietnam to secure the area around Nui Dat for the establishment of a base area for the 1st Australian Task Force (1 ATF)." .

lsd:Entity_10917 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Tabasco, also known as the Battle of Villahermosa, was a battle fought in 16 June 1847 during the Mexican_American War as part of the U.S. blockade of Mexican Gulf ports. Commodore Matthew C. Perry, commander of the U.S. Home Squadron had recently captured the port cities of Tuxpan and Carmen, 338 He next decided to move against the city of Villahermosa (present day Villahermosa), the capital of the state of Tabasco. In the aftermath of the U.S. victory, a movement in the states of Tabasco and Chiapas arose which sought to break the two states from Mexico and join with Guatemala, but Perry withheld support, and the movement eventually died off." .

lsd:Entity_10944 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Neapolitan campaigns of Louis the Great, also called the Neapolitan Adventure (Nápolyi kaland in Hungarian), was a war between the Kingdom of Hungary, led by Louis the Great, and the Kingdom of Naples. It was fought from 1347 until 1352." .

lsd:Entity_10960 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Four Days of Dunkirk (French: Quatre Jours de Dunkerque) is road bicycle racing around the Nord_Pas_de_Calais region of northern France." .

lsd:Entity_10982 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Earthquake in Ecuador 2016 occurred on 16 April 2016 at 18:58:37 ECT with a moment magnitude of 7.8 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of VIII (Severe). At 18:58 ECT on 16 April 2016, a 7.8 Mw earthquake struck the coast of Ecuador approximately 27 km (17 mi) south_southeast of Muisne, in the province of Esmeraldas, at a depth of 20.6 km (12.8 mi). This is the largest earthquake to strike Ecuador since the 1979 Tumaco earthquake. It was the worst natural disaster to hit Ecuador since the 1949 Ambato earthquake. In Colombia, near the city of Cali, one miner died as a result of the earthquake, becoming the only reported death outside of Ecuador." .

lsd:Entity_11026 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 Ryder Cup were held 1949–1949, at Ganton Golf Club in Scarborough, North Yorkshire, England." .

lsd:Entity_1103 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aeropuerto Internacional de Chinchero is an under construction airport in the town of Chinchero District, Cuzco, Peru." .

lsd:Entity_11068 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 European Figure Skating Championships were held 26 January 2015 – 01 February 2015 in Stockholm, Sweden. The event was the first figure skating ISU Championship to be held in Stockholm since 1947, when the city hosted the World Championships." .

lsd:Entity_11078 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Indian Premier League Final was a day/night Twenty20 cricket match played between the Chennai Super Kings and the Mumbai Indians on 2013 at Eden Gardens, Kolkata to determine the winners of the 2013 Indian Premier League, the sixth annual season of the professional Twenty20 cricket tournament in India. Heading into the tournament, as with the previous seasons, both the Chennai Super Kings and Mumbai Indians were considered to be amongst the favourites due to their past performances and squads. Both the Chennai Super Kings and the Mumbai Indians were a part of the original eight teams of the Indian Premier League when its first season was held in 2008. The Final was at the neutral venue of Eden Gardens, Kolkata and saw Chennai and Mumbai face each other for the fourth time this season in a rematch of the 2010 Final, won by Chennai." .

lsd:Entity_11095 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Thailand Open Badminton Championships (officially known as the Toyota Thailand Open Badminton Championships 2018 for sponsorship reasons) was a badminton tournament which took place at Nimibutr Stadium in Bangkok, Thailand, from 10 to 15 July 2018 and had a total purse of $350,000. The 2018 Thailand Open Badminton Championships was the thirteenth tournament of the 2018 BWF World Tour and also part of the Thailand Open Badminton Championships championships, which had been held since 1984. This international tournament was held at Nimibutr Stadium in Bangkok, Thailand." .

lsd:Entity_11116 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 Amstel Gold Race was the 26th edition of the annual road bicycle racing \"Amstel Gold Race\", held on Saturday 27 April 1991, in the Dutch province of Limburg. The race stretched 244 kilometres, with the start in Heerlen and the finish in Maastricht." .

lsd:Entity_11124 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dubica (Croatian: Bitka kod Dubice) was fought on 16 August 1513 between the Croatia in personal union with Hungary and the Ottoman Empire." .

lsd:Entity_11134 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 4:44 Tour was a concert tour by Jay_Z, in support of his thirteenth studio album 4:44 (2017)." .

lsd:Entity_11159 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 2007, unanimously adopted on 14 September 2011, after recalling resolution 1786 (2007) on the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia (ICTY), the Council reappointed Serge Brammertz as prosecutor of the Tribunal, countermanding the Tribunal's statute." .

lsd:Entity_11190 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Men's United States Open (squash) 2013 is the men's edition of the 2013 United States Open, which is a PSA World Series event Gold(Prize money: $115,000). The event took place at the Daskalakis Athletic Center in Philadelphia in the United States of America from the 13th of October to the 18th October." .

lsd:Entity_11222 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bandy World Championship 2015 was the XXXVth Bandy World Championships. At the Federation of International Bandy congress held during the Bandy World Championship 2014 in 2014, it was announced that Khabarovsk in Russia had been elected as host city. It was also decided the Group A tournament would be played around the end of March/beginning of April, which means it would take place when the national bandy leagues in the major bandy playing countries, Finland, Norway, Russia and Sweden, would be finished for the season. Latvia won Bandy World Championship 2014 and was thus promoted to Division A for 2015." .

lsd:Entity_11240 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Primera División season was the 25th edition of Spain's highest women's association football league. The season played from 02 September 2012, and end on 05 May 2013. A total of 16 teams contested the league, 14 of which already contested in the 2011–12 Primera División." .

lsd:Entity_11261 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 UCI Road World Championships took place in Agrigento, Italy between 1994_28 August 1994." .

lsd:Entity_11279 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Freestyle World Ski Championships 1993 were held between 11 March 1993 and 14 March 1993 at the Altenmarkt im Pongau ski resort in Austria." .

lsd:Entity_11357 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Tacx Pro Classic was a road bicycle racing stage race held annually in June in Zeeland, Netherlands. In 2008, the race was organized as a 2.1 event on the UCI Europe Tour as the organizers of the one_day race Delta Profronde and of the OZ Wielerweekend operated a fusion to create the stage race. In 2012, it was renamed Ronde van Zeeland Seaports, coming back to its one_day race roots with a 1.1 ranking on the UCI Europe Tour." .

lsd:Entity_11379 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In 29 November 1993 an event known as the \"1993 Jolimont Centre siege\" took place resulting in the serious wounding on one person and the death of another over the course of two hours. The Jolimont Centre, located in Canberra, the Australian Capital Territory was the home of the Department of Industrial Relations and radio station 2CA. About 7:30 a.m., on 29 November 1993, 47_year_old Felipe Ruizdiaz shot and wounded local pool manager, Geoff McGibbon, at the Dickson Swimming Pool in Canberra before crashing his utility vehicle rigged with petrol and gas canisters through the front glass walls of the six storey Jolimont Centre, home of the Department of Industrial Relations in Canberra in an apparent revenge attack on his estranged wife." .

lsd:Entity_11383 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Action Comics #1 (cover dated June 1938) is the first issue of the original run of the comic book/magazine series Action Comics. Action Comics #1 was an anthology, and contained eleven features: Published on June 30, 1938 (cover_dated June), by National Allied Publications, a corporate predecessor of DC Comics, it is considered the first true superhero comic; and though today Action Comics is a monthly title devoted to Superman, it began, like many early comics, as an anthology. Liebowitz would later say that selecting Superman to run in Action Comics #1 was \"pure accident\" based on deadline pressure and that he selected a \"thrilling\" cover, depicting Superman lifting a car over his head. Mayer found the rejected Superman comic strips, and Sullivan told Siegel and Shuster that if they could paste them into 13 comic book pages, he would buy them. On February 22, 2010, a copy of Action Comics #1 CGC Grade 8.0 sold at auction for US$1 million, becoming the first million_dollar comic book. EC and Mad publisher William Gaines, whose father was also a comic book publisher and had business dealings with DC Comics at the time Action Comics #1 was published, claimed in a Comics Journal interview that he at one point had dozens of copies of the issue around his house, but they were probably all thrown out. DC reprinted Action Comics #1 in 1988 as part of the 50th anniversary celebration of Superman that year." .

lsd:Entity_11406 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The January 28 Incident or Shanghai incident (28 January 1932 – 03 March 1932) was a conflict between the Republic of China (1912–1949) and the Empire of Japan. In Chinese literature it is known as the January 28 Incident (simplified Chinese: 一·二八事变; traditional Chinese: 一·二八事變; pinyin: Yī Èrbā Shìbiàn), while in Western sources it is often called the Shanghai War of 1932 or the Shanghai incident. In Japan it is known as the first Shanghai incident, alluding to the second Shanghai incident, which is the Japanese name for the Battle of Shanghai that occurred during the opening stages of the Second Sino_Japanese War in 1937. The Japanese issued an ultimatum to the Shanghai Municipal Council demanding public condemnation and monetary compensation by the Chinese for any Japanese property damaged in the monk incident, and demanding that the Chinese government take active steps to suppress further anti_Japanese protests in the city. During the afternoon of 28 January 1932, the Shanghai Municipal Council agreed to these demands. However, at midnight on 28 January 1932, Japanese carrier aircraft bombed Shanghai in the first major aircraft carrier action in East Asia. Though the opening battles took place in the Hongkew district of the International Settlement, the conflict soon spread outwards to much of Chinese_controlled Shanghai. The majority of the concessions remained untouched by the conflict, and it was often the case that those in the Shanghai International Settlement would watch the war from the banks of Suzhou Creek." .

lsd:Entity_11476 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1962–63 Taça de Portugal, the 23rd season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 30 June 1963 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: Sporting CP and Vitória S.C.. Vitória S.C. defeated Sporting CP 4–0 to claim their sixth Taça de Portugal." .

lsd:Entity_11493 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Tilia Slovenia Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Portorož, Slovenia between 07 August 2017 – 12 August 2017." .

lsd:Entity_11510 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pla was a battle on 15 January 1811 between an Imperial French column made up of two Italian brigades on one side and a Spanish division under the command of Pedro Sarsfield on the other. The action was fought near El Pla de Santa Maria, north of Valls, Catalonia, Spain." .

lsd:Entity_11526 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Danish Superliga season was the 26th season of the Danish Superliga, which decides the Denmark football championship. As Denmark dropped from nineteenth to twenty_second place in the 2015 UEFA association coefficient rankings at the end of the 2014–15 Danish Superliga, the champion of the league will qualify for the UEFA Champions League; that club will commence its campaign in the second qualifying round. FC Vestsjælland and Silkeborg IF finished the 2014–15 Danish Superliga in 11th and 12th place, respectively, and were relegated to the 2015–16 Danish 1st Division." .

lsd:Entity_11565 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 MBNA All_American Heroes 400 was an NASCAR Winston Cup Series Delaware 400 held on 22 September 2002 at Dover International Speedway, in Dover, Delaware. Contested over 400 laps on the 1_mile (1.6 km) concrete speedway, it was the 28th Delaware 400 of the 2002 NASCAR Winston Cup Series season. Jimmie Johnson of Hendrick Motorsports won the Delaware 400. Dover International Speedway (formerly Dover Downs International Speedway) is a Delaware 400 track in Dover, Delaware, United States of America. In 2002, the name changed to Dover International Speedway from Dover Downs International Speedway after Dover Downs Gaming and Entertainment split, making Dover Motorsports." .

lsd:Entity_11622 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Maxwell & Jill Scott: The Tour is a co_headlining concert tour by American recording artists Maxwell and Jill Scott. Heavily playing in the United States, the tour supported Maxwell's fourth studio album BLACKsummers'night." .

lsd:Entity_11660 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Land of Kings is a music and arts festival that took place at various locations throughout the London Borough of Hackney from 2009 to 2015. Dalston Darlings, Dan Beaumont, Dark Shaft, DJ King Knut, Dollop DJs, Dreamtrak, Durrr, Eglo Live Band, Eliphino, Elliot Yorke, Esser (band), Factory Floor, Famy, Fever Dream, Five Easy Pieces, Four Aces, Funkineven, Gang Colours DJ set, Great Waves, Gwilym Gold DJ set, Hackney Film Festival, Hall of Mirrors, Hammer & Tongue, Hannah Holland, Hatcham Social, Is Tropical, Johnno & Charlotte (Bugged Out), Kutmah, Kwes, Kwesachu, Land Shapes, Legacy In The Dust, Let Us Be Golden, Literary Death Match, Living in A Disco, London Fields Radio, Make It Stop Mum, Male Bonding (band), Maria Minerva, Maurice Fulton, Mo Kolours, Moshi Moshi DJs, My Panda Shall Fly, Naomii, Night Angles, NTS DJs, NZCA/Lines, O Children, Ollie Rant, Owiny Sigoma Soundsystem, Public Information DJs, Raf Daddy, Ray Mang, Richard Clancy, Rory Phillips, Royalty, Slagbox, Slow Club, Soft Rocks, Speech Debelle, Spirit of Mantra, Standard Planets, Stay+, Stealing Sheep, Stoke Newington International Airport, Tayo Popoola, The Austerity Games, The Horrors DJ set, The Invisible (band), The Lovely Jonjo, To The Lazer Cave DJs, Toy, Trevor Jackson (musician), Trophy Wife, Virginia Wing, We Were Evergreen, Wicked Wicked, Winstan Whitter, ZTG. The 2 Bears, Art Macabre, Antepop, Boxed In, Brolin, Capablanca, Chloe Black, Chris Troy, Crucial & Fresh, Dalston Darlings: Loose Women, Don Letts, Esther Joy Lane, Flying Ibex, Flyte, Ghost Culture, Gideon Reeling: The Big Queen's Navy, Hairy Hands, Hot Breath Karaoke, Huntleys + Palmers, HVOB, Increments: Vitamins, Koreless, Krankbrother, Laish, Landshapes, Lena Willikens, Let's Be Brief: Place Is The Space, Lex Luger, London Short Film Festival, Looking Glass Cocktail Club, Lost Picture Show, Meilyr Jones, Mo Kolours, Moshi Moshi DJs, Moxie, My Panda Shall Fly, Nathan Fake, Nick Rasie, NZCA Lines, Pool DJs, Portico, Psychemagik, Public Information DJs, Russ Chimes, Soft Rocks, Soul Jazz Sound System, Tape Club, Thomas McCarthy, Thris Tian, Thump DJs, Toby Gale, Tom Vek (solo/AV set), Tony Poland, Ultrademon, Waze & Odyssey, Wolf Music, Wyles & Simpson, Zanzibar Chanel." .

lsd:Entity_11677 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 NCAA Men's Division I Basketball Tournament involved 68 teams playing in a single_elimination tournament to determine the men's National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA) Division I college basketball national champion for the 2015–16 season. The 78th edition of the Tournament began on 15 March 2016, and concluded with the championship game on 04 April 2016 at NRG Stadium in Houston. Regional Semifinals and Finals (Sweet Sixteen and Elite Eight) National Semifinals and Championship (Final Four and Championship) NRG Stadium in Houston hosted the Final Four for the second time in 2016, Houston's third Final Four overall." .

lsd:Entity_11682 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Labour Party (UK) leadership election was held on 21 July 1994 after the sudden death of the incumbent Leader of the Labour Party, John Smith, on 12 May. Margaret Beckett had been the Deputy Leader of the Labour Party, and following Smith's death was serving as acting Leader of the Labour Party; she was the first female Labour MP ever to stand for the leadership of the party (and remained the only such MP until Diane Abbott announced her candidacy for the 2010 leadership election). John Prescott, who had stood at the 1992 deputy leadership election and lost to Beckett, stood again for both Leader of the Labour Party and deputy Leader of the Labour Party." .

