lsd:Entity_12353 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Halola, known in the Philippines as Typhoon Goring, was a small but long_lived tropical cyclone in July 2015 that traveled 7,640 km (4,750 mi) across the Pacific Ocean. On July 21, Halola regained typhoon status and later peaked with 10_minute sustained winds of 150 km/h (90 mph) and a minimum pressure of 955 hPa (mbar; 28.20 inHg)." .

lsd:Entity_12366 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1880 United States presidential election in Pennsylvania took place on 02 November 1880, as part of the 1880 United States presidential election. Pennsylvania voted for the Republican nominee, James A. Garfield, over the Democratic nominee, Winfield Scott Hancock." .

lsd:Entity_12373 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 WGC_Accenture Match Play Championship was a golf tournament held February 17–21 at the The Golf Club at Dove Mountain at Dove Mountain in Marana, northwest of Tucson. It was the 12th WGC Match Play and the first of four World Golf Championships held in 2010." .

lsd:Entity_12411 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Women's Equality Day is celebrated in the United States of America on August 26 to commemorate the 1920 adoption of the Nineteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution (Amendment XIX) to the United States of America Constitution, which prohibits the states and the federal government from denying the right to vote to citizens of the United States of America on the basis of sex. It was first celebrated in 1972, designated by Congress in 1973, and is proclaimed each year by the United States of America President. In 1971, following the 1970 nationwide Women's Strike for Equality, and again in 1973, as the battles over the Equal Rights Amendment continued, Congresswoman Bella Abzug of New York introduced a resolution to designate August 26 as Women's Equality Day. In 1972, President Richard Nixon issued Proclamation 4147, which designated August 26, 1972 as \"Women's Rights Day\" and was the first official proclamation of Women's Equality Day. 52, which stated that August 26 would be designated as Women's Equality Day and that \"the President is authorized and requested to issue a proclamation in commemoration of that day in 1920 on which the women in America were first guaranteed the right to vote\". As of 2018, every president since Richard Nixon has issued a proclamation designating August 26 as Women's Equality Day." .

lsd:Entity_12425 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Medina de Rioseco, also known as the Battle of Moclín, was fought during the Peninsular War on 14 July 1808 when a combined body of Spain militia and regulars moved to rupture the French line of communications to Madrid. But in the event, Medina de Rioseco proved to be the solitary French triumph in an invasion of Spain that ultimately failed to seize the country's major cities or to pacify its rebellious provinces, and which met outright disaster at Bailén, forcing French forces—Bessières' victorious corps included—to fly over the Ebro in retreat. By 14 July 1808 Cuesta had drawn up the Spain force near Medina de Rio Seco, with Blake commanding the forward position on a small elevation and Cuesta hovering about a mile to the rear (near the village proper), with many of the best troops. Medina de Rioseco was a sorely contested battle, with the Spain infantry attack upon the ridge—conducted with \"precision and audacity\"—coming close to victory. Cuesta's divisions (though not the commander himself) received special praise for nearly securing a dramatic victory even after Blake's rout: The battle of Rio Seco, though unfortunate, was far from dishonourable to Spain prowess... Under circumstances the most unfavourable and dispiriting, the second line of the Spaniards fought with a courage and pertinacity worthy of a better general... That, after the defeat of the first line, the issue of the battle should even for a time have become doubtful, is a circumstance honourable to the courage of the Spain troops. Another contemporary, General Maximilien Sebastien Foy, described the Spain force at Medina de Rioseco as: \"A splinter of the ancient Spain army which demonstrated what such an army could do: for an army new to the field, facing for the first time an experienced foe, it was a lot.\" Foy, however, does not fault Blake with agreeing to a pitched battle: denuded of cavalry, the Spain general faced the grim prospect of traversing an open countryside hounded by 1,500 French sabres under possibly the greatest cavalry commander of all time, General Lasalle." .

lsd:Entity_12486 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Minsk Offensive (Russian: Минская наступательная операция) was part of the second phase of the Belorussian Strategic Offensive of the Red Army in summer 1944, commonly known as Operation Bagration. By 26 June, OKH had finally realised that the developing Operation Bagration was the main Soviet offensive, and that Minsk was its objective. The bulk of 5th Guards Tank Army, accompanied by the rifle divisions of 11th Guards Army, attacked straight down the Minsk road, forcing the German infantry back into Borisov by 29 June 1944: a screen of Soviet troops was left on the road to prevent any more elements of Fourth Army escaping into Minsk. Within the broader strategic framework of Operation Bagration, the Minsk Offensive was a complete success: The 3rd and 2nd Belorussian Fronts were subsequently committed to the third 'pursuit' phase of the strategic offensive in the Vilnius and Belostock Offensives respectively." .

lsd:Entity_12498 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Love Hurts Tour was the third solo concert tour by American singer_actress Cher Bono. Encouraged by the reception of the album Love Hurts, the European smash single \"The Shoop Shoop Song\", and after the performance of \"Could've Been You\" at Top of the Pops – considered as a tour preview, Cher Bono performed six shows as a tour preview at the Sands Atlantic City in Atlantic City, New Jersey. The opening songs Cher Bono used a basic model, “Love and Understanding” had changed wig, “The Shoop Shoop Song” was dates in which she used the smooth wig (like the cover of the album “Love Hurts”) and the wig Video Clip “Save Up All Your Tears”." .

lsd:Entity_12524 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Buddhist crisis (Vietnamese: Biến cố Phật giáo) was a period of political and religious tension in South Vietnam between May and 02 November 1963, characterized by a series of repressive acts by the South Vietnamese government and a campaign of civil resistance, led mainly by Buddhist monks. The crisis ended with a 1963 South Vietnamese coup by the Army of the Republic of Vietnam, and the arrest and assassination of President Ngô Đình Diệm on 02 November 1963. On November 1, 1963, after six months of tension and growing opposition to the regime, generals from the Army of the Republic of Vietnam conducted a 1963 South Vietnamese coup, which led to the fall of the Diem government and the arrest and assassination of Ngo Dinh Diem." .

lsd:Entity_12564 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Qatar Total Open was a professional women's tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place at the Khalifa International Tennis and Squash Complex in Doha, Qatar from 23 February 2015 to 28 February 2015." .

lsd:Entity_12620 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bailén was fought in 1808 between the Spanish Army of Andalusia, led by Generals Francisco Castaños and Theodor von Reding, and the Imperial French Army's II corps d'observation de la Gironde under General Pierre Dupont de l'Étang. The heaviest fighting took place near Bailén (sometimes anglicized Baylen), a village by the Guadalquivir river in the Jaén province of southern Spain. In 1808, following the widespread uprisings against the French occupation of Spain, Napoleon organized French units into flying columns to pacify Spain's major centres of resistance. In the talks, Dupont had agreed to surrender not only his own but Vedel's force as well even though the latter's troops were outside the Spanish encirclement with a good chance of escape; a total of 17,000 men were captured, making Bailén the worst defeat suffered by the French in the entire Peninsular War. Alarmed by these developments, Napoleon Peninsular War of the Spanish theatre and invaded Spain with the Grande Armée. Between 1807 and 1808, thousands of French troops marched into Spain to support a Spanish invasion of Portugal orchestrated by Napoleon, who used the opportunity to initiate intrigues against the Spanish royal family. To this end, Napoleon dispatched a number of flying columns to throttle the rebellion by seizing and pacifying Spain's major cities: from Madrid, Marshal Jean_Baptiste Bessières pushed northwest into Old Castile with 25,000 men and sent a detachment east into Aragón, aiming to capture Santander with one hand and Zaragoza with the other; Marshal Bon Adrien Jeannot de Moncey marched toward Valencia with 29,350 men; and General Guillaume Philibert Duhesme marshalled 12,710 troops in Catalonia and put Gerona under siege. Dupont slipped away from Andújar unobserved and at dawn on 19 July 1808, his vanguard under Brigadier Théodore Chabert made contact with Reding's leading elements (veterans of the Walloon Guard) just shy of Bailén. Fate was particularly cruel to the victors of Bailén: Castaños was himself routed by Marshal Lannes at the Battle of Tudela in 1808, while Reding was ridden down and trampled by the French cavalry at the Battle of Valls in 1809, dying of his wounds. Marshal Soult overran much of Andalusia the following year, and on 21 January 1810 his men recovered the lost Eagles from the cathedral of Bailén. F. L. Lucas's novel The English Agent – A Tale of the Peninsular War (1969), the account of a British Army officer gathering information before the first British landings, is about the Battle of Bailén and its aftermath." .

lsd:Entity_12627 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The year 2016 in Glory is the 5th year in the history of the Glory, an international kickboxing event. Glory 27: Chicago was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Sears Centre in Hoffman Estates. Glory 28: Paris was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the AccorHotels Arena in Paris. Glory 29: Copenhagen was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Forum Copenhagen in Copenhagen, Denmark. Glory 30: Los Angeles was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Citizens Business Bank Arena in Ontario, California, USA. Glory 31: Amsterdam was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Amsterdam RAI in Amsterdam. Glory 32: Virginia was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Ted Constant Convocation Center in Norfolk, Virginia, USA. Glory 33: New Jersey was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Sun National Bank Center in Trenton, New Jersey, USA. Glory 34: Denver was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the 1stBank Center in Broomfield, Colorado, USA. Glory 35: Nice was a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the Palais Nikaia in Nice, France. Glory 36: Oberhausen is a kickboxing event held on 2016 at the König Pilsener Arena in Oberhausen." .

