    rdfs:label "The 2000 UCI Cyclo_cross World Championships were held in Sint_Michielsgestel, Netherlands on Saturday January 28 and Sunday 29 January 2000." .

lsd:Entity_26249 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Eva Blanco Puig (February 17, 1981 – 20 April 1997) was a Spain high school student who was murdered in Algete, Community of Madrid. While it was unsolved, the case was popularly known in Spain as the Eva Blanco Case (Caso Eva Blanco), the murder of Eva Blanco (Crimen de Algete) and Operation Gang (Operación Pandilla), the code name given to the investigation by the Spain Civil Guard. The murder weapon was a navaja between 8 and 10 centimeters long and 1 cm wide. All attempts to discover the meaning of \"343110\" were unsuccessful, with some hypothesis revolving around the fact that \"34\" is the phone prefix of Spain, and \"110\" is Algete's postal code. The proposition was unanimously rejected by Spain judicial organizations who considered it \"simple_minded,\" \"disproportionate,\" \"useless,\" and a potential stigma for Algete residents who refused to provide a DNA sample without court order and were in their legal right to do so. Six years later, in 2013, the number of people investigated had risen to 1,503, and 208 men from Algete and other towns had had their DNA tested, including criminals who were on leave at the time of the murder." .

lsd:Entity_26316 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Norwegian Figure Skating Championships was held in Tønsberg from 11 January 2008 to 13, 2008." .

lsd:Entity_26370 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "It is commonly used interchangeably with the terms such as Political Islam or Islamic fundamentalism, giving this term more than one sense and referring to different concepts. There was no need for any other term, until the rise of an ideological and political interpretation of Islam challenged scholars and commentators to come up with an alternative, to distinguish Islam as modern ideology from Islam as a faith... To all intents and purposes, Islamic fundamentalism and Islamism have become synonyms in contemporary American usage. A writer for the International Crisis Group maintains that \"the conception of 'Political Islam'\" is a creation of Americans to explain the Iranian Islamic Revolution and apolitical Islam was a historical fluke of the \"short_lived era of the heyday of secular Arab nationalism between 1945 and 1970\", and it is quietist/non_Political Islam, not Islamism, that requires explanation." .

lsd:Entity_26406 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cal_UCLA rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the California Golden Bears football team of the University of California, Berkeley and UCLA Bruins football team of the University of California, Los Angeles." .

lsd:Entity_26420 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Winter Universiade, the VIII Winter Universiade, took place in Livigno, Italy." .

lsd:Entity_26445 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Juno Awards of 1975, representing Canadian music industry achievements of the previous year, were awarded on 24 March 1975 in Toronto at a ceremony in the Canadian National Exhibition." .

lsd:Entity_26458 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Germany's Aims in the First World War (German title: Griff nach der Weltmacht: Die Kriegzielpolitik des kaiserlichen Deutschland 1914–1918) is a book by German Historian Fritz Fischer. The book included a memorandum by the then German Chancellor Bethmann_Hollweg dated 9 September 1914 which set out a plan for Germany to dominate Europe. On publication, the book caused controversy in West Germany as it challenged the view that Hitler was an aberration by emphasising the continuity in German foreign policy in 1914 and 1939." .

lsd:Entity_26471 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1893 Virginia gubernatorial election was held on 07 November 1893 to elect the Governor of Virginia." .

lsd:Entity_26477 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Fengshen was the strongest storm of the 2002 Pacific typhoon season. By July 15, Fengshen attained typhoon status, and after initially moving to the north, it turned toward the northwest. The Joint Typhoon Warning Center estimated peak winds of 270 km/h (165 mph 1‐minute winds), and the agency estimated that Fengshen was a typhoon for five days. While near peak intensity, Typhoon Fengshen underwent the Fujiwhara effect with 2002 Pacific typhoon season, causing the latter storm to loop to its south. The cyclone quickly strengthened into Tropical Storm Fengshen just six hours after forming. After a 13 km (8 mi) wide eye developed, the Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) upgraded Fengshen to a typhoon on July 15 to the southwest of Wake Island; the JTWC also upgraded the storm the same day. By late on July 18, the JMA estimated that Fengshen attained maximum sustained winds of 185 km/h (115 mph 10‐minute), around the same time the JTWC upgraded the storm to a typhoon. Around that time of its weakening, Fengshen began undergoing the Fujiwhara effect with 2002 Pacific typhoon season; the latter looped to the south of Fengshen and dissipated on July 29. On July 21, Fengshen began re_intensifying, and the JMA reported that the typhoon again reached winds of 185 km/h (115 mph 10‐minute). Fengshen retained much of its intensity until July 22, being a typhoon for five days. After several days as a powerful typhoon, Fengshen began a weakening trend due to decreased outflow and dry air. On July 24, Fengshen turned more to the west while passing to the south of mainland Japan, and the next day it weakened below typhoon intensity." .

lsd:Entity_26496 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Malawian democracy referendum, 1993 was held in Malawi on 14 June 1993." .

lsd:Entity_26508 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Copa Centroamericana (English: 2011 Central American Cup) was the eleventh edition of Copa Centroamericana (formerly known as the Copa Centroamericana), an international association football championship for national teams affiliated with the Central American Football Union (UNCAF) of the CONCACAF region. It took place in Panama from 14 January 2011 to 23 January 2011." .

lsd:Entity_26513 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2001 Cypriot legislative election were held in Cyprus on 27 May 2001. The result was a victory for Progressive Party of Working People, which won 20 of the 56 seats." .

lsd:Entity_26536 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Women's European Water Polo Championship took place at the Pieter van den Hoogenband Swim Stadium in Eindhoven, the Netherlands, from January 18 to 28, 2012." .

lsd:Entity_26549 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2000 Summer Olympics – men's 110 metres hurdles as part of the athletics at the 2000 Summer Olympics were held at Stadium Australia on Sunday 24 September 2000 and Monday 25 September 2000." .

lsd:Entity_26568 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Copa del Rey Final was the 104th final since its establishment. The match took place on 2006 at the Estadio Santiago Bernabéu in Madrid. The match was contested by Espanyol and Real Zaragoza, and it was refereed by Luis Medina Cantalejo." .

lsd:Entity_26645 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2020 IIHF World Championship would have been hosted from 8 to 24 May 2020 by Switzerland, as the International Ice Hockey Federation announced on 15 May 2015. The seedings in the preliminary round are based on the 2019 International Ice Hockey Federation World Ranking, as of the end of the 2019 IIHF World Championship, using the serpentine system." .

lsd:Entity_26672 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The first season of American Idol premiered on June 11, 2002 (under the full title American Idol, season 1) and continued until September 4, 2002. The winner, Kelly Clarkson, signed with RCA Records, the label in partnership with American Idol's 19 Recordings. Controversy erupted when the winner of Season 1, Kelly Clarkson, was invited to sing \"The Star_Spangled Banner\" in a deal arranged by her then management company, 19 Entertainment, at a special commemoration for the first anniversary of September 11 attacks at Washington's Lincoln Memorial. Re_edited episodes of American Idol season 1 were being shown in syndication as American Idol Rewind. Rewind also showcased footage that was not seen in the initial run of season 1, including Kelly Clarkson's full audition." .

lsd:Entity_26762 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Lower Austrian state election was held on 09 March 2008 to elect the members of the Landtag of Lower Austria." .

lsd:Entity_26767 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Toppserien was the twenty_fourth season of top_tier women's association football in Norway since its establishment in 1987. The season ran from 05 April 2010 to 11 November 2010, and was won by Stabæk Fotball Kvinner, their first ever title. As of 2010, the women's association football club Team Strømmen are affiliated with Lillestrøm SK and have changed their name to LSK Kvinner. This is the second instance in Norway of a leading women's club becoming affiliated with a leading men's club, the first occurring in 2009 when Asker Fotball relocated to Bærum and became Stabæk Fotball Kvinner Fotball." .

lsd:Entity_26776 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cage to Rattle Tour is a current concert tour by American rock band Daughtry in support of their fifth studio album, Cage to Rattle." .

lsd:Entity_26803 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Charleston Open, also known by its sponsored name Volvo Car Open and formerly the Family Circle Cup from 1973 to 2015, is a Women's Tennis Association_affiliated professional tennis tournament for women, held every year since 1973. The tournament is played on the green clay court at the Family Circle Tennis Center (which contains the 10,200_seat Volvo Car Stadium) on Daniel Island in Charleston, South Carolina, USA. From its inception in 1973 to 2000, the tournament was held at the Sea Pines Plantation on Hilton Head Island with the exception of 1975 and 1976 when it was played on Amelia Island off the coast of Florida. The event moved to Charleston in 2001. From 1973 to 2015, the title sponsor was Family Circle magazine, which had made it the longest_running title sponsor in professional tennis." .

lsd:Entity_26877 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1835 was adopted unanimously by United Nations Security Council on 27 September 2008. The 5984th meeting of the UN United Nations Security Council took place on 27 September 2008. The provisions of Resolution 1835 were terminated by United Nations Security Council Resolution 2231 effective on Implementation Day of the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action, 16 January 2016." .

lsd:Entity_26883 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "cross_country skiing at the 2013 Winter Universiade was held at the Lago di Tesero Cross_Country Ski Stadium in Tesero from 12 December 2013 to 21 December 2013." .

lsd:Entity_26886 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cyzicus was fought in 193 between the forces of Septimius Severus and his rival for the empire, Pescennius Niger, who was defeated. The battle took place in the context of the Year of the Five Emperors, a tumultuous period in the Roman Empire when Emperor Pertinax was assassinated by the Praetorian Guards. The battle was fought near Cyzicus in Asia Minor." .

lsd:Entity_26926 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 FIFA Women's World Cup was the inaugural FIFA Women's World Cup, the world championship for women's national association football teams. It was won by the United States women's national soccer team, whose captain April Heinrichs formed a forward line dubbed the \"triple–edged sword\" with Carin Jennings and Michelle Akers. The United States women's national soccer team defeated Norway 2–1 in the final in front of a crowd of 65,000 people at Guangzhou's Tianhe Stadium. The draw for the group stage was held on 1991 at the Tianhe Stadium in Guangzhou, China. There were 99 goals scored in 26 matches, for an average of 3.81 goals per match. Michelle Akers of the United States women's national soccer team won the FIFA Women's World Cup award for scoring ten goals." .

lsd:Entity_26932 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Six Gallery reading (also known as the Gallery Six reading or Six Angels in the Same Performance) was an important poetry event that took place on Friday, 07 October 1955, at 3119 Fillmore Street in San Francisco. Conceived by Wally Hedrick, this event was the first important public manifestation of the Beat Generation and helped to herald the West Coast literary revolution that continued the San Francisco Renaissance." .

lsd:Entity_26966 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The sailing at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's sailboard was a sailing event on the sailing at the 2004 Summer Olympics program in Agios Kosmas Olympic Sailing Centre." .

lsd:Entity_26993 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sis Hores de Cançó in Canet de Mar was a mass festival of Catalan singing in a time when Catalan, as a language and as a culture, was somehow persecuted by the Spanish Francoist dictatorship. The first edition of Sis Hores de Cançó took place on 18 September 1971." .

lsd:Entity_26998 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Deutschland Cup was a one_off football competition played in 27 November 1990, to celebrate German reunification. The match was played one week after the dissolution of the East German Football Association and its merger with the German Football Association, and featured the reigning champions of East and West Germany, Dynamo Dresden and FC Bayern Munich respectively. The match featured the reigning cup winners of East and West Germany, Dynamo Dresden and 1." .

lsd:Entity_27033 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006–07 Umaglesi Liga was the eighteenth season of top_tier football in Georgia. It began on 26 29 July 2006 and ended on 20 May 2007." .

lsd:Entity_27043 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cape Sarych was a naval engagement fought off the coast of Sarych in the Black Sea during the World War I." .

lsd:Entity_27069 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Thirty Meter Telescope protests are a series of protests and demonstrations that began on the Hawaii in the United States over the choosing of Mauna Kea for the site location of the Thirty Meter Telescope. Protests began locally within the state of Hawaii on 07 October 2014 but went global within weeks of the April 2, 2015 arrest of 31 people who had blockaded the roadway to keep construction crews off the summit. While opposition against the observatories on Mauna Kea has been ongoing since the first telescope, built by the University of Hawaii, this protest may be the most vocal. On the \"Big Island\" of Hawaii, Mauna Kea is considered the highest island mountain in the world, measuring roughly 33,000 feet from the base deep under the Pacific Ocean. Although polls, some of them highly criticized, indicate that a majority of Hawaii residents support the Thirty Meter Telescope, opposition to the project and other observatories has existed since 1964. The Thirty Meter Telescope (TMT) is a proposed extremely large, segmented mirror telescope, planned for the summit of Mauna Kea. However, Mauna Kea's summit is considered the most sacred of all the mountains in Hawaii to many Native Hawaiian people. The Hawaii Board of Land and Natural Resources conditionally approved the Mauna Kea site for the TMT in February 2011. A planned ceremony at the base of the mountain was scheduled by the group, Mauna Kea Anaina Hou, in opposition of the telescope and in a press release dated that day, the organization Sacred Mauna Kea stated: \"Native Hawaiians and non_Hawaiians will gather for a peaceful protest against the Astronomy industry and the State of Hawaii’s ground_breaking ceremony for a thirty_meter telescope (TMT) on the summit of Mauna Kea.\" Construction was halted for one week at the request of Hawaii state governor David Ige on April 7, 2015 after the protest on Mauna Kea continued and demonstrations began to appear over the state. The days actions were described in a court declaration filed in connection to a Mauna Kea access case by Hawaii County Police, which claims that there was a \"significant risk\" that certain protesters would \"respond with violence\" if officers forcefully separated protesters blocking the road. The protest continued into August 2019 at the entrance of the Mauna Kea access road, in front of Pu'u Huluhulu on Hawaii Route 200. On July 20, 2019 an online petition titled \"A Call to Divest Canada's Research Funding for the Thirty Meter Telescope on Mauna Kea\" has been posted on Change.org." .

lsd:Entity_27103 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 1932 Honduran general election (Spanish: Elecciones generales de Honduras de 1932) was held in Honduras on 28 October 1932. As a result, the National Party of Honduras candidate, Tiburcio Carías Andino, won the election by a margin of some 20,000 votes\"." .

lsd:Entity_27107 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Screaming Bloody Murder Tour is a concert tour by rock band Sum 41, taking place between 2010–11 and resuming again in 2012, in support of their fifth full_length studio album Screaming Bloody Murder." .

lsd:Entity_27127 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Diu was a naval battle fought on 03 February 1509 in the Arabian Sea, in the port of Diu, India, between the Portuguese Empire and a joint fleet of the Sultan of Gujarat, the Mamlûk Burji Sultanate of Egypt, and the Zamorin of Calicut with support of the Republic of Venice and the Ottoman Empire. A cunning and pragmatic ruler, Malik Ayyaz turned the city into the main port of Gujarat (known to the Portuguese as Cambaia) and one of the main entrepôts between India and the Persian Gulf, avoiding Portuguese hostility by pursuing a policy of appeasement and even alignment – up until Hussain unexpectedly sailed into Diu. On 2 03 February 1509, the Portuguese sighted Diu from atop the crow nests." .

lsd:Entity_27180 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Volta a Catalunya was the 87th edition of the Volta a Catalunya road bicycle racing, which took place from 21 May 2007 to 27 May 2007, in Catalonia. The race began in Salou with a team time trial and ended in Barcelona. Twenty_five teams of up to eight riders started the race: 21 May 2007: Salou to Salou, 15.7 km (9.8 mi) (TTT) 22 21 May 2007: Salou to Perafort, 170 km (110 mi) 23 21 May 2007: Perafort to Tàrrega, 182.1 km (113.2 mi) 24 21 May 2007: Tàrrega to Vallnord_Arinsal (Andorra), 201.1 km (125.0 mi) 25 21 May 2007: Sornas to Vallnord_Arcalís (Andorra), 17.1 km (10.6 mi) (ITT) 26 21 May 2007: Llívia to Lloret de Mar, 177.1 km (110.0 mi) 27 May 2007: Lloret de Mar to Barcelona, 119.3 km (74.1 mi) As of 27 May 2007, after the Volta a Catalunya." .

lsd:Entity_27193 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Revolt of the Languedoc winegrowers was a mass movement in 1907 in Languedoc and the Pyrénées_Orientales of France that was repressed by the government of Georges Clemenceau. On 11 March 1907 the signal for the revolt was given by a group of Minervois vine growers in the village of Argeliers. On 31 11 March 1907 there were 500–600 protesters in Bize_Minervois." .

lsd:Entity_27206 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Championship runs alongside the 2009 IIHF InLine Hockey World Championship Division I tournament and took place between 2009 and 2009 in Ingolstadt, Germany and the Saturn Arena. Seven of the eight teams automatically qualified for the 2009 IIHF InLine Hockey World Championship while the eighth spot was awarded to the winner of the 2008 IIHF Men's InLine Hockey World Championship Division I tournament. The World Championship groups are named Group A and Group B while the 2009 IIHF InLine Hockey World Championship Division I tournament uses Group C and Group D, as both tournaments were held in Ingolstadt, Germany." .

lsd:Entity_2727 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Egyptian revolution of 1952 (Arabic: ثورة 23 يوليو 1952), also known as the 1952 coup d'état d'état (Arabic: إنقلاب 23 يوليو 1952) or 23 July 1952 revolution, began on 23 July 1952, by the Free Officers Movement, a group of army officers led by Mohammed Naguib and Gamal Abdel Nasser to overthrow King Farouk and the Muhammad Ali Dynasty, and aimed at changing the political leadership in Egypt. However, the movement had more political ambitions, and soon moved to abolish the constitutional monarchy and aristocracy of Egypt and Sudan, establish a republic, end the British occupation of the country, and secure the independence of Sudan (previously governed as an Anglo_Egyptian Sudan). The revolution was faced with immediate threats from Western imperial powers, particularly the United Kingdom, which had occupied Egypt since 1882, and France, both of whom were wary of rising nationalist sentiment in territories under their control throughout Africa and the Arab world. These two issues conflated four years after the revolution when Egypt was invaded by Britain, France, and Israel in the Suez Crisis of 1956. The revolution is commemorated each year on 23 July 1952. The anniversary of the coup d'état is commemorated on Revolution Day, an annual public holiday in Egypt, on 23 July 1952." .

lsd:Entity_27291 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 United States presidential election in Massachusetts took place on 06 November 1956, as part of the 1956 United States presidential election, which was held throughout all contemporary 48 states. Massachusetts voted decisively for the Republican nominee, incumbent President of the United States Dwight D. Eisenhower of Pennsylvania, over the Democratic nominee, former Governor Adlai Stevenson of Illinois. This remains the last presidential election in which Massachusetts voted more Republican than the nation, as the state would trend dramatically toward the Democratic Party beginning in 1960 United States presidential election in Massachusetts. In his initial 1952 United States presidential election in Massachusetts campaign, Eisenhower won back Massachusetts by a closer 54–45 margin, but the popular incumbent, who governed in a very moderate way that appealed to New England voters, was able to more than double his margin of victory in the state in the 1956 election. No Republican would carry Massachusetts in a presidential election again until Ronald Reagan won the state in 1980." .

lsd:Entity_27308 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "After Miklós Horthy was chosen Regent of Hungary on 1 March 1920, Charles I of Austria, who reigned in Hungary as Charles IV of Habsburg's attempts to retake the throne of Hungary, made two unsuccessful attempts to retake the throne." .

lsd:Entity_27336 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 FIFA U_17 World Championship, the fourth edition of the tournament, was held in the cities of Florence, Montecatini Terme, Viareggio, Massa, Carrara, and Livorno in Italy between 16 August 1991 and 31 August 1991." .

lsd:Entity_27379 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Petropavlovsk was a military operation in the Pacific Theatre of the Crimean War. There were three potential bases for the Russians: the island of Novo_Arkhangelsk, capital of Russian America (modern Alaska), Okhotsk on the Sea of Okhotsk, and the largest Russian settlement on the Pacific Coast, Petropavlovsk_Kamchatsky on the Kamchatka Peninsula." .

lsd:Entity_27392 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Austrian Football First League (German: Erste Liga, also known as ADEG Erste Liga due to sponsorship) was the 37th season of the Austria second_level association football league. It began on 14 13 July 2010 and ended on 28 27 May 2011. The size of the league was reduced from twelve to ten teams for this season after a reform of the second_ and third_level tiers of the Austria association football league pyramid. Wacker were originally to be replaced by SK Austria Kärnten, who finished the 2009–10 Bundesliga season in last place; however, Kärnten were not awarded a professional licence by the Bundesliga licensing board and hence relegated to the Austria Regionalliga. As part of the reform of the Austria second and third levels, the reserve teams of Bundesliga clubs Red Bull Salzburg and Austria Vienna were both relegated to the Regionaliga at the end of the 2009–10 season." .

lsd:Entity_27418 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Black Arrow(Hebrew: מבצע חץ שחור Mivtza Ḥetz Shaḥor) was an Israeli military operation carried out in Gaza City (while under Egyptian control) on 28 February 1955. On 28 February 1955, Ariel Sharon, commander of the Paratroop Brigade was issued the go_ahead to initiate Operation Black Arrow." .

lsd:Entity_27432 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 35th International Emmy Awards took place on 2007, at the Hilton New York in New York City, United States and hosted by American actor Roger Bart. The nominees for the 35th International Emmy Awards were announced by the International Academy of Television Arts & Sciences, in 2007, at a press conference at MIPCOM in Cannes." .

lsd:Entity_27458 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Australian Open was a tennis tournament played on outdoor grass courts at the Kooyong Stadium in Melbourne in Australia and was held from 21 December 1974 to 1975." .

lsd:Entity_27506 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 15 to 19 August 2012 at Plovdiv, Bulgaria. The annual week_long rowing regatta is organized by World Rowing Federation (the International Rowing Federation), and held at the end of the northern hemisphere summer. Because the 2012 Summer Olympics was the major rowing event in 2012, the programme was limited to non_Olympic events, and the World Rowing Junior Championships were held at the same time." .

lsd:Entity_27510 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1876 United States presidential election in California was held on 07 November 1876 as part of the 1876 United States presidential election." .

lsd:Entity_27520 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Emilia_Romagna regional election took place on 28 March 2010–29 28 March 2010. Errani (–10.6% compared to 2005 Emilia_Romagna regional election) and the Democrats (–7.7%) lost ground to the Five Star Movement, whose candidate won a surprising 7.0% of the vote, in what was the worst result for the centre_left in a regional election in Emilia–Romagna. The other surprise of the election was Lega Nord, which gained 13.7% of the vote, up from 4.8% in 2005 Emilia_Romagna regional election." .

lsd:Entity_27544 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Hong Kong Sevens was the 41st edition of the Hong Kong Sevens tournament, and the seventh tournament of the 2015–16 World Rugby Sevens Series. The tournament was played on 2016–2016 at Hong Kong Stadium in Hong Kong." .

lsd:Entity_27579 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Third Encirclement Campaign against Hubei_Henan_Anhui Soviet was an encirclement campaign launched by the Chinese Nationalist Government that was intended to destroy the communist Hubei–Henan–Anhui Soviet and its Chinese Red Army in the local region. It was responded by the Communists' third counter_encirclement campaign at Hubei–Henan–Anhui Soviet (Chinese: 鄂豫皖苏区第三次苏区反围剿), also called by the communists as the third counter_encirclement campaign at Hubei–Henan–Anhui Revolutionary Base (Chinese: 鄂豫皖革命根据地第三次反围剿), in which the local Chinese Red Army successfully defended their soviet republic in the border region of Hubei, Henan, and Anhui provinces against the Nationalist attacks from 01 November 1931 to 17 June 1932." .

lsd:Entity_2758 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Westel 900 Budapest Open was a women's tennis tournament played on clay court in Budapest in Hungary that was part of WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 1999 WTA Tour. It was the fourth edition of the tournament and was held from 19 April 1999 until 25 April 1999." .

lsd:Entity_27587 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1920 Victorian state election was held in the Australia state of Victoria on Thursday 1920 to elect the 65 members of the state's Legislative Assembly. The Nationalist party had reunited after the 1917 Victorian state election in which the Nationalist members supporting John Bowser defeated those supporting the former Premier of Victoria Alexander Peacock over his decision to increase country rail fares, and formed a majority government with 40 members." .

lsd:Entity_27618 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Théodore Gouvy International Festival (Festival international Louis Théodore Gouvy) is an annual festival of classical music held in honour of Franco_German composer, Louis Théodore Gouvy. The festival has been established by the Institut Louis Théodore Gouvy and the Choeur d'hommes de Hombourg_Haut (male choir founded in 1865 with Gouvy's support)." .

lsd:Entity_27676 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rock Springs massacre, also known as the Rock Springs Riot, occurred on 02 September 1885, in the present_day United States city of Rock Springs in Sweetwater County, Wyoming. The massacre in Rock Springs was one among several instances of violence culminating from years of anti_Chinese sentiment in the United States. The massacre in Rock Springs touched off a wave of anti_Chinese violence, especially in the Puget Sound area of Washington Territory. At the time of the massacre, there were about 150 white miners and 331 Chinese miners in Rock Springs. In the two years before the massacre, a \"Whitemen's Town\" was established in Rock Springs. No evidence has been uncovered to prove that the national Knights of Labor organization was behind the massacre at Rock Springs. At 7:00 a.m. on 02 September 1885, ten white men, in ordinary garb and miner's uniforms, arrived at coal pit number six at the Rock Springs mine. The situation in Rock Springs was stabilized as early as 15, when Warren first requested the removal of federal troops, but the mines at Rock Springs remained closed for a time. The massacre at Rock Springs led to other incidents of anti_Chinese aggression, primarily in Washington Territory, though there were incidents in Oregon and other states as well. Other states reported incidents as well: As far away as Augusta, Georgia, anger was expressed against the Chinese in response to the massacre at Rock Springs. In general, the locations in Rock Springs associated with the massacre have been surrounded and absorbed by the city's growth." .

lsd:Entity_27682 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1971 Motor Trend 500 was the first race in NASCAR's Winston Cup era (also known as the Winston Cup Grand National Series) that took place on 10 January 1971. 191 laps on a road course at Riverside International Raceway in Riverside, California that spanned a total distance of 2.620 miles (4.216 km). Defending NASCAR Grand National West series champion Ray Elder won the race; making it the first time that the 500_mile event at Riverside was won by a manufacturer other than Ford." .

lsd:Entity_27714 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Men's European Volleyball League was the 5th edition of the European Volleyball League, organised by Europe's governing volleyball body, the European Volleyball Confederation. The Final Four was held in Bursa, Turkey from 19 to 20 July 2008." .

lsd:Entity_27753 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Commonwealth Fencing Championships was held in Largs, Scotland between the 10th and 15 November 2014 at the Inverclyde National Sports Training Centre." .

lsd:Entity_27782 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Manzikert in 1054 was a successful defense of the city of Manzikert by Byzantine forces under Basil Apocapes against the Seljuk dynasty led by sultan Toğrül." .

lsd:Entity_27796 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Royal Charter Storm (also known as the Great storm of 1859) of 25 October 1859 and 26 October 1859 was considered to be the most severe storm to hit the Irish Sea in the 19th century, with a total death toll estimated at over 800. The storm drifted northwards, hitting Anglesey by about 8 p.m. and not reaching maximum force at the River Mersey until midday on 26 October 1859, then continued northwards to affect Scotland." .

lsd:Entity_27826 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Festival International de Programmes Audiovisuels (FIPADOC) or International Documentary Festival FIPADOC (French: Festival International de Programmes Audiovisuels (FIPA)), founded in 1987 by Michel Mitrani (1930_1996), was first held in Cannes in 1987., In 2019, the FIPA became FIPADOC,,, an international festival specializing in non_fiction films for all screens and all formats. The festival was moved to Nice in its eighth year (1995), and, since 1997, has been held in Biarritz, France." .

lsd:Entity_27871 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's high jump at the 2008 Olympic Games took place on 17 August 2008–19 August at the Beijing National Stadium. The event was won by Andrey Silnov of Russia, the nation's second victory (after 2000) in the athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's high jump." .

lsd:Entity_27907 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "NHK News Ohayō Nippon (Japanese: NHKニュース おはよう日本) is a Japanese morning television show on NHK General TV, anchored mainly by Kozo Takase and Maho Kuwako, from the NHK studios at NHK Broadcasting Center in Tokyo, Japan. It is simulcast on NHK World Premium to countries other than Japan (except weekday edition, from 4:30–6:00 a.m. JST)." .

lsd:Entity_27911 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bob Bryan and Mike Bryan won in the final 6–2, 6–3 against Juan Ignacio Chela and Nicolás Massú." .

lsd:Entity_27917 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Erika was the strongest and longest_lasting tropical cyclone in the 1997 Atlantic hurricane season. It developed from a tropical wave on 03 September 1997 and moved west_northwestward across the tropical Atlantic Ocean, steadily intensifying until it attained hurricane status on September 4, becoming the fifth named storm and third hurricane of the season. Erika was the only tropical cyclone in the Atlantic Ocean in the months of August and September, the first occurrence of such event in an Atlantic hurricane season since 1929." .

lsd:Entity_27957 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rosmalen Grass Court Championships, current sponsored name the Libéma Open (formerly known as the Ricoh Open, Heineken Trophy, Ordina Open, UNICEF Open, and Topshelf Open), is a professional tennis tournament held in the town of Rosmalen, on the outskirts of the city of 's_Hertogenbosch (Den Bosch), the Netherlands. The men's and women's tennis matches are played on outdoors grass court at the Autotron convention center, and constitute a stage on the Association of Tennis Professionals and the Women's Tennis Association. The next year, 1990, the tournament became part of the newly founded Association of Tennis Professionals and was officially called the Continental Grass Court Championships." .

lsd:Entity_27991 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 UK Championship was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place at the Bournemouth International Centre in Bournemouth, England. The event started on 2000 and the televised stages were shown on BBC between 25 November 2000 and 03 December 2000. This was to be the last UK Championship to be held in Bournemouth, where the event was hosted since the 1998." .

lsd:Entity_28029 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 World Indoor Bowls Championship was held at the Coatbridge indoor bowling club, North Lanarkshire, Scotland, from 1984–1984." .

lsd:Entity_28059 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1937 All_Ireland Senior Hurling Championship Final was a hurling match played at Fitzgerald Stadium in Killarney, County Kerry on 1937 to determine the winners of the All_Ireland Senior Hurling Championship 1937, the 51st season of the All_Ireland Senior Football Championship, a tournament organised by the Gaelic Athletic Association for the champions of the three hurling provinces of Ireland." .

lsd:Entity_28063 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013–14 Hessenliga of the Hessenliga, the highest association football league in the German state of Hesse, was the sixth season of the league at tier five (V) of the German football league system and the 36th season overall since establishment of the league in 1978, then as the Oberliga Hessen. No team was promoted from the Hessenliga to the Regionalliga Südwest above as 2013–14 champions TGM SV Jügesheim decided to withdraw from the league while 1." .

lsd:Entity_28108 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Cooke's Spring was a typical Apache Wars skirmish which took place in 1857. Apache raiders were tracked through the frontier by a party of United States of America Army cavalry who intercepted the native warriors at Cooke's Spring in the Black Range of New Mexico." .

lsd:Entity_28158 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FA Women's Cup Final was the 47th final of the FA Women's Cup, England's primary cup competition for women's association football teams. The final was contested between Birmingham City L.F.C. and Manchester City W.F.C. on 13 May 2017 at Wembley Stadium in London." .

lsd:Entity_28169 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1979 Women's Nordic Football Championship was the sixth edition of the Women's Nordic Football Championship tournament. It was held from 05 July 1979 to 08 July 1979 in Oslo, Fredrikstad and Setskog in Norway." .

lsd:Entity_28224 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kentucky Apple Festival is an annual festival held in Paintsville, Kentucky during the first Friday and Saturday in October. Eventually, the Kentucky Apple Festival became the more popular of the two and the Johnson County Fair became nonexistent." .

lsd:Entity_28236 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 4 September 1782 was a small naval engagement which was fought off the Île_de_Batz between a French naval frigate Hébé and a Royal Naval frigate HMS Rainbow. On 04 September 1782 the 44 gun frigate HMS Rainbow under Captain Henry Trollope armed entirely with carronades was off the French coast near the Île_de_Batz when a frigate was sighted." .

lsd:Entity_28247 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 UEFA Intertoto Cup finals were won by FC Schalke 04, Villarreal Club de Fútbol, and A.C. Perugia Calcio." .

lsd:Entity_28263 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Flugplatzrennen (known alternately as either 1961 Flugplatzrennen or 5th Flugplatzrennen) was a motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 17 September 1961 at Zeltweg Air Base, Austria. The race was run over 80 laps of the circuit, and was dominated by British driver Innes Ireland in a Lotus 21." .

lsd:Entity_28265 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1962 British Empire and Commonwealth Games – men's 4 × 110 yards relay as part of the athletics at the 1962 British Empire and Commonwealth Games was held at the Perry Lakes Stadium on Saturday 1962." .

lsd:Entity_28297 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Equatorial Express crash, also known as the 2005 Baney plane crash occurred on 16 July 2005 after an Equatorial Express Airlines Antonov An_24 crashed into a side of a mountain near Baney, Equatorial Guinea." .

lsd:Entity_28300 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 Frauen DFB Pokal was the 15th season of the cup competition, Germany's second_most important title in women's football. In the final which was held in Berlin on 24 June 1995, FSV Frankfurt defeated TSV Siegen 3–1, thus winning their fourth cup title." .

lsd:Entity_28316 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sambat (Filipino: Labanan sa Sambat, Spanish: Batalla de Sambat) was the culminating battle of the first revolts of the Katipunan in Laguna. The battle was the final major action for the Katipunan chapter of \"Maluningning\" ending in the defeat of the rebels and martial law in Laguna province. The attacks on the Laguna towns of Lumban, Paete, Pakil, Siniloan, Cavinti, Santa Cruz, Magdalena and Pagsanjan were tactically successful in gaining arms and recruiting the cuadrilleros or local native police of each town." .

lsd:Entity_28349 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Glory By Honor XII was the 12th Glory By Honor professional wrestling event produced by Ring of Honor (ROH). It took place on 26 October 2013, at the Frontier Fieldhouse in Chicago Ridge. Glory By Honor XII featured seven professional wrestling matches, which involved different wrestlers from pre_existing scripted feuds, plots, and storylines that played out on ROH's television programs." .

lsd:Entity_28392 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Scottish Cup was the 128th season of Scotland's most prestigious football knockout competition. The tournament began on 04 August 2012 and ended on 26 May 2013. From round four onwards, selected matches from the Scottish Cup are broadcast live in Ireland and the United Kingdom by BBC Scotland and Sky Sports." .

lsd:Entity_28411 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Carson Sink UFO incident is a unidentified flying object incident alleged by John L. McGinn and John R. Barton, two United States of America Air Force Colonels, to have taken place near Carson Sink in western Nevada in the United States of America while they were en route from California to Colorado in a B_25 on 24 July 1952." .

lsd:Entity_28416 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Yokohama_e (横浜絵; literally “Yokohama pictures”) are ukiyo_e Japanese woodblock printing depicting non_East Asian foreigners and scenes of Yokohama. The port of Yokohama was opened to foreigners in 1859 and ukiyo_e artists, primarily of the Utagawa school, produced more than 800 different woodblock printing in response to a general curiosity about these strangers." .

lsd:Entity_28439 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Victory Boogie_Woogie is the last, unfinished creative work work of the Dutch abstract painter Piet Mondrian. Left incomplete in 1944, since 1998 it has been in the collection of the Kunstmuseum Den Haag in The Hague." .

lsd:Entity_28445 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Scuttling of SMS Cormoran off Guam on 07 April 1917 was the result of the United States entry into World War I and the internment of the German merchant raider SMS Cormoran. Captain Zuckschwerdt pulled into Apra Harbor, Guam on December 14 with the intention of receiving coal from the Americans on the island. When war was declared on 07 April 1917, the United States Marines and sailors on Guam were notified and set out from their base." .

lsd:Entity_28463 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Strikeforce: Heavy Artillery was a mixed martial arts event held by Strikeforce on 15 May 2010, in St. Louis, Missouri, United States of America at the Enterprise Center. Bobby Lashley, who was originally set to fight on the Strikeforce: Nashville card, was rumored to have fought on this card, however he was moved to the card for Strikeforce: Los Angeles." .

lsd:Entity_28514 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 71st United States Women's Open Championship, held 07 July 2016–10 at CordeValle Golf Club in San Martin, California, southeast of San Jose. The United States Women's Open Championship is the oldest of the five current major championships and the third of the 2016 season." .

lsd:Entity_28518 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Aer Lingus Flight 164 was a scheduled Boeing 737 passenger flight that was hijacked on 02 May 1981, en route from Dublin Airport in Ireland to Heathrow Airport in the United Kingdom." .

lsd:Entity_28522 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Red Ford or Battle of the String of Lorne was a battle in 1294 or else after September 1296 between Clan Campbell and Clan MacDougall in Lorne, Scotland. The battle was on the borders of Loch Awe and Lorne, with the site and battle named Red Ford (Scottish Gaelic: Ath Dearg) after the ford which ran red with blood where the battle took place." .

lsd:Entity_28526 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 62nd United States Women's Open Championship, held June 28 to July 1 at the Pine Needles Lodge and Golf Club in Southern Pines." .

lsd:Entity_28542 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 European Aquatics Championships took place in Glasgow, Edinburgh and Luss in the central belt of Scotland, from 3 to 12 August 2018. The Tollcross International Swimming Centre hosted the swimming events with the diving being hosted by the Royal Commonwealth Pool in Edinburgh. The open water swimming competition took place at Loch Lomond, while Scotstoun Sports Campus hosted the synchronised swimming events." .

lsd:Entity_28558 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Eruption of Mount Tarawera occurred in the early hours of 10 June 1886 in the North Island near Rotorua then extended to Waimangu, New Zealand." .

lsd:Entity_28636 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "These are the results for the girls' singles event at the tennis at the 2014 Summer Youth Olympics – girls' singles. Xu Shilin of People's Republic of China won the gold medal, defeating Iryna Shymanovich of Belarus in the final, 6–3, 6–1." .

lsd:Entity_28676 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Family Circle Cup was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Family Circle Tennis Center in Charleston in the United States of America and was part of WTA Tier I tournaments of the 2001 WTA Tour. It was the 29th edition of the tournament and ran from 16 April 2001 through 22 April 2001." .

lsd:Entity_28679 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2013 Cebu local elections was held in the Province of Cebu last 2013 within the Philippine general election. On September 28, 2012, Hilario Davide III, was announced by the Liberal Party as its candidate for governor." .

lsd:Entity_28751 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Evonne Goolagong Cawley and Peggy Michel were the defending champions." .

lsd:Entity_28780 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Southeastern 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series racing event that was held on 17 March 1974, at Bristol Motor Speedway in Bristol, Tennessee. Bristol Motor Speedway is a NASCAR short track venue located in Bristol, Tennessee. Donnie Allison, Cale Yarborough, Bobby Isaac, Benny Parsons, and Bobby Allison would be the respective leaders for certain parts of the race." .

lsd:Entity_28787 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Avon Championships of Florida was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Sportatorium in Hollywood, Florida in the United States that was part of the 1979 Avon Championships circuit. It was the third and final edition of the tournament and was held from 22 January 1979 through 28 January 1979." .

lsd:Entity_28844 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Emotional Roadshow World Tour (stylized as EMØTIØNAL RØADSHØW WØRLD TØUR) was the fifth concert tour by American music duo Twenty One Pilots, in support of their album Blurryface." .

lsd:Entity_28848 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Asturian parliamentary election was held on Sunday, 25 May 2003, to elect the 6th General Junta of the Principality of Asturias. The 1999 Asturian regional election was held on 13 June 1999, setting the election date for the General Junta on Sunday, 25 May 2003." .

lsd:Entity_28876 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Bulgarian Cup Final was the 20th final of the Bulgarian Cup (in this period the tournament was named Cup of the Soviet Army), and was contested between FC Septemvri Sofia and PFC Lokomotiv Plovdiv on 1960 at Vasil Levski National Stadium in Sofia." .

lsd:Entity_28882 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Meridional Gothic (French: gothique méridional) is a specific and militant style of Gothic architecture developed in the South of France, especially in the Toulouse region. Meridional Gothic, as its name suggests, is found in the southern part of France, mainly in the regions where Catharism had developed, and which were subjected to religious and military repression from the North. Romanesque architecture persisted for longer in the south of France than in the north, and the transition to Gothic was gradual." .

lsd:Entity_28901 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle for No.3 Post (28 May 1915–30 May 1915) was fought during the Gallipoli Campaign in the World War I, between the forces of the New Zealand Mounted Rifles Brigade and the Turkish 19th Division. The battle for No.3 Post seemed a major event to those involved, but in General Ian Hamilton's dispatches the fight only received a brief mention: \"On 28 May 1915, at 9 p.m., a raid was made on a Turkish post overlooking the beach 1,200 yards north of Kaba Tepe, H.M.S." .

lsd:Entity_28940 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 Air Canada Silver Broom, the men's World Curling Championships, was held at the Utica Memorial Auditorium in Utica, New York, United States of America between 1970_1970." .

lsd:Entity_28958 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bow Mistress Stakes is a Tasmanian Racing Club Group 3 Thoroughbred horse race held under Weight for age conditions, for fillies and mares aged three years old and upwards, over a distance of 1,200 metres at the Elwick Racecourse, Glenorchy, Australia in February." .

lsd:Entity_28972 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "An Nevis independence referendum was held in Nevis on 10 August 1998. If it had passed, Nevis would have seceded from the Saint Kitts and Nevis." .

lsd:Entity_2901 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Typhoon Noul (pronounced ), known in the Philippines as Typhoon Dodong, was a relatively small but powerful tropical cyclone that affected several areas but caused minor damage. The sixth named storm and third typhoon of the 2015 Pacific typhoon season, Noul formed as a tropical depression over the eastern Caroline Islands on May 2, 2015. On May 5, an eye developed in the center of the storm while Noul was near Yap State, prompting the JTWC to upgrade the storm to typhoon status. At 18:00 UTC that day, the JMA upgraded Noul to a severe tropical storm, and later to typhoon status at 06:00 UTC on May 6. Due to radial outflow, low vertical wind shear, and water temperatures above 30°C, Noul started rapid deepening on May 9 while approaching the Philippine island of Luzon, forming a sharply well_defined 35 km (20 mi) eye embedded in a deepening symmetric core; therefore, the JTWC upgraded it to a super typhoon late on the same day. On May 11, Noul impacted the Ryukyu Islands of Japan as a weakening typhoon." .

lsd:Entity_29022 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Universe 1961, the 10th Miss Universe pageant, was held on 15 July 1961 at the Miami Beach Auditorium in Miami Beach, United States." .

lsd:Entity_29106 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Baduhenna Wood was a battle, possibly fought (but not proven) near Heiloo, Netherlands, in 28 AD between the Frisii and a Roman army led by the Roman general Lucius Apronius." .

lsd:Entity_29183 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Perch was a British offensive of the Second World War which took place from 7 to 14 June 1944, during the early stages of the Battle of Normandy." .

lsd:Entity_29187 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 UCI Mountain Bike World Championships were held in Vail, Colorado, United States of America from 18 to 19 September 1994." .

lsd:Entity_29217 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup was the fifth edition of the FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup, governed by FIFA. It took place in Dubai, the United Arab Emirates between 16 November 2009 and 22 November 2009. All matches are listed as local time in Dubai, (UTC+4) There were 284 goals scored in 32 matches, for an average of 8.88 goals per match." .

lsd:Entity_29240 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Pemaquid (02 August 1689–3, 1689) was a successful attack by a large band of Abenaki Indians on the English fort at Pemaquid, Fort Charles, then the easternmost outpost of colonial Massachusetts (present_day Bristol, Maine)." .

lsd:Entity_29287 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Mr Gay World 2010, the 2nd Mr Gay World pageant, was held at the Nedre Vollgate 11, in Oslo, Norway on 2010. Charl Van Den Berg of South Africa, was crowned Mr Gay World 2010 by outgoing titleholder Max Krzyzanowski from Ireland." .

lsd:Entity_29317 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Xingyang was a battle fought in 190 in the late Eastern Han dynasty as part of the Campaign against Dong Zhuo." .

lsd:Entity_2935 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1991 World Table Tennis Championships were held in Chiba from 24 April 1991 to 06 May 1991. On 1991, they agreed to the creation of the unified table tennis team to compete at the 1991 World Table Tennis Championships." .

lsd:Entity_29397 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Pan American Women's Handball Championship was the ninth edition of the Pan American Women's Handball Championship, which took place in Santo Domingo from 31 May 2007 to 04 June 2007." .

lsd:Entity_29449 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 Singaporean general election was held on 07 May 2011 to elect the next Government of Singapore. The governing People's Action Party (PAP) has been in power since Singapore's independence in 1965, and is currently led by the Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong. The election saw a heavier use of social and online media compared to 2006 Singaporean general election, especially to evade censorship in Singapore. Lee Kuan Yew was also quoted as saying that a younger generation was required to \"carry Singapore forward in a more difficult and complex situation\" while Lee Hsien Loong declared the party \"would change the way it governs\" and do some \"soul_searching\"." .

lsd:Entity_29452 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Beirut (12 July 1941) marked the end of hostilities in the Syria–Lebanon campaign of World War II." .

lsd:Entity_29557 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Traditional Berber religion is the ancient and native set of beliefs and deities adhered to by the Berbers (Berbers autochthones) of North Africa. Many ancient Berbers beliefs were developed locally, whereas others were influenced over time through contact with other traditional African religions and ancient Egyptian religion, or borrowed during antiquity from the Punic religion from Carthage, Judaism, Iberian mythology, and the Hellenistic religion. Some of the ancient Berbers beliefs still exist today subtly within the Berbers popular culture and tradition. Syncretic influences from the traditional Berbers religion can also be found in certain other faiths. The veneration (not worship) of saints which exists among the modern Berbers in the form of Maraboutism—which is widespread in northwest Africa—may or may not contain traces of prior beliefs or customs concerning the dead. Some scholars distinguish the relationships between the Phoenicians and the Berbers in two phases: When the Phoenicians established in Northwest Africa, they stayed in the coastal regions to avoid wars with the Berbers." .

lsd:Entity_29577 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "They were defeated in the semifinals by eighth seed Dinara Safina and Katarina Srebotnik who, in turn, had to give way in the finals to the unseeded Nathalie Dechy and Vera Zvonareva, the new champions." .

lsd:Entity_29615 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Philippines earthquake occurred on 15 October 2013 at 8:12:31 PST in Bohol, an island province located in Central Visayas, Philippines. It was the deadliest earthquake in the Philippines in 23 years since the 1990 Luzon earthquake. The 15 October 2013 magnitude (Mw) 7.2 Bohol earthquake produced an approximate 50 kilometres (31 mi) long, 12 kilometres (7.5 mi) wide northeast trending zone of uplift with an approximate 8 kilometres (5.0 mi) long discontinuous ground rupture indicating predominantly reverse‐slip movement on a southeast dipping fault. The earthquake struck as the Philippines was observing the Muslim holiday of Eid_al_Adha." .

lsd:Entity_29619 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Grant was a tropical cyclone that affected Queensland and the Northern Territory in late 21 December 2011 and early 02 January 2012." .

lsd:Entity_29623 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Ailette was a battle that took place during the First World War in 23 August 1918, on the banks of the Ailette between Laon and Aisne. Though it was a largely unknown battle of the Hundred Days Offensive during the First World War, the Ailette was the scene of fierce fighting between the French and German forces for its control from August to September 1918, before it returned to the French in October." .

lsd:Entity_29644 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Virginia Slims of Houston was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the NRG Arena in Houston, Texas in the United States that was part of the 1978 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held from 16 January 1978 through 22 January 1978." .

lsd:Entity_29657 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Price Chopper 400 was the 29th of 36 scheduled stock car races of the 2009 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series and the third in the ten_race season_ending Chase for the Sprint Cup. It was held on 04 October 2009, in Kansas City, Kansas, at Kansas Speedway, before a crowd of 100,000 spectators. The 2009 Price Chopper 400 was the twenty_ninth of thirty_six scheduled stock car races of the 2009 NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, and the third in the ten_race season_ending Chase for the Sprint Cup. It was held on 04 October 2009, in Kansas City, Kansas, at Kansas Speedway, an intermediate track that holds NASCAR races." .

lsd:Entity_29669 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 45th Grey Cup was the Canadian Football Council's (CFC) championship game of the 1957 season, which was played on 30 November 1957. The Hamilton Tiger_Cats defeated the Winnipeg Blue Bombers 32–7 at Varsity Stadium in Toronto." .

lsd:Entity_29676 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa del Rey de Baloncesto 2003–04 was the 68th edition of the Copa del Rey de Baloncesto. It was organized by the ACB and was disputed in Seville in the Palacio Municipal de Deportes San Pablo between 26 February 2004 and 29 February 2004." .

lsd:Entity_29767 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "figure skating at the 2016 Winter Youth Olympics at the 2016 Winter Youth Olympics took place at the Hamar Olympic Amphitheatre in Hamar, Norway from 12 to 21 February 2016. The overall quota for the figure skating competition was 76 total skaters, consisting of 38 men and 38 ladies. The International Skating Union published a complete list of entries on 4 12 February 2016. figure skating at the 2016 Winter Youth Olympics events will take place 13 12 February 2016–16 12 February 2016." .

lsd:Entity_29770 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Beach Soccer World Cup 1995 was the first edition of the Beach Soccer World Championships, the most prestigious competition in international beach soccer contested by men's national teams until 2005, when the competition was then replaced by the second iteration of a world cup in beach soccer, the better known FIFA Beach Soccer World Cup. The tournament took place at Copacabana Beach in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. The hosts and heavy favourites Brazil won the tournament by beating the United States 8–1 in the final, coming from behind to claim their first world title. The event was notable for featuring many high profile ex_association football, fuelling its popularity, including the likes of the Brazilians Zico, Júnior and Cláudio Adão, Italian 1982 World Cup winners Alessandro Altobelli and Claudio Gentile, Franco Causio, England's Gary Stevens and Luther Blissett, West German international Rainer Bonhof and brothers René and Willy van de Kerkhof of the Netherlands' 1978 World Cup runners_up squad. The presence of Zico as part of the Brazilian squad, who made over 70 appearances for the Brazilian national association football team, gained considerable attention in the local press prior to the start of the championship. Koch Tavares, the tournament organisers, decided that as the first World Cup of beach soccer, since the sport is a derivative of association football, it would be fitting for the six winners of the FIFA World Cup of football throughout history (as of 1995) to field a team in Rio (being hosts Brazil, Argentina, Italy, Uruguay, England and Germany) and as such the aforementioned nations were invited to play, all of which accepted the opportunity." .

lsd:Entity_29818 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SuperBrawl III was the third SuperBrawl professional wrestling pay_per_view (PPV) event produced by World Championship Wrestling (WCW). It took place on 21 February 1993 from the Asheville Civic Center in Asheville." .

lsd:Entity_29826 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Almond Blossom Cross Country (Portuguese: Cross Internacional das Amendoeiras em Flor) is an annual international cross country running competition which takes place in Albufeira, Portugal, in early March. The competition was first organised in 1977 through a joint partnership of the Federação Portuguesa de Atletismo (Portuguese Athletics Federation), the District of Faro and Turismo de Portugal, with the aim of promoting both sport and tourism in the region. The Almond Blossom Cross Country competition is international in its nature, attracting a large number of foreign athletes each year, but it is also a significant draw for Portugal's top domestic runners. Only two Portuguese athletes have won at the World Cross Country Championships (Carlos Lopes and Albertina Dias) and both have been victorious in Albufeira: Lopes won the first ever Almond Blossom race in 1977 while Dias won the 1989 women's race." .

lsd:Entity_29867 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Invicta International Airlines Flight 435 was a Vickers Vanguard 952, flying from Bristol Lulsgate to Basel_Mulhouse, which crashed into a forested hillside near Hochwald, Switzerland on 10 April 1973. The aircraft somersaulted and broke up, killing 108 people, with 37 survivors. 108 passengers and crew were killed, while 37 others survived the crash; 36 of these were injured, while one air hostess was unhurt." .

lsd:Entity_29894 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1992 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1991–92 Taça de Portugal, the 52nd season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 24 May 1992 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides from the same city: Boavista F.C. and Porto. Boavista F.C. defeated Porto 2–1 to claim the Taça de Portugal for a fourth time in their history. In Portugal, the final was televised live on RTP. As a result of Boavista F.C. winning the Taça de Portugal, the Panteras qualified for the 1992 Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira where they took on their cup opponents who won the 1991–92 Primeira Divisão." .

lsd:Entity_29900 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Melodifestivalen 2005 was the selection for the 45th song to represent Sweden at the Eurovision Song Contest. Alcazar and Alcastar got to number one the week before the final, Jimmy Jansson got to the number one some weeks after that with Vi kan gunga, Martin Stenmarck's winner Las Vegas hit the top spot the week after that, while Nanne Grönvall and Håll om mig topped the charts the week after that. The semifinals for Melodifestivalen 2005 began on 12 February 2005." .

lsd:Entity_29948 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Set The World On Fire Tour was the fifth concert tour by American recording artist Alicia Keys in support of her fifth album, Girl on Fire." .

lsd:Entity_29952 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Polish Cup was the fifty_eighth season of the annual Poland association football knockout tournament. It began on 19 July 2011 with the first matches of the Extra Preliminary Round and ended on 24 April 2012 with the Final." .

lsd:Entity_29973 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The winner was Lena Philipsson with \"Det gör ont\" who later that year came in 5th place in Eurovision Song Contest 2004 in Turkey with the English_language version Det gör ont. The semifinals for Melodifestivalen 2004 began on 2004." .

lsd:Entity_29981 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 BWF World Tour, known as 2018 HSBC BWF World Tour for sponsorship reasons, was the first season of the BWF World Tour of badminton, a circuit of 26 tournaments which led up to the World Tour Finals tournament. Below is the schedule released by Badminton World Federation: Below are the 2018 BWF World Tour performances by country." .

lsd:Entity_30008 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Solitude Grand Prix was a non_Championship motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 23 July 1961 at the Solitudering, near Stuttgart. The race was run over 25 laps of the circuit, and was won by Innes Ireland in a Lotus 21." .

lsd:Entity_30035 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Food City 500 was the sixth stock car race of the 2001 NASCAR Winston Cup Series season. It was held on 25 March 2001 at the Bristol Motor Speedway in Bristol, Tennessee." .

lsd:Entity_30043 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Football League One play_off Final was a football match contested by Brentford F.C. and Yeovil Town F.C.. It was played on 2013 at Wembley Stadium to decide the third team to be promoted from League One to the Championship for the 2013–14 season. Brentford F.C. secured a place in the final by beating Swindon Town 5–4 on penalties, after the two_legs finished 4–4 on aggregate. Yeovil Town F.C. secured a place in the final by beating Sheffield United 2–1 on aggregate. Yeovil went on to win the final 2–1 against Brentford F.C.." .

lsd:Entity_30091 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 FIVB World Grand Prix was the first edition of the women's volleyball tournament, annually arranged by FIVB. It was played by eight countries from 28 May 1993 to 1993. 1993 Fédération Internationale de Volleyball World League." .

lsd:Entity_30225 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pro Bowl is the all_star game of the National Football League (NFL). The first official Pro Bowl was played in 1951, three weeks after the 1950 NFL Championship Game (between 1939 and 1942, the NFL experimented with all_star games pitting the league's champion against a team of all_stars). The concept of an all_star game was not revived until June 1950, when the newly christened \"Pro Bowl\" was approved. Immediately prior to the Pro Bowl, following the 1949 season, the All_America Football Conference, which contributed three teams to the NFL in a partial merger in 1950, held its own all_star game, the Shamrock Bowl. The early Pro Bowl, contested by the National Football League's Eastern and Western Division stars and played at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum, featured the same uniforms from the 1950s to mid_1960s; the Eastern team wore scarlet jerseys with white numerals and a white crescent shoulder stripe, white pants with red stripe, red socks, and a plain red helmet. As the NFC–AFC format was not used between 2014 through 2016, team 1 sported a white uniform with bright orange and team 2 sported a gray uniform with volt green. While players are financially compensated for participating in the Pro Bowl, for a star player, the pay can be less than 1% of their salary. Voting by fans makes up 1/3 of the vote for Pro Bowl players." .

lsd:Entity_30236 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Asian Men's Handball Championship was the tenth Asian Men's Handball Championship. It took place from 10 to 19 February 2002 in Isfahan, Iran." .

lsd:Entity_30266 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sant'Egidio was fought on 12 July 1416 at Sant'Egidio, near Umbertide (central Italy) between the condottiere Braccio da Montone and the troops of Perugia, under Carlo I Malatesta." .

lsd:Entity_30277 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959 All England Badminton Championships was a badminton tournament held at Wembley, London, England, from 18 March 1959–21 March 1959." .

lsd:Entity_30284 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Tour of Hainan was the eighth edition of the Tour of Hainan cycling stage race. It was rated as a 2.HC event on the UCI Asia Tour, and was held between 20 October 2013 and 28 October 2013, in Hainan." .

lsd:Entity_30287 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 La Flèche Wallonne was the 63rd edition of La Flèche Wallonne cycle race and was held on 14 April 1999. The race started in Charleroi and finished in Huy." .

lsd:Entity_30296 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Australian federal election was held in Australia on 24 November 2007. Considering two_party estimates going back to the 1949 election, the swing to Labor in 2007 was the fourth_largest two_party_preferred swing, behind John Curtin and Labor in 1943 on 7.9 per cent, Malcolm Fraser and the Coalition in 1975 on 7.4 per cent, and Gough Whitlam and Labor in 1969 on 7.1 per cent. Post_election, ALP secretary Tim Gartrell commented on pre_election campaign billboard ads featuring a picture of John Howard stating \"Working families in Australia have never been better off\", which looked like Liberal Party advertisements, were actually paid for by the Labor Party. The first meeting of the 41st Parliament after the 2004 Australian federal election was on 16 November 2004, hence the parliament would have expired on 2007 had it not been dissolved earlier. On 14 October, John Howard gained the agreement of the governor_general, Major_General Michael Jeffery, to dissolve the House of Representatives and hold a general election for the House and half the Senate on 24 November 2007. During the last term of parliament before the 2007 election, the deadline for new voter enrolment was brought forward from 7 working days after the issue of the writ to the same day. On 14 October, Howard announced a 24 November 2007 election. The Reserve Bank of Australia adjusted interest rates upwards by another 0.25 per cent, the sixth rise since the last election, to a 10_year high of 6.75 per cent, and the first time the Bank had been changed rates during an election campaign. 2007 saw, for the first time during an election campaign, a 0.25_point interest_rate rise to 6.5 per cent by the Reserve Bank, the sixth rise since the last election in 2004. In 24 November 2007 interest rates were raised for the sixth time since the 2004 Australian federal election, to a 10_year high of 6.75 per cent. In response to Labor criticism of the government on the rate rises, Howard stated in 2007 \" can scour every transcript, and I will make them available, of every interview that I gave during that election campaign and he will find no such commitment. During 2007 Immigration Minister Kevin Andrews's decision to cut Australia's refugee intake and expressing public concern about Sudanese migrants was branded as racist." .

lsd:Entity_30330 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Samarra, also called Operation Baton Rouge, took place in 2004 during the Iraq War. The city of Samarra in central Iraq had fallen under the control of insurgents shortly after insurgents had seized control of Fallujah and Ramadi. In preparation for an offensive to retake Fallujah, on 01 October 2004, 5,000 American and Iraqi troops assaulted Samarra and secured the city after three days of fighting." .

lsd:Entity_30360 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Federation Cup was the tenth edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. 31 nations participated in the tournament, which was held at Ellis Park in Johannesburg, South Africa from 20 March 1972–25 March 1972. South Africa became the first team other than United States or Australia to win the Fed Cup. All ties were played at Ellis Park in Johannesburg, South Africa on hard courts." .

lsd:Entity_30370 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 39th United States Women's Open Championship, held July 12–15 at Salem Country Club in Peabody. Hollis Stacy won her third United States Women's Open Championship, one stroke ahead of runner_up Rosie Jones." .

lsd:Entity_30388 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miracle of Cordoba (known in Austria national association football team as Das Wunder von Córdoba (The Miracle of Córdoba) and in Germany as Die Schmach von Córdoba or Die Schande von Córdoba (The Disgrace of Córdoba)), describes the game of association football between Austria national association football team and defending World Champions Germany national association football team that occurred on 21 June 1978 at the conclusion of the second round of the 1978 FIFA World Cup, in Córdoba, Argentina. The game is fondly remembered by fans of the Austrian national team for being the first time in 47 years that Austria national association football team had defeated Germany (during partition, called Germany national association football team). A draw between the Dutch and the Italians combined with a decisive five goal victory by Germany national association football team over Austria national association football team would give the West Germans the advantage over the Dutch, and a berth in the Final. If the Netherlands–Italy game had a winner, Germany national association football team could still qualify for the third place match by beating Austria national association football team, or in case of an Italian loss, even with a draw. Hans Krankl then scored to put Austria national association football team ahead, but Bernd Hölzenbein equalised for Germany national association football team a few minutes later. Then in 1982, at the next World Cup in Spain, Germany national association football team and Austria national association football team met again, in a final group_stage game in Gijón." .

lsd:Entity_30414 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Operation in Somalia II (UNOSOM II) was the second phase of the United Nations intervention in Somalia, from 01 March 1993 until 28 March 1995, after the country had become involved in civil war in 1991. It did not formally take over operations in Somalia until UNITAF was dissolved on 1993. UNOSOM II's mandate ended in 28 March 1995 when US ships off the coast of Somalia assisted in the safe departure of the remaining UNOSOM troops. The withdrawal of UN troops from Somalia was completed on 28 March 1995." .

lsd:Entity_30443 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Piltown took place near Piltown, County Kilkenny in 1462 as part of the Wars of the Roses." .

lsd:Entity_30470 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hurricane Joaquin (Spanish: , wah_KEEN) was a powerful tropical cyclone that devastated several districts of the Bahamas and caused damage in the Turks and Caicos Islands, parts of the Greater Antilles, and Bermuda. The tenth named storm, third hurricane, and second major hurricane of the 2015 Atlantic hurricane season, Joaquin evolved from a non_tropical low to become a tropical depression on 28 September 2015, well southwest of Bermuda. With all 34 deaths attributed to the storm occurring at sea, Joaquin has the highest offshore death toll for any Atlantic hurricane since the Escuminac hurricane in 1959, which killed 35 people in the Northumberland Strait. Convective showers and thunderstorms steadily increased on September 27, and at 03:00 UTC on 28 September 2015 the NHC assessed the system to have become a tropical depression, situated roughly 405 mi (650 km) southwest of Bermuda." .

lsd:Entity_30472 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Junior men's race at the 2001 IAAF World Cross Country Championships – junior men's race was held at the Hippodrome Wellington in Ostend, Belgium, on 2001." .

lsd:Entity_30477 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Island Games were the fifth Island Games, and were held on the Isle of Wight, from 03 July 1993 to 1993." .

lsd:Entity_30482 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Sunrise Over Tiananmen Square (French: Le jour se lève sur la place Tienanmen) is a 1998 National Film Board of Canada short film animated documentary directed by Shui_Bo Wang." .

lsd:Entity_30509 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Three Nationals Figure Skating Championships included the Czech Figure Skating Championships, Slovakia, and Polish Figure Skating Championships. The event was held on 16 December 2010–18, 2010 at the DOXXbet Arena in Žilina, Slovakia." .

lsd:Entity_30537 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1922 UCI Road World Championships was the second edition of the UCI Road World Championships, the annual world championships for road bicycle racing. The championships took place in Liverpool, United Kingdom on 03 August 1922." .

lsd:Entity_30541 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "This page shows the results of the diving for men and women at the diving at the 1991 Pan American Games, held from 02 August 1991 to 18 August 1991 in Havana, Cuba." .

lsd:Entity_30552 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Beach Volleyball World Championships is a beach volleyball event, that was held from 26 June 2009 to 2009 in Stavanger, Norway. The Beach Volleyball World Championships are organized every two years, and Norway is the first Northern European country to host the event." .

lsd:Entity_30586 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Premios Juventud (Youth Awards) were broadcast by Univision on 17 July 2008." .

lsd:Entity_30599 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Jibrieni was an attack on 17 December 1941 by a Soviet submarine on an Axis convoy and its Romanian escorts off the coast of the Romanian village of Prymorske (today Prymorske, Ukraine). On 17 December 1941, the Soviet M_class submarine M_59 carried out an attack against an Axis convoy near the coastal town of Prymorske in 17 December 1941." .

lsd:Entity_30643 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jorma Panula Conducting Competition, is a music competition for young European conductors held in Vaasa, (Finland). The first competition, named after the conductor and conducting professor Jorma Panula, was organized in 1999 with the idea of being a platform for young conductors starting their professional careers." .

lsd:Entity_30649 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kraśnik started on 23 August 1914, in the province of Galicia and the adjacent areas across the border in the Russian Empire, in northern Austria (in present_day Poland), and ended two days later. The battle was also the first of a series of engagements between Austria_Hungary and Russian Empire all along the Galicia front. Going into the battle of Kraśnik, the Austro_Hungarian forces enjoyed two key advantages over their Russian opponents: superior numbers and a better strategic position." .

lsd:Entity_30679 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Tiburon Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Tiburon, United States of America between 26 September 2016 and 02 October 2016." .

lsd:Entity_30714 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 IRB Tbilisi Cup was the second edition of this international rugby union tournament, created by the International Rugby Board. It was played from 14 to 22 June 2014 at the Avchala Stadium in Tbilisi. The hosts Georgia were joined by Spain and two teams that regularly take part in the IRB Nations Cup, Argentina XV and Emerging Italy." .

lsd:Entity_30737 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wilberforce Eaves and Ernest Lewis defeated Walter Bailey and Charles Simond 6–4, 6–4, 6–3 in the All Comers' Final, but the reigning champions Herbert Baddeley and Wilfred Baddeley defeated Eaves and Lewis 8–6, 5–7, 6–4, 6–3 in the Challenge Round to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1895 Wimbledon Championships." .

lsd:Entity_30743 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Point Pleasant — known as the Battle of Kanawha in some older accounts — was the only major action of Dunmore's War. Along the Ohio River near modern Point Pleasant, forces under the Shawnee Chief Cornstalk attacked Virginia militia under Colonel Andrew Lewis, hoping to halt Lewis's advance into the Ohio Valley. After the battle, the Virginians, along with a second force led by Lord Dunmore, the Royal Governor of Virginia, marched into the Ohio Valley and compelled Cornstalk to agree to a treaty, ending the war. The Battle of Point Pleasant forced Cornstalk to make peace in the Dunmore's War, ceding to Virginia the Shawnee claims to all lands south of the Ohio River (today's states of Kentucky and West Virginia). By the end of that year, the same militiamen who had fought at Point Pleasant managed to drive Lord Dunmore and the British troops supporting him out of Virginia. It cites Point Pleasant as a \"battle of the Revolution\". Nevertheless, the Battle of Point Pleasant is honored as the first engagement of the American Revolution during \"Battle Days\", an annual festival in modern Point Pleasant, now a city in West Virginia." .

lsd:Entity_30774 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Test 219 was a nuclear test conducted by the Soviet Union in the atmosphere via ICBM. The test was performed on 24 December 1962 over the Novaya Zemlya test range." .

lsd:Entity_30799 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1144 papal election followed the death of Pope Celestine II and resulted in the election of Lucius II. Pope Celestine II died on 1144 at Rome, after a pontificate of only five months. The elect took the name Lucius II and received episcopal consecration on 1144." .

lsd:Entity_30811 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Auburn–Georgia Tech football rivalry is an American college football rivalry between the Auburn Tigers and Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets football Yellow Jackets. On 25 November 1892, 1920, Georgia Tech Yellow Jackets football defeated Auburn at Grant Field by a score of 34 to 0 for a share of the SIAA title." .

lsd:Entity_3083 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 29 March 2007 Baghdad bombings was a 29 March 2007, double suicide attack on the al_Shaab market in a Shiite district of northeastern Baghdad. The death toll eventually climbed to 82 killed and 138 people were also wounded." .

lsd:Entity_30836 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2009 Namibian general election were held in Namibia on 27 November 2009–28 November 2009. Voting ended on 28 November 2009 and official election results, released on 4 December, showed that Hifikepunye Pohamba and his SWAPO Party were re_elected, each with over 75% of the vote." .

lsd:Entity_30851 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Bangkok Challenger II was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Bangkok, Thailand between 09 January 2017 and 14 January 2017." .

lsd:Entity_30869 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 United Kingdom general election was held on 15 October 1964, five years after the previous election, and thirteen years after the Conservative Party, first led by Winston Churchill, had entered power. It resulted in the Conservatives, led by its fourth leader, Alec Douglas_Home, narrowly losing the election to the Labour Party, led by Harold Wilson, with Labour having an overall majority of four seats. It resulted in Labour ending its thirteen years in opposition and led Harold Wilson to become, at the time, the youngest Prime Minister of the United Kingdom since Lord Rosebery in 1894. Both major parties had changed leadership in 1963; after the sudden death of Hugh Gaitskell early in the year, Labour chose Harold Wilson (who was then thought of as being on the party's centre_left), while Alec Douglas_Home (then the Earl of Home) had taken over as Conservative leader and Prime Minister of the United Kingdom in the autumn after Harold Macmillan announced his resignation. This proved to be unworkable, and Harold Wilson called a snap election in 1966 United Kingdom general election. Harold Wilson became Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, replacing Alec Douglas_Home. The four_seat majority was not sustainable for a full Parliament, and Harold Wilson called another 1966 United Kingdom general election." .

lsd:Entity_30876 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The murder of Shalhevet Pass was a shooting attack carried out in Hebron, West Bank, on 26 March 2001, in which a Palestinian sniper killed 10_month_old Israel infant Shalhevet Pass. According to Deborah Sontag of the New York Times, the murder became a \"potent Israel symbol as an innocent victim of the raging violence.\" At 4:00 pm on 26 March 2001, Shalhevet was shot in her stroller while accompanied by her parents from a parking lot by Hebron's Avraham Avinu neighborhood, where the family lived. The murder, which occurred during the Second Intifada, produced vocal outrage in Israel and abroad." .

lsd:Entity_30905 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "One of the first streams of Romanesque architecture in Europe from the 10th century and the beginning of 11th century is called First Romanesque or Lombard Romanesque. Catalan architect Josep Puig i Cadafalch suggested that what was formerly considered the late form of pre_Romanesque architecture in Catalonia bore features of Romanesque and thus classified it as First Romanesque (primer romànic). To avoid the term Pre_Romanesque, which is often used with a much broader meaning than is generally suited to refer to early Medieval and early Christian art, and in Spain may also refer to the Visigothic, Asturias, Mozarabic and Repoblación art forms, Puig i Cadafalch preferred to use the term \"First Romanesque\" or \"first Romanesque art\" to designate those Catalan anticipations of the Romanesque itself." .

lsd:Entity_30911 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kaleidoscope World Tour was a Club Life Tour tour in support of his album Kaleidoscope." .

lsd:Entity_30920 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1931 Fuyun earthquake (富蕴地震) occurred on 10 August 1931 at 21:18 UTC. The epicenter was near Fuyun County of northern Xinjiang, People's Republic of China." .

lsd:Entity_30924 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Aintree 200 was a motor race, run to Formula One rules, held on 27 April 1963 at Aintree Motor Racing Circuit, England. The race was run over 50 laps of the circuit, and was won by British driver Graham Hill in a BRM P57." .

lsd:Entity_30961 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1996 South Ossetian presidential election were held in South Ossetia on 10 November 1996." .

lsd:Entity_3097 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Strong Heart (Korean: 강심장; Hanja: 强心臟; RR: Gang Sim Jang; MR: Kang Sim Jang) is a South Korean talk show or talk battle broadcast by Seoul Broadcasting System." .

lsd:Entity_30973 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Tony Bettenhausen 200 was the seventh round of the 1963 USAC Championship Car season, held on 18 August 1963, at the 1_mile (1.6 km) Milwaukee Mile, in West Allis, Wisconsin. Jim Clark and Team Lotus had finished second at the 1963 Indianapolis 500 after a controversy surrounding the lack of a black flag for winner Parnelli Jones, whose car was leaking oil." .

lsd:Entity_31011 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rewind Tour was the twelfth headlining concert tour by American country music trio Rascal Flatts, in support of their ninth studio album Rewind (2014)." .

lsd:Entity_31057 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 UCI Africa Tour was the eighth season of the UCI Africa Tour. Adil Jelloul of Morocco was the defending champion of the 2010–11 UCI Africa Tour. Tarik Chaoufi of Morocco was crowned as the 2011–12 UCI Africa Tour champion." .

lsd:Entity_31106 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Biathlon World Cup was a multi_race series over a season of biathlon, organised by the International Biathlon Union. The season started on 02 December 2018 in Pokljuka, Slovenia and ended on 24 March 2019 in Oslo Holmenkollen, Norway." .

lsd:Entity_31110 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships were held in Lipno nad Vltavou, Czechoslovakia under the auspices of International Canoe Federation. The mixed C2 team event was not held after having been done so at the 1965 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships." .

lsd:Entity_31124 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Coppa Federale Siciliana (which translates as the Sicilian Federal Cup) was a Sicilian football competition which took place during 23 May 1920. Sicilian football clubs were not entered into the Italian Football Championship after the war and the tournament was organized in an attempt to set up a viable alternative for the four main clubs on the island at the time; Palermo FC, Messinese, Calcio Catania and S.C. Marsala 1912." .

lsd:Entity_31159 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Virginia Slims of Indianapolis was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Indianapolis Racquet Club in Indianapolis, Indiana in the United States and was part of the 1986 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the seventh edition of the tournament and ran from 27 October 1986 through 02 November 1986." .

lsd:Entity_31221 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First Battle of Algeciras was a naval battle fought on 06 July 1801 (17 messidor an IX of the French Republican Calendar) between a squadron of British Royal Navy ships of the line and a smaller French Navy squadron at anchor in the fortified Spanish port of Algeciras in the Strait of Gibraltar. Discovering the French at anchor in Algeciras on the morning of 06 July 1801, Saumarez launched an immediate attack on the anchorage through the complicated shoals of Bay of Gibraltar." .

lsd:Entity_31300 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "John Newcombe and Tony Roche beat Rosewall and Stolle in the final, 3–6, 8–6, 5–7, 14–12, 6–3 to win the Gentlemen's Doubles title at the 1968 Wimbledon Championships." .

lsd:Entity_31342 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fayal was a naval engagement between the United States and the United Kingdom fought in 27 September 1814 during the War of 1812 just off the Portuguese city of Horta, Faial Island, in the Azores." .

lsd:Entity_31354 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Wrocław Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Wroclaw, Poland between 16 February 2015 and 22 February 2015." .

lsd:Entity_31365 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 Florida Federal Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the East Lake Woodlands Racquet Club in Tampa, Florida in the United States that was part of the Colgate Series circuit of the 1980 WTA Tour and classified as category AAA. It was the eighth edition of the tournament and was held from 10 November 1980 through 16 November 1980." .

lsd:Entity_31389 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Señorita Panamá 1993, the 11th Señorita Panamá pageant, was held in Atlapa Convention Centre, Panama city, Panama, 1993, after weeks of events. About 15 contestants from all over Panama competed for the prestigious crown. At the conclusion of the final night of competition, outgoing titleholder Giselle González crowned María Sofía Velásquez as the new Señorita Panamá." .

lsd:Entity_31410 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Épila was fought on 21 July 1348 near Zaragoza, in what is now Spain, between the supporters of the Union of Aragon and King Peter IV, led by Don Lope de Luna. There followed the final battle on 21 July 1348. 'In Épila' 'Army of Lope de Luna' The battle began on the morning of 21 July 1348 with an attempt by Union troops, led by Juan Ximénez de Urrea, Jr., to occupy Épila. The victory of the royalist troops at the battle of Épila was complete and final." .

lsd:Entity_31440 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 EuroBasket Women, commonly called EuroBasket Women 2013, was the 34th regional championship held by FIBA Europe. The competition was held in France from 15 June 2013–30 June 2013. This was the fourth time that the EuroBasket Women has been hosted by France. Spain women's national basketball team defeated France 70–69 in the final to win the title. The draw took place on September 21, 2012 at 16:30 local time at Paris, France." .

lsd:Entity_31442 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Norman Brookes and Anthony Wilding defeated Karl Behr and Beals Wright 6–4, 6–4, 6–2 in the All Comers' Final to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1907 Wimbledon Championships." .

lsd:Entity_31462 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Human World Tour is the second solo concert tour by American recording artist, Brandy Norwood. The tour primarily visited North America and Europe, with a few dates in Asia. During the tour, Brandy Norwood performed her hits from previous albums along with a few selections from Human." .

lsd:Entity_31498 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Ninety_Six was a siege in western South Carolina late in the American Revolutionary War. From 22 May 1781 to June 18, 1781, Continental Army Major General Nathanael Greene led 1,000 troops in a siege against the 550 Loyalists in the fortified village of Ninety Six. The British Army's \"southern strategy\" for winning the American Revolutionary War, which had been successful in taking Charleston and winning submission of much of South Carolina and Georgia, hit a stumbling block in March 1781, after General Lord Cornwallis defeated Continental Army General Nathanael Greene at the Battle of Guilford Courthouse in Greensboro, North Carolina. The order of battle for the forces involved in the battle was: The British outpost at Ninety Six was garrisoned by 550 experienced Loyalists, such as De Lancey's Brigade, formed into Provincial regiments (regular army troops who had been recruited from Loyalists in New York, New Jersey, and South Carolina) under the command of Lieutenant Colonel John Cruger. Greene and about 1,000 men arrived outside Ninety Six on 22 May 1781, the same day that Andrew Pickens and Henry \"Light_horse Harry\" Lee began to besiege nearby Augusta, Georgia." .

lsd:Entity_31507 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Slovenian Supercup was a association football that saw the 2008–09 PrvaLiga champions Maribor face off against Slovenian Cup winners NK Interblock. The match was held on 2009 at Ljudski vrt in Maribor." .

lsd:Entity_31533 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1926 Volta a Catalunya was the eighth edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 22 August 1926 to 29 August 1926." .

lsd:Entity_31542 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Boxers Tour was a concert tour by Morrissey, taking place in 03 February 1995." .

lsd:Entity_31562 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Toan Thang I (\"Complete Victory\") was a U.S. Army, Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN), 1st Australian Task Force and Royal Thai Volunteer Regiment operation conducted between 08 April 1968 and 31 May 1968 in the Vietnam War." .

lsd:Entity_31567 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Challenger Banque Nationale de Saguenay was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It took place in Saguenay, Canada between October 17 and 23 October 2016." .

lsd:Entity_31592 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Monaco Grand Prix (formally known as the Formula 1 Grand Prix de Monaco 2018) was a Formula One motor race that was held on 27 May 2018 at the Circuit de Monaco, a street circuit that runs through the Monaco. It was the 6th round of the 2018 Formula One World Championship, the 76th time the Monaco Grand Prix had been held, and the 65th time it had been a round of the Formula One World Championship since the inception of the series in 1950. Daniel Ricciardo led going into the first corner with the top 6 staying in the same order." .

lsd:Entity_31646 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Navratilova and Fleming lost in the final 6–4, 6–4 against Raffaella Reggi and Sergio Casal." .

lsd:Entity_31658 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Serie A is the 52nd season of the women's football top level league in Italy. The season is scheduled to run from 15 21 September 2018 to 20 April 2019. Brescia, who finished second in Serie A last season, and Res A.S. Roma Women, who finished eighth, sold their licenses respectively to Milan and A.S. Roma Women." .

lsd:Entity_31664 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 FIFA Confederations Cup Final was a association football match to determine the winners of the 2001 FIFA Confederations Cup. The match was held at International Stadium Yokohama, Yokohama, Japan national football team, on 10 June 2001 and was contested by Japan national football team and France national association football team. France national association football team won the match 1–0 with the only goal coming after thirty minutes when Patrick Vieira headed in over the advancing keeper Yoshikatsu Kawaguchi from the edge of the penalty area after a long pass from Frank Leboeuf in midfield. This was Japan national football team's first_ever final in a senior FIFA competition, and is also the one of three senior FIFA final with an appearance from an AFC team up to date." .

lsd:Entity_31678 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Yevpatoria gas explosion took place on 24 December 2008 with an explosion in an apartment block in Yevpatoria, a Ukraine Black Sea resort town. On 27 the Ukraine Cabinet of Ministers ordered the central government agencies and the Yevpatoria administration to provide the victims of the blast and the families of those killed with new housing and all financial compensations they are entitled to by January 1, 2009." .

lsd:Entity_3168 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Luxembourg National Division was the 102nd season of top_tier football in Luxembourg. The league season started on 02 August 2015 and ended on 22 May 2016, with a promotion/relegation playoff following on 27 May. F91 Dudelange claimed the title on goal difference over 2014–15 Luxembourg National Division Fola Esch. A match was played between Wiltz, the twelfth_placed team in the 2015–16 Luxembourg National Division and Jeunesse Canach, the third_placed team in the 2015–16 Luxembourg Division of Honour." .

lsd:Entity_31743 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Albanian Cup Final was the 57th final of the Albanian Cup. The final was played at Niko Dovana Stadium in Durres on 2009. The match was contested by KF Tirana, who beat Elbasani in their semi_final, and Flamurtari Vlorë who beat Shkumbini." .

lsd:Entity_31753 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The In Rock World Tour was a successful worldwide concert tour by British hard rock group Deep Purple. In 04 July 1970, Deep Purple's MKII released In Rock." .

lsd:Entity_3176 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rafah massacre occurred on 12 November 1956, during Israel's occupation of the Sinai Peninsula and All_Palestine Protectorate following the Suez Crisis." .

lsd:Entity_3181 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Zengcheng riot (6•11事件) began on 10 June 2011 in Xintang town (新塘镇), Zengcheng District, Guangdong, People's Republic of China and is ongoing. The incident started late Friday 10 June 2011 with the riot lasting several days at Xintang." .

lsd:Entity_31824 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Shopian took place on 03 July 1819 between an expeditionary force from the Sikh Empire and Jabbar Khan, the governor of the Durrani Empire province of Kashmir. On 03 July 1819, the Sikh army attempted to march through Shupiyan to Srinagar but was stopped by a Durrani army headed by Jabbar Khan." .

lsd:Entity_31835 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 Indonesian Women's Open Tennis Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Gelora Senayan Stadium in Jakarta, Indonesia and was part of Tier IV of the 1993 WTA Tour. It was the inaugural edition of the tournament and was held from 26 April 1993 through 02 May 1993." .

lsd:Entity_31852 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sparrsätra, fought between the forces of King Eric XI of Sweden and rebels led by Holmger Knutsson, took place in 1247 near Enköping in Sweden. The main source for what was at stake during the battle is the Annals of Sigtuna, which relates that at Sparrsätra, in 1247, the rural community of Uppland lost its freedom and was charged with paying taxes, including taxes on grain production and the right of laying up a ship: The archaeologist Mats G. Larsson has commented that it is hardly surprising that such fundamental changes in the structure of society would meet fierce resistance. There may have been additional reasons why the battle took place in 1247. The battle took place at Sparrsätra, which is located in Uppland, north of Enköping." .

lsd:Entity_31950 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bilbao, part of the War in the North in the Spanish Civil War, had the Nationalist Army captured Bilbao and the rest of the Basque Country that was still being held by the Republic. The Basque people in Spain generally inhabit four provinces: Navarre, Álava, Gipuzkoa and Biscay. By 11 19 June 1937 the Basque forces had fallen back to the city of Bilbao, which was defended by a series of rushed fortifications called the \"Bilbao's Iron Ring\"." .

lsd:Entity_31972 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 European Curling Championships were held November 22 to 29 at the Palladium de Champéry in Champéry, Switzerland. Switzerland last hosted the European Curling Championships in 2010, when it was also held in Champéry. This edition of the European Curling Championships marks the first appearance of Israel at an international curling event." .

lsd:Entity_31997 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 24th edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 09 March 1989 to 15 March 1989. The race started in Bacoli and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

lsd:Entity_3202 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 French Open – Men's Doubles was held from 29 May until 1989 on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland Garros in Paris, France. Jim Grabb and Patrick McEnroe won the title, defeating Mansour Bahrami and Éric Winogradsky in the final." .

lsd:Entity_32042 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Gould Twin Dixie 125 were the second and third rounds of the 1979 IndyCar season, held on 22 April 1979, at Atlanta Motor Speedway, Hampton, Georgia." .

lsd:Entity_32079 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Brobacka was a battle fought in Alingsås between Sweden and Denmark, on 09 August 1566." .

lsd:Entity_32108 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Who concert disaster occurred on 03 December 1979, when British rock band the Who performed at Heritage Bank Center (now known as Heritage Bank Center) in Cincinnati, Ohio, United States, and a crush of concert_goers outside the Coliseum's entry doors resulted in the deaths of 11 people." .

lsd:Entity_32116 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bellator MMA: Season Ten was the tenth season for Bellator MMA, a mixed martial arts promotion. It began on 28 February 2014 and aired on Paramount Network." .

lsd:Entity_32122 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 NHK Trophy was the final event of six in the 2014–15 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating, a senior_level international invitational competition series. It was held at the Namihaya Dome in Ōsaka Prefecture on 28 November 2014–30." .

lsd:Entity_32126 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Scottish Parliament general election, to fill 129 seats, took place on 06 May 1999. Following the election, the Labour Party and the Liberal Democrats formed the Scottish Executive, with Labour Member of the Scottish Parliament (MSP) Donald Dewar becoming First Minister. The Conservative Party, still recovering from their wipeout in the 1997 UK general election across Scotland, failed to win a single constituency seat but did manage to win 18 seats through the Additional Member System. Following the election the new parliament met in the General Assembly Hall of the Church of Scotland in Edinburgh for the first time on Wednesday 12 06 May 1999 although the actual devolution of powers from Westminster to the Scottish Parliament did not take place until midnight on Thursday 1 July 1999, almost two months later." .

lsd:Entity_32133 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1961 Burundian legislative election were held in Burundi on 08 September 1961 in order to install a government to rule the country following independence from Belgium on 1 July 1962." .

lsd:Entity_32156 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Southeast Asian Games (Thai: กีฬาแห่งเอเชียตะวันออกเฉียงใต้ 2007, romanized: Kila haeng echiye tawan oak chiyeng tai 2007), officially known as the 24th Southeast Asian Games, was a Southeast Asian multi_sport event held in Nakhon Ratchasima (Korat), Thailand. This was the sixth time Thailand hosted the Southeast Asian Games and its first time since 1995. It was opened by Prince Vajiralongkorn, the Crown Prince of Thailand at the 80th Birthday Stadium. A general assembly of the SEA Games Federation Council during the 2003 Southeast Asian Games in Hanoi, Vietnam approved Thailand's hosting of the games. On 24, northeast Thailand's Nakhon Ratchasima Province authorities met to discuss the 2007 SEA Games schedule, and progress on the province's under_construction US$65 million sports complex. On 2007, Prince Vajiralongkorn, on behalf of his father, King Bhumibol Adulyadej presented the royal flame to the games organising committee chairman, Suvit Yodmani. After that, the flame was relayed from Bangkok to provinces such as Chonburi, Samut Songkhram, Prachuap Khiri Khan, Ratchaburi, Suphanburi, Nakhon Sawan, Phichit, Phitsanulok, Phetchabun, Loei, Nong Khai, Sakhon Nahkhon, Mukdahan, Ubon Ratchatthani, Surin, Roi Et and Khon Kaen before arrived in the host province, Nakhon Ratchasima on 2007. In Nakhon Ratchasima, the flame was relayed across its districts such as Phimai, Choke Chai, Pak Thong Chai, Pak Chong and Soongnern before arrived at Nakhon Ratchasima City, the host city of the games on 2007. The shape of the boats that resemble the Pimai castle, represents the host city Nakhon Ratchasima, the Northeast region of Thailand and its civilisation one thousand years ago. It is one of the oldest stable cat breeds in Thailand and named after the Nakhon Ratchasima. The24th Southeast Asian Games had 39 venues for the games, 28 in Nakhon Ratchasima, 6 in Bangkok and 5 in Chonburi. The Host Thailand performance was its best ever yet in Southeast Asian Games history and emerged as overall champion of the games." .

lsd:Entity_32186 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating was the ISU Grand Prix of Figure Skating series of the 2010–2011 figure skating season. The Grand Prix series began on 22 October 2010 and ended on 12 December 2010. Skaters who reached the age of 14 by 2010 were eligible to compete on the senior Grand Prix circuit." .

lsd:Entity_32195 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Berlin state election, 2001 was held on 21 October 2001 to elect the members of the 15th Abgeordnetenhaus of Berlin. Prior to the election, Mayor Klaus Wowereit had led a minority government of the Social Democratic Party (SPD) and The Greens since June, which had replaced a coalition between the Christian Democratic Union (CDU) and SPD which collapsed in June. Summary of the 21 October 2001 election results for the Abgeordnetenhaus of Berlin." .

lsd:Entity_32199 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2014 South American Games – women's 1500 metre freestyle competition at the 2014 South American Games took place on March 10 at the Estadio Nacional Julio Martínez Prádanos. The swimming at the 2010 South American Games – women's 1500 metre freestyle champion was Kristel Köbrich of Chile." .

lsd:Entity_3221 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Medal Winners Open is an invitational pro–am figure skating competition organized by the Japan Skating Federation and sanctioned by the International Skating Union (ISU). In place of Medal Winners Open, the promoters presented Medal Winners Gala on January 14, 2017 in Yokohama, Japan. The inaugural competition was held on 2012 in Saitama, Japan." .

lsd:Entity_32219 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Rolling Stones' The Rolling Stones 3rd European Tour 1965 was a concert tour by the band. The tour commenced on 15 June 1965 and concluded on 29 June 1965." .

lsd:Entity_32229 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Indonesia Super Series Premier was the sixth super series tournament of the 2011 BWF Superseries. The tournament was held in Jakarta, Indonesia from 21 June 2011–26 June 2011 and had a total purse of $600,000." .

lsd:Entity_32253 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Ironman World Championship was a long distance triathlon competition that was held on 2014 in Kailua_Kona, Hawaii. The 2014 Ironman series consisted of 29 Ironman races plus the 2013 Ironman World Championship which was itself a qualifier for the 2014 Championship." .

lsd:Entity_32256 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Greek raid on Alexandria (1825) was an unsuccessful attempt organized by Greek bruloteer Constantine Kanaris to destroy the Egyptian fleet at its base in Alexandria in 1825 during the Greek War of Independence." .

lsd:Entity_32273 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shaba I was a conflict in Zaire's Katanga Province lasting from March 8 to 26 May 1977. The banks feared that the war would now bankrupt Zaire's government. Observers were unsure of whether the war had ended with a victory for the Zaire government or had devolved into a guerrilla war. The poor performance of Zaire's military during Shaba I gave evidence of chronic weaknesses." .

lsd:Entity_32334 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The We Are Pioneers World Tour is the first headlining tour by American Country music group, The Band Perry. The tour began on 08 November 2013 in Gothenburg, Sweden and ended on 03 October 2014 in Primm, Nevada." .

lsd:Entity_32368 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "5th Thailand National Games (Thai: กีฬาเขตแห่งประเทศไทย ครั้งที่ 5, also known as the 1971 National Games and 1971 Interprovincial Games) were held in Nakhon Sawan, Thailand from 2 to 1971, with contests in 13 sports. The 1971 Thailand National Games featured 10 Olympic sports contested at the 1971 Southeast Asian Peninsular Games, 1974 Asian Games and 1972 Summer Olympics." .

lsd:Entity_32399 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 59th Technology & Engineering Emmy Awards was held on 08 January 2008 at the 2008 International Consumer Electronics Show in Las Vegas." .

lsd:Entity_32416 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "24 Hours in Cyberspace (1996) was \"the largest one_day online event\" up to that date, headed by photographer Rick Smolan with Jennifer Erwitt, Tom Melcher, Samir Arora and Clement Mok. 24 Hours in Cyberspace was an online project which took place on the then_active website, cyber24.com (and is still online at a mirror website maintained by Georgia Tech). The goal was not to show pictures of website and computer monitors, but rather images of people whose lives were affected by the use of the growing Internet." .

lsd:Entity_32447 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Copa do Brasil was the 1st staging of the Copa do Brasil. The competition started on 19 July 1989, and concluded on 02 September 1989, with the second leg of the final, held at the Estádio Olímpico Monumental in Porto Alegre, in which Grêmio Football Porto Alegrense lifted the trophy for the first time with a 2_1 victory over Sport Recife." .

lsd:Entity_32453 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Naval Battle of the Han River (28 September 1951–30 September 1951) was fought during the Korean War." .

lsd:Entity_32505 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Danish Cup Final were the final and deciding match of the 2006–07 Danish Cup. It took place on Thursday 17 May 2007 at Parken Stadium in Copenhagen." .

lsd:Entity_32513 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Welsh Open was a professional ranking snooker tournament that took place between 13 February 2012–19 February 2012 at the Newport Centre in Newport, Wales. The breakdown of prize money for this year is shown below: These matches were8 February 2012held 2012 13 February 2012 at the World Snooker Academy, Sheffield, England." .

lsd:Entity_32521 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 25th Golden Raspberry Awards, or Razzies, were held on 26 February 2005 at the Ivar Theatre in Hollywood, California to honor the worst films the film industry had to offer in 2004. To celebrate the 25th anniversary of the Golden Raspberry Awards, four special categories—Worst Razzie Loser of Our First 25 Years, Worst \"Comedy\" of Our First 25 Years, Worst \"Drama\" of Our First 25 Years, and Worst \"Musical\" of Our First 25 Years—were created." .

lsd:Entity_32564 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2023 Africa Cup of Nations (also referred to as AFCON 2023 or CAN 2023) is scheduled to be the 34th edition of the Africa Cup of Nations, the biennial international men's association football championship of Africa organized by the Confederation of African Football (CAF). Bids: Rejected Bids: After the final vote at the CAF Executive Committee meeting, on 20 September 2014, the CAF announced the hosts for the 2019, 2021 Africa Cup of Nations and 2023 AFCON tournaments: 2019 to Cameroon, 2021 Africa Cup of Nations to Côte d'Ivoire, and 2023 to Guinea. Consequently, Côte d'Ivoire, original hosts of 2021 Africa Cup of Nations, would host the 2023 Africa Cup of Nations, and Guinea, original hosts of 2023, would host the 2025 Africa Cup of Nations." .

lsd:Entity_32568 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Poznań Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place at the Park Tenisowy Olimpia in Poznań, Poland from 9 to 11 July 2016 2015, including the qualifying competition in the first two days. The prize money has decreased to €42,500 + Hospitality, and because of calendar congestion caused by the 2016 Summer Olympics no top 100 player entered that year, which had not happened in Poznań Open before." .

lsd:Entity_32586 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The War of the Mantuan Succession (1628–31) was a peripheral part of the Thirty Years' War. The war, fought among the backers of rival claimants, pitted France against the Habsburgs in a contest for control of northern Italy. Duchy of Mantua is the ancestral city where the male line of the Gonzaga dynasty ruled, first as marquesses, then after 1540 as dukes, in vassalage to the Holy Roman Empire. He sought to re_attach the Duchy of Mantua to the Holy Roman Empire; Ferrante being in the Imperial_Spanish camp, was a useful tool to that purpose. The resulting French_Habsburg war over the succession was just one of the many theatres of the Thirty Years' War, fought all over Europe." .

lsd:Entity_32605 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Trough (March or 1756) was a skirmish of the early French and Indian War (1754–63) fought between Native Americans and United Kingdom settlers in the valley of the South Branch Potomac River in what is now northern Hardy County, USA." .

lsd:Entity_32621 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Hungarian Challenger Open is a professional tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It takes place in Budapest, Hungary between 06 February 2017 and 12 February 2017." .

lsd:Entity_32674 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hialeah Turf Cup Handicap is a discontinued American Thoroughbred horse race open to horses aged three and older that was run each year at Hialeah Park Race Track in Hialeah until the track closed at the end of the 2001 racing season." .

lsd:Entity_32691 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of María (15 June 1809) saw a small Spanish army led by Joaquín Blake y Joyes face an Imperial French corps under Louis Gabriel Suchet. María de Huerva is located 17 kilometres (10.6 mi) southwest of Zaragoza, Spain." .

lsd:Entity_32702 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On the morning of 04 September 2005, six days after Hurricane Katrina struck New Orleans, members of the New Orleans Police Department (NOPD) who were allegedly responding to a call of an officer under fire shot and killed two civilians at the Danziger Bridge: 17_year_old James Brissette and 40_year_old Ronald Madison. On 04 September 2005, almost a week after Hurricane Katrina struck New Orleans, several New Orleans Police Department (NOPD) officers arrived at the Danziger Bridge. The officers involved in the shooting were taken into custody on January 2, 2007, and were indicted for murder and attempted murder. On December 19, 2016, New Orleans Mayor Mitch Landrieu announced a settlement agreement between the city and the families of the Danziger Bridge shootings, plus two other cases involving \"lethal confrontations between officers and civilians in the aftermath of Hurricane Katrina." .

lsd:Entity_32719 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Pride Shockwave is the international PPV broadcast name for the mixed martial arts and kickboxing event co_promoted by the Pride Fighting Championships and K_1 on 28 August 2002. It was held at the Japan National Stadium in Tokyo." .

lsd:Entity_32726 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Aberto de Tênis do Rio Grande do Sul was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Porto Alegre, Brazil between 28 September 2015 and 04 October 2015." .

lsd:Entity_32739 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Finnish League Cup is the 16th season of the Finnish League Cup, Finland's second_most prestigious cup association football tournament. The group stage matches will be played from 13 January 2012 to 2012." .

lsd:Entity_32767 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Germany participated in the Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016 with the song \"Ghost\" written by Thomas Burchia, Anna Leyne and Conrad Hensel. ARD and NDR had initially announced on 19 November Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015 that they had selected the soul and R&B singer_songwriter Xavier Naidoo to represent Germany in Stockholm. Performing in position 10, Germany placed twenty_sixth (last) out of the 26 participating countries with 11 points. Prior to the 2016 Contest, Germany had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest fifty_nine times since its debut as one of seven countries to take part in 1956. NDR confirmed that Germany would participate in the 2016 Eurovision Song Contest on 27 May Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015. Following NDR's announcement on 19 November Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015 that Naidoo had been selected to represent Germany, negative reactions were expressed by both the German public and media. Unser Lied für Stockholm (English: Our Song for Stockholm) was the competition that selected Germany's entry for the Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 2016. As a member of the \"Big 5\", Germany automatically qualified to compete in the final on 2016. This would have been regularly decided via a draw held during the semi_final allocation draw on 2016, however, prior to the draw, ARD requested of the European Broadcasting Union that Germany be allowed to broadcast and vote in the second semi_final on 2016, which was approved by the contest's Reference Group. Germany was subsequently placed to perform in position 10, following the entry from Sweden and before the entry from France. Germany placed twenty_sixth (last) in the final, scoring 11 points: 10 points from the televoting and 1 point from the juries. Voting during the three shows was conducted under a Eurovision Song Contest 2016 that involved each country now awarding two sets of points from 1_8, 10 and 12: one from their professional jury and the other from televoting." .

lsd:Entity_32793 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 GP2 Series season was the forty_sixth season of the second_tier of Formula One feeder championship and also eighth season under the GP2 Series moniker and also the first season after merging with the GP2 Asia Series. The title was won by Italian Davide Valsecchi, driving for DAMS." .

lsd:Entity_32810 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1993 European Open Water Swimming Championships was the third edition of the European Open Water Championships and took part from 1993_1993 in Slapy, Czech Republic." .

lsd:Entity_32855 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 PDC European Tour Event 1 was the first of eight PDC European Tour events on the 2013 PDC Pro Tour. The tournament took place at the Butlins Minehead in Minehead, England, from 2013–2013." .

lsd:Entity_32864 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The MTV Australia Awards 2008 is the new title for the Australian MTV Awards previously known as the MTV Australia Video Music Awards. The show was held at Sydney's Australian Technology Park in Redfern on 2008 and aired live on MTV Australia and Fox8 on the Foxtel, Austar and Optus platforms in Australia and 28 April on SKY Digital in New Zealand." .

lsd:Entity_32910 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 German Grand Prix was a Formula Two race held on 03 August 1952 at the Nürburgring. It was race 6 of 8 in the 1952 Formula One season, in which each Grand Prix was run to Formula Two rules rather than the Formula One regulations normally used. The 18_lap race was won by Ferrari driver Alberto Ascari after he started from pole position. The date was 03 August 1952, the earliest anyone would claim the Championship until Jim Clark seized the crown on 1 August in 1965, also at the Nürburgring." .

lsd:Entity_32919 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 PGA Championship was the 29th PGA Championship, held June 18–24 at Plum Hollow Country Club in Southfield, Michigan, a suburb northwest of Detroit. The match play format at the PGA Championship in 1947 called for 12 rounds (216 holes) in seven days: Runyan did not advance in the eleven_way playoff for the one final spot in the match play field." .

lsd:Entity_32969 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 FIFA World Cup knockout stage was a single_elimination tournament involving the four teams that qualified from 1974 FIFA World Cup. The knockout stage began with the third place play_off on 6 July and ended with the 1974 FIFA World Cup Final on 1974, both at the Olympiastadion in Munich." .

lsd:Entity_3299 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1804 United States presidential election in Vermont took place between November 2 and 1804, as part of the 1804 United States presidential election." .

lsd:Entity_33011 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics – men's 400 metres hurdles at the 2004 Summer Olympics as part of the athletics at the 2004 Summer Olympics were held at the Olympic Stadium of Athens from 23 August 2004 to 26. The qualification period for athletics was 1 January 2003 to 2004." .

lsd:Entity_33016 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Texaco/Havoline 200 was the fourteenth round of the 1998 CART FedEx Champ Car World Series season, held on 16 August 1998, at Road America in Elkhart Lake, Wisconsin." .

lsd:Entity_33035 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 FIVB Volleyball World League was the 28th edition of the annual men's international volleyball tournament played by 36 teams between 02 June 2017 and 08 July 2017. The previous highest set score was 44–42, which had happened twice (in the 1999 FIVB Volleyball World League match between Canada and Brazil, and in the 2016 FIVB Volleyball World League World Olympic Qualifying Tournament match between France and Australia)." .

lsd:Entity_33044 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2013 Turkish Super Cup (Turkish: TFF Süper Kupa) was the 40th edition of the Turkish Super Cup since its establishment as Presidential Cup in 1966. The match was contested between the 2012 Turkish Super Cup–13 Süper Lig champions Galatasaray S.K. and the 2012 Turkish Super Cup–13 Turkish Cup winners Fenerbahçe FC, making the game the 375th edition of Kıtalar Arası Derbi. The game was a rematch of the 2012 Turkish Super Cup, which was won by title holders Galatasaray S.K.. Galatasaray S.K. last won the cup in 2012 Turkish Super Cup against Fenerbahçe FC, and Fenerbahçe FC last won the cup in 2009 against Beşiktaş. Galatasaray S.K. in 1996 and 2012 Turkish Super Cup, and Fenerbahçe FC in 1973 and 1985. Galatasaray S.K. were champions in the regular season, finishing ten points ahead of Fenerbahçe FC. In the 2013 Turkish Cup Final they were crowned champions against rivals Trabzonspor by a decisive 1–0 win. This was a consecutive win for Fenerbahçe FC, as they were title holders of the 2012 Turkish Super Cup Turkish Cup Final." .

lsd:Entity_33063 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 European Championship was the tenth edition of the Professional Darts Corporation tournament, the European Championship, which saw the top players from the twelve European tour events compete against each other. The tournament took place from 2017–2017 at the Ethias Arena in Hasselt, Belgium." .

lsd:Entity_33084 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 California gubernatorial election occurred on 07 November 2006. The incumbent Republican Governor of California, Arnold Schwarzenegger, won re_election for his first and only full term. Under the California Constitution, the Governor of California serves a four_year term, with a maximum limit of two consecutive terms. Arnold Schwarzenegger was elected during the 2003 California gubernatorial recall election and served out the remainder of Gray Davis's term; he was eligible to serve until 2011." .

lsd:Entity_33112 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sino_Burmese War (Chinese: 清緬戰爭; Burmese: တရုတ်_မြန်မာ စစ် (၁၇၆၅–၆၉)), also known as the Qing invasions of Burma or the Myanmar campaign of the Qing dynasty, was a war fought between the Qing dynasty of China and the Konbaung Dynasty of Burma (Myanmar). Nonetheless, the war, which claimed the lives of over 70,000 Chinese soldiers and four commanders, is sometimes described as \"the most disastrous frontier war that the Qing dynasty had ever waged\", and one that \"assured Burmese independence\". At first, the Emperor envisaged an easy war, and sent in only the Green Standard troops stationed in Yunnan. The Qing kept a heavy military lineup in the border areas of Yunnan for about one decade in an attempt to wage another war while imposing a ban on inter_border trade for two decades. The Burmese control of the Shan States (which covered the present_day Kachin State, Shan State and Kayah State) came in 1557 when King Bayinnaung of the Toungoo dynasty conquered all of the Shan States. The annexed border states ranged from Mogaung and Bhamo in present_day Kachin State to Hsenwi State (Theinni) and Kengtung State (Kyaingtong) in present_day Shan State to Sipsongpanna (Kyaingyun) in present_day Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture, Yunnan. In 1758–59, King Alaungpaya, the founder of the dynasty, sent an expedition to the farther Shan States (present_day Kachin State and northern and eastern Shan State), which had been annexed by the Qing over two decades earlier, to reestablish Burmese authority. Nonetheless, the Emperor kept a heavy military lineup in the border areas of Yunnan for about one decade in an attempt to wage another war while imposing a ban on inter_border trade for two decades. The war is considered the peak of Konbaung Dynasty military power." .

lsd:Entity_33124 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1904 Prince Edward Island general election was held in the Canada province of Prince Edward Island on 1904. The election was won by the governing Liberals, led by incumbent Premier Arthur Peters." .

lsd:Entity_33129 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Haguenau (18 November 1793 – 22 December 1793) saw a Republican French army commanded by Jean_Charles Pichegru mount a persistent offensive against a Coalition army under Dagobert Sigmund von Wurmser during the War of the First Coalition. Haguenau is a city in Bas_Rhin department of France, located 29 kilometres (18 mi) north of Strasbourg." .

lsd:Entity_33142 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Jarafa mosque massacre was an attack on members of Ansar al_Sunna praying at a mosque in Jarafa, a village in the outskirts of Omdurman, Sudan on 08 December 2000." .

lsd:Entity_33174 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "athletics at the 2008 Summer Olympics were held during the last ten days of the games, from 15 August 2008 to 24 August 2008, at the Beijing National Stadium. Despite a haul of 100 medals at the games as a whole, People's Republic of China athletes took home two bronze medals from the athletics events. During the 2008 Summer Olympics, 17 new Olympic records and 5 new world records were set in the athletics events." .

lsd:Entity_33178 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Proximus Diamond Games was a women's professional tennis Diamond Games played on indoor hard courts at the Sportpaleis in Antwerp, Belgium that was part of the Tier II category of the 2006 WTA Tour. It was the sixth edition of the Diamond Games and was held from 10 February until 18 February 2007 2002." .

lsd:Entity_33198 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 IPP Open and the Orto_Lääkärit Open were professional tennis tournaments played on indoor hard courts. The two events took place together at the Tali Tennis Center in Helsinki, Finland, on 11 November 2013–17 November 2013." .

lsd:Entity_3321 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Rennae Stubbs and Todd Woodbridge won the title by defeating Lisa Raymond and Leander Paes 6–4, 5–7, in the final." .

lsd:Entity_33237 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1800 United States presidential election was the fourth quadrennial United States presidential election. In what is sometimes referred to as the \"Revolution of 1800\", Vice President of the United States Thomas Jefferson of the Democratic_Republican Party defeated incumbent President of the United States John Adams of the Federalist Party. The Democratic_Republicans nominated a ticket consisting of Vice President of the United States Thomas Jefferson of Virginia and former Senator Aaron Burr of New York. While the 1800 election was a re_match of the 1796 United States presidential election, it ushered in a new type of American politics, a two_party republic and acrimonious campaigning behind the scenes and through the press. Although the congressional election of 1800 turned over majority control of the House of Representatives to the Democratic_Republicans by 68 seats to 38, the United States presidential election had to be decided by the outgoing House that had been elected in the congressional election of 1798 (at that time, the new presidential and congressional terms all started on March 4 of the year after a national election). True or not, House Whigs, who from the start of the 1800 campaign viewed Jefferson as their candidate for president and Burr for vice president, faced two abhorrent possible outcomes when they met to vote: A–the Federalists manage to engineer a victory for Burr, or, B–the Federalists refuse to break the deadlock leaving a Federalist—Secretary of State John Marshall—as Acting President of the United States." .

lsd:Entity_33241 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Claudy bombing occurred on 31 July 1972, when three car bombs exploded mid_morning on the Main Street of Claudy in County Londonderry, Northern Ireland." .

lsd:Entity_33284 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1999 Nepalese legislative election were held in Nepal on 03 May 1999 and 17 May 1999." .

lsd:Entity_33341 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cara Black and Leander Paes won the title, defeating Liezel Huber and Jamie Murray in the final 7–6(8–6), 6–4." .

lsd:Entity_33350 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Immae was fought in 272 between the Roman army of Emperor Aurelian, and the armies of the Palmyrene Empire, whose leader, Queen Zenobia, had usurped Roman control over the eastern provinces. Both armies took the field \"near\" Antioch on the Orontes at Immae (close by Reyhanli, Turkey) in traditional battle formations with infantry in the center and cavalry on the flanks. His infantry was no match for the battle_hardened legionaries and immediately ordered a full retreat to Antioch on the Orontes." .

lsd:Entity_33367 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 77th running of the 1993 Tour of Flanders cycling race in Belgium was held on Sunday 04 April 1993. Belgian Johan Museeuw won his first 1993 Tour of Flanders in a two_man sprint against Frans Maassen. The race started in Sint_Niklaas and finished in Meerbeke (Ninove)." .

lsd:Entity_33388 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Challenge Bell was a tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Club Avantage Multi_Sports in Quebec City in Canada that was part of WTA Tier III tournaments of the 2002 WTA Tour. It was the 10th edition of the National Bank Cup, and was held from 16 September 2002 through 22 September 2002." .

lsd:Entity_33401 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Verizon Ladies First Tour was a co_headlining concert tour by American recording artists Beyoncé, Alicia Keys and Missy Elliott. ; Keys' second studio album, The Diary of Alicia Keys and Beyoncé's first studio album, Dangerously in Love. That's exactly the aura Beyoncé gave off as she began her closing set of the Verizon Ladies First Tour, which also features Alicia Keys, Missy Elliott and Tamia\"." .

lsd:Entity_33405 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Inauguration of George H. W. Bush as the 41st President of the United States was held on Friday, 20 January 1989, at the West Front of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 51st United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the only term of both George H. W. Bush as President and Dan Quayle as Vice President." .

lsd:Entity_3346 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1903 World Figure Skating Championships is an annual figure skating competition sanctioned by the International Skating Union in which figure skating compete for the title of World Champion. The competition took place from 20 February 1903 to 21 at the Yusupovsky Garden in Saint Petersburg, Russian Empire and was timed to 200_year Jubilee of Saint Petersburg." .

lsd:Entity_33481 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Rugby League World Cup (also referred to as the World Series) was the seventh tournament for the Rugby League World Cup. The Kangaroos showed they were worthy World Champions with a comprehensive 25–0 win at Headingley in front of a disappointing crowd of 7,680 which was over 11,000 less than had attended the 1970 World Cup Final between Great Britain and Australia national rugby league team at the same venue." .

lsd:Entity_33515 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Bumps race is a form of rowing race in which a number of boats chase each other in single file, each crew attempting to catch and ‘bump’ the boat in front without being caught by the boat behind. Bumps racing in fours is also the format of inter_house rowing at Eton College and Shrewsbury School." .

lsd:Entity_33522 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 IFK Göteborg season was IFK Göteborg 108th in existence, their 81st season in Allsvenskan and their 37th consecutive season in the league. IFK Göteborg also participated in one competition in which the club continued playing in for the 2014 IFK Göteborg season, 2013–14 Svenska Cupen. IFK Göteborg qualified for the 2013 Svenska Supercupen by winning the 2012–13 Svenska Cupen on 2013. IFK Göteborg qualified for the 2013–14 UEFA Europa League by merit of winning the 2012–13 Svenska Cupen." .

lsd:Entity_33568 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Prosperita Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Ostrava, Czech Republic between 25 April 2016 and 01 May 2016." .

lsd:Entity_33585 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1938 World Men's Handball Championship was the first ever handball World Championship. It was played in Germany on 05 February 1938 and 06 February 1938. Contested by just 4 national teams, the tournament was won by hosts Germany." .

lsd:Entity_33614 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Essendon Football Club and Hawthorn Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 28 September 1985. It was the 89th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1985 VFL season." .

lsd:Entity_33659 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "This Battle of Megiddo is recorded as having taken place in 609 BC when Pharaoh Necho II of Ancient Egypt led his army to Carchemish (northern Syria) to join with his allies, the fading Neo_Assyrian Empire, against the surging Neo_Babylonian Empire. Jehoahaz was taken captive to Ancient Egypt, where he became the first king of Judah to die in exile. From being wounded by an arrow to his burial in Jerusalem, the story resembles perhaps too closely stories from I and II Kings about Kings Ahab of Israel and Ahaziah of Judah, events which occurred at least two centuries before Josiah's death." .

lsd:Entity_33683 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Northern European Gymnastics Championships was an artistic gymnastics competition held in the city of Limerick in the mid_west of the Ireland. The event was held between the 18th and 20 September 2015 at the University of Limerick. Saturday 18 September 2015 (Team and Individual All_Around competition) Sunday 2015 (Men's and Women's Individual Apparatus Finals)" .

lsd:Entity_33700 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "WrestleMania VIII was the eighth annual WrestleMania professional wrestling pay_per_view (PPV) event produced by the World Wrestling Federation (WWF, now WWE). It took place on 05 April 1992, at the RCA Dome in Indianapolis. WrestleMania VIII would also be the first time since the inaugural WrestleMania that the closing match was not contested for the WWF Championship." .

lsd:Entity_33718 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 CONCACAF Women's Championship determined the CONCACAF's two qualifiers for the FIFA Women's World Cup 1995 — the winner the United States women's national soccer team and the runner_up Canada. The tournament took place in Montreal, Quebec, Canada between 13 August 1994 & 21 1994 and consisted of 5 teams. The 1994 CONCACAF's Women's Championship was the third staging of the CONCACAF's Women's Championship." .

lsd:Entity_33729 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Drumclog was fought on 01 June 1679, between a group of Covenanters and the forces of John Graham of Claverhouse, at Drumclog, in South Lanarkshire, Scotland. The Battle of Drumclog is celebrated by some in Scotland as a victory for religious freedom. The Covenanters' flag from this battle at Drumclog now resides in a museum in Scotland." .

lsd:Entity_33749 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2013 Rwandan parliamentary election were held in Rwanda between 16 September 2013 and 18 September 2013." .

lsd:Entity_33765 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Hunger War or Famine War was a brief conflict between the allied Kingdom of Poland, and Grand Duchy of Lithuania, against the Teutonic Order in summer 1414 in an attempt to resolve territorial disputes. According to Johann von Posilge, 86 knights of the Teutonic Order died from plague following the war. After the Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War of 1410–1411 not all issues between the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and Teutonic Order were settled. In the summer of 1414, armies of King Jogaila and Grand Duke Vytautas invaded Prussia, ruled by the State of the Teutonic Order." .

lsd:Entity_33773 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 AFC Champions League was the 30th edition of the top_level Asian club association football tournament organized by the Asian Football Confederation (Asian Football Confederation), and the 9th under the current AFC Champions League title. The winners, Al Sadd Sports Club qualified for the 2011 FIFA Club World Cup in Japan. The Asian Football Confederation approved criteria for participation in the 2011 and 2012 seasons. On 30, the Asian Football Confederation announced 12 more MA's that were keen to join the ACL, in addition to ten participating national associations. All losers from the qualifying play_off entered the 2011 Asian Football Confederation Cup group stage." .

lsd:Entity_33782 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The alpine skiing at the 1960 Winter Olympics – men's downhill competition of the alpine skiing at the 1960 Winter Olympics was held at Squaw Valley Ski Resort on Monday, February 22." .

lsd:Entity_33869 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Petit Le Mans was the 2014 edition of the Petit Le Mans automotive endurance race, held on September 30–2014, at the Road Atlanta circuit in Braselton, Georgia, United States of America." .

lsd:Entity_33884 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sevenoaks railway accident occurred on 24 August 1927 between Dunton Green railway station and Sevenoaks railway station." .

lsd:Entity_33905 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1923 French Grand Prix (formally the XVII French Grand Prix) was a Grand Prix motor race held at Tours on 02 July 1923. The race was run over 35 laps of the 22.83 km circuit for a total distance of just under 800 km and was won by Henry Segrave driving a Sunbeam." .

lsd:Entity_33948 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1966 FIBA Africa Championship for Women was the 1st FIBA Africa Championship for Women, played under the rules of FIBA, the world governing body for basketball, and the FIBA Africa thereof. The tournament was hosted by the Guinea from 11 April 1966 to 14, 1966." .

lsd:Entity_3396 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Alexandretta was the first clash between the forces of the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate in Syria. It was fought in early 971 near İskenderun, while the main Fatimid army was besieging Antioch, which the Byzantines had captured two years previously. The defeat at İskenderun, coupled with the Qarmatian invasion of southern Syria, forced the Fatimid caliphate to lift the siege and secured Byzantine Empire control of Antioch and northern Syria. Seized with the spirit of jihad and aiming to legitimize their rule, the Fatimid caliphate used the Byzantine Empire advance on Antioch and the \"infidel\" threat as a major item in their propaganda aimed towards the newly conquered region, along with promises to restore just government. At some point, a Fatimid detachment—according to Ibn al_Dawadari 4,000 men under a Berber chieftain called Aras and a former emir of Tarsus, Ibn az_Zayyat—moved north against İskenderun, where the Byzantine Empire relief army had camped. The first clash between the eastern Mediterranean's two foremost powers thus ended in a Byzantine Empire victory, which on the one hand strengthened the Byzantine Empire position in northern Syria and on the other weakened the Fatimid caliphate, both in lives lost and in morale and reputation. In the end, Ja'far was unable to resist the Qarmatians and their Arab Bedouin allies; making the fatal choice of confronting them in the desert, he was defeated and killed in battle in August 971. Although the emperor advanced deep into Muslim lands and even threatened to take Jerusalem, his death in January 976 lifted the Byzantine Empire danger for the Fatimid caliphate: the Byzantines would never again try to advance far beyond their northern Syrian possessions around Antioch." .

lsd:Entity_33988 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Die Wende and Peaceful Revolution (German: Friedliche Revolution) was the process of sociopolitical change that led to the opening of East Germany's borders with the west, the end of the Socialist Unity Party of Germany (SED) in the East Germany (East Germany) and the transition to a parliamentary democracy, which enabled the German reunification in 1990. After the fall of the Berlin Wall, in demonstrations calling for German reunification, this morphed into \"Wir sind ein Volk\" (\"We are one people\")." .

lsd:Entity_34007 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th Independent Spirit Awards, honoring the best in independent filmmaking for 1989, were announced on 24 March 1990 at the Hollywood Roosevelt Hotel in Los Angeles." .

lsd:Entity_34050 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Memorial Cup occurred May 6–13 at the brand new Saskatchewan Place in Saskatoon, Saskatchewan. It was the 71st annual Memorial Cup competition and determined the major junior ice hockey champion of the Canadian Hockey League (CHL). In the first ever all_WHL final (in fact, an all_Saskatchewan final), an overflow, bi_partisan crowd of more than 11,000 saw Swift Current beat Saskatoon in overtime to win their first Memorial Cup. The Saskatoon Blades represented the Western Hockey League as the host team at the 1989 Memorial Cup. The Broncos swept Saskatoon in four games, ending their season, however, the club qualified for the 1989 Memorial Cup as the host team of the event." .

lsd:Entity_34062 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gumbinnen Operation, also known as the Goldap Operation (or Goldap_Gumbinnen Operation, Russian: Гумбиннен_Гольдапская наступательная операция), was a Soviet offensive on the Eastern European theatre of World War II late in 1944, in which forces of the 3rd Belorussian Front attempted to penetrate the borders of East Prussia." .

lsd:Entity_34084 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Superleague Greece was the 77th season of the highest association football league of Greece and the seventh under the name Super League. Olympiacos F.C. were the defending champions, having won their 39th Greek championship in the 2011–12 Superleague Greece." .

lsd:Entity_34152 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Vienna Offensive was launched by the Soviet 2nd and 3rd Ukrainian Fronts in order to invade Vienna, Austria, during World War II. Joseph Stalin reached an agreement with the Western Allies prior to 13 April 1945 concerning the relative postwar political influence of each party in much of Eastern and Central Europe; however, these agreements said virtually nothing about the fate of Austria, then officially considered to be merely the Ostmark area of Greater Nazi Germany after the Anschluss. After they took Sopron and Nagykanizsa, they crossed the border between Hungary and Austria. Having secured his right wing by 2nd Ukrainian Front, Tolbukhin was now ready to advance into Austria and take Vienna. On 02 April 1945, Vienna Radio denied that the Austrian capital had been declared an open city. Central Vienna was now cut off from the rest of Austria. Austrian politician Karl Renner astutely set up a Provisional Government in Vienna sometime in April with the tacit approval of the victorious Soviet forces, and declared Austria's secession from the Third Reich. This order of battle shows what remained \"on paper\" of the German armies that fought in Hungary and Austria." .

lsd:Entity_34173 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2000 Summer Olympics – men's 100 metre breaststroke event at the 2000 Summer Olympics took place on 16 September 2000–17 September at the Sydney Olympic Park Aquatic Centre in Sydney, Australia. Domenico Fioravanti made an Olympic milestone to become Italy's first ever gold medalist in swimming." .

lsd:Entity_34209 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Clausura 2018 Copa MX (officially the Clausura 2018 Copa Corona MX for sponsorship reasons) was the 79th staging of the Copa MX, the 51st staging in the professional era and is the twelfth tournament played since the 1996–97 edition. The tournament began on 09 January 2018 and ended on 2018. As winners, Necaxa earned a spot to face Monterrey (winners of Apertura 2017 Copa MX edition), in the 2018 Supercopa MX. The Liga MX teams in Pot 1 are the four best teams in the Apertura 2017 Copa MX classification table not participating in the 2018 CONCACAF Champions League as well as the defending champion. Pot 2 contains the next four best Liga MX clubs in the Apertura 2017 Copa MX classification table not participating in the 2018 CONCACAF Champions League. Pot 3 contains the next five best Liga MX clubs in the Apertura 2017 Copa MX classification table not participating in the 2018 CONCACAF Champions League." .

lsd:Entity_34218 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "anti_revisionism is a position within Marxism–Leninism which emerged in the 1950s in opposition to the reforms of Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev. Because different political trends trace the historical roots of revisionism to different eras and leaders, there is significant disagreement today as to what constitutes anti_revisionism. anti_revisionism is a position within Marxism–Leninism based on its interpretation by Stalin, who supported the dictatorship of the proletariat, drastic and fast_paced economic transformation in the short_term, and violent confrontation with capitalist powers." .

lsd:Entity_3423 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Linlithgow Bridge is a battle that took place on 04 September 1526 in the village of Linlithgow Bridge, outside the Scottish town of Linlithgow." .

lsd:Entity_34230 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Helsingborg was fought on 08 July 1362 between the Danish and Hanseatic fleets. As part of the ongoing trading and territorial disputes between the Hanseatic League, Sweden, and Denmark, the Hanseatic cities made an agreement with Sweden and Holstein to jointly attack Denmark, the agreed targets being Helsingborg and Copenhagen. As Wittenborg's fleet sailed through the narrow Øresund en route for Copenhagen he was persuaded to attack the town of Helsingborg and its fortified citadel." .

lsd:Entity_34293 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bandy World Championship 1973 was the eighth Bandy World Championships and was contested between four men's bandy playing nations. The championship was played in Moscow and Krasnogorsk (Soviet Union national bandy team) on 17 February 1973–24, 1973." .

lsd:Entity_3431 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 China International Guangzhou was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Guangzhou, People's Republic of China between 09 March 2015 and 15 March 2015." .

lsd:Entity_34321 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships were held in Augsburg, Germany under the auspices of International Canoe Federation for the record_tying third time. Augsburg hosted the championships previously in 1957 and 1985 when the city was part of West Germany, and matches the times hosted by Spittal, Austria (1963, 1965, 1977), Meran, Italy (1953, 1971, 1983), and Bourg St._Maurice, France (1969, 1987, 2002 ICF Canoe Slalom World Championships)." .

lsd:Entity_34335 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Itamar attack, also called the Itamar massacre, was an attack on a Jewish family in the Israeli settlement of Itamar in the West Bank that took place on 11 March 2011, in which five members of the same family were murdered in their beds. At midday on Friday, 11 March 2011, they decided that they would enter Itamar later that night, and carry out an attack armed with knives. In response to the Itamar attack, on 13 March, the Israeli cabinet approved the construction of 500 housing units in Gush Etzion, Ma'ale Adumim, Ariel and Modi'in Illit, areas of the West Bank that Israel intends to keep under any permanent accord with the Palestinians. An opinion poll conducted by the Harry S. Truman Research Institute for the Advancement of Peace and the Palestinian Center for Policy and Survey Research found, according to the groups, \"In light of the attack in Itamar, 59% among Israelis oppose and 33% support the government policy to relax the security measures in the West Bank such as the removal of road blocks.\" The groups interviewed 1,270 adults face_to_face in the West Bank, East Jerusalem, and Gaza Strip from 17 to 19 11 March 2011. Gary Rosenblatt, editor_in_chief of The Jewish Week, commented that reactions to the Itamar killings may have been more muted given that the victims were religious Israelis living in a small West Bank settlement." .

lsd:Entity_34341 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Davis Cup was the 105th edition of the Davis Cup, a tournament between national teams in men's tennis. Argentina Davis Cup team won their first Davis Cup title, after 4 runner_up finishes, defeating Croatia in the final. Federico Delbonis defeated Ivo Karlović in the final match to give Argentina Davis Cup team its first Davis Cup title, after a huge comeback from Juan Martín del Potro against Marin Čilić in the fourth match. Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below). Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below). Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below). Seeding: The seeding was based on the Davis Cup Rankings of 2016 (shown in parentheses below)." .

lsd:Entity_34350 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tuyuhun invasion of Gansu was a battle between the Tuyuhun Kingdom and the Tang Dynasty in 623. In 623, the Tuyuhun departed from their homeland in the pastures surrounding Qinghai Lake, known in Mongolian as Kokonur, and invaded Gansu." .

lsd:Entity_34378 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Eerste Divisie, known as Jupiler League for sponsorship reasons, was the sixty_second season of Eerste Divisie since its establishment in 1955. 2016–17 Eerste Divisie champions VVV_Venlo gained promotion to the 2017–18 Eredivisie, and was replaced by Go Ahead Eagles, who finished bottom in the 2016–17 2017–18 Eredivisie. Ten teams, two from the 2017–18 Eredivisie and eight from the Eerste Divisie, played for two spots in the 2018–19 2017–18 Eredivisie, the remaining eight teams playing in the 2018–19 Eerste Divisie." .

lsd:Entity_34382 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 All England Badminton Championships (officially known as the Yonex All England Badminton Championships 2007 for sponsorship reasons) was a badminton tournament which took place at Arena Birmingham in Birmingham, England, from 6 to 11 March 2007 and had a total purse of $200,000. The 2007 All England Badminton Championships was the third tournament of the 2007 BWF Super Series and also part of the All England Badminton Championships championships, which had been held since 1899. This international tournament was held at Arena Birmingham in Birmingham, England." .

lsd:Entity_34412 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wintour is Coming was a headlining concert tour by American rock band Fall Out Boy in support of their 2015 album, American Beauty/American Psycho." .

lsd:Entity_34422 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 German Open Badminton Championships was the first grand prix gold and grand prix tournament of the 2012 BWF Grand Prix Gold and Grand Prix. The tournament was held in innogy Sporthalle, Mulheim an der Ruhr, Germany from 28 February 2012 until 04 March 2012 and had a total purse of $125,000." .

lsd:Entity_34449 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1916 Irondale earthquake struck in the north–central region of the U.S. state of Alabama on 18 October 1916. Even an earthquake similar in size to the 1916 Irondale event today could damage thousands of buildings and cause up to $1 billion in damage. On February 4, 1886, the first recorded earthquake from Alabama hit the western part of the state, jolting people in Sumter and Marengo counties. Following minor events in 1917, 1927, 1931, and 1939, threatening activity resumed in the late 1950s, when, in 1957, an earthquake near Birmingham extended from northern Alabama to Georgia and Tennessee, cracking and displacing small objects. The Geological Survey of Alabama predicts a 90 percent chance of an earthquake of 6.0 or greater in the area by 2040. The shaking from such an earthquake would spread again into the northern segment of Alabama. The South Carolina seismic zone has produced earthquakes as powerful as 7.3 on the Richter scale, and a large earthquake originating from it could easily reach Alabama. The earthquake originated near the city of Irondale, 3 miles (5 km) north of Birmingham, striking just after 4 P.M. local time. Because the earthquake took place before Alabama had a seismograph network, its Richter scale magnitude of 5.1 is an estimate, but it is still considered the most powerful earthquake in state history, affecting seven other states. He claims that \"If there is an earthquake in one of the zone areas, North Alabama is certainly going to feel it." .

lsd:Entity_34468 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Gulf Coast campaign or the Spanish conquest of West Florida in the American Revolutionary War, was a series of military operations primarily directed by the governor of Spanish Louisiana, Bernardo de Gálvez against the British province of West Florida. Begun with operations against British positions on the Mississippi River shortly after Britain and Spain went to war in 1779, Gálvez completed the conquest of West Florida in 1781 with the successful siege of Pensacola. Spain officially entered the American Revolutionary War on 1779, with a formal declaration of war by King Charles III." .

lsd:Entity_34500 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Paris–Nice was the tenth edition of the Paris–Nice cycle race and was held from 25 March 1952 to 30 March 1952. The race started in Paris and finished in Nice." .

lsd:Entity_34521 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Triplemanía XIV was the fourteenth Triplemanía professional wrestling show promoted by AAA. The show took place on 18 June 2006 in Naucalpan, Mexico. The following year Peña and AAA held their first Triplemanía event, building it into an annual event that would become AAA's Super Bowl event, similar to the WWE's WrestleMania being the biggest show of the year. The Triplemanía XIV show featured seven professional wrestling matches with different wrestlers involved in pre_existing scripted feuds, plots and storylines." .

lsd:Entity_34540 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Miller Lite 250 was the fourth round of the 2002 CART season, held on 02 June 2002 at the Milwaukee Mile oval in West Allis, Wisconsin." .

lsd:Entity_34566 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Old Dominion 500 was a NASCAR Winston Cup Series race that took place on 29 September 1974, at Martinsville Speedway in Martinsville, Virginia. Today, the race is a part of the First Data 500 annual series of Martinsville races that occur in the autumn for the NASCAR Sprint Cup Series. Canadian Earl Ross (driving for Junior Johnson) defeated his United States of America opponent Buddy Baker by more than one lap, lapping the entire field in the process. Petty, Yarborough and Pearson managed to win 27 out of 30 races in 1974 and the only other drivers to win a race were Bobby Allison, who won twice, and Earl Ross." .

lsd:Entity_34624 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Live Bootleg! Tour was a 1978 United States concert tour by the rock group Aerosmith. The tour began on 09 August 1978 in Boston, Massachusetts and ended on 12 December 1978 in Wichita, Kansas." .

lsd:Entity_34634 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 10 January 2010 Battle of Beledweyne began on 10 January 2010 when Ahlu Sunna Waljama'a forces attacked and captured East Beledweyne which was being held by the Hizbul Islam insurgent group. It became the first battle in months were al_Shabaab and Hizbul Islam groups cooperated against the Transitional Federal Government. Fighting started on early 10 January 2010, as forces loyal to the Transitional Federal Government_aligned Ahlu Sunnah Waljama'a (which controls large parts of central Somalia) attacked the city of Beledweyne which was held by Islamist Insurgents loyal to Hizbul Islam. It was also reported that Transitional Federal Government forces led by Tawane Hure joined the battle to support Ahlu Sunnah Waljama'a and forces loyal to Harakat al_Shabaab Mujahedeen (Somalia's most powerful insurgent group), which had been at war with Hizbul Islam since September 2009 joined to back Hizbul Islam fighters for the first time in months." .

lsd:Entity_34644 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Revolution Technologies Pro Tennis Classic was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place in Indian Harbour Beach, United States, on 2016–08 May 2016." .

lsd:Entity_34717 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Quebec Conference, codenamed \"QUADRANT\", was a highly secret military conference held during World War II by the governments of the United Kingdom, Canada, and the United States. It took place in Quebec City on 17 August 1943–24, 1943, at both the Citadelle and the Château Frontenac. Couture found a leather portfolio with a gold inscription \"Churchill_Roosevelt, Quebec Conference, 1943.\"" .

lsd:Entity_34727 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2012 Faroe Islands Premier League was the seventieth season of top_tier football on the Faroe Islands. The season began on 24 March 2012 with a match between B36 Tórshavn and FC Suðuroy, and ended on 06 October 2012." .

lsd:Entity_34753 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Surfer is a integrated advertising campaign launched in 1999 by Diageo to promote Guinness_brand draught stout in the United Kingdom." .

lsd:Entity_34807 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ghost Stories Tour was an intimate concert tour by British band Coldplay, consisting of nine shows at a handful of low_key venues, during eight non_consecutive months in 2014, reaching North America, Europe, Asia and Oceania. The concerts were part of Coldplay's campaign in support of their sixth studio album Ghost Stories (2014). In 21 March 2014, Coldplay debuted the album Ghost Stories at an intimate gig in Los Angeles. The band performed at E_Werk in Cologne, Germany on 25 April 2014 and performed at the Beacon Theatre in New York City, New York on 5 May, prior to the release of Ghost Stories. On 20 October, Coldplay announced the release of a new concert film and live album, Ghost Stories Live 2014, which was released on 24 November." .

lsd:Entity_34848 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bundesvision Song Contest 2012 was the eighth edition of the annual Bundesvision Song Contest musical event. The contest was held on 2012 at the Max_Schmeling_Halle, Berlin, following Tim Bendzko's win in the 2011 contest in North Rhine_Westphalia with the song \"Wenn Worte meine Sprache wären\". This was the second time that the Max_Schmeling_Halle arena had hosted the contest after previously hosting in 2010, and Berlin's third time of hosting the contest, after previously hosting in 2007, and 2010." .

lsd:Entity_34895 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ha My Massacre was a massacre purportedly conducted by the Republic of Korea Marine Corps on 25 February 1968 of unarmed citizens in Hà My village, Điện Dương commune, Điện Bàn District, Quảng Nam in South Vietnam." .

lsd:Entity_34899 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950–51 FA Cup was the 70th season of the world's oldest association football cup competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup, commonly known as the FA Cup. Newcastle United F.C. won the competition for the fourth time, beating Blackpool 2–0 in the 1951 FA Cup Final at Wembley, London. The 1951 FA Cup Final took place on Saturday, 28 April 1951 at Wembley and ended in a victory for Newcastle United F.C. over Blackpool by 2–0, with both goals scored by Jackie Milburn." .

lsd:Entity_34921 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Anglo_Egyptian treaty of 1936 (officially, The Treaty of Alliance Between His Majesty, in Respect of the United Kingdom, and His Majesty, the King of Egypt) was a treaty signed between the United Kingdom and the Kingdom of Egypt. Under the terms of the treaty, the United Kingdom was required to withdraw all its troops from Egypt, except those necessary to protect the Suez Canal and its surroundings, numbering 10,000 troops plus auxiliary personnel. The treaty was to last for 20 years; it was negotiated in the Zaafarana palace, signed in London on 26 August 1936 and ratified on 22 December. The 1936 treaty did not resolve the question of Sudan, which, under the terms of the existing Anglo_Egyptian Condominium Agreement of 1899, stated that Sudan should be jointly governed by Egypt and United Kingdom, but with real power remaining in British hands." .

lsd:Entity_34934 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Ramsele witch trial, which took place in 1634, is one of the few known Swedish witch trials before the great witch mania of 1668–1676. In the year of 1634 a man and several women were put on trial in the city of Ramsele in Ångermanland in Norrland in Sweden." .

lsd:Entity_34942 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 27, adopted on 01 August 1947, called for the opposing Netherlands and Indonesia Republicans in the Indonesian National Revolution to lay down their arms and to allow a peaceful mediation of the conflict." .

lsd:Entity_34977 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 French Open – Mixed Doubles was held from 29 May until 1989 on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland Garros in Paris, France. Manon Bollegraf and Tom Nijssen won the title, defeating Horacio de la Peña and Arantxa Sánchez Vicario in the final." .

lsd:Entity_35009 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Lake Huleh in 26 June 1157, a Crusader army led by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem was ambushed and badly defeated by Nur ad_Din Zangi, the emir of Aleppo and Damascus. The Hula Valley is located in the northeast part of modern_day Israel. At the time of the battle, the area belonged to the Kingdom of Jerusalem." .

lsd:Entity_35017 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 FEI World Equestrian Games were held in Mill Spring, North Carolina, U.S. at the Tryon International Equestrian Center, from September 11 to 2018. This is the eighth edition of the FEI World Equestrian Games, which are held every four years and run by the International Federation for Equestrian Sports (FEI)." .

lsd:Entity_35023 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Portuguese Grand Prix (formally the XXIII Grande Premio de Portugal) was a Formula One motor race held on 25 September 1994 at the Circuito do Estoril. Which also left Gerhard Berger to take pole position for Ferrari ahead of Hill, David Coulthard in the other Williams followed by Hakkinen 4th in the leading McLaren_Peugeot, followed by Alesi who was 5th in the second Ferrari, Ukyo Katayama 6th for Tyrrell_Yamaha, as the top 10 was completed by Brundle 7th in the second McLaren_Peugeot, Barrichello 8th for Jordan ahead of Frentzen 9th for Sauber and Jos Verstappen 10th for Benetton. Soon afterwards, Jos Verstappen was able to pass Martin Brundle's McLaren for 5th and the order after the second round of pit stops had seen no change of the order as it was Damon Hill who made it 3 wins on the trot ahead of Williams teammate David Coulthard who was 2nd which also gave Williams the lead in the Constructors Championship with their 1_2 result ahead Mika Hakkinen who third for McLaren, Barrichello 4th for Jordan, Verstappen 5th for Benetton & Martin Brundle 6th in the second McLaren rounding out the top 6." .

lsd:Entity_35054 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A 2013 Norwegian parliamentary election was held in Norway on 8 09 September 2013 and 09 September 2013 to elect all 169 members of the unicameral Norwegian Parliament. The election was the fourth for incumbent Prime Minister of Norway Jens Stoltenberg, whose party was previously defeated in the 2001 2013 Norwegian parliamentary election, but who won both the 2005 2013 Norwegian parliamentary election and the 2009 Norwegian parliamentary election (though in the latter election, the opposition narrowly received more votes than the coalition) leading the red–green coalition." .

lsd:Entity_35080 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2019 Kentucky gubernatorial election took place on 05 November 2019, to elect the Governor of Kentucky and lieutenant Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky. The Democratic nominee, Kentucky Attorney General Andy Beshear, defeated Republican incumbent Matt Bevin by just over 5,000 votes, or 0.37%, making this the closest gubernatorial election in Kentucky since 1899 by total votes, and the closest ever by percentage. No Republican Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky has ever been elected to more than one term, even after the state's constitutional prohibition of governors serving consecutive terms was repealed in 1992. Bevin is only the second Republican Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky in the last 50 years. Andy Beshear is the first Governor of Kentucky of Kentucky to be a direct relative of a former Governor of Kentucky. According to the Kentucky state constitution, the swearing in of a Kentucky Governor of Kentucky must be held on the fifth Tuesday following the election (December 10). This process, which is enforced through the Goebel Election Law, has only been used once, during the 1899 Kentucky gubernatorial election. On November 11, U.S. Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell, a Kentucky Republican, announced that \"all indications are\" Beshear would be the next Governor of Kentucky. Beshear was sworn in as Governor of Kentucky on 2019." .

lsd:Entity_35118 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1895 United Kingdom general election was held from 13 July 1895 to 07 August 1895." .

lsd:Entity_35161 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 AMD at The Glen was a NASCAR Nextel Cup Series Go Bowling at The Glen that was held on 13 August 2006 at Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. Contested over 90 laps on the 2.45 mile (3.942 km) road course, it was the 22nd Go Bowling at The Glen of the 2006 NASCAR Nextel Cup Series. Kevin Harvick of Richard Childress Racing won the Go Bowling at The Glen. Watkins Glen International (nicknamed \"The Glen\") is an automobile Go Bowling at The Glen track located in Watkins Glen, New York at the southern tip of Seneca Lake. In 1968 the Go Bowling at The Glen was extended to six hours, becoming the 6 Hours of Watkins Glen. Kurt Busch had the pole position for this Go Bowling at The Glen, in Penske Racing's #2 Miller Lite_sponsored Dodge. Busch was sent to the rear of the field and the Go Bowling at The Glen then got controlled by Tony Stewart and Kevin Harvick who passed Tony during the final 3 laps to win the Go Bowling at The Glen." .

lsd:Entity_35166 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Eurocard German Open was a women's tennis event that was played from 06 May 2002 to 12 May 2002. It was played at the Rot_Weiss Tennis Club Berlin in the German capital of Berlin." .

lsd:Entity_35247 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Noordhorn, fought on 30 September 1581, was a pitched battle of the Dutch Revolt, fought between a Spanish army commanded by Colonel Francisco Verdugo – consisting of Walloon, German, Spanish, and Albanian soldiers – and a Dutch States rebel army under the Englishman John Norreys – comprising English, Scottish, Dutch, and Walloon troops – in the province of Friesland. On 30 September 1581 Verdugo forced Norreys to give battle using a strategy of attrition. In early September, after a failed raid on his camp, Verdugo was sure of Norreys' intentions of giving battle, and thus he advanced his army to near Norreys' camp near the village of Noordhorn." .

lsd:Entity_35251 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Danish Figure Skating Championships (Danish: Danmarks Mesterskaberne 2001) was held in Aalborg from 05 January 2001 to 7, 2001." .

lsd:Entity_35264 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Prairie III was a U.S. Marine Corps operation in Quảng Trị Province, South Vietnam that sought to eliminate People's Army of Vietnam (PAVN) forces south of the Vietnamese Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) that took place from 19 March to 19 April 1967. Operation Prairie III concluded on 19 April 1967, the Marines had lost 56 killed and claiming that the PAVN suffered 252 killed and 4 captured." .

lsd:Entity_35275 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Gwangju Open was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Gwangju, South Korea between 13 August 2018 and 19 August 2018." .

lsd:Entity_35287 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Dhala took place in 20 March 1965 during the Aden Emergency." .

lsd:Entity_35300 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Vítkov Hill was a part of the Hussite Wars. Vítkov Hill was located on the edge of the city of Prague and the battle occurred in a vineyard established by Sigismund's father, Charles IV. One of the most important points in the fortifications of Prague was Vítkov Hill. In honor of this battle, Vítkov Hill was renamed Žižkov after Jan Žižka." .

lsd:Entity_35306 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Helen Gourlay and Kerry Harris won in the final 6–0, 6–4 against Coleman and Krantzcke." .

lsd:Entity_35327 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Fei River, also known as \"Feishui\" (simplified Chinese: 淝水之战; traditional Chinese: 淝水之戰; pinyin: Féishŭi zhī zhàn) was a battle in AD 383, where Fu Jiān (Chinese: 苻堅) of the Di Former Qin Empire was decisively defeated by the outnumbered Jin (Han Chinese_led) army of Eastern Jin. (The location of the battle, the Fei River, no longer exists, but is believed to have flowed through modern Lu'an, Anhui, near the Huai River.) The aftermath of the battle included the Former Qin Empire falling into massive civil war and its eventual destruction, ensuring the survival of Eastern Jin and other Chinese regimes south of the Yangtze River (长江). In October 383, the Former Qin forces under Fu Rong captured the important Jin city of Shouyang (壽陽, in modern Lu'an, Anhui)." .

lsd:Entity_3533 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1959–60 Alpha Ethniki was the 24th season of the highest association football league of Greece and the first season of the nationwide league after the league replaced the Panhellenic Championship. The season began on 25 October 1959 and ended on 31 July 1960 with the play_off matches. The Hellenic Football Federation took the decision to reduce the number of teams to 16 and as a consequence a promotional play_off round was formed to decide the last two places of the championship." .

lsd:Entity_35354 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Ethias Trophy was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Mons, Belgium between 30 September 2013 and 06 October 2013." .

lsd:Entity_35378 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 5th Hum Awards ceremony, presented by PEL, powered by QMobile and brought by Kashmir Cooking Oil & Banaspati took place in Lahore on 29 April 2017 at 7:30pm PST." .

lsd:Entity_35385 a lsd:Context_narration ;
