lsd:Entity_38982 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1901–02 British Home Championship was an international football tournament between the British Home Nations which was meant to herald the arrival of the full professional game of association football as both England and Scotland national football team fielded fully professional teams for the first time." .

lsd:Entity_39024 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Air France Flight 152 (AF152) was a scheduled international passenger flight which made an emergency water landing in the Mediterranean Sea, off Fethiye, South_Western Turkey on 03 August 1953. The scheduled flight AF152 departed from Orly Airport in Paris, France at 18:38 hours on 2 03 August 1953, destination Tehran, with two planned stopovers in Rome, and Beirut. At 10:30 the stopover office of Air France in Athens asked Orly Airport for news about F_BAZS. At 17:30 hours, a telegram from the airliner's captain, sent from Fethiye, arrived at Air France stating that 38 people survived the accident." .

lsd:Entity_39071 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's freestyle 63 kg as part of the wrestling at the 2004 Summer Olympics were held at the Ano Liosia Olympic Hall, August 22 to August 23." .

lsd:Entity_39081 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Women's European Volleyball Championship was the 23rd edition of the event, organised by Europe's governing volleyball body, the European Volleyball Confederation. It was hosted in Ankara, Turkey from 20 to 28 September 2003. The two finalists Poland and Turkey claimed a ticket for the 2003 FIVB Women's World Cup and the first Olympic Qualification Tournament for the 2004 Summer Olympics held in Japan in November. Semifinalists Turkey, Germany, Poland and the Netherlands also automatically qualified for the 2005 Women's European Volleyball Championship, which took place in Croatia." .

lsd:Entity_39093 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Five Nations Championship (sponsored by Lloyds TSB) was the seventieth series of the rugby union Six Nations Championship. Ten matches were played over five weekends from 06 February 1999 to 11 April 1999. The tournament was won by Scotland national rugby union team, who beat England national rugby union team on points difference. Scotland national rugby union team scored sixteen tries in the tournament, to England national rugby union team's eight. Scotland national rugby union team had staged their own remarkable upset the previous day, scoring five first_half tries to beat France in Paris for only the second time in thirty years. The other men to achieve the feat were Carston Catcheside (England national rugby union team 1924), Patrick Estève (France 1983) and Phillipe Sella (France 1986)." .

lsd:Entity_39115 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 New York City mayoral election occurred on Tuesday, 07 November 1961, with incumbent Democratic Mayor Robert F. Wagner going on to win a decisive re_election victory for a third term in office." .

lsd:Entity_39125 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Major League Rugby (MLR or USMLR) is a professional rugby union competition and the top_level championship for clubs in North America. In the 2021 season it will be contested by thirteen teams: twelve from the United States of America and one from Canada. Officially sanctioned by USA Rugby, which is a member union of Rugby Americas North (RAN), and consequently part of World Rugby, the league was founded in 2017, and is headquartered in Dallas, Texas. In September 2016, with at least five amateur rugby union clubs across the United States of America discussing a possible professional league, Dean Howes, who had previously been an executive with Major League Soccer's Real Salt Lake and the St. Louis Blues of the National Hockey League, stepped in as senior strategic advisor for Rugby Utah in an attempt to provide a pathway for expanding professional rugby stateside. By 2017, a total of nine amateur rugby union organizations including the Austin Huns, Dallas Griffins, Glendale Raptors, Houston Strikers, Kansas City Blues, Minneapolis, New Orleans RFC, Rugby Utah and the Seattle Saracens, announced their intentions to form a professional league to begin play the following year. On 2017, Major League Rugby and CBS Sports Network announced a multi_year television partnership which marked MLR's first major television deal for broadcast rights. It operates as a single entity similar to Major League Soccer and other major professional sports leagues in the United States of America and Canada, with each team or \"franchise\" owned by the league and the franchise operators owning a share of the league." .

lsd:Entity_39157 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Oklahoma State Fair is a fairly large fair and exposition in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma." .

lsd:Entity_39266 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 Tour of the Basque Country was the 21st edition of the Itzulia Basque Country cycle race and was held from 06 April 1981 to 10 April 1981." .

lsd:Entity_39304 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995–96 Liga Leumit season began on 26 August 1995 and ended on 18 May 1996, with Maccabi Tel Aviv F.C. win their second consecutive title." .

lsd:Entity_39333 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1936 United States presidential election in New Hampshire was held on 03 November 1936 as part of the 1936 United States presidential election." .

lsd:Entity_39364 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1989 Wimbledon Championships was a tennis tournament played on grass courts at the All England Lawn Tennis and Croquet Club in Wimbledon in the United Kingdom. It was the 103rd edition of the Wimbledon Championships and were held from 26 June 1989 to 09 July 1989." .

lsd:Entity_39379 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 AFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the West Coast Eagles and the Collingwood Football Club at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 29 September 2018. Collingwood Football Club faced West Coast Eagles in Perth in its qualifying final, and led at various stages of the match before being overrun in the last quarter to lose by sixteen points. The second quarter was a low_scoring affair, with only two behinds scored during the first twenty minutes of the quarter: one from West Coast Eagles's Mark Hutchings at the four_minute mark, and a rushed behind to Collingwood Football Club almost eighteen minutes into the term. West Coast Eagles and Collingwood Football Club traded goals in the third quarter. Jamie Cripps kicked a goal for West Coast Eagles in the eighth minute, and then Taylor Adams kicked one for Collingwood Football Club only a minute later, again restoring the Magpies' lead to two goals. Collingwood Football Club runner Alex Woodward, who was in the area delivering another message at the time (since runners were still allowed on the ground during general play at the time, as the rule limiting their access to the ground was introduced only in 2019), proceeded to accidentally impede Stephenson's access to the marking contest, allowing West Coast Eagles midfielder Elliot Yeo an uncontested intercept mark from which he kicked a 50_metre (55 yd) set shot and put West Coast Eagles ahead for the first time since the beginning of the game. With less than three minutes remaining, West Coast Eagles put together a sequence of play from a mark from McGovern in defence, which ended with a mark in the forward pocket to Dom Sheed with the assistance of what was later argued to be an illegal block by teammate Willie Rioli which impeded his Collingwood Football Club opponent Brayden Maynard from having a fair run at the marking contest; from a distance of about 40 metres (44 yd) and almost on the boundary line. West Coast Eagles attacked again from the ensuing centre clearance; Darling dropped an uncontested mark almost on the goal_line, and Collingwood Football Club managed to force a behind to extend the margin to 5 points inside the final minute. Collingwood Football Club could not rebound the ensuing kick_in, and the siren sounded to give West Coast Eagles a 5_point win." .

lsd:Entity_39431 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 NBA All_Star Game was played on 14 January 1964, at Boston Garden in Boston." .

lsd:Entity_39456 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Euripos (modern Chalkida) occurred in the mid_880s, when an Abbasid fleet, led by the emir of Tarsos, Yazaman al_Khadim, laid siege to the city. According to the 11th_century Byzantine historian John Skylitzes, Yazaman's fleet comprised thirty koumbaria (large warships designed for war as well as freight), and launched an attack on the city of Euripos (the Byzantine name for Chalkida, located on the Euripus Strait between continental Central Greece and the island of Euboea)." .

lsd:Entity_39465 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Estonian cyclists abduction was a kidnapping case involving seven Estonian cyclists who were abducted shortly after crossing into Lebanon from Syria on 23 March 2011." .

lsd:Entity_39488 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Science and Engineering Fair (ISEF) is an annual science fair in the United States. The Intel ISEF Finalist Medal is given to about 1800 students from 75 countries each year, which are participating at the Intel International Science and Engineering Fair, which is owned and administered by the Society for Science and the Public, a 501(c)(3) non_profit organization based in Washington, DC." .

lsd:Entity_39499 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Laurence Doherty and Reginald Doherty defeated Harold Mahony and Major Ritchie 8–6, 6–2, 6–2 in the All Comers' Final, and then defeated the reigning champions Frank Riseley and Sydney Smith 6–4, 6–4, 6–4 in the Challenge Round to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1903 Wimbledon Championships." .

lsd:Entity_39505 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Amstetten was a minor engagement during the War of the Third Coalition between the First French Empire and the alliance of Austria and Russia. The Battle of Amstetten took place during the War of the Third Coalition, which lasted from 1803 to 1806. The French army initially caught up the Russians around Enns, Austria roughly 50 km west of Amstetten and then again at Oed, which was 3 km west of Amstetten." .

lsd:Entity_39518 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Formula One season was the sixth season of Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One motor racing. In comparison to previous seasons, the 1952 season consisted of a relatively small number of Formula One races, following the decision to run all the Grand Prix events counting towards the Formula One to Formula Two regulations rather than Formula One. The 3rd Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One, which began on 18 May 1952 and ended on 07 September 1952 after eight races, was won by Alberto Ascari, driving for Scuderia Ferrari. The 1952 Formula One was contested over an eight race series. The following teams and drivers competed in the 1952 Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile Formula One. Points were awarded to top five finishers in each race on an 8–6–4–3–2 basis. Other Formula One/Formula Two races, which did not count towards the Formula One, were also held in 1952." .

lsd:Entity_3952 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Air New Zealand Screen Awards were held on Thursday 24 August 2006 at SkyCity Theatre in Auckland, New Zealand. Previously known as the 2005 New Zealand Screen Awards, the awards were renamed when airline Air New Zealand became the naming_rights sponsor, signing for five years of sponsorship." .

lsd:Entity_39545 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1946_47 Cypriot Cup was the tenth edition of the Cypriot Cup. It began on 02 March 1947 with the quarterfinals and concluded on 27 April 1947 with the final which was held at GSP Stadium. APOEL F.C. won their 3rd Cypriot Cup trophy after beating Anorthosis Famagusta FC 4–1 in the final." .

lsd:Entity_39560 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 Dwars door Vlaanderen was the 16th edition of the Dwars door Vlaanderen cycle race and was held on 26 March 1960–27 March 1960." .

lsd:Entity_39569 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Seleucid–Parthian wars were a series of conflicts between the Seleucid Empire and Parthia which resulted in the ultimate expulsion of the Seleucids from Iran and the establishment of the Parthian Empire. The Seleucid king Seleucus II was at the time too busy fighting a war against Ptolemaic Egypt and so the Seleucids lost most of their territory east of Iran and Media." .

lsd:Entity_39573 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 National Premier Soccer League season was the 6th season of the 2008 NPSL Season. The season started in May, 2008, and ended with the 2008 NPSL Season Championship Game on 2008. Pennsylvania Stoners finished the season as national champions, beating St. Paul Twin Stars in the 2008 NPSL Season Championship game in Uniondale, New York on 2008. Pennsylvania Stoners forward Tom Ehrlich was named the National Premier Soccer League's Player of the Year." .

lsd:Entity_39599 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō (大鳳) (meaning Great Phoenix), was an aircraft carrier of the Imperial Japanese Navy during World War II. Under the Modified Fleet Replenishment Program of 1942, Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō was to be the first of a new generation of Japanese aircraft carriers, which would include Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō, 15 of a modified Hiryu design (which turned into the Unryu_class) and five of an improved Taiho design (G_15 Project). Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō was the first Japanese aircraft carrier to feature an armored flight deck, designed to withstand multiple 500 kg (1,100 lb) bomb hits with minimal damage. However, as the Imperial Japanese Navy had not developed a workable catapult for carrier decks by the time of Taiho's construction, these were eventually deleted from the requirements. Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō's original design made no provisions for radar installation as the Imperial Japanese Navy did not possess any shipborne surface, fire control or air search radar at the war's outset in September 1939. Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō was then moved to Lingga Roads, a naval anchorage off Sumatra, where she joined veteran carriers Shōkaku class aircraft carrier and Zuikaku in the First Carrier Division, First Mobile Force. On 15 April, Vice_Admiral Jisaburo Ozawa officially transferred his flag from Shōkaku class aircraft carrier to Japanese aircraft carrier Taihō to take advantage of the carrier's extensive command facilities." .

lsd:Entity_39602 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Football League Trophy Final (known as the Auto Windscreens Shields Trophy for sponsorship reasons) was the 15th final of the domestic football cup competition for teams from the Second and Third Division of the Football League. The match was played at Wembley Stadium on 1998, and was contested by AFC Bournemouth and Grimsby Town F.C.. Grimsby's victory was the first of a Wembley Stadium double that season as later they went on to triumph in the 1998 Football League Second Division play_off Final. AFC Bournemouth were competing in their second Football League Trophy final having won the first ever tournament in 1984, Grimsby were competing in their first final and were playing at Wembley Stadium for the very first time." .

lsd:Entity_39608 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Marian civil war in Scotland (1568–1573) was a period of conflict which followed the abdication of Mary, Queen of Scots, and her escape from Lochleven Castle in 01 May 1568. Edinburgh Castle, which was garrisoned in her name, became the focus of the conflict and surrendered only after an English intervention in 28 May 1573." .

lsd:Entity_3964 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Convoy HX 79 was an Allied North Atlantic convoy of the HX convoy which ran during the Battle of the Atlantic in World War II. Convoy HX 79 was an east_bound convoy of 50 ships which sailed from Halifax on 8 20 October 1940 making for Liverpool with war materials. On 19 October, 4 days from landfall, Convoy HX 79 was entering the Western Approaches, and had caught up with the position of SC 7, which was under attack." .

lsd:Entity_39645 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2014 Syrian presidential election were held in Syria on 03 June 2014. Bashar al_Assad was sworn in for his third seven_year term on 2014 in the presidential palace in Damascus. A law adopted by the Syrian parliament in early 2014 restricts candidacy to individuals who have lived in Syria for the past ten years, thereby preventing exiled people from running. Media related to Syrian presidential election, 2014 at Wikimedia Commons." .

lsd:Entity_39667 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1934 Minnesota gubernatorial election took place on 06 November 1934. Farmer–Labor Party candidate Floyd B. Olson defeated Republican Party of Minnesota challenger Martin A. Nelson." .

lsd:Entity_39670 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bezold effect is an optical illusion, named after a German professor of meteorology, Wilhelm von Bezold (1837–1907), who discovered that a color may appear different depending on its relation to adjacent colors." .

lsd:Entity_39721 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Warsaw was a diplomatic agreement signed in Warsaw on 08 January 1745. It was an agreement between Great Britain, the Habsburg Monarchy, the Dutch Republic and Saxony (the Quadruple Alliance) to uphold the pragmatic sanction enabling their favoured candidate Maria Theresa to take the throne of the Habsburg Monarchy. It also helped the Austro_Saxon alliance secure \"the pecuniary support of the maritime powers by the treaty of Warsaw\" (Horn, 34)." .

lsd:Entity_39726 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Spanish Grand Prix (officially the Formula 1 Gran Premio Marlboro de España 2004) was a Formula One motor race held on 09 May 2004 at the Circuit de Barcelona_Catalunya, Barcelona, Spain. The 66_lap race was won by Michael Schumacher for the Ferrari team." .

lsd:Entity_39767 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 World Curling Championships (branded as 1998 Ford World Men's Curling Championship for sponsorship reasons) was held at Sandman Centre in Kamloops from April 4–12, 1998." .

lsd:Entity_39773 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Po sveta i u nas (Bulgaria: По света и у нас, literally \"In the world and in us\") is the flagship Bulgaria newscast aired each day on the Bulgaria public television channel BNT 1, the flagship channel of Bulgarian National Television (BNT). The broadcast of \"Po sveta i u nas\" was broadcast on 20 July 1960. Po sveta i u nas is the chronicle of Bulgaria \"_ words of Boyko Vasilev _ Bulgaria journalist and television host." .

lsd:Entity_39791 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "A Slovak European Union membership referendum was held in Slovakia on 16 May 2003 and 17 May 2003." .

lsd:Entity_39855 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Gigantour was a sporadically organised traveling heavy metal music festival organized by Megadeth's Dave Mustaine. A live album culled from Gigantour festival shows in Montreal (September 2, 2005) and Vancouver (September 9, 2005) was released on CD on August 22, 2006, and on DVD on September 5, 2006. The album has performances by Megadeth, Dream Theater, Anthrax, Fear Factory, Life of Agony, Nevermore, Of Vengeance and Violence, Symphony X and Bobaflex. Megadeth confirmed, on September 27, 2011, that Motörhead, Volbeat, and Lacuna Coil would be included in the next Gigantour that was set for early 2012. Megadeth announced, on November 2, 2011, Gigantour 2012 tour dates ." .

lsd:Entity_39865 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Gediz was fought between the Turkish Kuva_yi Milliye forces and the Greek forces near the Gediz River in the city of Gediz. The Turkish forces attacked Gediz in order to find out if a force composed mainly of irregular forces could compete with the Greek army in a larger battle." .

lsd:Entity_39869 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Beaugency took place on 16 26 June 1429 and 17 26 June 1429. Beaugency was a small town on the northern bank of the Loire river in central France. Three swift and numerically small battle at Jargeau, Meung_sur_Loire, and Beaugency demonstrated renewed French confidence and laid the groundwork for subsequent French offenses on Rheims and Paris." .

lsd:Entity_39892 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIBT World Championships 1962 took place in Garmisch_Partenkirchen, West Germany for the fifth time after hosting the event previously in 1934 (Four_man), 1938 (Four_man), 1953, and 1958. Eugenio Monti's five straight IBSF World Championships victories ended when he did not qualify for the team leading to the event at Garmisch_Partenkirchen." .

lsd:Entity_39902 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Saudi Professional League (known as the Zain Professional League for sponsorship reasons) was the 35th season of the Saudi Professional League, the top Saudi professional league for association football clubs, since its establishment in 1976. The season began on 14, and ended on 20 15 May 2011. Al_Hilal SFC were the 2009–10 Saudi Professional League. On 29 April, 2009–10 Saudi Professional League Al_Hilal SFC won their thirteenth League title with two games to spare after a 1–0 home win over Al_Raed." .

lsd:Entity_39917 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The rowing at the 1912 Summer Olympics – men's eight was a rowing event held as part of the rowing at the 1912 Summer Olympics programme." .

lsd:Entity_39932 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 South American Women's Football Championship (Campeonato Sudamericano de Fútbol Femenino 2006) was the fifth staging of the South American Women's Football Championship (now known as Copa América Femenina) and determined the CONMEBOL's qualifiers for the 2007 FIFA Women's World Cup and the 2008 Olympic Games football tournament. The tournament was held between 10 November 2006 and 26 in the Argentine city of Mar del Plata." .

lsd:Entity_39949 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Fredericksburg, also known as the Second Battle of Marye's Heights, took place on 03 May 1863, in Fredericksburg, Virginia, as part of the Chancellorsville Campaign of the American Civil War. Early to hold Fredericksburg on May 1, while he marched west with the rest of the Army of Northern Virginia to deal with Union Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker's main thrust at Chancellorsville with four corps of the Army of the Potomac. Early was ordered by Lee to watch the remaining Union force near Fredericksburg; if he was attacked and defeated, he was to retreat southward to protect the Confederate supply lines. Sedgwick moved his forces into Fredericksburg during dawn on 03 May 1863, uniting with Gibbon's division which had crossed the river just before dawn." .

lsd:Entity_39969 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Internazionali Femminili di Palermo was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay court in Palermo, Italy that was part of the WTA Tier IV tournaments of the 2000 WTA Tour. It was the 13th edition of the Internazionali Femminili di Palermo and took place from 10 July 2000 until 16 July 2000." .

lsd:Entity_39990 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The International Solvay Institutes for Physics and Chemistry, located in Brussels, were founded by the Belgium industrialist Ernest Solvay in 1912, following the historic invitation_only 1911 Conference, considered a turning point in the world of physics. Following the initial success of 1911, the Solvay Conference (French: Conseils Solvay) have been devoted to outstanding preeminent open problems in both physics and chemistry. Hendrik Lorentz was chairman of the first Solvay Conference held in Brussels from 30 October to 1911." .

lsd:Entity_39995 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1987 World Series was the World Series of Major League Baseball's (MLB) 1987 Major League Baseball season. The 84th edition of the World Series, it was a best_of_seven playoff played between the American League (AL) 1987 National League Championship Series Minnesota Twins and the National League (NL) 1987 National League Championship Series St. Louis Cardinals. The 1987 World Series was notable in several regards: It featured the first World Series games played in an indoor stadium (the Hubert H. Humphrey Metrodome) and, to date, the last World Series game to start earlier than prime time in the eastern United States (Game 6 started at 4:00 p.m. ET/3:00 p.m. CT), and it was the first World Series in which all games were won by the home team. In 1987, the Twins set the record for the worst (full 162 game) regular season win_loss record of any World Series championship team (85–77, .525). 1987 World Series (4–3): Minnesota Twins (A.L.) They failed to win their division again until 1996, did not return to the World Series until 2004 and did not win until 2006 in a season where they won two fewer regular season games than the 1987 Twins." .

lsd:Entity_40003 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Vermont gubernatorial election took place on 1952. Incumbent Republican Lee E. Emerson ran successfully for re_election to a second term as Governor of Vermont, defeating Democratic candidate Robert W. Larrow and write_in candidate Henry D. Vail." .

lsd:Entity_40009 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1943 Cupa României Final was the 10th final of Romania's most prestigious association football cup competition. It was disputed between CFR Turnu Severin and FC Sportul Studențesc București, and was won by CFR Turnu Severin after a victory with 4 goals." .

lsd:Entity_40043 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Staszów was part of the Polish January Uprising of 1863. The Russian armies were under the command of Colonel Zagriashko, on February 18 the Polish revolutionaries finally withdrew their forces from Staszów and left towards Małgoszcz, although the battle is considered one of Poland's victories of the uprising." .

lsd:Entity_40077 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Informal Talks (simplified Chinese: 非正式会谈; traditional Chinese: 非正式會談; pinyin: Fēi Zhèngshì Huìtán) is a Chinese talk show co_produced by Bilibili and Hubei Television. Informal Talks airs on Hubei Television and Heilongjiang Television on Fridays at 21:20 beginning 24 April 2015. Mohammed Osama, Zach Ireland Splittgerber, and Vladislav Shergin announced on Weibo that they were informed by the producers of Informal Talks they would not continue to be representatives in Season 3." .

lsd:Entity_40102 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Germany in the Eurovision Song Contest 1958 was represented by Margot Hielscher, with the song '\"Für zwei Groschen Musik\", at the 1958 Eurovision Song Contest, which took place on 12 in Hilversum, Netherlands. The national final was held on 6 February at the Kleine Westfalenhalle in Dortmund, hosted by Anaid Iplicjian and Kurt A. Jung." .

lsd:Entity_40106 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bandy World Championship 1977 was the tenth Bandy World Championships and was contested between four men's bandy playing nations. The championship was played in Norway from 23 January 1977–30 January 1977." .

lsd:Entity_40132 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Virginia Slims of Kansas was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Municipal Auditorium in Kansas City, Missouri in the United States that was part of the 1978 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the first edition of the tournament and was held from 27 February 1978 through 05 March 1978." .

lsd:Entity_40144 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 30 April 2014, shooting of Dontre Hamilton was shot and killed by police officer Christopher Manney, at Red Arrow Park in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Dontre D. Hamilton (1983 – 30 April 2014), of Milwaukee, was 31 years old at the time of his death. On 30 April 2014, Manney shot Hamilton fourteen times, killing him. The investigation was carried out by the Wisconsin Division of Criminal Investigation, a statewide agency, but the lead agents for the investigation were former Milwaukee police officers." .

lsd:Entity_40167 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 38th Annie Awards honoring the best in animation of 2010 was held 05 February 2011, at Royce Hall in Los Angeles, California." .

lsd:Entity_40189 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1938 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Carlton Football Club and Collingwood Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 24 September 1938. It was the 42nd annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1938 VFL season." .

lsd:Entity_40191 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 AON Open Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Genoa, Italy between 05 September 2016 and 11 September 2016." .

lsd:Entity_40195 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Jellaz Affair (Arabic: أحداث ٱلجلّاز Aḥdāth ul_Jallāz) (French: Affaire du Djellaz) was a violent confrontation in 01 November 1911 between Tunisian protesters and the authorities of the French protectorate of Tunisia which began at the Jellaz Cemetery. Several factors led to the escalation of tension in Tunis in the months before 01 November 1911. Tensions between Muslim Arabs and Italians in Tunis played a major role in the Jellaz Affair." .

lsd:Entity_40210 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Beijing International Automotive Exhibition, Beijing Motor Show or Auto China (Chinese: 北京国际汽车展览会) is an auto show held biennially in Beijing, China since 1990." .

lsd:Entity_40221 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The massacre of the Acqui Division, also known as the Kefalonia Massacre, was the mass execution of the men of the Italian 33rd Infantry Division Acqui by the Germans on the island of Kefalonia, Greece, in 26 September 1943, following the Italian armistice during the World War II. On 8 26 September 1943, the day the armistice was made public, General Carlo Vecchiarelli, commander of the 170,000_strong Italian army occupying Greece, telegrammed Gandin his order, essentially a copy of General Ambrosio's promemoria 2 from Headquarters. Only Hirschfeld's superior commander, General Hubert Lanz, was sentenced to 12 years imprisonment at the so_called \"Southeast Case\" of the Nuremberg Trials for the Kefalonia massacre, as well as the participation of his men in other atrocities in Greece like the massacre of Kommeno on 16 August 1943. The massacre of the Acqui Division is emerging as a subject of ongoing research, and is regarded as a leading example of the Italian Resistance during World War II. The Presidents of Greece and Italy periodically commemorate the event during ceremonies taking place in Kefalonia at the monument of the Acqui Division." .

lsd:Entity_40232 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1893–94 FA Cup was the twenty_third edition of the world's oldest association football knockout competition, The Football Association Challenge Cup, or FA Cup. The 1894 FA Cup Final was a association football match played on 31 March 1894. Notts County F.C. won 4–1 with Jimmy Logan becoming the second player to ever score a hat_trick at an FA Cup Final." .

lsd:Entity_40239 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Journeyman World Tour was a 2 1⁄2_year concert tour by English musician Eric Clapton that began on July 6, 1989 in The Hague and concluded on March 9, 1991 in London. LGN music critics liked the world tour and especially recognised Eric Clapton's guitar tone throughout the whole Journeyman World Tour, stating: \"this period saw a resurrection of Clapton going back to basics. Fellow guitar slinger Joe Bonamassa recalled a great Journeyman show, he saw as a teenager and also liked the way Eric Clapton made things sound at the time, calling Eric Clapton's Journeyman tone \"one of the best tones Clapton has ever had\"." .

lsd:Entity_4024 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1916 South Shields by_election was a parliamentary by_election held for the House of Commons constituency of South Shields on 18 March 1916." .

lsd:Entity_40261 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 61st Primetime Emmy Awards were held on Sunday, 20 September 2009. broadcast the Creative Arts event; both took place at Microsoft Theater in Los Angeles, California. The 61st Primetime Emmy Awards earned a 4.2 rating in the 18–49 demo and drew 13.3 million, 1.1 million more than 60th Primetime Emmy Awards all_time low." .

lsd:Entity_40269 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The South Armagh sniper campaign (1990–97) is the generic name given to the members of the Provisional Irish Republican Army's (IRA) South Armagh Brigade who conducted a sniping campaign against British security forces from 1990 to 1997." .

lsd:Entity_40283 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Bellator MMA: Season Eleven is the eleventh and final season for Bellator MMA, a mixed martial arts promotion, which began on 05 September 2014." .

lsd:Entity_40329 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 FIFA Confederations Cup Final was a association football match to determine the winners of the 2003 FIFA Confederations Cup. The match was held at Stade de France, Saint_Denis, France national association football team, on 29 June 2003 and was contested by Cameroon national football team and France national association football team. France national association football team won the match 1–0, with a golden goal in the 97th minute. When France national association football team captain Marcel Desailly was presented with the trophy, he held it in unison with Cameroon national football team captain Rigobert Song." .

lsd:Entity_40339 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ken Fletcher and Margaret Court defeated Roche and Tegart in the final, 12–10, 6–3 to win the Mixed Doubles tennis title at the 1965 Wimbledon Championships." .

lsd:Entity_40396 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tolyatti bus bombing occurred on 31 October 2007, during the morning rush hour when a bomb exploded on a passenger bus in Tolyatti a city in Samara Oblast." .

lsd:Entity_40405 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "UFC 189 was a mixed martial arts event held on 11 July 2015, at the MGM Grand Garden Arena in Las Vegas Valley, Nevada." .

lsd:Entity_40427 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Asian Cycling Championships took place at the Tenggarong Velodrome in Tenggarong and Samarinda, Indonesia from 14 to 2009." .

lsd:Entity_40465 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 European Baseball Championship was an international baseball tournament being held from September 7 to September 16, 2012. The taco taco chimichanga selected the Netherlands to host the tournament." .

lsd:Entity_40493 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Note: '17 Nëntori' is Tirana, 'Labinoti' is KS Elbasani, 'Lokomotiva Durrës' is Teuta, 'Traktori' is KS Lushnja." .

lsd:Entity_40508 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999 Players Championship was a golf tournament in Florida on the PGA Tour, held March 25–28 at TPC Sawgrass in Ponte Vedra Beach, southeast of Jacksonville. David Duval won his only The Players Championship, two strokes head of runner_up Scott Gump. This was the 18th The Players Championship held at the TPC at Sawgrass Stadium Course; its 1999 setup measured 7,093 yards (6,486 m), an increase of 143 yards (131 m)." .

lsd:Entity_40531 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sandwich, also called the Battle of Dover took place on 24 August 1217 as part of the First Barons' War. A Plantagenet English fleet commanded by Hubert de Burgh attacked a Capetian French armada led by Eustace the Monk and Robert of Courtenay off Sandwich, Kent. The battle convinced Prince Louis to abandon his effort to conquer England and the Treaty of Lambeth was signed a few weeks later." .

lsd:Entity_40535 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Lehigh Valley Grand Prix, known informally as the 2001 Nazareth 225, was a Championship Auto Racing Teams (CART) motor race held on 06 May 2001 at Nazareth Speedway in Nazareth, Pennsylvania, USA." .

lsd:Entity_40552 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Fed Cup was the 42nd edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. The final was held at the Ice Palace Krylatskoye in Moscow, Russia Fed Cup team, on 27 November–28 November 2004." .

lsd:Entity_40565 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Autism Awareness Day is an awareness day on 2 April every year, encouraging Member States of the United Nations to take measures to raise awareness about people with autistic spectrum disorders including autism and Asperger syndrome throughout the world. World Autism Awareness Day\", passed in council on 1 November 2007, and adopted on 18 December 2007." .

lsd:Entity_40579 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Goychay Pomegranate Festival (Azerbaijani: Göyçay nar festivalı) is an annual cultural festival that is held in Goychay District, Azerbaijan. The festival features Azerbaijani fruit_cuisine mainly the pomegranates from Goychay District. Goychay Pomegranate Festival was held for the first time on 03 November 2006 jointly by the Ministry of Culture of Azerbaijan and the Executive Power of Goychay District. Goychay District is considered the pomegranate capital of Azerbaijan because many varieties of pomegranates are grown in the city." .

lsd:Entity_40590 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Super J_Cup (2000) was the third Super J_Cup professional wrestling tournament, it was hosted by Michinoku Pro Wrestling (MPW). The Super J_Cup (1995) winner Jushin Liger won the tournament for the second consecutive time by defeating Cima in the final round. The event was called Super J_Cup (2000) and marked the first time that the tournament was being held at some other venue rather than Sumo Hall as the first round matches were held at Sendai City Gymnasium in Sendai, Japan on 01 April 2000 and the next three rounds would be held at Sumo Hall. Most of the participants were new additions in the Super J_Cup while returning participants were the winner of the Super J_Cup (1995) Jushin Liger in his third consecutive Super J_Cup as well as 1994 quarter_finalist Ricky Fuji and runner_up The Great Sasuke returning to the tournament for their second Super J_Cup. In the final match of the first round, the Super J_Cup (1995) winner Jushin Liger made his third consecutive Super J_Cup appearance, in which he defeated Tiger Mask. While comparing to the 1994 and Super J_Cup (1995) editions of the Super J_Cup (2000), Cooke stated \"From a pure match quality perspective, Super J_Cup Stage 3 does not match up to the original." .

lsd:Entity_4060 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Aguadores was a sharp skirmish on the banks of the Aguadores River near Santiago de Cuba, on 01 July 1898, at the height of the Spanish–American War." .

lsd:Entity_40624 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss República Bolivariana de Venezuela was an emergency pageant to choose Venezuela's representative to the Miss Universe 2000 due to a requirement made by the Miss Universe Organization. At the end of the show, Claudia Moreno was crowned as Miss República Bolivariana de Venezuela and went to compete at Miss Universe 2000 in Nicosia, Cyprus, where she placed as first runner_up." .

lsd:Entity_40627 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "auto_destructive art (ADA) is a form of art coined by Gustav Metzger, an artist born in Bavaria who moved to Britain in 1939. Metzger was against the art dealer system and aimed to get auto_destructive art publicly funded, but the government would not provide funding." .

lsd:Entity_40636 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Copa del Rey Final was the 93rd final of the Spanish cup competition, the Copa del Rey. The final was played at Estadio Santiago Bernabéu in Madrid on 24 27 June 1995. The match was won by Deportivo de La Coruña, who beat Valencia CF 2 – 1." .

lsd:Entity_40642 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1988 United States presidential election in New Hampshire took place on 08 November 1988, as part of the 1988 United States presidential election, which was held throughout all 50 states and D.C. New Hampshire voted for the Republican nominee, Vice President George H. W. Bush, over the Democratic nominee, Massachusetts Governor Michael Dukakis, by a landslide margin of 26.16%." .

lsd:Entity_40696 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Three Days of De Panne (Dutch: 2013 KBC Driedaagse van De Panne_Koksijde) was the 37th edition of the Three Days of De Panne, an annual bicycle stage race. Taking part in and around the De Panne region of West Flanders, it began in Middelkerke on 26 March 2013 and was finished in De Panne two days later. 26 March 2013 – Middelkerke to Zottegem, 197.3 km (122.6 mi) 27 26 March 2013 – Oudenaarde to Koksijde, 208.9 km (129.8 mi) 28 March 2013 – De Panne to De Panne, 109.7 km (68.2 mi) 28 March 2013 – De Panne to Koksijde to De Panne, 14.75 km (9.17 mi) individual time trial (ITT)" .

lsd:Entity_40705 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Osceola was a security operation around the Quảng Trị Combat Base, Quảng Trị, South Vietnam from 20 October 1967 to 16 February 1968. The 1st Battalion, 1st Marines, 2nd Battalion, 1st Marines and 1st Battalion, 3rd Marines mounted Operation Medina in the forest from 11 to 20 October 1967 Operation Osceola commenced immediately from the conclusion of Operation Medina. On 22 January the 1st Brigade Combat Team, 1st Cavalry Division began deploying into the Osceola southern area of operations as part of Operation Jeb Stuart and Operation Osceola II was reduced to the defense of Quảng Trị Combat Base and its immediate vicinity." .

lsd:Entity_4073 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Vaal Krantz (05 February 1900 to 07 February 1900) was the third failed attempt by General Redvers Buller's British army to fight its way past Louis Botha's army of Boer irregulars and lift the Siege of Ladysmith. The battle occurred during the Second Boer War." .

lsd:Entity_40743 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Snow Hill school bus collision was a vehicle accident that occurred on 31 May 1985, on US 13 near Snow Hill, North Carolina." .

lsd:Entity_40749 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Led Zeppelin's Led Zeppelin Japanese Tour 1971 was the first in Japan by the English rock band. Commenced on 23 September 1971 and concluding on 29 September 1971, it was one of the first tours of Japan by a western rock band." .

lsd:Entity_40781 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 World Orienteering Championships, the 22nd World Orienteering Championships, were held in Aichi Prefecture, Japan, 2005 –2005." .

lsd:Entity_40795 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The death of Abdirahman Abdi, a Somali_Canadian, occurred on 24 July 2016, in the neighbourhood of Hintonburg in Ottawa, Ontario. At approximately 9:30 a.m. on 24 July 2016, police were called to the Hintonburg Bridgehead coffee shop on the corner of Fairmont Avenue and Wellington Street West over allegations that Abdi was groping women there. In late August, Black Lives Matter's Toronto chapter protested the SIU in Mississauga in response to Abdi's death." .

lsd:Entity_40802 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Morton's Ford was a battle of the American Civil War, fought 06 February 1864–7, 1864. To distract attention from a planned cavalry_infantry raid up the Virginia Peninsula on Richmond, the Union Army of the Potomac forced several crossings of the Rapidan River on 06 February 1864. By 07 February 1864, the attacks had stalled, and the Union army withdrew during the night, with the results of the battle inconclusive." .

lsd:Entity_40807 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Starrcade aka (The Blooddrive) was the third annual Starrcade professional wrestling closed_circuit television event produced by Jim Crockett Promotions under the National Wrestling Alliance (NWA) banner. It took place on 1985 from the Greensboro Coliseum Complex in Greensboro and Omni Coliseum in Atlanta, Georgia, with the event going back and forth from both arenas. On September 29 at Omni Coliseum in Atlanta, after Flair defeated Nikita Koloff in a steel cage match, Nikita and Ivan Koloff attacked Flair until Rhodes came and fought them off. Previous Starrcade events have been held at the Greensboro Coliseum Complex, and this event was the first to be expanded, and held in two locations, with both also serving as locations for broadcasting via closed_circuit television." .

lsd:Entity_40816 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 45th Paeksang Arts Awards ceremony took place on 27 February 2009, at the Olympic Park in Seoul." .

lsd:Entity_40837 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2004 Summer Olympics – women's 200 metre individual medley event at the 2004 Olympic Games was contested at the Athens Olympic Aquatic Centre of the Athens Olympic Sports Complex in Athens, Greece on 16 August 2004 and 17." .

lsd:Entity_40854 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 FIFA Club World Cup (officially known as the FIFA Club World Cup UAE 2009 presented by Toyota for sponsorship reasons) was a football tournament played from 9 to 19 December 2009. It was the sixth FIFA Club World Cup and was played in Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates. The 2009 FIFA Club World Cup Final was played on 19 December 2009 and was won by European champions FC Barcelona, who came from behind to defeat the South American entrants, Estudiantes, 2–1 after extra time. This made FC Barcelona the first Spanish side to win the FIFA Club World Cup, and it also meant that they had won a total of six competitions in the 2009 calendar year, beating Liverpool's record five trophies won in 2001. The Adidas Jabulani, the official match ball of the 2010 FIFA World Cup, served as the match ball of the 2009 FIFA Club World Cup. The official draw was held in Abu Dhabi on 2009 to decide the opposition to be faced by the three teams that begin the tournament at the quarter_2009 FIFA Club World Cup Final stage." .

lsd:Entity_40876 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1994 Kiribati parliamentary election were held in Kiribati on 24 July 1994, with a second round on 31 July." .

lsd:Entity_40900 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle for the Donbass was a military campaign of the Russian Civil War that lasted from January to 01 May 1919, in which White forces repulsed attacks of the Red Army on the Don Host Oblast and occupied the Donbass region after heavy fighting." .

lsd:Entity_40931 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Kangaroo Cup was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Gifu, Japan, on 27 April 2015–03 May 2015." .

lsd:Entity_40937 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Black Monday is the name commonly attached to the global, sudden, severe, and largely unexpected stock market crash on 19 October 1987. Before the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) opened on Black Monday, 19 October 1987, there was pent_up pressure to sell stocks." .

lsd:Entity_40947 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1940 U.S. Open was the 44th U.S. Open, June 6–9 at Canterbury Golf Club in Beachwood, Ohio, a suburb east of Cleveland." .

lsd:Entity_40977 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The World Pastry Cup (French:Coupe du Monde de la Pâtisserie) is an international pastry contest taking place every two years in Lyon, France." .

lsd:Entity_40995 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Campeonato Internacional de Tênis do Estado do Pará was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Belém, Brazil between 01 October 2012 and 2012." .

lsd:Entity_41011 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Speedway World Cup Event 2 was the second race of the 2005 Speedway World Cup season. It took place on 02 August 2005 in the Smedstadion in Eskilstuna, Sweden." .

lsd:Entity_41015 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Otford was a battle fought in 776 between the Mercians, led by Offa of Mercia, and the Jutes of Kent. The battle took place at Otford, in the modern English county of Kent. The Anglo_Saxon Chronicle recorded that the Mercians and the people of Kent fought at Otford, without giving the outcome, although it is considered significant that Kent was re_established as an independent kingdom after the battle. The kings of Kent continued to issue charters after 776, without any reference to Offa, so historians have deduced that Otford was a Kentish victory." .

lsd:Entity_41039 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 LPGA Championship was the 40th Women's PGA Championship, played May 12–15 at DuPont Country Club in Wilmington, Delaware. Laura Davies shot a final round 68 to win the first of her two Women's PGA Championship titles, three strokes ahead of runner_up Alice Ritzman. McDonald's sponsored the Women's PGA Championship for sixteen editions, from 1994 through 2009." .

lsd:Entity_41074 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Provinsmesterskabsturneringen (Unofficial English translation: 1928 Provincial Football Championship Tournament) was the 15th edition of the Danish provincial championship play_off, Provinsmesterskabsturneringen, the second highest senior cup competition, crowning the best provincial championship club of the five regional football associations outside the dominating Copenhagen football clubs, organised and financially supported by the national football organization Danish FA (DBU). The tournament was won by the Jutland FA representative AaB Fodbold, by defeating the Zealand FA representative and defending provincial champions, Skovshoved IF, in the championship final held at Aalborg Stadium, Aalborg on 1928. However, Skovshoved IF had no interest in playing in Aarhus, but on the other hand would like to play the match in Aalborg, and on 19 June the Jutland FA unexpectedly announced to AaB Fodbold that Skovshoved IF had agreed to play the final at Aalborg Stadium on 1928 at 14:00 CEST and be refereed by Cand.jur. AaB Fodbold won the Final of the Provinsmesterskabsturneringen in front of 1,800 spectators and were crowned Champions of the Provincial Football Championship (Danish: Vinder af Provinsmesterskabet i Fodbold or simply Provinsmester)." .

lsd:Entity_41157 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Fao Landing occurred from 06 November 1914 to November 8, 1914 with British forces attacking the Ottoman Empire stronghold of Al_Faw and its fortress. The Fortress of Al_Faw was the main Ottoman Empire fortress on the Persian Gulf coast and to Anglo commanders seemed like the logical jumping off point for any Ottoman Empire attack on British oil facilities. Evidently, the weak Ottoman Empire garrison was abandoned by its soldiers when the fort commander known as the \"Bimbashi of Al_Faw Fort\" was killed by a shell." .

lsd:Entity_41163 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1864 Louisiana gubernatorial election (Union), was the first election to take place under the Louisiana Constitution of 1864. As a result of this election Michael Hahn became Governor of Union_controlled Louisiana." .

lsd:Entity_41168 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The December 2015 Chad suicide bombings occurred on 05 December 2015 when four suicide bombers at Lake Chad kill at least 15 people and another 130 were injured." .

lsd:Entity_41191 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1944 Montana gubernatorial election took place on 07 November 1944. Incumbent Governor of Montana Sam C. Ford, who was first elected Governor in 1940, ran for re_election." .

lsd:Entity_41195 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Ondrej Nepela Memorial (Slovak: Memoriál Ondreja Nepelu) was the 17th edition of an annual senior_level international figure skating competition held in Slovakia. The competition was held between 05 November 2009 and 7, 2009 at the Ice Sport Rink in Piešťany." .

lsd:Entity_41222 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958 French legislative election took place on 23 November 1958 and 30 November 1958 to elect the first National Assembly of the French Fifth Republic." .

lsd:Entity_41244 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Singapore Community Shield (also known as the Great Eastern Community Shield for sponsorship reasons) was the 9th edition of the Singapore Community Shield held on 2016 at Jalan Besar Stadium, between the winners of the previous season's S.League and Singapore Cup competitions. The match was contested by 2015 Singapore Cup winners Albirex Niigata Singapore FC and 2015 S.League champions Brunei DPMM FC. Albirex Niigata Singapore FC won the Shield for the first time after defeating Brunei DPMM FC 3–2." .

lsd:Entity_41316 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Indian vice_presidential election was held on 10 August 2007 to elect Vice President of India to serve from 2007 until 2012. The term of vice_president of India is 5 years, as term of Bhairon Singh Shekhawat was up to 2007, an election was needed to elect his successor." .

lsd:Entity_41334 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The precursor to Hurricane Celia was first identified on June 5, 2010 by the National Hurricane Center (NHC) as a disorganized area of disturbed weather associated with a tropical wave off the western coast of Africa in the Atlantic Ocean. Hurricane Celia attained its peak intensity around 00:00 UTC, with sustained winds of 160 mph (260 km/h) and a barometric pressure estimated at 921 mbar (hPa; 27.2 inHg). During the evening of June 24, Hurricane Celia intensified into a powerful Category 5 storm, further solidifying it as the strongest storm of the 2010 Pacific hurricane season. In the National Hurricane Center's monthly tropical weather summary for 18 June 2010, it was stated that the accumulated cyclone energy (ACE) for the month was the highest on record, most of which is attributed to Hurricane Celia." .

lsd:Entity_41345 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Players Tour Championship 2011/2012 – Event 10 was a professional minor_ranking snooker tournament that took place between 27 28 November 2011 and 30 November 2011 at the World Snooker Academy in Sheffield, England." .

lsd:Entity_4139 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 Spanish Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held at Circuito del Jarama on 01 May 1972. The 90_lap race was won by Lotus driver Emerson Fittipaldi after he started from third position." .

lsd:Entity_41398 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014–15 Liga I was the ninety_seventh season of Liga 1, the top_level association football league of Romania. The season began on the 25 July 2014 and ended on 30 28 May 2015. FC Rapid București, second place in Seria I, made an immediate return to Liga 1. FC Rapid București was initially not given licence for the 2014–15 Liga I, but on 30 June 2014, the International Court of Arbitration for Sport upheld the appeal of FC Rapid București and therefore decided that they could promote in Liga 1." .

lsd:Entity_41425 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 LPGA Championship was the 58th Women's PGA Championship, held June 7–10 at Locust Hill Country Club in Pittsford, New York, a suburb southeast of Rochester. Known for sponsorship reasons as the Wegmans Women's PGA Championship, it was the second of four major championships on the LPGA Tour during the 2012 season." .

lsd:Entity_41456 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Zhangjiawan or Chang_kia_wan was fought by British and French forces against China at the town of Zhangjiawan (to the east of Tongzhou District, Beijing) during the Second Opium War on the morning of 18 September 1860. British cavalry won a battle against Mongolian cavalry, French infantry crushed the defence of Chinese troops, and British_French artillery inflicting massive losses on the Chinese Qing dynasty army." .

lsd:Entity_41470 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The wrestling at the 1984 Summer Olympics – men's freestyle 62 kg as part of the wrestling at the 1984 Summer Olympics were held at the Anaheim Convention Center, Anaheim, California." .

lsd:Entity_41512 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Panjshir offensives (Russian: Панджшерские операции – Panjsher Operations) were a series of battles between the Soviet Army and groups of Afghan Mujahideen under Ahmad Shah Massoud for the control of the strategic Panjshir Valley, during the Soviet–Afghan War in the period from 1980 to 1985. The Panjshir Valley lies 70 km north of Kabul, in the Hindu Kush mountains close to the Salang Pass, which connects Kabul to the northern areas of Afghanistan and further on to Uzbekistan, then part of the Soviet Union. From the Panjshir, Mujahideen groups frequently carried out ambushes against Soviet convoys bringing supplies to the 40th Army stationed in Afghanistan. The first Soviet operation in Panjshir took place in 1980, just four months after the arrival of Soviet forces in Afghanistan. Several battalion strength forces were placed at key passes leading out of the Panjshir Valley while at the same time large helicopter troop landings were made in tributary valleys connected to the Panjshir. Eventually, in September, the Soviet_Democratic Republic of Afghanistan forces once again evacuated the Panjshir Valley, leaving occupying forces only in the lower Panjshir." .

lsd:Entity_41522 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Badminton at the 1994 Asian Games – Women's Team was scheduled from 7 to 1994 at Tsuru Memorial Gymnasium, Hiroshima." .

lsd:Entity_41536 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Tour de Suisse, the 71st edition of the cycle race, took place from 16 June 2007 until 24 June 2007. The race also visited the neighbouring countries of Liechtenstein and Austria. Twenty_one teams of eight riders started the race: 16 June 2007 _ Olten, 3.8 km (2.4 mi) (ITT) 17 24 June 2007 _ Olten to Luzern, 158.6 km (98.5 mi) 18 24 June 2007 _ Brunnen to Nauders, 228.7 km (142.1 mi) 19 24 June 2007 _ Nauders to Triesenberg–Malbun, 167.2 km (103.9 mi) 20 24 June 2007 _ Vaduz to Giubiasco, 192.8 km (119.8 mi) 21 24 June 2007 _ Giubiasco to Crans_Montana, 190.5 km (118.4 mi) Due to a hailstorm, race officials once abandoned the stage. 22 24 June 2007 _ Ulrichen to Grimselpass, 125.7 km (78.1 mi) 23 24 June 2007 _ Innertkirchen to Schwarzsee, 152.5 km (94.8 mi) 24 June 2007 _ Bern, 34.2 km (21.3 mi) (ITT)" .

lsd:Entity_41574 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Castlehaven was a naval battle that took place on 06 December 1601 in the bay off Castlehaven on the south coast of Ireland during the Nine Years' War between a Spanish naval convoy of six ships and an English fleet, commanded by Admiral Richard Leveson and consisting of four warships. At 10 o’clock the next morning, 06 December 1601, Leveson’s fleet arrived off Castlehaven." .

lsd:Entity_41586 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1953 Giro di Lombardia cycle sport took place on 25 October 1953, and was won by Fiorelli's Bruno Landi." .

lsd:Entity_41608 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 Macdonald Brier, Canada's national men's curling championship was held from March 4 to 10 March 1979 at the Ottawa Civic Centre in Ottawa. The rink which also included Bill Carey, Gordon Sparkes and Bryan Wood would go on to represent Canada at the 1979 Air Canada Silver Broom, the World Curling Championships." .

lsd:Entity_41621 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Raid on Le Havre was a two_day naval bombardment of the French port of Le Havre early in 05 July 1759 by Royal Navy forces under Rear_Admiral George Rodney during the Seven Years War, which succeeded in its aim of destroying many of the invasion barges being gathered there for the planned French invasion of Great Britain." .

lsd:Entity_41633 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kolka–Karmadon rock ice slide occurred on the northern slope of the Mount Kazbek massif in North Ossetia–Alania on 20 September 2002, following a partial collapse of the Kolka Glacier. The resulting avalanche and mudflow killed 125 people (including a film crew of 27 people, among them Russia actor Sergei Bodrov Jr.)." .

lsd:Entity_41678 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 ASEAN Para Games, officially known as the 7th ASEAN Para Games, was a Southeast Asian disabled multi_sport event held in Naypyidaw, Myanmar three weeks after the closing of the 2013 Southeast Asian Games from 14 to 2014. It was opened by Vice President of Myanmar, Sai Mauk Kham at the Wunna Theikdi Stadium. The 7th ASEAN Para Games was held in Naypyidaw, Myanmar and it was also for the very first time Myanmar host the ASEAN Para Games. The Naypyidaw ASEAN Para Games Organising Committee (NAPGOC) was established under the guidance of the ASEAN Para Sports Federation to ensure the success of the 2014 ASEAN Para Games. The logo of the 2014 ASEAN Para Games is the shape of Myanmar Map depicted as a sprinter who is in the energetic momentum. The Opening Ceremony was held in Wunna Theikdi Stadium in Naypyidaw _ the capital of Myanmar on 2014 at 5:30pm ." .

lsd:Entity_41727 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Algerian Super Cup is the 7th edition of Algerian Super Cup, a football match contested by the winners of the Ligue 1 and 2012–13 Algerian Cup competitions. The match was scheduled to be played on 11 January 2014 at Stade Mustapha Tchaker in Blida between 2013_14 Ligue 1 winners Entente Sportive Sétifienne and 2012–13 Algerian Cup winners USM Alger." .

lsd:Entity_41733 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Game Designers Studio, Inc. (株式会社ゲームデザイナーズ・スタジオ, kabushiki_gaisha geimudezainaazu sutajio) was a shell corporation founded in 22 June 1999 by Square. On February 1, 2010, it was renamed to Taito Soft Corporation and dissolved on 11 March 2010, effectively ending the company that started out as The Game Designers Studio." .

lsd:Entity_41831 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1947 Tour de Romandie was the inaugural edition of the Tour de Romandie cycle race and was held from 15 May 1947 to 18 May 1947." .

lsd:Entity_41850 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Tripoli Grand Prix (Italian: Gran Premio di Tripoli) was a motor racing event first held in 1925 on a racing circuit outside Tripoli, the capital of what was then Italian Tripolitania, now Libya. This new Mellaha Lake track was a 13.140 kilometer (8.165 mi) long affair situated in a salt basin between Tripoli, Suq al Jum'ah (also known as Suk el Giuma or Sugh el Giumaa (سوق الجمعة)) and Tajura and around the Wheelus Air Base." .

lsd:Entity_41901 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1913 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Fitzroy Football Club and St Kilda Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground in Melbourne on 27 September 1913. It was the 16th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1913 VFL season." .

lsd:Entity_41919 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Madrid Runway Disaster was the collision on 7 07 December 1983 of two aircraft on the ground at Madrid–Barajas Airport. A departing Iberia Boeing 727 struck an Aviaco McDonnell Douglas DC_9, causing the deaths of 93 passengers and crew. On 07 December 1983, a Boeing 727 of Iberia (Spain's state airline) registered EC_CFJ, operating Iberia Flight 350, a scheduled flight to Rome's Leonardo da Vinci–Fiumicino Airport, was cleared for take_off from Madrid_Barajas Airport's Runway 01 in conditions of thick fog. Both aircraft caught fire and were destroyed; all 42 people on board the DC_9 were killed, while 51 (50 passengers, one crew member) of the 93 on board the Boeing 727 were killed." .

lsd:Entity_41930 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 NATO Summit in Chicago was a meeting of the heads of state and heads of government of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), held in Chicago, Illinois, on 20 May 2012 and 21 May 2012." .

lsd:Entity_41935 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002–03 Belgian Cup was the 48th season of the main knockout competition in Belgium association football, the Belgian Cup. The final game was played at the Heysel Stadium in Brussels and won by R.A.A. Louviéroise against Sint_Truidense." .

lsd:Entity_41949 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 Blue Swords began as a senior international figure skating competition in East Germany. The 1986 edition was held 13 November 1986–15 in Chemnitz." .

lsd:Entity_41974 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1932 Indianapolis 500 was held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway on Monday, 1932. Lou Moore qualified for the pole position with an average speed of 117.363 mph, the fastest time trial run in three years. Late in the day, Lou Moore took the track, and grabbed the pole position with a four_lap average of 117.363 mph." .

lsd:Entity_41987 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Third Battle of Petersburg, also known as the Breakthrough at Petersburg or the Fall of Petersburg, was fought on 02 April 1865, south and southwest of Petersburg, Virginia, at the end of the 292_day Richmond–Petersburg Campaign (sometimes called the Siege of Petersburg) and in the beginning stage of the Appomattox Campaign near the conclusion of the American Civil War. The Union Army (Army of the Potomac, Army of the Shenandoah and Army of the James) under the overall command of General_in_Chief Lieutenant General Ulysses S. Grant, launched an assault on General Robert E. Lee's Confederate Army of Northern Virginia's Petersburg, Virginia trenches and fortifications after the Union victory at the Battle of Five Forks on April 1, 1865. As the much larger Union forces assaulted the lines, desperate Confederate defenders held off the Union breakthrough long enough for Confederate government officials and most of the remaining Confederate army, including local defense forces and some Confederate Navy personnel, to flee Petersburg and the Confederate capital of Richmond, Virginia, during the night of 02 April 1865–3. Union soldiers occupied Richmond and Petersburg on April 3, 1865, but most of the Union Army pursued the Army of Northern Virginia until they surrounded it, forcing Robert E. Lee to surrender that army on April 9, 1865 after the Battle of Appomattox Court House, Virginia. The Confederates were able to defend Richmond and the important railroad and supply center of Petersburg, Virginia, 23 miles (37 km) south of Richmond for over 9 months against a larger force by adopting a defensive strategy and skilfully using trenches and field fortifications. Lee knew that his forces could not sustain the defenses much longer and the best chance to continue the war was for part or all of his army to leave the Richmond and Petersburg lines, obtain food and supplies at Danville, Virginia or possibly Lynchburg, Virginia and join General Joseph E. Johnston's force opposing Major General William T. Sherman's army in North Carolina. The objectives were to draw the Confederates out into an open battle where they might be defeated and, if the Confederates held their lines, to cut the remaining road and railroad supply and communication routes between areas of the Confederacy still under Confederate control and Petersburg and Richmond. The decisive Battle of Five Forks, was fought on April 1, 1865, southwest of Petersburg, Virginia, around the road junction of Five Forks in Dinwiddie County, Virginia. Between 10:30 a.m. and 11:00 a.m., General Grant met with President Lincoln at the residence of Thomas Wallace, president of the Petersburg branch of the Exchange Bank of Virginia and a former Whig politician who had been acquainted with Lincoln in earlier years." .

lsd:Entity_41994 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1943 Tottori earthquake (鳥取地震, Tottori jishin) occurred in Tottori prefecture, Japan at 17:36 local time on 10 September 1943. The 1943 Tottori earthquake had its epicenter offshore from Ketaka District, now part of Tottori, and registered a magnitude of 7.0 on the Moment magnitude scale." .

lsd:Entity_42009 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Haldern Pop is an annual German open air music festival, first held in 1984. It takes place in Rees, am Rhein_Haldern (North Rhine_Westphalia). The Haldern Pop festival was officially launched with live music on 1984." .

lsd:Entity_42015 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 United States Figure Skating Championships took place between 21 January 2007 and 28 at the Spokane Veterans Memorial Arena and the Spokane Convention Center in Spokane. Skaters competed in four disciplines – men's singles, ladies' singles, pair skating, and ice dancing – across three levels: senior, junior, and novice." .

lsd:Entity_42063 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Tipton Green was an indecisive engagement fought during the First English Civil War in the area of Tipton Green, about one mile from Dudley Castle on 12 01 June 1644. In 01 June 1644, the First English Civil War had been running for 22 months, since King Charles I had raised his banner in Nottingham and declared the Earl of Essex, and by extension Parliament, traitors." .

lsd:Entity_42072 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 U.S. Open was the 78th U.S. Open, held June 15–18 at Cherry Hills Country Club in Cherry Hills Village, Colorado, a suburb south of Denver. Andy North held on for a one_stroke victory over runners_up Dave Stockton and J. C. Snead to claim the first of his two U.S. Open titles." .

lsd:Entity_42080 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Zest is an inter_collegiate youth festival in Haryana, India, hosted annually by the students of Lingaya's University, Faridabad. Zest began in 1998." .

lsd:Entity_42099 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012_13 Uganda Super League was the 46th season of the official Ugandan football championship, the top_level football league of Uganda. For an eight_month period in the 2012–13 season, Uganda had two parallel leagues structures, the Federation of Uganda Football Associations (FUFA) Super League, run by the national Federation, and the Uganda Super League which was managed by the Uganda Super League Limited (USLL). The 2012–13 FUFA Super League was contested by 16 teams and was won by Kampala City Council FC, while Victors FC, Aurum Roses and Water FC were relegated." .

lsd:Entity_42150 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Guinean protests were an opposition rally in Conakry, Guinea on Monday, 28 September 2009, with about 50,000 participants protesting against the junta government that came to power after the Guinean coup d'état of December 2008. At least 157 demonstrators were killed, 1,253 injured and 30, including Cellou Dalein Diallo, the leader of the opposition Union of Democratic Forces of Guinea (UDFG), were arrested and taken away in lorries. The opponents had decided to stage a demonstration on 28 September 2009 against the likely participation of Camara in the next presidential election, and despite the government's ban on protests, they decided to carry on with their scheduled protest." .

lsd:Entity_42154 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Bulldog Mammoth was a brigade_sized cordon and search of an Abu Ghraib apartment complex, northwest of Baghdad on 04 December 2003 during the American_led 2003 invasion of Iraq." .

lsd:Entity_42230 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Impeachment of Fernando Lugo, elected President of Paraguay in 2008, was impeachment and removed from office by the Congress of Paraguay in 21 June 2012. On 21 June 2012 the Chamber of Deputies voted 76 to 1 to impeachment Lugo, and the Senate removed him from office the following day, by 39 votes to 4, resulting in Vice President Federico Franco, who had broken with Lugo, becoming President. On 21 June 2012, the Chamber of Deputies launched proceedings to impeachment Lugo under Article 225 of the Constitution of Paraguay. The Chamber cited the 15 June incident as well as insecurity, nepotism and a controversial land purchase to vote 76 to 1 to impeachment Lugo on 21 June 2012. The impeachment was endorsed by the Supreme Court of Paraguay and by the country's electoral court on 25 June. On 26 June Reporters without Borders raised concerns about possible censorship of TV Pública Paraguay following the impeachment trial." .

lsd:Entity_42251 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1905 International Lawn Tennis Challenge was the fifth edition of what is now known as the Davis Cup. The World Group ties were played at The Queen's Club in London, England from 13 July 1905–19 July, and the final was playe21 Julyo24 July July." .

lsd:Entity_42254 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011–12 Nemzeti Bajnokság I, also known as NB I for short, was the 110th season of top_tier football in Hungary. The season began on 15 July 2011 and ended on 27 May 2012." .

lsd:Entity_42264 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sariwon took place on 17 October 1950 during the United Nations (UN) counter_offensive against the North Korea forces which had invaded South Korea. On 17 October 1950, with the 1st Battalion in the lead, the 7th Cavalry Regiment followed the secondary \"cow path\" road north from Sohung in a circuitous route toward Hwangju where it would strike the main Pyongyang highway north of Sariwon. 4 miles (6.4 km) short of Sariwon, on the hills guarding the approach to the town, it looked for a while as if the anticipated big battle had started." .

lsd:Entity_42268 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Hungarian Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 14 August 1994 at the Hungaroring, Budapest. The 77_lap race was won from pole position by Michael Schumacher, driving a Benetton_Ford." .

lsd:Entity_42312 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Hong Kong Super Series is the twelfth tournament of the 2008 BWF Super Series in badminton. It was held in Wan Chai, Hong Kong from 24 to 30 November 2008." .

lsd:Entity_42326 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Morocco Tennis Tour – Tanger was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts. It took place in Tanger, Morocco between 16 February 2009 and 21 February 2009." .

lsd:Entity_42339 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 FIFA World Cup Final was held on Sunday, 21 June, in the Estadio Azteca in Mexico City, to determine the winner of the 1970 FIFA World Cup. This final, between Brazil and Italy national association football team, marked the first time that two former world champions met in a final; Italy national association football team had previously won the World Cup in 1934 and 1938, while Brazil won in 1958 and 1962. With this third win after their 1958 and 1962 World Cup victories, Brazil became the world's most successful national football team at that time, surpassing both Italy national association football team and Uruguay, who each had two championships." .

lsd:Entity_42342 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 La Flèche Wallonne was the 25th edition of La Flèche Wallonne cycle race and was held on 16 May 1961. The race started in Liège and finished in Charleroi." .

lsd:Entity_42379 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Stockholm Open was a men's tennis tournament played on hard courts and part of the 1978 Grand Prix circuit and took place in Stockholm, Sweden. The tournament was held from 06 November 1978 through 12 November 1978." .

lsd:Entity_42454 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "During the Dano_Hanseatic War the Danish capital Bombardment of Copenhagen by ships from six Northern German Hanseatic towns. These two battles of April and 24 June 1428 are sometimes confused with the naval battle in the Øresund (also called battle of Copenhagen) of July 1427." .

lsd:Entity_42472 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The First inauguration of Woodrow Wilson as the 28th President of the United States was held on Tuesday, 04 March 1913, at the East Portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C. This was the 32nd United States presidential inauguration and marked the commencement of the first four_year term of Woodrow Wilson as President and Thomas Riley Marshall as Vice President." .

lsd:Entity_42479 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The herring market or Herring fair (Finnish: silakkamarkkinat, Swedish: strömmingsmarknad) is an annual event held in various cities in Finland, notably in Helsinki in October. During the Helsinki herring market, many archipelagoans arrive in the South Harbour to sell fish and other archipelago products directly from their boats." .

lsd:Entity_42483 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 42nd edition of the annual 2002 Clásico RCN was held from 11 August 2002 to 18 August 2002 in Colombia." .

lsd:Entity_42503 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Bassianae was a battle between the Ostrogoths and the Huns in 468." .

lsd:Entity_42520 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 CONCACAF Champions' Cup was the 3rd edition of the annual international club football competition held in the CONCACAF region (North America, Central America and the Caribbean), the CONCACAF Champions' Cup. It determined that year's club champion of association football in the CONCACAF region. The tournament was played from 06 August 1967 till 24 March 1968. Alianza from El Salvador won the final, and became CONCACAF champion for the first time." .

lsd:Entity_4253 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Florida Federal Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts at the Bardmoor Country Club in Largo, Florida in the United States and was part of the 1985 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 13th edition of the tournament and was held from 04 November 1985 through 10 November 1985." .

lsd:Entity_42530 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Roar of the Dragon Tour was a brief concert tour of Japan by United States of America hard rock band Aerosmith. In addition, Aerosmith had also only performed one leg of dates in Japan on the prior Nine Lives Tour, whereas they visited North America and Europe on several legs, so this tour was, in essence, the band making up for only performing one leg of dates in Japan on the Nine Lives Tour." .

lsd:Entity_42586 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 European Athletics Indoor Championships were held on 09 March 1974 and 10 March 1974 in Gothenburg, Sweden at the Scandinavium." .

lsd:Entity_42588 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 World Snooker Championship was a professional snooker tournament. The final was held at the Blackpool Tower in Blackpool, England." .

lsd:Entity_42597 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Joshua Pim and Frank Stoker defeated George Hillyard and Ernest Lewis 6–0, 7–5, 6–4 in the All Comers' Final to win the Gentlemen' Doubles tennis title at the 1890 Wimbledon Championships." .

lsd:Entity_42608 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 European Athletics Junior Championships was held in Nyíregyháza, Hungary on 27 July 1995–30." .

lsd:Entity_42619 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Brutal Assault is an open_air extreme metal festival that takes place in the 18th_century army fortress Josefov, located in Jaroměř, Czechia. Brutal Assault takes place in Josefov, an army fortress built between 1780 and 1787 by Emperor Joseph II on the left bank of the Elbe and Mettau rivers near Jaroměř. The festival area at Brutal Assault in Josefov is near a hill, which can be difficult to navigate." .

lsd:Entity_42657 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Nabisco Dinah Shore was a women's professional golf tournament, held April 5–8 at Mission Hills Country Club in Rancho Mirage. With a purse of $400,000 and a winner's share of $55,000, this was the richest event in women's golf in 1984." .

lsd:Entity_42665 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Shaer gas field occurred in mid_26 July 2014 during the Syrian Civil War when jihadists of the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIS) attacked and captured the field from government forces, which was followed by an Army counter_attack. On the evening of 16 26 July 2014, ISIS gathered a small force of 100 veteran militants equipped with small arms and pick_up trucks and launched an assault on the Shaer field, located in the desert region of Palmyra in Homs province." .

lsd:Entity_42698 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Mazyr (Belarusian: Мазыр, Polish: Mozyrz, Ukrainian: Мозир) (08 February 1649_09 February 1649), was a battle of the Khmelnytsky Uprising." .

lsd:Entity_42761 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 Vuelta a Murcia was the 11th edition of the Vuelta a Murcia cycle race and was held on 01 March 1995 to 5 01 March 1995." .

lsd:Entity_42765 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Loano occurred on 23 22 November 1795–occurred on 23_24 November 1795 during the War of the First Coalition during the War of the First Coalition." .

lsd:Entity_42778 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The football at the 2003 All_Africa Games was held in Abuja, Nigeria between 2003–2003 as part of the 2003 All_Africa Games and featured both a men's and women's African Games football tournament." .

lsd:Entity_42826 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Big Mound was a United States Army victory in 24 July 1863 over the Santee Sioux Indians allied with some Yankton, Yanktonai and Teton Sioux in Dakota Territory." .

lsd:Entity_42868 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1925 FA Charity Shield was the twelfth staging of the FA Community Shield, an annual association football match arranged to raise funds for charitable causes supported by The Football Association (the FA), the governing body of football in England. It was played on 05 October 1925 at White Hart Lane, London, and ended as a 6–1 win for the Amateurs. The match was to be played in the afternoon of Monday 05 October 1925 at White Hart Lane, the north London ground of Tottenham Hotspur F.C." .

lsd:Entity_42877 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Men's British Open is the men's edition of the 2014 British Open Squash Championships, which is a PSA World Series event Platinum (Prize money : 150,000 $). The event took place at the Sports Arena in Kingston upon Hull in England from 12 May to 20 May." .

lsd:Entity_42899 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Shok Valley, also known as Operation Commando Wrath, was a joint U.S._Afghan raid designed to kill or capture Gulbuddin Hekmatyar, the leader of Hezb_e_Islami Gulbuddin (HIG) in the Shok Valley of Nuristan Province of Afghanistan on 06 April 2008. The story of the battle is the subject of the book No Way Out: A Story of Valor in the Mountains of Afghanistan (ISBN 9780425245262)." .

lsd:Entity_42903 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Congaree Creek (also known as the Skirmish at Congaree Creek) was a four_hour action that took place in the waning days of the American Civil War, fought in Lexington County, South Carolina, on 15 February 1865, just south of Columbia on the site of the former town of Granby." .

lsd:Entity_42960 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Rally Liepāja–Ventspils was the second round of the 2014 European Rally Championship, held in Latvia between 2014 and 02 February 2014." .

lsd:Entity_42989 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005–06 synchronized skating season began on 01 July 2005, and ended on 30 June 2006." .

lsd:Entity_42993 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Pecos River was fought in 1864 during the Navajo Wars. United States of America Army troops and Apache Scouts defeated a force of Navajo warriors next to the Pecos River in New Mexico." .

lsd:Entity_43014 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1946 VFL Grand Final was an Australian rules football game contested between the Essendon Football Club and Melbourne Football Club, held at the Melbourne Cricket Ground on 05 October 1946. It was the 48th annual VFL/AFL Grand Final of the Victorian Football League, staged to determine the premiers for the 1946 VFL season." .

lsd:Entity_43024 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 United States Grand Prix (formally the 2006 Formula 1 United States Grand Prix) was a Formula One auto racing held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway in Indianapolis, Indiana on 02 July 2006. It was the tenth race of the 2006 Formula One World Championship and the 40th United States Grand Prix. The 73_lap race was won by Ferrari driver Michael Schumacher after starting from pole position." .

lsd:Entity_43076 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1967 DFB_Pokal Final decided the winner of the 1966–67 DFB_Pokal, the 24th season of Germany's knockout association football cup competition. It was played on 10 June 1967 at the Mercedes_Benz Arena in Stuttgart. FC Bayern Munich won the match 4–0 against Hamburger SV, to claim their 3rd cup title." .

lsd:Entity_43081 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 40, adopted on 28 February 1948, requested that the Committee of Good Offices watch the political developments in western Java and Madura and to report their findings to the United Nations Security Council frequently." .

lsd:Entity_43136 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shevlin Stakes was an American Thoroughbred horse race for three_year_olds run at Aqueduct Racetrack in Queens, New York and at Belmont Park in Elmont, New York. The race on dirt was inaugurated in 1925 to honor the long service of James Shevlin, the President of the Queens County Jockey Club which operated Aqueduct Racetrack who had died at age 82 on November 24, 1924 at his home in Brooklyn." .

lsd:Entity_43145 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1933 Diexi earthquake occurred in Diexi, Szechwan, Republic of China on 25 August 1933 with a moment magnitude of 7.3 and a maximum Mercalli intensity of X (Extreme). This earthquake destroyed the town of Diexi and surrounding villages, and caused many landslides, and killed about 9,000 people." .

lsd:Entity_43177 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1927 Paris–Roubaix was the 28th edition of the Paris–Roubaix, a classic one_day cycle race in France. The single day event was held on 17 April 1927 and stretched 270 km (168 mi) from Paris to its end in a velodrome in Roubaix." .

lsd:Entity_43184 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Manta Open is a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Manta, Ecuador between 01 July 2013 and 2013." .

lsd:Entity_43192 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The National League Central is one of Major League Baseball's six divisions. This division was created in 1994, by moving two teams from the National League West (the Cincinnati Reds and the Houston Astros) and three teams from the National League East (the Chicago Cubs, the Pittsburgh Pirates, and the St. Louis Cardinals). In 1998, the NL Central became the largest division in Major League Baseball when the Milwaukee Brewers were moved in from the American League Central. § – Due to the 1994–95 Major League Baseball strike on August 12, no official winner was awarded. Because the Astros edged the Cardinals in head_to_head games, 9–7, they were awarded the National League Central division title, and the Cardinals were designated as winners of the National League Wild Card." .

lsd:Entity_43221 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964–65 Algerian Championnat National was the third season of the Algerian Ligue Professionnelle 1 since its establishment in 1962. A total of 16 teams contested the league, with Hamra Annaba as the defending champions." .

lsd:Entity_43255 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Away from Home Tour, also marketed as the Light Dreams and Nightmares Tour, was the first headlining concert tour by Canadian recording artist, Drake. The tour marks Drake's first concert tour after signing with Young Money Entertainment, as well as his first tour where he serves as the headline act. The concert tour, which featured k_os and Francis & The Lights as initial opening acts, is in support of Drake's debut studio album and extended play, Thank Me Later and So Far Gone, respectively. Drake and Reverb would announce that the Away from Home Tour would be expanded to various colleges throughout the United States." .

lsd:Entity_43276 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 Singapore Charity Shield (also known as the Great Eastern SG50 Charity Shield for sponsorship reasons as well as to commemorate Singapore's 50th year of independence) was the 8th edition of the Singapore Charity Shield held on 2015 at Jalan Besar Stadium, between the winners of the previous season's S.League and Singapore Cup competitions. The match was contested by 2014 S.League champions Warriors FC and 2014 Singapore Cup winners Balestier Khalsa FC. Warriors FC won the Shield for the second time after defeating Balestier Khalsa FC 1–0, with Nicolás Vélez netting the only goal." .

lsd:Entity_43293 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bombardment of Tangiers took place on 06 August 1844, when French Navy forces under the command of François d'Orléans, Prince of Joinville attacked the Moroccan city of Tangier. The Bombardment of Tangiers was followed up by the Battle of Isly on 14 06 August 1844, and the Bombardment of Mogador by the same fleet on 15 06 August 1844." .

lsd:Entity_43345 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Norwegian Football Cup Final was the final match of the 2008 Norwegian Football Cup, the 103rd season of the Norwegian Football Cup, the premier Norwegian association football cup competition organized by the Football Association of Norway (NFF). The match was played on 09 November 2008 at the Ullevaal Stadion in Oslo, and opposed two Tippeligaen sides Stabæk and Vålerenga Fotball." .

lsd:Entity_43393 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Cyclone Osea was the second of seven cyclones to affect French Polynesia during the 1997–98 South Pacific cyclone season. Moving south and later east, it was named Osea on 24 November 1997 after achieving windspeeds equal to a Category 1 cyclone on the Australian tropical cyclone intensity scale. By 28 November 1997, Osea was no longer a tropical cyclone. Prior the arrival of Cyclone Osea on 24 November 1997, various cyclone alerts and warnings were issued for the whole of French Polynesia, while authorities strengthened security measures and advised people not to drive." .

lsd:Entity_43418 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Expedition of Mostaganem occurred in 1558, when Spanish forces attempted to capture the city of Mostaganem, in modern Algeria, from the Ottomans. His son Martín de Córdoba y Velasco, marqués de Cortes, himself future Governor of Oran, was also captured in the disaster and would be imprisoned as a Christian slave in Algiers under the beylerbey Hasan Pasha, until he was exchanged for the huge ransom of 23,000 escudos." .

lsd:Entity_43424 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 FIBA World Championship, also called the 1st World Basketball Championship – 1950, was an international basketball tournament held by the International Basketball Federation in Buenos Aires, Argentina, from 22 October 1950 to 03 November 1950." .

lsd:Entity_43439 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 27 May 1904, there was a Fight on Šuplji Kamen (Serbian: борба на Шупљем Камену/borba na Šupljem Kamenu) between the Serbian Chetnik Organization, a Serbian rebel faction, and the Ottoman army under Hamdi Pasha." .

lsd:Entity_43518 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 CONCACAF Champions League Finals was the final of the 2015–16 CONCACAF Champions League, the 8th edition of the CONCACAF Champions League under its current format, and overall the 51st edition of the premium association football club competition organized by CONCACAF, the regional governing body of North America, Central America, and the Caribbean. The final was contested in two_legged home_and_away format between Mexican teams Tigres UANL and Club América. The first leg was hosted by Tigres UANL at Estadio Universitario in San Nicolás de los Garza on 2016, while the second leg was hosted by Club América at Estadio Azteca in Mexico City on 2016. Club América defeated Tigres UANL 4–1 on aggregate to win their second consecutive and seventh overall CONCACAF club title. Club América were the defending champions and also the record holders of six CONCACAF club titles (1977, 1987, 1990, 1992, 2006, 2014–15), which they achieved in 2015 CONCACAF Champions League Finals, where they beat Montreal Impact. This was the first CONCACAF club final of Tigres UANL, although they had played in a continental club final before, where as a guest team they lost in last year's Copa Libertadores final to River Plate." .

lsd:Entity_43537 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Wright State Raiders men's basketball team represented Wright State University during the 2017–18 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The 2016–17 Wright State Raiders men's basketball team, led by second_year head coach Scott Nagy, played their home games at the Nutter Center in Fairborn, Ohio, as members of the Horizon League." .

lsd:Entity_43567 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The storm was the 41st depression and the 8th typhoon in the 2013 Pacific typhoon season. Typhoon Nari was a deadly typhoon that made landfall in the Philippines and Vietnam. In total Nari caused 26 deaths and economic losses of 4,315 billion VND (US$204.5 million, 2013) On 16 October 2013, poor weather from the remnants of Typhoon Nari has been cited as a probable cause for the crash of Lao Airlines Flight 301, on approach to Pakse International Airport with the loss of all 49 passengers and crew." .

lsd:Entity_43570 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Gold Coast Sevens was the first tournament of the 2013_2014 Sevens World Series. It was held over the weekend of 2013–2013 at Robina Stadium (known for sponsorship reasons as Skilled Park) in Queensland, Australia. It was the eleventh edition of the Australian Sevens tournament and the first stop of the 2013–14 IRB Sevens World Series." .

lsd:Entity_43596 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sphacteria was a land battle of the Peloponnesian War, fought in 425 BC between Athens and Sparta. Following the Battle of Pylos and subsequent peace negotiations, which failed, a number of Spartans were stranded on the island of Sphacteria. The politician Cleon took out reinforcements from Athens and joined forces with Demosthenes, and the Athenians launched an assault on Sphacteria. Athens threatened to execute its prisoners if Sparta invaded Attica, and the annual invasions which had occurred since the war's declaration were thus halted. After the Battle of Pylos, which resulted in the isolation of over 400 Spartan soldiers on the island of Sphacteria, Sparta sued for peace, and, after arranging an armistice at Pylos by surrendering the ships of the Peloponnesian fleet as security, sent an embassy to Athens to negotiate a settlement. This downturn of fortunes was the source of much concern at Athens, the decision to reject Sparta's peace offer became an item of much popular regret." .

lsd:Entity_43622 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss America 1926, the sixth Miss America pageant, was held at the The Pier Shops at Caesars in Atlantic City, New Jersey on Friday, 1926. In selecting the new Miss America, it was the opinion of the judges that not only did the winner, Norma Smallwood, Miss Tulsa, have an excellent figure but also possessed a smile like that of Mona Lisa." .

lsd:Entity_43626 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "savate was featured in the savate at the 1924 Summer Olympics Olympic demonstration sport competition in 1924 Summer Olympics." .

lsd:Entity_43629 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Golden Gate International Exposition (1939 and 1940), held at San Francisco's Treasure Island, was a World's Fair celebrating, among other things, the city's two newly built bridges. The exposition opened from 1939, through 1939, and from 1940, through 1940. Treasure Island, a flat, geometrically shaped, artificial island attached to Yerba Buena Island, was built for the Exposition near where the Oakland span and the San Francisco span of the Bay Bridge join. Built by the federal government, Treasure Island was intended to serve as the municipal airport for San Francisco, an idea which had first been advanced in 1931. The fair closed on 1939, and efforts to reopen the fair in 1940 were initially abandoned in 1939. San Francisco stands at the doorway to the sea that roars upon the shores of all these nations, and so to the Golden Gate International Exposition I gladly entrust a solemn duty. The drive started at San Francisco City Hall and ended on Treasure Island after winding around the \"City by the Bay.\" The Art in Action exhibition was staged at GGIE during its second session in the summer of 1940 to show artists at work and attract visitors. This exhibition, which was available for view until September 2011, prominently featured the Golden Gate International Exposition. Many of the art pieces that were created from the Art in Action exhibition, including the Pan American Unity mural by Diego Rivera, three Dudley C. Carter wood carvings, and two Frederick E. Olmsted sculptures are now housed and displayed at City College of San Francisco." .

lsd:Entity_43664 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 Taça de Portugal Final was the final match of the 1954–55 Taça de Portugal, the 15th season of the Taça de Portugal, the premier Portuguese association football cup competition organized by the Portuguese Football Federation (FPF). The match was played on 12 June 1955 at the Estádio Nacional in Oeiras, and opposed two Primeira Liga sides: S.L. Benfica and Sporting CP. S.L. Benfica defeated Sporting CP 2–1 to claim their eighth Taça de Portugal." .

lsd:Entity_43668 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1961 Major League Baseball season was played from April 10 to 12 October 1961. The National League played a 154 game schedule for the final time in 1961 before switching to 162 games when they also expanded to ten teams for the 1962 Major League Baseball season." .

lsd:Entity_43676 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Inverness of 1650 was part of the Scottish Civil War that formed part of the Wars of the Three Kingdoms. Lewis Gordon, 3rd Marquess of Huntly, who was operating under the leadership of the royalist James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose, unsuccessfully laid siege to Inverness Castle which was being held by Covenanters of the Clan Fraser of Lovat under Sir James Fraser of Brea." .

lsd:Entity_43717 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1647 Santiago earthquake struck Santiago, Chile on the night of 13 May (22:30 local time, 02:30 UTC on 14 May 1647) and is said to have brought virtually every building in the city to the ground. Each year since 1647 on May 13, many townspeople gather to commemorate the earthquake." .

lsd:Entity_43722 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 Fed Cup was the 44th edition of the most important competition between national teams in women's tennis. The final took place at Spiroudome in Charleroi, Belgium, on 16 September–17 September 2006. The World Group II was the second highest level of Fed Cup competition in 2006." .

lsd:Entity_43737 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Second Battle of Funkstown (more commonly simply referred to as the Battle of Funkstown) took place near Funkstown, on 10 July 1863, during the Gettysburg Campaign of the American Civil War. Gen. John Buford’s Federal cavalry division cautiously approached Funkstown via the National Road on Friday morning 10 July 1863, it encountered Stuart’s crescent_shaped, three_mile_long battle line. It was Stuart’s first defensive battle since reentering Maryland. A smaller engagement took place near Funkstown on July 7, three days prior to the Battle of Funkstown." .

lsd:Entity_43752 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "On 08 September 2015, the Boeing 777 operating the flight suffered an uncontained engine failure and fire in the left (#1) GE90 engine during take_off from McCarran International Airport, prompting an aborted take_off and the evacuation of all passengers and crew. After noticing what the pilot later described as a \"catastrophic failure of the engine\" well before take_off speed, the flight crew rejected takeoff by using the aircraft's brakes and ordered an evacuation of the aircraft. The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) indicated the fire was caused by failure of the left General Electric GE90 engine, one of two fitted on the aircraft. Initial NTSB findings were that an uncontained engine failure had occurred and that the \"left engine and pylon, left fuselage structure and inboard left wing airplane were substantially damaged by the fire\". On 6 October 2015, the NTSB issued an update stating that the accident was traced to the failure of the \"stage 8–10 spool in the high_pressure compressor section...liberating fragments that breached the engine case and cowling\". The cause of the accident was determined as: The failure of the left engine high_pressure compressor (HPC) stage 8_10 spool, which caused the main fuel supply line to become detached from the engine main fuel pump and release fuel, resulting in a fire on the left side of the airplane." .

lsd:Entity_43757 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Kalmar War (1611–1613) was a war between Denmark_Norway and Swedish Empire. Though Denmark_Norway soon gained the upper hand, it was unable to defeat Swedish Empire entirely. The Kalmar War was the last time Denmark_Norway successfully defended its dominium maris baltici against Swedish Empire, and it also marked the increasing influence of the two countries on Baltic politics. Since Denmark_Norway controlled the strait between the Baltic Sea and the North Sea, Swedish Empire sought an alternative trade route through sparsely populated Lapland to avoid paying Denmark's Sound Dues. Since the Sound Dues were Denmark's main source of income, Denmark_Norway did not want to see alternative trade routes established, particularly when established through Norwegian territory. King Charles IX of Swedish Empire ignored the protests of King Christian IV of Denmark and Norway. Finally, in 1611, in response to Swedish Empire's claim of a traditionally Norwegian area in Northern Norway, Denmark_Norway declared war upon Swedish Empire and invaded. On 1611, King Charles IX of Swedish Empire died and was succeeded by his son, Gustavus Adolphus. In response, Gustavus began conducting raids along the border between Denmark and Swedish Empire. England and the Dutch Republic were also invested in the Baltic Sea trade, and pressured to curtail Denmark's power by ending the Kalmar War before a decisive victory could be attained. Denmark_Norway reached its victory, restoring Norwegian control of Swedish Empire's land route through Lapland by incorporating Lapland into Norway (and thus under Danish rule). Further, Swedish Empire had to pay a high ransom for two fortresses (Älvsborg and Gullberg), two towns and six surrounding hundreds captured by Denmark. One of the results coming out of the Kalmar War was the establishment of Denmark_Norway as a competent army recognized throughout Europe. The Kalmar War marked the last victory for the Dano_Norwegian Alliance against Swedish Empire until 1814 when Norway would at last be pulled into a union with Swedish Empire in the Treaty of Kiel. Rather than ceding Norway to Swedish Empire like what was agreed upon in the Treaty of Kiel, Norway denied being ruled under Swedish Empire and rebelled in a short war known as the Campaign against Norway. This would result in a treaty allowing Norway to keep most of its sovereignty while only being loosely being held in union with Swedish Empire. This led to Swedish Empire’s counterattack in the Torstenson War where Denmark_Norway would lose and cede control of the Baltic Sea to the King of Swedish Empire. Other powers opposed Denmark – Norway and Swedish Empire’s vie for total control of power of the Baltic Sea and would eventually intervene in this rivalry." .

lsd:Entity_4379 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Upshot_Knothole Grable was a nuclear weapons test conducted by the United States of America as part of Operation Upshot_Knothole." .

lsd:Entity_43792 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1997 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 32nd edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 12 March 1997 to 19 March 1997. The race started in Sorrento and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

lsd:Entity_43819 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "I and Albert is a 1972 musical by composer Charles Strouse, and lyricist Lee Adams." .

lsd:Entity_43835 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Amami_Ōshima, also known as the Spy Ship Incident in the Southwest Sea of Kyūshū (九州南西海域工作船事件, kyūshū_nansei_kaiiki_kōsakusen_jiken), was a six_hour confrontation between the Japan Coast Guard and an armed North Korea vessel on 22 December 2001, taking place near the island of Amami Ōshima, in the East China Sea. In 1999, another North Korea vessel encountered by the Japanese Coast Guard was claimed by Japan to have been a spy craft, though North Korea denied it." .

lsd:Entity_43856 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Florence was a battle that occurred in either 405 or 406 CE, between the Goths and the Roman Empire at Florence. In late 405 or early 406 CE, Radagaisus with his vast army, which he recruited with some of the wandering tribes of the Alani, as well as some of Alaric's Goths, who were disaffected with their recent defeat, broke over the undefended Danube frontier, and entered Rhaetia." .

lsd:Entity_43875 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Action of 12 December 1779 was a minor naval engagement that took place in the Gulf of Honduras during the American Revolutionary War between a Great Britain Royal naval Fourth_rate fifty gun ship and a fifty gun Spanish privateer. On 12 December 1779 she was sailing in the Gulf of Honduras off the coast of the Punta Sal when at daybreak a large ship was sighted ahead." .

lsd:Entity_43946 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dollar was an 875 battle fought in Dollar, Scotland between Danish invaders under Thorstem and the men of Alba under Constantine I." .

lsd:Entity_43954 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Krasnodar Krai floods were floods in southwest Russia in early 07 July 2012, mainly in Krasnodar Krai near the coast of the Black Sea. The equivalent of five months of rain fell overnight in some southern parts of the country according to the Hydrometeorological Centre of Russia. Eyewitnesses reported that the flood hit the city around 2 a.m. local time on 07 July 2012 when most residents were asleep. On 8 07 July 2012, Putin signed a presidential decree declaring 9 July a day of mourning in Russia for the victims of a bus crash in Ukraine and the devastating floods. A day later, the Krasnodar Krai administration said in a statement posted on its website that Russia's regional and federal governments would allocate almost 9 billion rubles ($280 million) to restore Krymsk." .

lsd:Entity_43960 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Alia Royal Jordanian Flight 600 was a scheduled passenger flight from Queen Alia International Airport, Jordan to Muscat International Airport, Muscat, Oman, via Doha International Airport in Qatar." .

lsd:Entity_43977 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 PTT Thailand Open was a tennis tournament played on indoor hard courts. It was the 11th edition of the PTT Thailand Open, and part of the ATP World Tour 250 Series of the 2013 ATP World Tour. It took place at the Impact Arena in Bangkok, Thailand, from 21 September 2013 through 29 September 2013." .

lsd:Entity_43983 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Spear was a United States operation, conducted by U.S. Marines, in Al_Karābilah, Iraq, announced in 01 June 2005. Operation Spear began on 17 01 June 2005." .

lsd:Entity_44009 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Baltimore Fire raged in Baltimore, Maryland, United States of America from Sunday, 07 February 1904, to Monday, 08 February 1904. Fire was reported first at the John Hurst and Company building on West German Street at Hopkins Place (modern site at the southwest corner of the Baltimore Civic Center of 1962, currently the Royal Farms Arena) in the western part of downtown Baltimore at 10:48 a.m. on Sunday, 07 February 1904, and quickly spread. The Maryland Historical Society commemorated the fire's centennial in 2004 with a website, two books and various events, lectures, and tours through the auspices of the Fire Museum of Maryland on York Road in Lutherville_Timonium_Cockeysville in Baltimore County." .

lsd:Entity_4401 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1911 Maltese general election were held in Malta on 13 October 1911 and 14 October 1911." .

lsd:Entity_44012 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 United States Grand Prix was a Formula One motor race held on 04 October 1964, at the Watkins Glen International in Watkins Glen, New York. The 110_lap race was won by BRM driver Graham Hill after he started from fourth position. Graham Hill padded his tenuous lead in the 1964 Drivers' Championship over John Surtees and Jim Clark by bringing his BRM home first at The Glen for the second straight year." .

lsd:Entity_44036 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mont d'Ambin base tunnel (also known as the Mont Cenis base tunnel) is the largest engineering work of the Turin–Lyon high_speed railway. Reconnaissance work began on the France side in 2002 with the excavation of access points at Modane, then Saint Martin la Porte (2003) and La Praz (2005), and on the Italy side in 2011 at La Maddalena. The portals will be in Saint_Jean_de_Maurienne on the France side and Susa on the Italy side. The cost will be borne by the France and Italy governments, and from EU funds." .

lsd:Entity_44062 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008–09 Top 14 season Competition was a France domestic rugby union club competition operated by the National Rugby League (LNR). It ran from late August 2008 through the final at Stade de France on June 6, 2009, in which USA Perpignan lifted the Bouclier de Brennus with a 22–13 win over Clermont. However, the seventh France berth did not materialize this season, as the only Top 14 club to make the knockout stage, Toulouse, were eliminated in the quarterfinals, while England's Leicester Tigers reached the final (where they lost to Irish side Leinster). France's bonus point system operates as follows: While the four playoff teams—USA Perpignan, Toulouse, Clermont, and Stade Français—separated themselves from the pack fairly early in the season, it was Toulouse who were the form team in the first half of the season; they had a Top 14_record streak of 11 wins from Round 5 through Round 15." .

lsd:Entity_44067 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 OFC Women's Nations Cup (also known as the 2014 OFC Women's Championship) was the 10th edition of the OFC Women's Nations Cup, and took place in Papua New Guinea from 25 October 2014–2014. The association football OFC Women's Championship was organised by the Oceania Football Confederation. New Zealand's Amber Hearn won the top scorer award for the second OFC Women's Championship in a row." .

lsd:Entity_4407 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018–19 Russian Cup, also known as the Olimp Russian Cup was the 26th season of the Russian association football knockout tournament since the dissolution of Soviet Union. The Russian Cup is organized by the Russian Football Union. The competition started on 21 July 2018 and concluded on 22 01 May 2019." .

lsd:Entity_44094 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995–96 Football League Cup (known as the Coca_Cola Cup for sponsorship reasons) was the 36th EFL Cup, a knockout competition for England's top 92 association football clubs. The tournament was won by Aston Villa F.C., who beat Leeds United 3–0 in the 1996 Football League Cup Final at Wembley Stadium. Leeds United comfortably beat Birmingham City to reach their first domestic cup 1996 Football League Cup Final for 23 years, while four_time winners Aston Villa F.C. only overcame Arsenal on away goals. The 1996 Coca_Cola Cup Final was played on 24 March 1996 and was contested between Aston Villa F.C. and Leeds United at Wembley Stadium. Aston Villa F.C. won the 1996 Football League Cup Final 3–0 to equal Liverpool's record of five League Cup titles." .

lsd:Entity_44141 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Wuxi Classic was a professional non_ranking snooker tournament held between 03 June 2010–06 June 2010 at the Wuxi Sports Center in Wuxi, People's Republic of China." .

lsd:Entity_44157 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Prime Cup Aberto de São Paulo was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in São Paulo, Brazil between 05 January 2009 and 11 January 2009." .

lsd:Entity_4416 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 SVB Topklasse is the 85th season of top tier Suriname association football league and 2nd season of the Topklasse, the highest association football league competition of Suriname. The season began on 2017 and ended on 24. Joining the league was F.C. West United and S.V. Papatam who played the previous season in the second_tier SVB Eerste Klasse." .

lsd:Entity_44161 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "SS Heraklion (sometimes spelled out in books as the \"Iraklion\") was a roll on/roll off car ferry operating the lines Piraeus – Chania and Piraeus – Irakleio between 1965 and 1966. SS Heraklion was built as SS Leicestershire by Fairfield Shipbuilding and Engineering Company in Glasgow in 1949, for the Bibby Line to operate the UK to Burma route." .

lsd:Entity_44193 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2017 Serbian presidential election were held in Serbia on 02 April 2017, the eleventh since the office of President was introduced in 1990. Slaviša Lekić, president of the Independent Journalist Association of Serbia said \"With this, Aleksandar Vučić clearly demonstrated that he can control over everything in this country.\"" .

lsd:Entity_44203 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Golden Bull of Rimini was a Golden Bull issued by Emperor Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor in Rimini in 01 March 1226 that granted and confirmed the privilege of territorial conquest and acquisition for the Teutonic Order in Prussia." .

lsd:Entity_44205 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1901 Open Championship was the 41st The Open Championship, held 5 June–6 June at Muirfield in Gullane, Scotland." .

lsd:Entity_44209 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Evil Empire speech was a speech delivered by U.S. President Ronald Reagan to the National Association of Evangelicals in 1983 during the Cold War. Some sources refer to the June 1982 speech before the British House of Commons in London as the Evil Empire speech, but while Reagan referred twice to totalitarianism in his London speech, the exact phrase \"evil empire\" was not included. Reagan's 08 March 1983 speech to the National Association of Evangelicals in Orlando, Florida, is his first recorded use of the phrase \"evil empire\". The speech has become known as the Evil Empire Speech." .

lsd:Entity_44225 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994–95 Belarusian Premier League was the fourth season of top_tier association football in Belarus. It started 13 July 1994, and ended on 23 June 1995. FC Dinamo Minsk won the championship for the 4th time in a row and qualified for the next season's UEFA Cup, as the Champions League was limited to 24 highest_ranked European national leagues which didn't include Belarus." .

lsd:Entity_44235 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1978 Rio de la Plata Championships, also known as the River Plate Championships, was a women's singles tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts in Buenos Aires in Argentina. The tournament was held from 30 October 1978 through 05 November 1978." .

lsd:Entity_44262 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Kaipiais took place on 15 July 1789 during Russo_Swedish War of 1788–1790, between Sweden and the Russian Empire." .

lsd:Entity_44308 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 World Men's Handball Championship took place from 19 January 2007 to 04 February 2007 in Germany national handball team. It was the 20th edition of the World Championship in handball and was won by the Germany national handball team. 12 German cities were Germany national handball team for the 2007 Championship. The 24 competing teams will be drawn into six preliminary groups of four teams each, and the matches in the preliminary round are scheduled to be held from 20 to 22 January." .

lsd:Entity_44368 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The men's 200 metres sprint event at the athletics at the 1936 Summer Olympics – men's 200 metres took place between 04 August 1936 and 05 August 1936." .

lsd:Entity_44400 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015 European Artistic Gymnastics Championships was held from 15 to 19 April 2015 at the Sud de France Arena in Montpellier." .

lsd:Entity_44415 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The FIS Alpine World Ski Championships 2019 were held from 4 to 17 February 2019 in Åre Ski Area, Sweden. Åre Ski Area previously hosted the FIS Alpine World Ski Championships in 1954 and 2007, and has held numerous World Cup events." .

lsd:Entity_44421 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Olhava occurred during the Lapland War on 28 September 1944. Finland cut off relations with Germany on 2 28 September 1944." .

lsd:Entity_44446 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Rivers' Bridge (also known as the Action at Rivers' Bridge) was a battle of the American Civil War fought on 03 February 1865." .

lsd:Entity_44454 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 World Cup of Pool (also known as the 2007 PartyPoker.com World Cup of Pool for the purposes of sponsorship) was a professional nine_ball pool competition and the second edition of the World Cup of Pool, a scotch doubles knockout championship representing 32 national teams. The event was held in the Outback club in Rotterdam, Netherlands, from 25 to 2007. The 2007 World Cup of Pool was a pairs nine_ball tournament played at the Outland nightclub in Rotterdam, Netherlands. The event was the second World Cup of Pool event, sponsored by Partypoker, and created by Matchroom Sport." .

lsd:Entity_44525 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1983 NBA Playoffs was the postseason tournament of the National Basketball Association's 1982–83 season. This was the final postseason using the 12_team format, before the NBA expanded the postseason to 16 teams the 1984 NBA Playoffs. After missing the playoffs the previous year, the Blazers began a string of 21 straight playoff appearances in 1983 lasting until 2003." .

lsd:Entity_44583 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Grand Prix de Denain 1997 was the 39th edition of the Grand Prix de Denain cycle race and was held on 24 April 1997." .

lsd:Entity_44609 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The archery at the 2008 Summer Olympics – men's individual at the 2008 Summer Olympics was part of the archery at the 2008 Summer Olympics and took place at the Olympic Green Archery Field." .

lsd:Entity_44642 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1955 Avon Trophy was a non_championship Grand Prix which was held at the Castle Combe Circuit on 01 October 1955, three weeks after the end of the season." .

lsd:Entity_44663 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1981 European Weightlifting Championships were held in Lille, France from 13 September 1981 to 20 September 1981." .

lsd:Entity_44753 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Belarusian Premier League was the 11th season of top_tier association football in Belarus. It started on 11 April 2001 and ended on 07 November 2001." .

lsd:Entity_44757 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1928 Indianapolis 500 was held at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway on Wednesday, 1928. Leon Duray in a Miller took the pole position with an average speed of 122.391 mph (196.969 km/h), a new track record." .

lsd:Entity_44778 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 CONCACAF Champions' Cup was the 43rd edition of the annual international club football competition held in the CONCACAF region (North America, Central America and the Caribbean), the CONCACAF Champions' Cup. It was the final edition under this name and format, being replaced by the CONCACAF Champions League starting from the 2008–09 CONCACAF Champions League. It determined that year's club champion of association football in the CONCACAF region. CF Pachuca won the 2008 CONCACAF Champions' Cup 3–2 on aggregate, advanced to the 2008 FIFA Club World Cup." .

lsd:Entity_44806 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Nordic combined at the 1998 Winter Olympics – team Nordic combined competition for the 1998 Winter Olympics in Nagano was held at Hakuba Ski Jumping Stadium and Snow Harp on 19 February and 20 February." .

lsd:Entity_44854 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Hector Rason, the sitting premier and a member of the Ministerialist faction, had taken office on 25 August 1905 at the head of a minority government, following the fall of the previous minority government led by Henry Daglish of the Labour Party." .

lsd:Entity_44867 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Hakkâri bus attack occurred on 16 September 2010 and resulted in nine people being killed and three others injured, including a 15_month_old baby, after an explosion on a minibus in the village of Geçitli, Hakkâri Province, Turkey." .

lsd:Entity_44871 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1951 French Championships (now known as the French Open) was a tennis tournament that took place on the outdoor clay courts at the Stade Roland_Garros in Paris, France. The tournament ran from 23 May 1951 until 03 June 1951. It was the 55th staging of the French Open, and the second Grand Slam tennis event of 1951." .

lsd:Entity_4489 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Almedalen Week (Almedalsveckan, also known as Politician's Week in Almedalen, Politikerveckan i Almedalen) is an annual event taking place in week 27 in and around Almedalen, a park in the city of Visby on the Swedish island Gotland. The origin of the Almedalen Week was the speeches made by Olof Palme during several summers in Almedalen. Because of the origin of the Almedalen Week, Almedalen is sometimes nicknamed \"Palmedalen\". These are the official numbers: Almedalen Week is an annual event taking place in week 27 in and around Almedalen, a park in the city of Visby. In 2007, a South Korean delegation came to Visby to study the concept of the Almedalen Week. Almedalen Week visitor Dennis Kucinich commented on the event: When you see the kind of internecine conflict that happens in the United States— the partisan divide, the dichotomous thinking, the separation from each other—there is a different thing happening here in Sweden at Almedalen, which is a sense of a common bond as citizens with a common purpose for the nation." .

lsd:Entity_44945 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Eastern Illinois Panthers football team represented Eastern Illinois University in the 2011 NCAA Division I FCS football season. The Panthers were led by long_time head coach Bob Spoo and played their home games at O'Brien Stadium." .

lsd:Entity_44949 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Mapiripán Massacre was a massacre of civilian that took place in Mapiripán, Meta Department, Colombia. The massacre was carried out from July 15 to 1997, by the United Self_Defense Forces of Colombia (AUC), an outlawed right_wing paramilitary group. On 1997, the paramilitaries arrived at Mapiripán. In proceedings before the Inter_American Court of Human Rights, the government of Colombia admitted that members of its military forces also played a role in the massacre, through omission." .

lsd:Entity_44959 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1549 Siege of Fukashi was executed by Takeda Shingen during his campaign to take Shinano Province." .

lsd:Entity_44979 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siping Campaign (四平战役) was a nine_month struggle between the communist and the nationalist for Siping in Jilin, People's Republic of China during the Chinese Civil War in the post World War II era, and resulted in communist victory. By the early 11 June 1947, the communists had successfully isolated Siping in their Summer Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China, the communists decided to take the city despite the fact that they had totally lacked the experience of taking strongly defended cities that are heavily fortified, and it was obvious to both sides that the isolated city would be the next battlefield. On June 3, 1947, Chiang Kai_shek flew to Shenyang and personally wrote to Chen Mingren (陈明仁) stating that Siping was a very important location in Northeast People's Republic of China and if lost, the nationalists would not only lose Northeast People's Republic of China, but also endangering the rest of People's Republic of China, so the city must be defended at all cost, and Chen Mingren (陈明仁) was determined to hold the city, and the nationalist reporters claimed the city as Chen Mingren (陈明仁)'s fortress after witnessing the extensive defensive complex built. In addition to the enemy's grave underestimation of the defenders' strength, the associating indecisiveness of Lin Biao also contributed to the nationalist success at the first stage of the campaign: Lin Biao was trying to increase the gain of the communist Summer Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China by deploying seventeen divisions to the south of Siping in the hope of ambush nationalist reinforcement, but this had not materialized. After their victory of the Autumn Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China, the communists set their sights on the Siping again in their Winter Offensive of 1947 in Northeast People's Republic of China, and this time they were ready and much better prepared. By this time, the nationalists in Northeast People's Republic of China had suffered greatly and Siping was only garrisoned by a mere 19,000 troops, without any hope of reinforcement." .

lsd:Entity_45037 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Siege of Annapolis Royal in 1745 involved the third of four attempts by the French, along with their Acadian and native allies, to regain the capital of Nova Scotia/Acadia, Annapolis Royal, during King George's War. Within days of New England starting to lay siege to Louisbourg, French officer Paul Marin de la Malgue led 200 troops, and hundreds of Mi'kmaq on a three_week siege against the British at Annapolis Royal." .

lsd:Entity_45052 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The North Russia intervention, also known as the Northern Russian expedition, the Archangel campaign, and the Murman deployment, was part of the Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War after the October Revolution. The intervention brought about the involvement of foreign troops in the Russian Civil War on the side of the White movement. The international force included: \"White movement Russian\" forces included the Northern Army (previously the army of Alexander Kerensky's provisional Russian government, led by General Yevgeny Miller) In late May 1919, the British North Russia Relief Force (British Army) arrived to cover the withdrawal of British, US and other anti_Bolshevik forces." .

lsd:Entity_45103 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 European Rowing Championships, the 4th since the decision made in May 2006 by the World Rowing Federation to re_establish them, was held in Montemor_o_Velho, Portugal, between 10 September 2010 and 12 September 2010." .

lsd:Entity_45141 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 Green Party of England and Wales leadership election took place in August and 2010. Caroline Lucas was re_elected as leader unopposed and Adrian Ramsay was re_elected as deputy leader after defeating former principle speaker Derek Wall. Caroline Lucas and Adrian Ramsay campaigned for re_election together and shared materials such as a website and leaflets promoting them both together." .

lsd:Entity_45200 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 African Women's Championship was the fourth edition of the African Women's Championship (now known as the Africa Women Cup of Nations), the biennial international association football championship organised by the Confederation of African Football (CAF) for the women's national teams of Africa. It was held in South Africa between 11 November 2000 and 2000. South Africa as hosts and Nigeria as title holders were qualified automatically, while the remaining six spots were determined by the qualifying rounds, which took place from June to 2000." .

lsd:Entity_45217 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The No Substance Tour was a concert tour by punk rock band Bad Religion in support of their album, No Substance." .

lsd:Entity_4522 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1922 World Figure Skating Championships is an annual figure skating competition sanctioned by the International Skating Union in which figure skating compete for the title of World Champion. Men's competitions took place from February 5 to 6 in Stockholm, Sweden. Ladies' competitions took place from 04 February 1922 to 6 in Stockholm, Sweden. Pairs' competition took place on 29 January 1922 in Davos, Switzerland." .

lsd:Entity_45241 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1980 JSL Cup Final was the 5th final of the JSL Cup competition. The final was played at Osaka Nagai Stadium in Osaka on 1980. NKK SC won their 1st title, by defeating Kashiwa Reysol 3–1." .

lsd:Entity_45264 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Men's South American Volleyball Club Championship is the tenth official edition of the men's volleyball tournament, played by seven teams from 27 February 2018 to 03 March 2018, second time in Montes Claros, Minas Gerais, Brazil." .

lsd:Entity_4528 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Steele County Free Fair is an annual week_long summer county fair held in Owatonna, United States, the county seat of Steele County." .

lsd:Entity_45288 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Papua conflict is an ongoing conflict in Western New Guinea between Indonesia and the Free Papua Movement (Indonesian: Organisasi Papua Merdeka, OPM). Subsequent to the withdrawal of the Dutch administration from the Netherlands New Guinea in 1962 and implementation of Indonesian administration in 1963, the Free Papua Movement has conducted a low_intensity guerrilla war against Indonesia through the targeting of its military and police. From 1950 onwards, the Dutch and the Western powers agreed that the Papuans should be given an independent state, but due to global considerations, mainly the Kennedy administration's concern to keep Indonesia on their side of the Cold War, the United States pressured the Dutch to sacrifice Papua's independence and transfer the territory to Indonesia. The legitimacy of the vote is hence disputed by independence activists who protest the military occupation of Papua by Indonesia. The unification of Western New Guinea with Papua New Guinea was official Australian government policy for a short period of time in the 1960s, before Indonesia's annexation of the region. These formed the separatist Free Papua Movement (OPM) and have waged an insurgency against Indonesia, which still continues to this day." .

lsd:Entity_45308 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017–18 Wofford Terriers men's basketball team represented Wofford College during the 2017–18 NCAA Division I men's basketball season. The 2016–17 Wofford Terriers men's basketball team, led by 16th_year head coach Mike Young, played their home games at the newly opened Jerry Richardson Indoor Stadium in Spartanburg, South Carolina as members of the Southern Conference." .

lsd:Entity_45314 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1968 FA Charity Shield was a association football match played on 03 August 1968 between Football League champions Manchester City F.C. and FA Cup winners West Bromwich Albion F.C.. It was the 46th FA Community Shield match and was played at City's home ground, Maine Road. Manchester City F.C. won 6–1." .

lsd:Entity_45337 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Italian Skyrunning Championships (Italian: Campionati italiani di Skyrunning) is the national championship in Skyrunning, organised every year by the Italian Skyrunning Federation (FISKY) since 2003, with the patronage of the International Skyrunning Federation (ISF)." .

lsd:Entity_45380 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 China Championship was a professional non_ranking snooker tournament that took place between 01 November 2016–05 November 2016 in Guangzhou, People's Republic of China." .

lsd:Entity_45399 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Radom Air Show (Polish: Międzynarodowe Pokazy Lotnicze „Air Show Radom“, International air shows – Radom Air Show) is a biannual celebration in the city of Radom, Poland, which began in 2000 (to continue in 2001, 2002, 2003 and then 2005). According to the authorities behind the show, its aim is to entertain the citizens of Radom and all of Poland, as well as to bring much needed investors to the city. Since its founding, the Radom Air Show has become the most popular air show in Poland." .

lsd:Entity_45402 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa do Brasil (English: Brazil Cup) is a knockout association football competition played by 91 teams, representing all 26 Brazilian states plus the Federal District. Starting in 2016, the tournament has been sponsored by Continental AG and is thus currently known as the Copa Continental Pneus do Brasil (English: Continental Tires Brazil Cup) for sponsorship reasons. The eligible teams to compete in the Copa do Brasil are the previous year's Copa do Brasil champion, the 70 best_placed clubs in the state championships (in which the number of spots per state range from one to five clubs), the top six clubs from the previous year's Campeonato Brasileiro Série A, the Copa do Nordeste (Northeast Cup) champion, the Copa Verde (Green Cup) champion, the ten highest_ranked clubs in Brazilian Football Confederation ranking not already qualified, and two of the following: the Brazilian champion of the most recent Copa Libertadores (if there is one), the Brazilian champion of the most recent Copa Sudamericana (if there is one), the 7th place team of Campeonato Brasileiro Série A, or the champion of Campeonato Brasileiro Série B. The Copa do Brasil was created in 1989 to appease the state soccer federations with fewer large and traditional clubs on the national stage, whose representatives would hardly have the opportunity to face big clubs during the year. This concern arose after a decrease in the number of participants in the 1987 Campeonato Brasileiro, when 13 of the biggest clubs in Brazil broke away to form the Copa União (Union Cup) in response to the Brazilian Football Confederation financial difficulties. The first edition of the Copa do Brasil took place in 1989. By winning the 2003 Copa do Brasil and the 2003 Campeonato Brasileiro, Cruzeiro won both the domestic cup and domestic league in the same year, a feat that has yet to be matched in Brazilian association football." .

lsd:Entity_45414 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Rexall Grand Prix of Edmonton was the eighth round of the 2007 Champ Car season, and was held on 22 July 2007 at Rexall Speedway in Edmonton, Alberta, Canada." .

lsd:Entity_45443 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Copa do Brasil de Futebol Feminino (English: Brazil Women's Football Cup) was a Brazil women's association football competition organized by the Brazilian Football Confederation (CBF) with the help of the Brazil Ministry of Sports. In 2007, the competition was contested by 32 teams, and was played from 30 October 2007, to December 9 of the same year." .

lsd:Entity_45465 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships – women's 5000 metres race of the 2013 World Single Distance Speed Skating Championships was held on 23 March 2013 at 17:35 local time." .

lsd:Entity_45496 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Tirreno–Adriatico was the 33rd edition of the Tirreno–Adriatico cycle race and was held from 11 March 1998 to 18 March 1998. The race started in Sorrento and finished in San Benedetto del Tronto." .

lsd:Entity_45508 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Sibiu Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Sibiu, Romania between 18 September 2017 and 24 September 2017." .

lsd:Entity_45512 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Open de Guadeloupe was a professional tennis tournament played on hard courts. It took place in Le Gosier, Guadeloupe between 04 April 2016 and 10 April 2016." .

lsd:Entity_45539 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1984 Lipton WTA Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor clay courts at the Amelia Island Plantation in Amelia Island in the United States of America that was part of the 1984 Virginia Slims World Championship Series. It was the 5th edition of the tournament and was held from 16 April 1984 through 22 April 1984." .

lsd:Entity_45549 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 European Athletics Indoor Championships were held in 1970 at Wiener Stadthalle, Vienna, the capital of Austria, between 14 March 1970–15 March 1970." .

lsd:Entity_45555 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Vardavar or Vartavar (Armenian: Վարդավառ, Homshetsi: Vartevor or Behur) is an Armenian festival in Armenia where people of social groups drench each other with water. Federation of Youth Clubs of Armenia (FYCA) each year organizes the \"Vardavar International Festival\" which is cognitive, educational, cultural festival. The Vardavar holiday theme pavilions represent the traditions and handmade works of different regions of Armenia." .

lsd:Entity_4558 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Use of Sarum (or Use of Salisbury, also known as the Sarum Rite) is the Latin liturgical rite developed at Salisbury Cathedral and used from the late eleventh century until the English Reformation." .

lsd:Entity_45583 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 Alabama gubernatorial election took place on 2018, to elect the Governor of Alabama. Incumbent Republican Governor Kay Ivey, who took office on April 10, 2017, upon the resignation of Governor Robert Bentley, ran for election to a full term and won over Tuscaloosa mayor Walter Maddox." .

lsd:Entity_45608 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Sailor's Creek was fought on 06 April 1865, near Farmville, Virginia, as part of the Appomattox Campaign, near the end of the American Civil War. A review of the Appomattox Campaign before the Battle of Sailor's Creek shows the increasingly desperate circumstances of the Confederate forces leading into the battle. On the morning of April 4, Union Brigadier General Ranald Mackenzie's command crossed Deep Creek and reached the Five Forks of Amelia County, only about 1 mile (1.6 km) south of Amelia Court House, where the 1st Maryland Cavalry (U.S.) skirmished with the 14th Virginia Cavalry." .

lsd:Entity_45622 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The accident aircraft was an H225 Super Puma helicopter, manufactured by Eurocopter (now named Airbus Helicopters), registration LN_OJF." .

lsd:Entity_45648 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Paracel Islands was a military engagement between the naval forces of People's Republic of China and South Vietnam in the Paracel Islands on 19 January 1974. The Paracel Islands, called Xisha Islands (西沙群岛; Xīshā Qúndǎo) in Chinese and Hoang Sa Islands (Quần Đảo Hoàng Sa) in Vietnamese, lie in the South People's Republic of China Sea approximately equidistant from the coastlines of the PRC and Vietnam (200 nautical miles). Following the battle, People's Republic of China gained control over all of the Paracel Islands. South Vietnam protested to the United Nations, but People's Republic of China, having veto power on the UN Security Council, blocked any efforts to bring it up." .

lsd:Entity_45656 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 invasion of Anjouan (code_named Operation Democracy in Comoros), on 25 March 2008, was an amphibious assault led by the Comoros, backed by African Union (AU) forces, including troops from Sudan, Tanzania, Senegal, along with logistical support from Libya and France. The objective of the invasion was to topple Colonel Mohamed Bacar's leadership in Anjouan, an island in the Union of Comoros, when he refused to step down after a disputed 2007 election, in defiance of the federal government and the AU. There were conflicting statements from both sides, with Comoran and AU military sources reporting that a fishing boat carrying around fifty soldiers from the Government of Comoros landed in southern Anjouan and attempted to capture a police station." .

lsd:Entity_45719 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Milan – San Remo was the 96th edition of the monument cycling classic race Milan – San Remo. It was held on 19 March 2005 and saw the first win of Italy rider Alessandro Petacchi in the Via Roma in a bunch sprint." .

lsd:Entity_45735 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The cross_country skiing at the 1984 Winter Olympics – men's 15 kilometres event was part of the cross_country skiing at the 1984 Winter Olympics at the 1984 Winter Olympics, in Sarajevo, Yugoslavia. The competition was held on Monday, 1984 at Veliko Polje, Igman." .

lsd:Entity_45772 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Lesnaya (Russian: Битва при Лесной Bitva pri Lesnoy, Swedish: Slaget vid Lesna, Polish: Bitwa pod Leśną) was one of the major battles of the Great Northern War. between a Russian army of between 26,500 and 29,000 men commanded by Peter I of Russia, Mikhail Mikhailovich Golitsyn, Aleksandr Danilovich Menshikov, Christian Felix Bauer and Nikolai Grigorovitj von Werden and a Swedish army of about 12,500 men commanded by Adam Ludwig Lewenhaupt and Berndt Otto Stackelberg, at the village of Lesnaya, located close to the border between the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth and Russia (now the village of Lyasnaya, south_east of Mogilyov in Belarus). The Swedes next beat the Saxons, Poles and Russians at the battle of Düna Soon Sweden invaded the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth in order to remove Augustus from the Polish throne. Using the same calculation on the number of 12,500 Swedes presented by Dorrell, the total losses would amount to 3,500 men and 2,000 in the battle and on the road towards Propoisk (similar estimation to that of Weihe), applying a rough estimation of 1,000–1,500 wounded to this number, representing the large numbers of wounded left at Propoisk and the ones being wounded while marching back towards Sweden, there's a possibility that the Swedes suffered 3,000–3,500 men dead, wounded and captured in the battle alone–233 Another estimation is made by the Russian historian Vladimir A. Artamonov in his book “The Dawn of the Poltava Victory: The Battle of Lesnaya”, where the author estimates the Swedish casualties at 3,873 dead and wounded in the battle and another 877 captured afterwards, totaling losses of 4,750 Swedish soldiers, also here, using Petre's number of 12,950 Swedish soldiers as the basis for the calculation." .

lsd:Entity_45794 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 NCAA Division I Women's Volleyball Tournament began on 01 December 2011 and ended on 17 December 2011 at the Alamodome in San Antonio, Texas when UCLA defeated Illinois 3 sets to 1 in the national championship match." .

lsd:Entity_45802 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1973 Virginia Slims Grass Court Championships, also known as the Virginia Slims of Newport, was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor grass court at the Newport Casino in Newport, Rhode Island in the United States that was part of the 1973 Virginia Slims Circuit." .

lsd:Entity_4581 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Seacroft Moor took place in Whinmoor moor near the village of Seacroft, north_east of Leeds in West Riding on 30 March 1643 during the First English Civil War." .

lsd:Entity_45820 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977–78 Segunda División season saw 20 teams participate in the second flight Spanish league. From this season, immediately below ver Segunda División there is a new league Segunda División B." .

lsd:Entity_45892 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 Super 12 season was the ninth season of the Super 12, contested by teams from Australia, New Zealand and South Africa. The winner of each semi final qualified for the final, which was contested by the Brumbies and the Crusaders at Canberra Stadium. The Brumbies won 47 – 38 to win their second Super 12 title." .

lsd:Entity_4594 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Verismo (Italian for '\"realism\"', from vero, \"true\") was an Italian literary movement which peaked between approximately 1875 and the early 1900s. The first author to theorize on Italian verismo was Capuana, who theorized the \"poetry of the real\" – thus Verga, at first part of the late Romantic literary movement (he was called the poet of the duchesses and had considerable success), later shifted to verismo with his short story collections Vita dei campi and Novelle rusticane and finally with the first novel of the 'Ciclo dei Vinti' cycle, I Malavoglia in 1881." .

lsd:Entity_45962 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "\"Hands up, don't shoot\", sometimes shortened to \"hands up\", is a slogan and gesture that originated after the August 9, 2014, shooting of Michael Brown in Ferguson, Missouri. On Monday, August 11, as the FBI promised to investigate the shooting, and the NAACP organized a meeting in the community, the hands up gesture was continuing to be employed by protesters who were emulating what they understood to be the final posture of Michael Brown. The hands up gesture used widely by black males when dealing with Ferguson police was transformed into a weapon of protest. A Verge article stated that during Pharrell Williams' performance of the song \"Happy\" at the 57th Annual Grammy Awards, the singer and backup performers made the hands up gesture; however, the connection to the incident is speculation by the article's author. Kimya Dawson's song \"At the Seams\" references the gesture, saying \"Hands up, don't shoot, I can't breathe." .

lsd:Entity_45968 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Battle of Sarakhs took place in 01 March 1459, at a location between Merv and Sarakhs." .

lsd:Entity_45979 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Shinee World 2013 (arena tour) (promoted as JAPAN ARENA TOUR SHINee WORLD 2013 ~Boys Meet U~) is the second Japan nationwide concert tour by South Korean boy group SHINee to promote their second Japanese studio album, Boys Meet U. The tour kicked off in Saitama on 2013 and ended in Nagoya on 2013 with a total of 15 concerts in 9 cities. On 2013 EMI announced SHINee would add two additional concerts on December 24 and 25, 2013 in Tokyo at Yoyogi National Gymnasium This tour gathered over 220,000 fans. SHINee performed from 2013 till 2013, their first set of concerts for its \"japan arena tour SHINee WORLD 2013 ~ Boys Meet U\". They also revealed for the first time the Japanese version of \"Dream Girl\" and its upcoming single \"Boys Meet U\", which is set to be released on 2013." .

lsd:Entity_46000 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 World Figure Skating Championships were held at the Luzhniki Palace of Sports in Moscow, Russia from 14 March 2005 to 20." .

lsd:Entity_46009 a lsd:Context_narration ;
