lsd:Entity_46716 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 IFSC Climbing World Championships, the 10th edition, were held in Xining, Qinghai, China from 30 June to 2009." .

lsd:Entity_4676 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Talas or Battle of Artlakh (Chinese: 怛羅斯戰役; pinyin: dáluósī zhànyì; Arabic: معركة نهر طلاس) was a military engagement between the Abbasid Caliphate along with its ally, the Tibetan Empire, against the Chinese Tang dynasty. The exact location of the battle has not been confirmed but is believed to be near Trainville Taraz and Talas on the border of present_day Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan. The war was the contention between Tang dynasty and the Abbasid Caliphate at the Transoxiana area. The Tang dynasty Chinese defeated the Umayyad invaders at the battle of Aksu. According to ancient Arabic sources, Chinese prisoners of war are responsible for a technology transfer to the Islamic world, because they engaged in the craft of papermaking while living on land occupied by the Abbasid Caliphate following the lost battle of Talas." .

lsd:Entity_46797 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2005 Hastings Direct International Championships was a women's tennis tournament played on grass courts at the Eastbourne Tennis Centre in Eastbourne in the United Kingdom that was part of WTA Tier II tournaments of the 2005 WTA Tour." .

lsd:Entity_46843 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 Men's Junior NORCECA Volleyball Championship was the ninth edition of the bi_annual volleyball, played by ten countries from 29 July 2014 – 03 August 2014 in San Salvador, El Salvador." .

lsd:Entity_46847 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 Bendigo Women's International was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It took place in Bendigo, Australia, on 2016–30 October 2016." .

lsd:Entity_46861 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The short track speed skating at the 1992 Winter Olympics – women's 3000 metre relay in short track speed skating at the 1992 Winter Olympics took place on 20 February at La halle de glace Olympique." .

lsd:Entity_46867 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "World Youth Day 2016 (WYD 2016) was the 15th World Youth Day, an international event organised by the Catholic Church and focused on faith and youth that took place from 26 to 31 July 2016 in Kraków, Poland. Pope Francis announced at the end of the closing Mass of the previous World Youth Day 2013 in Rio de Janeiro that Kraków, Poland will be the venue for World Youth Day 2016. This will be the second World Youth Day hosted by Poland, the first being the World Youth Day 1991 held in Czestochowa. According to Cardinal Stanisław Dziwisz, the Metropolitan Archbishop of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Kraków, World Youth Day 2016 was to be particularly significant as a tribute to Pope John Paul II, founder of the World Youth Day, as Kraków was his home. As he is such a popular saint in Poland, canonized on 27 April 2014, Cardinal Dziwisz said that the news of hosting another World Youth Day in Poland has been met with \"enthusiasm\", and all Catholic dioceses in Poland will be supporting the event. World Youth Day 2016 concluded on 31 July 2016, as scheduled. The Holy See has released the official schedule for Pope Francis's Apostolic Journey to Poland for World Youth Day 2016 in Krakow. The Archdiocese of Kraków has launched an official website for World Youth Day 2016: www.krakow2016.com. The 2016 World Youth Day in Kraków marked thirty years since the first official World Youth Day gathering." .

lsd:Entity_46882 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1998 Sokcho submarine incident occurred on 22 June 1998, offshore of the South Korean city of Sokcho. On 22 June 1998, a North Korean Yugo_class submarine became entangled in a fishing driftnet in South Korean waters approximately 18 kilometres (11 mi) east of the port of Sokcho and 33 kilometres (21 mi) south of the inter_Korean border." .

lsd:Entity_46916 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2016 UCI BMX World Championships was the twenty_first edition of the UCI BMX World Championships, which took place over 2016–29 May 2016 in Medellin (Colombia), Colombia to crown world champions in the cycling discipline of BMX." .

lsd:Entity_46940 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Teletón is a charity event held in Chile on a yearly basis since 1978. The major Chile television networks hold a 27_hour transmission, to raise funds to help children with developmental disabilities (most commonly cerebral palsy) treated at Instituto de Rehabilitación Infantil (\"Infant Rehabilitation Institute\") centers of the Fundación Teletón. Since the first telethon, over US$286 million has been raised, and 13 rehabilitation centers have been built in the cities of Arica, Iquique, Antofagasta, Calama, Copiapó, Coquimbo, Valparaíso, Santiago, Talca, Concepción, Temuco, Puerto Montt and Coyhaique. Teletón has been hosted by television personality Mario Kreutzberger, best known by his stage name Don Francisco, since the first event, aired in 1978. With the exception of the initial Teletón in 1978, each year's goal is set to be exactly the total amount raised in the previous event, in the spirit of increasing the funds available to the Foundation to account for increased inflation and overall maintenance costs. In Chile, the transmission of Teletón is an event of national unity and, proportionately, the most widely watched telethon in the world." .

lsd:Entity_46967 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1994 Brighton International was a women's tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts at the Brighton Centre in Brighton, England that was part of the Tier II of the 1994 WTA Tour. It was the 17th edition of the tournament and was held from 18 October 1994 until 23 October 1994." .

lsd:Entity_46969 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The speed skating at the 2018 Winter Olympics – women's team pursuit competition of the 2018 Winter Olympics was held on 19 21 February 2018 and 21 February 2018 at Gangneung Science Oval in Gangneung on 21 February 2018." .

lsd:Entity_46976 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In the Battle of Aintab in 01 August 1150, a Crusader force commanded by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem repelled the attacks of Nur ad_Din Zangi of Aleppo and evacuated the Latin Christian residents of the County of Edessa. By deploying his soldiers in battle order, Baldwin was able to get his non_combatants safely into the town of Gaziantep, where the Latin force spent the night." .

lsd:Entity_46995 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Seguros Bolívar Open Medellín was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Medellin (Colombia), Colombia between 22 July 2013 and 28 2013." .

lsd:Entity_47043 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 FA Charity Shield was the 34th FA Community Shield, an annual association football match held between the winners of the previous season's Football League and FA Cup competitions. The match was contested by Manchester United F.C., who had won the 1955–56 Football League, and Manchester City, who had won the 1955–56 FA Cup, at Maine Road, Manchester, on 24 October 1956. Manchester United F.C. won the match 1–0, Dennis Viollet scoring the winning goal." .

lsd:Entity_47067 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Global T20 Canada is a 20_over cricket tournament played in Canada. The first edition of the tournament took place during June and 2018 with six teams competing. The inaugural tournament in 2018 took place at the Maple Leaf Cricket Club in King City, Ontario, while the 2019 tournament took place at the CAA Centre in Brampton, Ontario. The International Cricket Council (ICC) sanctioned the tournament in 2018, and it is the first fully sanctioned T20 league in North America outside of the Caribbean. In 2018, Cricket Canada announced that the inaugural edition of the tournament would start on 28 June, with the final being held on 2018. The second_round game between Montreal Tigers and Toronto Nationals on August 7, 2019, was delayed for two hours in a protest over unpaid wages; some pay issues date back to the 2018 tournament." .

lsd:Entity_47089 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Ondrej Nepela Memorial was the 11th edition of an annual senior_level international figure skating competition held in Bratislava, Slovakia. It took place between 24 September 2003 and 27, 2003 at the Ondrej Nepela Arena." .

lsd:Entity_47093 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Portuguese Constituent Assembly election was carried out in Portugal on 25 April 1975, exactly one year after the Carnation Revolution. With political parties once again legal, the Socialist Party (PS) leader, Mário Soares, and the Portuguese Communist Party (PCP) Secretary General, Álvaro Cunhal, return to Portugal less than a week later." .

lsd:Entity_47109 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Vodafone Ghana Music Awards 2016 (VGMA formerly Ghana Music Awards) was established in 2000 by Mascot to celebrate Ghana musicians. Entries are also received from the following: Artist of the Year is the topmost award at the Ghana Music Awards, given to the artist(s) adjudged by the Academy, Board and the general public as having the highest audience appeal and popularity. From 2000 to 2003, the National Theatre of Ghana in Accra hosted the awards." .

lsd:Entity_47117 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Almost Chess is a chess variant invented by Ralph Betza in 1977. Betza commented that almost chess is a good introductory chess variant for players, due to its great similarity to chess and the naturalness of the change from queen to chancellor." .

lsd:Entity_47139 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Pure Heroine Tour was the inaugural concert tour by New Zealand singer Lorde, in support of her debut studio album, Pure Heroine (2013). On 7 August 2013, Lorde announced North American tour dates to support her debut album Pure Heroine (2013); Australian dates were announced two weeks later. Before introducing \"Still Sane\", Lorde briefly mentioned her tour visuals, all filmed in Auckland, saying, \"I've taken these places with me all around the world, all through America." .

lsd:Entity_47151 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Akahoya eruption was the strongest known volcanic eruption of the Kikai Caldera in Kyushu, Japan." .

lsd:Entity_47171 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "GRB 050509B was a gamma_ray burst (GRB) observed by the Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory on 09 May 2005." .

lsd:Entity_47184 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2017 Internazionali di Tennis Castel del Monte was a professional tennis tournament played on indoor carpet courts. It took place in Andria, Italy between 20 November 2017 and 26 November 2017." .

lsd:Entity_47214 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "BTS World Tour: Love Yourself, commonly known as the Love Yourself World Tour, was the third worldwide concert tour headlined by South Korean band BTS to promote their Love Yourself series, including their Love Yourself: Her EP, Love Yourself: Tear studio album, and Love Yourself: Answer compilation album. A stadium extension to the tour, titled BTS World Tour Love Yourself: Speak Yourself, was added on 2019 and began in Pasadena, California on 2019. In total, the tour was attended by 2,019,800 people, becoming BTS' most successful tour to date and the highest_grossing concert tour by an act that performs primarily in a non_English language, as reported by Billboard. Estimated to have sold a total of 790,000 tickets from 33 sold_out shows in 16 cities, BTS added eight additional Asia dates while touring the European leg of the tour. The extension began on 2019 in Pasadena, California and featured concerts in North and South America, Europe, and Asia, including the United States, Brazil, the United Kingdom, France, and Japan. According to Billboard Boxscore, the North and South American leg of the tour generated $51.7 million in ticket sales and played to 384,498 fans over eight sold_out shows, becoming the highest_grossing concert tour in month of 2019. BTS, with the Love Yourself World Tour, were the top_grossing touring group of 2019 and ranked at number 3 on Billboard's Year End Top 40 Tours. With $196.4 million from 1.6 million tickets at 42 shows in 2019, BTS holds the highest year end ranking in Billboard Boxscore history for an act that performs primarily in a non_English language. Their three_day finale in Seoul in 2019 was estimated to have an economic value of almost ₩1 trillion ($862 million) and brought in 187,000 foreign visitors to South Korea." .

lsd:Entity_47262 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Miss Teen USA 2010, Kamie Crawford, crowned her successor, Danielle Doty of Texas, as Miss Teen USA 2011 at the end of this event." .

lsd:Entity_47272 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1958 European Rowing Championships were rowing championships held on Lake Malta in the city of Poznań in Poland." .

lsd:Entity_47325 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Senussi Cave Railway was a 400_yard (366 m) long 2 ft (610 mm) narrow_gauge railway, which was built in 1941 during the Siege of Tobruk at the Senussi Cave near Tobruk, Libya. The Senussi Cave Railway lead from a military road that was blasted into the mountains on 1941 above a wadi east of Tobruk harbour to the Senussi Cave." .

lsd:Entity_47331 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Biber_Danube interglacial (German: Biber_Donau_Interglazial) or Biber_Danube interglacial period (Biber_Donau_Warmzeit) is a historical term for a hypothetical interglacial period of the Pleistocene epoch in the Alps, between the Biber and Danube glaciations." .

lsd:Entity_47334 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1974 Race of Champions was a non_championship Formula One race held at Brands Hatch on 17 01 March 1974. The 40_lap race featured both Formula One and Formula 5000 cars, and was won by Belgian driver Jacky Ickx in a Lotus_Cosworth, with Austria's Niki Lauda second in a Ferrari and Brazil's Emerson Fittipaldi third in a McLaren_Cosworth." .

lsd:Entity_47367 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2013 World Aquatics Championships – women's 800 metre freestyle event in swimming at the 2013 World Aquatics Championships took place on 2 August–3 August at the Palau Sant Jordi in Barcelona." .

lsd:Entity_47421 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1952 Open Championship was the 81st The Open Championship, held 9 July–11 July at Royal Lytham & St Annes Golf Club in Lytham St Annes, England." .

lsd:Entity_47441 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Adwa (Amharic: አድዋ; Tigrinya: ዓድዋ; Italian Adua, also spelled Adowa) was the climactic battle of the First Italo_Ethiopian War. The Ethiopian forces, who had high numerical superiority and weapons supplied by Russia and France, defeated the Italian invading force on Sunday 01 March 1896, near the town of Adwa. Adwa became a pre_eminent symbol of pan_Africanism and secured Ethiopia's sovereignty until the Second Italo_Ethiopian War forty years later. As a result, Italy and Ethiopia came into confrontation, in what was later to be known as the First Italo_Ethiopian War. Other sources assert that Leontiev did not in fact participate in the battle, rather he visited Ethiopia first unofficially in January 1895, and then officially as a representative of Russia in August 1895, but then left later that year, only to return after the Battle of Adwa. On the night of 29 February and the early morning of 01 March 1896, three Italian brigades advanced separately towards Adwa over narrow mountain tracks, while a fourth remained camped. As a direct result of the battle, Italy signed the Treaty of Addis Ababa, recognizing Ethiopia as an independent state. \"The confrontation between Italy and Ethiopia at Adwa was a fundamental turning point in Ethiopian history,\" writes Henze. On a similar note, the Ethiopian historian Bahru Zewde observed that \"few events in the modern period have brought Ethiopia to the attention of the world as has the victory at Adwa\". As the Afrocentric scholar Molefe Asante explains, After the victory over Italy in 1896, Ethiopia acquired a special importance in the eyes of Africans and black people all over the world alike ,as the only surviving African State that successfully defeated a European colonial power in open battle. Indeed, one student of Ethiopia's History, Donald N. Levine, points out that for the Italians Adwa \"became a national trauma which demagogic leaders strove to avenge. The Victory of Adwa is a public holiday in all regional states and charter cities across Ethiopia. In addition to Addis Ababa, other major cities in Ethiopia, including Bahir Dar, Debre Markos and the town of Adwa itself, where the battle took place, celebrate the Victory of Adwa in public ceremonies." .

lsd:Entity_47495 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Schweppes Oaks, registered as the Australasian Oaks, is a South Australian Jockey Club Group 1 Thoroughbred horse race for three_year_old fillies at Set Weights run over a distance of 2000 metres at Morphettville Racecourse, Adelaide, Australia in the SAJC Autumn Carnival." .

lsd:Entity_47556 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Macau Masters was a non_ranking team and six_red snooker variant invitational event that took place on 24 October 2018 and 25 October 2018." .

lsd:Entity_47587 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Greece participated in the Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015 in Vienna, Austria. Maria Elena Kyriakou was chosen to represent Greece in the 2015 contest with the song \"One Last Breath\". Prior to the 2015 contest, Greece had participated in the Eurovision Song Contest thirty_six times since its first entry in 1974, winning it in 2005 with the song \"My Number One\" performed by Elena Paparizou, and having placed third three times: in 2001 with the song \"Die for You\" performed by the duo Antique; in 2004 with \"Shake It\" performed by Sakis Rouvas; and in 2008 with \"Secret Combination\" performed by Kalomoira. On 24 July 2014 Greece confirmed their participation in the 2015 contest. Eurosong 2015 _ NERIT & MAD Show (60 years of music) was the Greek national final developed by NERIT to select the Greek entry for the Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest 2015. In addition to the performances of the competing entries, the interval acts featured guest performances by Eurovision Song Contest 2005 winner Helena Paparizou with HouseTwins, 2002 Cypriot Eurovision entrants One, 2011 Greek Eurovision entrant Loukas Giorkas, Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest 2014 Freaky Fortune and Riskykidd with the hosts Mary Sinatsaki and Ntoretta Papadimitriou, Greece in the Eurovision Song Contest Thanos Kalliris with the group Kings, Boys and Noise and 2015 Cypriot Eurovision entrant Giannis Karagiannis. Greece was placed into the first semi_final, to be held on 2015, and was scheduled to perform in the first half of the show." .

lsd:Entity_47614 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Capture of Cadiz in 1596 was an event during the Anglo_Spanish War, when English and Dutch troops under Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex and a large Anglo_Dutch fleet under Charles Howard, 1st Earl of Nottingham, with support from the Dutch United Provinces, raided the Spain city of Cádiz. In order to deny the raiders their prize the Spain set fire to their fleet anchored in the Bay of Cádiz; the attacking forces disembarked, captured, sacked, and burned the city and took hostage several of the city's prominent citizens, who were taken back to England to await payment of their ransom. At that moment there were in the Bay of Cádiz some 40 Spain ships, ranging from galleys to galleons, as well as 16 other vessels from the Spain convoy, which were disarmed and ready to depart for the West Indies." .

lsd:Entity_47620 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Discordianism is a paradigm based upon the book Principia Discordia, written by Malaclypse the Younger with Kerry Wendell Thornley in 1963, the two working under the pseudonyms Malaclypse the Younger and Omar Khayyam Ravenhurst. According to self_proclaimed \"crackpot historian\" Adam Gorightly, Discordianism was founded as a parody religion. Many outside observers still regard Discordianism as a parody religion, although some of its adherents use it as a legitimate religion or as a metaphor for a governing philosophy. The foundational document of Discordianism is the Principia Discordia, fourth edition, written by Malaclypse the Younger, an alias of Malaclypse the Younger. The Principia Discordia is a Discordian religious text written by Malaclypse the Younger (Malaclypse the Younger) with Kerry Wendell Thornley (Lord Omar Khayyam Ravenhurst).The phrase Principia Discordia, reminiscent of Newton's Principia Mathematica, is presumably intended to mean Discordant Principles, or Principles of Discordance. It is a compilation of early Discordian photos, tracts, art collages, and more including works by Discordianism founders Malaclypse the Younger (Malaclypse the Younger) and Kerry Wendell Thornley (Omar Khayyam Ravenhurst)." .

lsd:Entity_47694 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The shooting at the 2004 Summer Olympics – men's 50 metre rifle three positions competition at the 2004 Summer Olympics was held on 22 August 2004 at the Markopoulo Olympic Shooting Centre near Athens, Greece." .

lsd:Entity_47721 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2006 English Badminton Championships were held in Manchester on 03 February 2006–5, 2006." .

lsd:Entity_47821 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1964 United States presidential election in Alaska took place on 03 November 1964, as part of the 1964 United States presidential election. This is the first and only presidential election when Alaska has ever voted for a Democratic candidate, partially due to Johnson’s landslide victory throughout the rest of the country (except for the Deep South and Goldwater’s home state), and partially because during its first four presidential elections Alaska was not anomalously Republican relative to the nation at_large, which the state would become and remain very strongly from 1976 onwards." .

