lsd:Entity_50472 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Croatian Football Cup Final was a two_legged affair played between NK Istra 1961 and H.N.K. Hajduk Split." .

lsd:Entity_50483 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of LaFourche Crossing (also spelled Lafourche Crossing) was a battle in Lafourche Parish, United States and fought on 20 June 1863–21, 1863, during the American Civil War as part of a campaign known as Taylor’s Operations in West Louisiana." .

lsd:Entity_50487 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Open Seguros Bolívar was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Bogotá, Colombia between 11 July 2011 and 17 July 2011." .

lsd:Entity_50493 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Festival d'Avignon, or Avignon Festival, is an annual arts festival held in the France city of Avignon every summer in July in the courtyard of the Palais des Papes as well as in other locations of the city. Founded in 1947 by Jean Vilar, it is the oldest existent festival in France and one of the world's greatest. Alongside the official festival, the \"In\" one, a number of shows are presented in Avignon at the same time of the year and are known as the \"Off\". The festival is organised by a non_profit organisation (since 1980), which is administered by a board of trustees composed of: the France state, the city of Avignon, the Département of Vaucluse, the Région Provence_Alpes_Côte d'Azur, and seven public figures competent in the field of theatre. The Festival d'Avignon was founded by Jean Vilar in 1947. The very first Festival d'Avignon in 1947 set the scene as a showcase for unknown work and modern scripts. On the crest of the wave of contemporary tendencies to revolutionize theatrical practices, in 1966 Avignon's Théâtre des Carmes, co_founded by André Benedetto and Bertrand Hurault, staged a festival « Off », unofficial and independent. Due to the anti_authoritarian protest movements of May 1968, the 22nd edition of the Festival of Avignon hosted virtually no France shows, halving the number of planned shows down from a total of 83. Archives on the works of Jean Vilar and the totality of the 3,000 representations programmed by the Festival of Avignon since its 1947 debut are conserved at Maison Jean_Vilar, located in Avignon at 8, rue de Mons, Montée Paul_Puaux." .

lsd:Entity_50496 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980–81 FIBA European Cup Winner's Cup was the fifteenth edition of FIBA Europe's 2nd_tier level European_wide professional club basketball competition, contested between national domestic cup champions, running from 07 October 1980, to 18 March 1981. Pallacanestro Cantù defeated FC Barcelona, in the final held in Rome, winning the FIBA Europe European Cup Winner's Cup for a (European record) fourth time." .

lsd:Entity_50531 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "London Book Fair (LBF) is a large book_publishing trade fair held annually, usually in April, in London, England. Until 2006 the London Book Fair had been held at the Olympia exhibition centre, but it moved to the ExCeL Exhibition Centre in London's Docklands that year. Due to generally unfavourable feedback from attendees over the new location, such as the inconvenience of transport links or the infrastructure of the location, as well as the intervention resulting from other exhibitions and their wandering visitors, the book fair returned to west London in 2007 and took place at Earls Court Exhibition Centre from 16 to 18 April." .

lsd:Entity_50575 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Apostolicae curae is the title of a papal bull, issued in 1896 by Leo XIII, declaring all Anglican ordinations to be \"absolutely null and utterly void\". The final result was the papal bull Apostolicae curae, in which Anglican orders were declared to be invalid. Catholic World Report of 10 May 2017 published a comment by Edward Peters: The news item in The Tablet referred to Apostolicae curae as simply \"Leo XIII’s remarks\", although it was arguably an infallible exercise of papal extraordinary magisterium, or at least \"a prominent exercise of the ordinary papal magisterium which coalesced with several centuries of other ordinary exercises of papal_episcopal magisterium in rejecting the validity of Anglican orders to the point that Catholics must hold them invalid\", as indicated in the official commentary that accompanied the Apostolic Letter Ad tuendam fidem. The congregation's commentary listed Leo XIII's declaration in Apostolicae curae on the invalidity of Anglican ordinations as an example of \"those truths connected to revelation by historical necessity and which are to be held definitively, but are not able to be declared as divinely revealed\". In the essay, approved by the Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith, Ghirlanda commented that \"the ordination of ministers coming from Anglicanism will be absolute, on the basis of the Bull Apostolicae curae of Leo XIII of 13 September 1896\"." .

lsd:Entity_50583 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2011 Toray Pan Pacific Open was a women's tennis tournament played on outdoor hard courts. It was the 28th edition of the Pan Pacific Open, and was part of the Premier Series of the 2011 WTA Tour. It took place at the Ariake Coliseum in Tokyo, Japan, from September 26 through 2011." .

lsd:Entity_50597 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1949 FIVB Men's Volleyball World Championship was the first edition of the tournament, organised by the world's governing body, the Fédération Internationale de Volleyball. It was held in Prague, Czechoslovakia from 10 to 18 September 1949." .

lsd:Entity_50626 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2002 Islington London Borough Council election took place on 02 May 2002 to elect members of Islington London Borough Council in London, England. The whole council was up for election with boundary changes since the 1998 Islington London Borough Council election reducing the number of seats by 4." .

lsd:Entity_50651 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "1963 Peruvian general election were held in Peru on 09 June 1963 to elect the President and both houses of the Congress after the results of the 1962 Peruvian general election were annulled following a military coup." .

lsd:Entity_50657 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2003 Campeonato Brasileiro Série A was the 47th edition of the Campeonato Brasileiro Série A. The first edition with only a double round_robin and no playoffs, it began on 29 March 2003, and reached its end on 14 December 2003." .

lsd:Entity_50679 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1911 Ontario general election was the 13th general election held in the Province of Ontario, Canada. It was held on 11 December 1911, to elect the 106 Members of the 13th Legislative Assembly of Ontario (titled as Members of the Provincial Parliament or M.P.P.). The Ontario Conservative Party, led by Sir James Whitney, was elected for a third consecutive term in government, with a slight reduced majority in the Legislature." .

lsd:Entity_50692 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1950 Volta a Catalunya was the 30th edition of the Volta a Catalunya cycle race and was held from 17 September 1950 to 24 September 1950." .

lsd:Entity_50729 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010–11 Liga Leumit was the twelfth season since its introduction in 1999 and the 69th season of second_tier football in Israel. A total of sixteen teams contested the league, including twelve sides from the 2009–10 Liga Leumit, two promoted teams from the 2009–10 Liga Alef and two relegated teams from the 2009–10 2010–11 Israeli Premier League. Ironi Kiryat Shmona and Hapoel Ashkelon were directly promoted to the 2010–11 Israeli Premier League after finishing the 2009–10 Liga Leumit in the two top places. Maccabi Ahi Nazareth and Hapoel Ra'anana were directly relegated to the 2010–11 Liga Leumit after finishing the 2009–10 Liga Leumit in the bottom two places." .

lsd:Entity_50738 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1956 United States presidential election in Minnesota took place on 06 November 1956 as part of the 1956 United States presidential election. Minnesota was won by the Republican Party candidate, incumbent President of the United States Dwight D. Eisenhower won the state over former Illinois governor Adlai Stevenson by a margin of 101,777 votes, or 7.6%. The 1956 presidential election was a rematch of the 1952 election, in which Eisenhower also defeated Stevenson, both nationally and in Minnesota. As of the 2020 presidential election, this is the last time Minnesota was carried twice by the Republican nominee. The Democratic nomination campaign leading into 1956 presidential election may have had a major role in the end of the political career of Coya Knutson, the first woman to be elected to the United States House of Representatives from Minnesota." .

lsd:Entity_50743 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Finnish Championships in Athletics were held in the Tampere Stadium in Tampere from 24 July 2008 to 27 July 2008." .

lsd:Entity_50748 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Indian Wells Masters, also known as BNP Paribas Open and the WTA Indian Wells Open, is an annual tennis tournament held in early_ and mid_March at the Indian Wells Tennis Garden in Indian Wells, United States. The tournament is a Masters 1000 event on the Association of Tennis Professionals and is a Premier Mandatory event on the Women's Tennis Association. The tournament is played on hardcourt and is the best_attended tennis tournament outside the four Grand Slam tournaments. The tournament is played in the Indian Wells Tennis Garden (built in 2000) which has 29 tennis courts, including the 16,100_seat main stadium, which is the second largest tennis_specific stadium in the world. After the 2013 BNP Paribas Open the Indian Wells Tennis Garden started an expansion and upgrade of its facilities that includes a new 8,000 seat Stadium 2. The revamping of the tennis center also included a \"Pro Purple\" interior court color created specifically for the ATP Masters Series and first used at Indian Wells, citing the purple color being 180 degrees and exactly opposite the yellow of the ball. In 1996, the championship became one of the few fully combined events on both the Association of Tennis Professionals and Women's Tennis Association tours. The BNP Paribas Open has become one of the largest events on both men's and Women's Tennis Association." .

lsd:Entity_50765 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Newry and Armagh by_election, 1986 was caused by the resignation of incumbent Member of Parliament Jim Nicholson. The poll was held on 23 January 1986, and was unusual for a by_election in the turnout being higher than for the preceding general election, however Nicholson was not re_elected, (despite gaining an increase in his own vote) losing his seat to the nationalist SDLP candidate Seamus Mallon." .

lsd:Entity_50797 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Metaurus was a pivotal battle in the Second Punic War between Roman Republic and Carthage, fought in 207 BC near the Metauro River in Italy. The battle confirmed Roman supremacy over Italy. Dexter Hoyos believes Hasdrubal's death was foolish, as he could have given organisation and leadership to the remnants of the Carthaginian army and posed a lingering threat to Roman Republic in northern Italy. It is included in Edward Shepherd Creasy's The Fifteen Decisive Battles of the World (1851), the rationale being that it effectively removed the Carthaginian threat from Roman Republic's ascendancy to global dominion by leaving Hannibal stranded in Italy. Paul K. Davis sees its importance as the \"Carthaginian defeat ended the attempt to reinforce Hannibal, dooming his effort in Italy, and Roman Republic was able to establish dominance over Spain.\" The Battle of the Metaurus is overshadowed by other battles of the Second Punic War, such as Hannibal's great victory at the Battle of Cannae or his ultimate defeat at the Battle of Zama. F. L. Lucas's short story \"The Fortune of Carthage\" (Athenaeum, 28 January 1921) is about the battle's prelude, from Claudius Nero's viewpoint." .

lsd:Entity_50874 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 13th Chess Olympiad, organized by FIDE and comprising an open team tournament, as well as several other events designed to promote the game of chess, took place between 30 September 1958 and 23 October 1958, in Munich, West Germany." .

lsd:Entity_50878 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 698, adopted unanimously on 14 June 1991, after recalling Resolution 682 (1990) dealing with financial issues and all resolutions on Cyprus up to the most recent United Nations Security Council Resolution 697 (1991), the Council expressed its concern regarding the financial situation facing the United Nations Peacekeeping Force in Cyprus (UNFICYP), established in Resolution 186 (1964)." .

lsd:Entity_50895 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Pickens Forest was a U.S. Marine Corps operation in Quảng Nam, South Vietnam that took place from 16 July to 24 August 1970. Operation Pickens Forest concluded on 24 August 1970, the Marines had lost 4 killed and the PAVN/VC 99 killed." .

lsd:Entity_50964 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Deligrad was fought between Serbia and the Ottoman Empire in 1806. The First Serbian Uprising had begun in 1804 with the expulsion of the ruling janissary elite and the proclamation of an independent Serbia state by the revolution's leader, Karađorđe. The Serbia high command decided to meet the Ottoman Empire force under Ibrahim Bushati, pasha of Shkodër, at Deligrad. The Serbia rebels also attacked the Ottoman Empire positions numerous times and managed to capture nine Ottoman Empire cannons. The battle provided a decisive victory for the Serbs and bolstered the morale of the outnumbered Serbia rebels." .

lsd:Entity_50981 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Neretva '93 was an Army of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina (ARBiH) operation against the Croatian Defence Council (HVO) in 01 September 1993 on a 200 km long front from Gornji Vakuf to south of Mostar, one of its largest of the year, on areas which were included in the Croatian Republic of Herzeg_Bosnia." .

lsd:Entity_50989 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Wild West Show were traveling vaudeville performances in the United States and Europe that existed around 1870–1920. Western Films in the first half of the 20th century filled the gap left behind by Wild West Show." .

lsd:Entity_51001 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Agadir air disaster was a chartered Boeing 707 passenger flight on Sunday, 03 August 1975, that crashed into a mountain on approach to Agadir_Inezgane Airport." .

lsd:Entity_51038 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2001 Sicilian regional election was held on 24 June 2001. The election was competed by three competitors: Salvatore Cuffaro, House of Freedoms candidate; Leoluca Orlando, candidate of The Olive Tree and former mayor of Palermo; and Sergio D'Antoni, leader of European Democracy." .

lsd:Entity_5105 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 United States Grand Prix (formally the 2014 Formula 1 United States Grand Prix) was a Formula One motor race held on 02 November 2014 at the Circuit of the Americas in Travis County near Austin. It was the 17th round of the 2014 Formula One World Championship and the 36th running of the United States Grand Prix held as part of the series. The 56_lap race was won by Mercedes driver Lewis Hamilton after starting from second position. The 2014 United States Grand Prix was the 17th of the 19 rounds of the 2014 Formula One World Championship, and the 36th running of the United States Grand Prix in Formula One. It was held on 02 November 2014 at the 5.513_kilometre (3.426 mi) 20_turn Circuit of the Americas in Travis County, Austin. The United States Grand Prix's official name was the 2014 Formula 1 United States Grand Prix. Before the race, Mercedes driver Lewis Hamilton led the World Drivers' Championship with 291 points, ahead of teammate Nico Rosberg in second and Daniel Ricciardo third. It was Hamilton's tenth win of 2014, his second in Austin, and the 32nd of his career, breaking Nigel Mansell's record for the highest number of race victories for a British driver." .

lsd:Entity_51079 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012 Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council election took place on 03 May 2012 to elect members of Tameside Metropolitan Borough Council in England." .

lsd:Entity_51090 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2018 European Athletics Championships – men's high jump at the 2018 European Athletics Championships took place at the Olympiastadion Berlin on 9 August and 11 August." .

lsd:Entity_51092 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 2014 Asian Games – women's 800 metres event at the athletics at the 2014 Asian Games was held at the Incheon Asiad Main Stadium, Incheon, South Korea on 30 September – 1 October." .

lsd:Entity_51100 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1970 Wisconsin gubernatorial election was held on 1970. Democrat Patrick Lucey won the election with 54% of the vote, winning his first term as Governor of Wisconsin and defeating Republican Jack B. Olson." .

lsd:Entity_51122 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2012–13 Saudi Professional League (officially known as the Zain League, because of its sponsorship) was the 37th season of Saudi Professional League since its establishment in 1976. On 14, Al_Fateh SC won the title after a 1–0 home win over Al_Ahli." .

