lsd:Entity_52732 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Winter Soldier: Iraq & Afghanistan was an event at which more than 200 U.S. military veterans and active duty soldiers, as well as Iraqi and Afghan civilians, provided accounts of their experiences in Iraq and Afghanistan. It was organized by Iraq Veterans Against the War, and held from 13 March 2008 to 16 March 2008, timed for the fifth anniversary of the 2003 invasion of Iraq, at the National Labor College in Silver Spring, Maryland." .

lsd:Entity_52750 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Placilla was an engagement fought during the Chilean Civil War of 1891 between Balmacedist and Congressional forces on 28 August 1891." .

lsd:Entity_52768 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Shania Now Tour was the fourth headlining concert tour by Canadian recording artist Shania Twain, in support of her fifth studio album Now (2017)." .

lsd:Entity_52814 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Nobel Peace Prize was shared, in two equal parts, between the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) and Al Gore \"for their efforts to build up and disseminate greater knowledge about man_made climate change, and to lay the foundations for the measures that are needed to counteract such change\". The award was given immediate publicity: an Associated Press article published by USA Today on 2007 and headlined \"Gore, scientists share Nobel Peace Prize\" quoted Pachauri as saying \"All the scientists that have contributed to the work of the IPCC are the Nobel laureates who have been recognized and acknowledged by the Nobel Prize Committee\". On the same day, the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory listed its scientists who had contributed to the IPCC's work, and said that Pachauri had sent a letter to lead authors of the 2007 IPCC Fourth Assessment Report saying that he had \"been stunned in a pleasant way with the news of the award of the Nobel Peace Prize for the IPCC. In Oslo on 2007, the presentation was made with a speech by Ole Danbolt Mjøs as Chairman of the Norwegian Nobel Committee, and followed by Nobel Lectures given by Rajendra K. Pachauri, representing the IPCC, and Al Gore. The IPCC presented scientists who had \"contributed substantially to the preparation of IPCC reports\" with personalized certificates for \"contributing to the award of the Nobel Peace Prize for 2007 to the IPCC\". The certificates, which name the individual and feature a reproduction of the Nobel Peace Prize diploma, were sent to \"coordinating lead authors, lead authors, review editors, bureau members, staff of the technical support units and staff of the secretariat from the IPCC's inception in 1988 until the award of the prize in 2007.\" It would be correct to describe a scientist who was involved with AR4 or earlier IPCC reports in this way: 'X contributed to the reports of the IPCC, which was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 2007.'\"" .

lsd:Entity_52818 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1884 United States presidential election in Texas was held on 04 November 1884, as part of the 1884 United States presidential election." .

lsd:Entity_5284 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2014 U.S. Open Cup Final was played on 16 September 2014, at Subaru Park in Chester, Pennsylvania. Seattle Sounders FC won the match, defeating the Philadelphia Union. The United States Soccer Federation determined the host of the final with a coin flip on August 4, 2014. The winner of the toss was whoever was victorious in the FC Dallas/Philadelphia Union semifinal, meaning that the game would take place at Subaru Park in Chester, Pennsylvania. Attendance at Subaru Park was 15,256, the lowest for an Open Cup final in six years." .

lsd:Entity_52848 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1975 Chatham Cup was the 48th annual nationwide knockout association football competition in New Zealand. Christchurch United successfully defended their trophy, and also became only the second team to win the Chatham Cup/National League double." .

lsd:Entity_52859 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Dhalai was a battle fought between Pakistan and India before the start 1971 India_Pakistan War for the Bangladesh Liberation War. The battle started after an attack by Indian army on Pakistani border outpost (BOP) in East Pakistan on 28 October 1971 and lasted until 03 November 1971." .

lsd:Entity_52878 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The New Market train wreck happened when two Southern Railway passenger trains traveling at great speed collided head on near New Market, Tennessee, on Saturday, 24 September 1904, killing at least 56 passengers and crew and injuring 106." .

lsd:Entity_52948 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1795 French Directory election were held from 12 October to 1795 (20 Vendémiaire to 13 Brumaire Year IV) Constitution of the Year III. The election served as an alarm signal for the Republican political forces, as the monarchists, in a landslide, obtained 87 out of 161 elected seats inside the Legislative Body, as well a sympathizer Director: General Lazare Carnot, formerly a Jacobin and revolutionary chief." .

lsd:Entity_52960 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Paris–Roubaix was the 98th running of the Paris–Roubaix single_day cycling race, often known as the Hell of the North. It was held on 09 April 2000 over a distance of 272 kilometres (169.0 miles). These are the results for the 2000 edition of the Paris–Roubaix cycling classic, in which Johan Museeuw took his second Roubaix win. 9_04_2000: Compiègne–Roubaix." .

lsd:Entity_52963 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 Tour de Georgia was a seven_stage professional bicycle race held from 21 April 2008 through 27 April 2008 across the state of Georgia." .

lsd:Entity_52967 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1977 World Rowing Championships was the 6th World Rowing Championships. The championships were held from 19 to 28 August 1977 on the Bosbaan rowing lake in Amsterdam, Netherlands." .

lsd:Entity_52971 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Roan's Tan Yard, also known as the Battle of Silver Creek, was a minor battle fought during the American Civil War on 08 January 1862, in Randolph County. In 08 January 1862, Major W. M. G. Torrence of the Union Army located a Missouri State Guard base in Randolph County and attacked it on 08 January 1862 with elements of four cavalry regiments. When the American Civil War began in early 1861, the state of Missouri was politically divided. The Missouri State Guard then formed a camp in the vicinity of Yates, Missouri, in Randolph County, to attract and train new recruits. While scouting near Silver Creek in 08 January 1862, Union major, W. M. G. Torrence, of the 1st Iowa Cavalry Regiment, learned of the presence of a Missouri State Guard camp in the area. After the battle, the camp was destroyed; with the camp unusable, Missouri State Guard recruiting activities in Randolph County ceased." .

lsd:Entity_5300 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1954 Rugby Union European Cup was the fifth rugby union European championship, organised by Rugby Europe and the second (and last) with this name. As in the 1952 Rugby Union European Cup, France won the title after a victory over Italy in the final." .

lsd:Entity_53030 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1972 NCAA Skiing Championships were contested in Winter Park, Colorado Buffaloes at the 19th annual NCAA_sanctioned ski tournament to determine the individual and team national champions of men's collegiate alpine, cross country skiing, and ski jumping in the United States of America. This year's championships were contested at the Winter Park Resort in Winter Park, Colorado Buffaloes." .

lsd:Entity_53035 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Dual Alliance, 1879 (German: Doppelte Allianz, Hungarian: Kettős Szövetség) was a defensive alliance between Germany and Austria_Hungary, which was created by treaty on October 7th, 1879 as part of Germany's Otto von Bismarck's system of alliances to prevent or limit war. When Austria_Hungary and Germany formed an alliance in 1879, it was one of the more surprising alliances of its time." .

lsd:Entity_53053 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2008 World Championship of Ski Mountaineering was the fourth World Championships of Ski Mountaineering sanctioned by the International Council for Ski Mountaineering Competitions (ISMC), held in Portes du Soleil, Switzerland, from February 23 to 2008." .

lsd:Entity_53059 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2004 European Cup took place on 2004 and 2004 at the Zdzisław Krzyszkowiak Stadium in Bydgoszcz, Poland. This was the 25th European Cup and the first time the event has been hosted in Poland." .

lsd:Entity_53105 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2013 Sport 1 Open was a professional tennis tournament played on clay courts. It took place in Scheveningen, Netherlands between 08 July 2013 and 2013." .

lsd:Entity_53115 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2015–16 Football League (Greece) is the second division of the Greek professional football system and the sixth season under the name Stoiximan.gr Football League after previously being known as Beta Ethniki. Its season began on 27 September 2015 and ended on 29 01 May 2016." .

lsd:Entity_53138 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 44th Daytime Creative Arts Emmy Awards ceremony, which honors the crafts behind American daytime television programming, was held at the Pasadena Conference Center in Pasadena on 28 April 2017." .

lsd:Entity_53141 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1985 Scheldeprijs was the 72nd edition of the Scheldeprijs cycle race and was held on 30 July 1985." .

lsd:Entity_53168 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Empire Slovak Open (previously known as the Empire Trnava Cup) is a tournament for professional female tennis players played on outdoor clay court. The event is classified as a $100,000 ITF Women's Circuit tournament and has been held in Trnava, Slovakia, since 2009." .

lsd:Entity_53196 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Porcelain Tour was a 19 date concert tour by Matt Cardle in support of his 2013 album Porcelain." .

lsd:Entity_53212 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1980 United States presidential election in Montana took place on 04 November 1980, and was part of the 1980 United States presidential election. Montana overwhelmingly voted for the Republican nominee, Governor Ronald Reagan, over the Democratic nominee, President of the United States Jimmy Carter." .

lsd:Entity_53214 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "2011 French cantonal elections to elect half the membership of the general councils of France's 100 departments were held on 20 March 2011 and 27 March 2011. Since cantonal councillors are electors for the senatorial elections, and considering that the forces in the Senate are, as of 20 March 2011 almost balanced, the results of this election could play a role in the September 2011 senatorial elections." .

lsd:Entity_53245 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Ronnie Thomas was crowned NASCAR Rookie of the Year in a tight battle with Roger Hamby." .

lsd:Entity_53267 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of El Adde took place on 15 January 2016. Al_Shabaab militants launched an attack on a Kenyan_run AMISOM army base in the town of El Adde, Gedo, Somalia. On 15 January 2016, at 6:30 a.m before morning prayers, a suicide bomber detonated his explosive laden armoured personnel carrier to the front gate to an AMISOM garrison base in El_Adde, Gedo, Somalia." .

lsd:Entity_53320 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Russia participated in the Russia in the Eurovision Song Contest 2008 in Belgrade, Serbia. Dima Bilan represented Russia with the song \"Believe\", which qualified from the first semi_final of the competition and won the contest, placing 1st in the final with 272 points. The final took place on 2008 at the Akademicheskiy Concert Hall in Moscow, hosted by Oxana Fedorova and Oskar Kuchera. Among the competing artists, Dima Bilan represented Russia in the Eurovision Song Contest 2006; Premyer_Ministr represented Russia in the Eurovision Song Contest 2002 and Polina Smolova represented Belarus in the Eurovision Song Contest 2006. With changes to the format of the Eurovision Song Contest coming into effect in 2008, Russia was required to compete in one of two semi_finals in order to qualify to compete in the final. In the allocation draw on 2008, Russia was drawn to compete in the first semi_final on 2008. On 2008 during the draw for running order, Russia was drawn to receive the third of three wildcards for the first semi_final, allowing the Russian head of delegation to select the starting position for the nation. Russia won the 2008 Contest placing 1st and scoring 272 points. As the winning nation of the 2008 Contest and the host of the forthcoming contest, Russia pre_qualified to compete in the final of the Eurovision Song Contest 2009." .

lsd:Entity_53331 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1030, adopted unanimously on 14 December 1995, after recalling previous resolutions 968 (1994) and 999 (1995) on the situation in Tajikistan, the Council extended the mandate of the United Nations Mission of Observers in Tajikistan (UNMOT) until 15 June 1996 and addressed the process of national reconciliation in the country." .

lsd:Entity_53371 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The swimming at the 2000 Summer Olympics – women's 100 metre backstroke event at the 2000 Summer Olympics took place on 17 September 2000–18 September at the Sydney Olympic Park Aquatic Centre in Sydney, Australia. At only 16 years of age, Diana Mocanu made an Olympic milestone to become Romania's first ever gold medalist in swimming." .

lsd:Entity_53375 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "TWA Flight 800 (1964) was a Boeing 707 that crashed during take off on runway 25 at Leonardo da Vinci–Fiumicino Airport, Rome at 13:09 GMT on a flight to Athens International Airport, Greece on 23 November 1964. The accident killed 50 passengers and crew on board, with 23 passengers and crew surviving." .

lsd:Entity_53402 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "United Nations Security Council Resolution 1501, adopted unanimously on 26 August 2003, after recalling all previous United Nations Security Council resolution on the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, particularly United Nations Security Council resolution 1484 (2003) and 1493 (2003), authorised countries participating in Operation Artemis in Bunia to assist the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUC) as it was deployed around the town." .

lsd:Entity_53413 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2007 Karbala bombings refer to a series of bombings in Karbala, Iraq in 14 April 2007. A suicide bomber killed at least 42 people in Karbala on 14 April 2007 and injured more than 160." .

lsd:Entity_53417 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1986 English Professional Championship was a professional non_ranking snooker tournament, which took place in 04 February 1986 in Ipswich, England." .

lsd:Entity_53432 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Senior women's race at the 1992 IAAF World Cross Country Championships – senior women's race was held in Boston, Massachusetts, United States, at Franklin Park on 1992." .

lsd:Entity_5346 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Four Days' Battle, also known as the Four Days' Fight in some English sources and as Vierdaagse Zeeslag in Dutch, was a naval battle of the Second Anglo_Dutch War. Fought from 1 June to 4 14 June 1666 in the Julian or Old Style calendar that was then used in England, in the southern North Sea, it began off the Flemish coast and ended near the English coast. Dutch accounts referred to its dates as 11 June 1666 to 14 June 1666 by using the New Style calendar. Second Anglo_Dutch War arose from an escalation of existing commercial tensions between England and the Netherlands in 1664, involving English provocations in North America and West Africa. After Battle of Lowestoft, and concerned that the complete destruction of the Dutch fleet would leave the English fleet in a position to interfere with his plans in the Spanish Netherlands, Louis again offered mediation, but as he had already sent French troops into the Netherlands to assist the Dutch, and had also attempted to bring Denmark into an alliance with the Dutch Republic and France that was designed to put pressure on England, his credibility as mediator was undermined. The biggest sea battle of the Second Anglo_Dutch War and in the age of sail was undoubtedly a Dutch victory although both sides initially claimed they had won. After this battle, while the Dutch fleet was being repaired an English squadron, entered the Vlie estuary and burned 150 out of a fleet of 160 merchant ships, inflicting severe economic damage on the Netherlands. England had gone to war in the expectation of an early victory that would not overstretch its government's fragile financial position, but both it and the Netherlands had put such unprecedented efforts into providing ships and men that no decisive naval victory was possible, and severe English financial difficulties and the Dutch need to resume unrestricted commercial activity created the conditions for peace without resolving all the underlying causes for the conflict. The Four Days' Battle is dramatized in the Dutch film Michiel de Ruyter (2015), although it is not clear which phase of the battle is shown." .

lsd:Entity_53480 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Bluff Cove Air Attacks occurred 08 June 1982, during the Falklands War." .

lsd:Entity_535 a lsd:Context_narration ;
