lsd:Entity_55529 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Texas International Pop Festival was a music festival held at Lewisville, Texas, on Labor Day weekend, August 30 to September 1, 1969. On January 29, 2010, the Texas Historical Commission approved the placement of a state historical marker near Hebron Station, a Denton County Transportation Authority train station in eastern Lewisville, close to the former site of the festival stage." .

lsd:Entity_55535 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Dream 9 was a mixed martial arts event promoted by Fighting and Entertainment Group's mixed martial arts promotion Dream on 26 May 2009." .

lsd:Entity_5557 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2010 World Rowing Championships were World Rowing Championships that were held from 31 October 2010 to 07 November 2010 on Lake Karapiro near Cambridge, New Zealand. The annual week_long rowing regatta was organised by World Rowing Federation (the International Rowing Federation). The World Rowing Championships were previously held at Lake Karapiro in 1978. Rowing's international body said Lake Karapiro's 2010 World Rowing Championships raised the bar for the rest of the world and more international events would be held there. The event lost $2.2m and a report by SPARC (Sport And Recreation New Zealand, rebranded as Sport New Zealand in 2012) found that a lack of clarity around roles and responsibilities of the Karapiro 2010 Board was a factor, alongside others mostly relating to shortcomings in governance, inadequate financial management, and less revenue than expected from ticket sales. With the roaring success of the world championships behind them, Rowing New Zealand is eager to make further use of their world_class facility at Lake Karapiro." .

lsd:Entity_55586 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2000 Stockholm Open was an ATP men's tennis tournament played on hard courts, and held in Stockholm, Sweden. The tournament was held from 20 November 2000 through 26 November 2000." .

lsd:Entity_55601 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 Paris_Tours is the 103rd edition of this single day road bicycle racing event and is organized by the Amaury Sport Organisation (ASO), which also runs the Tour de France. The 230 km event took place on October 11, 2009 and was won by Philippe Gilbert, the Belgian rider for Silence–Lotto in 5 hours, 12 minutes, 23 seconds. Gilbert, along with countryman Tom Boonen (Quick Step) and Slovenia's Borut Božič (Vacansoleil), escaped with 7 km to go at the base of the Côte de l'Epan behind an attack from Gilbert's teammate Greg Van Avermaet." .

lsd:Entity_55615 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 3rd Golden Melody Awards ceremony (Chinese: 第三屆金曲獎) was held at the Sun Yat_sen Memorial Hall in Taipei on 09 November 1991." .

lsd:Entity_5563 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "Operation Perth was an Australian military operation in Urozgan undertaken in 01 July 2006 during the Afghanistan War." .

lsd:Entity_55639 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Cokeville Elementary School hostage crisis occurred on Friday, 16 May 1986, in Cokeville, United States of America, when former town marshal David Young, 43, and his wife Doris Young, 47, took 136 children and 18 adults hostage at Cokeville Elementary School. David and Doris both returned to Cokeville on 16 May 1986." .

lsd:Entity_55670 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1960 U_2 incident occurred on 01 May 1960, when an American U_2 spy plane was shot down by the Soviet Union Air Defence Forces while performing photographic aerial reconnaissance deep inside Soviet Union territory. The weather improved and on 01 May 1960, 15 days before the scheduled opening of the east–west summit conference in Paris, Captain Powers, flying Article 360, 56–6693 left the base in Peshawar on a mission with the operations code word GRAND SLAM to overfly the Soviet Union, photographing targets including the ICBM sites at the Baikonur Cosmodrome and Plesetsk Cosmodrome, then land at Bodø in Norway. Cannon said, Mr. Chairman, on 01 May 1960 the Soviet Union Government captured, 1300 miles inside the boundaries of the Russian Empire, an American plane, operated by an American pilot, under the direction and control of the U.S. Central Intelligence Agency, and is now holding both the plane and the pilot. Upon his capture, Francis Gary Powers told his Soviet Union captors what his mission had been and why he had been in Soviet Union airspace." .

lsd:Entity_55676 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 2009 San Luis Potosí Challenger was a professional tennis tournament played on outdoor red clay courts. It took place in San Luis Potosí, Mexico between 06 April 2009 and 2009." .

lsd:Entity_55684 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1995 U.S. Women's Open Golf Championship was the 50th United States Women's Open Championship, held July 13–16 at the East Course of Broadmoor Golf Club in Colorado Springs. The 1995 edition was the first million dollar purse at the United States Women's Open Championship, double that of 1990." .

lsd:Entity_55734 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "This Battle of Santa Cruz was a battle fought in the early stages of the Philippine–American War during General Henry W. Lawton's Laguna de Bay Campaign. At 10:30 a.m. on 09 April 1899, landing craft began offloading Lawton's troops south of the stronghold of Santa Cruz. Ashore in the afternoon of 09 April 1899, the troops set out for Santa Cruz in a long skirmish line, advancing through driving rain." .

lsd:Entity_55751 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1969–70 Michigan State Spartans men's basketball team represented Michigan State University in the 1969–70 NCAA Division I men's basketball season as members of the Big Ten Conference. They played their home games at Jenison Fieldhouse in East Lansing, Michigan and were coached by Gus Ganakas in his first year as head coach of the 1968–69 Michigan State Spartans men's basketball team. Prior to the season, on September 10, 1969, the 1968–69 Michigan State Spartans men's basketball team head coach, John E. Benington suffered a heart attack and died after jogging at Jenison Fieldhouse at the age of 47." .

lsd:Entity_55762 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 3rd Magritte Awards ceremony, presented by the Académie André Delvaux, honored the best films of 2012 in Belgium and took place on 2013, at the Square – Brussels Meeting Centre in the historic site of Mont des Arts, Brussels beginning at 8:00 p.m. CET. The nominees for the 3rd Magritte Awards were announced on 2013." .

lsd:Entity_55787 a lsd:Context_narration ;
