lsd:Entity_57154 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The athletics at the 1936 Summer Olympics – men's 400 metres hurdles event at the 1936 Summer Olympic Games took place on 03 August 1936 and 04 August 1936." .

lsd:Entity_57173 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1963 Giro del Trentino was the second edition of the Tour of the Alps cycle race and was held on 22 June 1963." .

lsd:Entity_57184 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Path of Totality Tour was a concert tour in support of Korn's tenth studio album The Path of Totality. Korn: The Path of Totality Tour – Live at the Hollywood Palladium was released on DVD and Blu_ray on September 4, 2012 by Shout!" .

lsd:Entity_5720 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Judicial Procedures Reform Bill of 1937, frequently called the \"court_packing plan\", was a legislative initiative proposed by U.S. President Franklin D. Roosevelt to add more justices to the U.S. Supreme Court in order to obtain favorable rulings regarding New Deal legislation that the Court had ruled unconstitutional. The bill came to be known as Roosevelt's \"court_packing plan,\" a phrase coined by Edward Rumely. The legislation was unveiled on 05 February 1937, and was the subject of Roosevelt's ninth Fireside chat on 1937. This reversal came to be known as \"the switch in time that saved nine\"; however, recent legal_historical scholarship has called that narrative into question as Roberts' decision and vote in the Parrish case predated both the public announcement and introduction of the 1937 bill. A series of Gallup Polls conducted between February and 1937 showed that the public opposed the proposed bill by a fluctuating majority. Reaction against the bill also spawned the National Committee to Uphold Constitutional Government, which was launched in 05 February 1937 by three leading opponents of the New Deal. The administration began making its case for the bill before the Senate Judiciary Committee on 1937." .

lsd:Entity_57214 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Saguntum (25 October 1811) saw the Imperial French Army of Aragon under Marshal Louis Gabriel Suchet fighting a Spain army led by Captain General Joaquín Blake. The Spain attempt to raise the siege of the Sagunto Castle failed when the French, Italians, and Poles drove their troops off the battlefield in rout. Sagunto lies a short distance from the east coast of Spain, about 30 kilometres (19 mi) north of Valencia. On 22 September, Suchet's entire army set out from Castellón de la Plana, brushing aside 500 Spain troops at Villarreal and the next day was before Sagunto Castle. The French marshal divided his army into a blockade force to surround Sagunto Castle and a covering force to defend against Spain interference. Martín's guerillas drove off a 1,000_strong relief column and then the Spain forced the 560 survivors to surrender on 3 25 October 1811 by exploding two mines under the walls. After providing a garrison for Sagunto Castle and detaching a brigade to escort the Spain prisoners to the rear, he had only 15,000 men available for field operations." .

lsd:Entity_57232 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Sheemore ambush was an ambush carried out by the Irish Republican Army (IRA) on 04 March 1921, during the Irish War of Independence." .

lsd:Entity_57265 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 24th Edition 1969 Vuelta a España (Tour of Spain), a long_distance bicycle stage race and one of the three grand tours, was held from 23 April 1969 to 11 May 1969." .

lsd:Entity_57269 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1934–35 FAI Cup was the fourteenth edition of Ireland premier cup competition, The Football Association of Ireland Challenge Cup or FAI Cup." .

lsd:Entity_57272 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The Players Tour Championship 2011/2012 – Event 7 (also known as the 2011 Kay Suzanne Memorial Trophy) was a professional minor_ranking snooker tournament that took place between 05 October 2011–09 October 2011 at the South West Snooker Academy in Gloucester, England." .

lsd:Entity_57294 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1909 World Allround Speed Skating Championships for men took place at 27 February 1909 and 28 February 1909 at the ice rink Gamle Frogner in Kristiania, Norway." .

