lsd:Entity_60984 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "In visual arts, music, and other media, minimalism is an art movement that began in post–World War II Western art, most strongly with American visual arts in the 1960s and early 1970s. The movement is often interpreted as a reaction against abstract expressionism and modernism; it anticipated contemporary postminimal art practices, which extend or reflect on minimalism's original objectives." .

lsd:Entity_60987 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1979 FA Charity Shield was the 57th Charity Shield, an annual English association football match played between the winners of the previous season's Football League and FA Cup. It was held at Wembley Stadium on 11 August 1979. The match was contested by Liverpool F.C., champions of the 1978–79 Football League and Arsenal F.C., who beat Manchester United in the final of the 1978–79 FA Cup. Watched by a crowd of 92,800, Liverpool F.C. won the match 3–1. This was Arsenal F.C.'s tenth Charity Shield appearance and Liverpool F.C.'s ninth. Arsenal F.C. made no changes to the first team which played in the FA Cup Final the previous season, whereas for Liverpool F.C. new signing Avi Cohen started on the bench. Liverpool F.C. qualified for the 1979 FA Charity Shield as winners of the previous season's First Division. Arsenal F.C. began the second half with more vigour, but struggled to suppress Liverpool F.C.'s attacks. Arsenal F.C. manager Terry Neill described the match as a \"great advertisement for association football\" and felt his side contributed greatly in periods." .

lsd:Entity_61033 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 13 September 2008 Delhi bombings were a series of five synchronised bomb blasts that took place within a span of few minutes on Saturday, 13 September 2008 at various locations in Delhi, India." .

lsd:Entity_61063 a lsd:Context_narration ;
    rdfs:label "The 1999–2000 Danish 1st Division season was the 55th season of the Danish 1st Division league championship and the 14th consecutive as a second tier competition governed by the Danish Football Union." .

